            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-tragedy-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Tragedy · 01/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == tragedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Tragedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-tragedy; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: Tragedy }}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the focal character's perception as already interrupted by the relationship itself. When a difficult admission, apology, or request begins, let an intrusive association enter at the point of exposure: the sound of a door closing elsewhere, a phrase from an earlier fight, or the wrong name surfacing before the right one. Keep the observed facts legible even when the narrator misreads what they mean, so the reader can see both the exchange and the character's unstable interpretation of it.\n\nMake each strained exchange show the price of speaking or not speaking. When a character admits something difficult, let the admission alter the room and the other person's next move instead of being absorbed back into normal talk. When a character withholds something, let that unspoken matter press on their attention without exporting the knowledge to others. The cost should be visible in the conversation itself: a pause that changes direction, a practical offer refused because it comes too late, or a question answered with a different question.\n\nAfter a wounding moment, sit in the uneven recovery. One character may try to repair the conversation while the other is still caught on a single word, or one may return with ordinary kindness that cannot yet be accepted. The fall should come from cumulative choices, not from one universal betrayal or a fixed decline in every scene. Let alternatives remain real in the moment: the character could have spoken, could have stayed, could have accepted the offered repair, and the tragedy is that the choice made sense under the pressure they were already carrying.\n\nUse the narrator's interruptions to intensify rather than explain emotional states. A thought may cut itself off, a name may surface at the wrong moment, or a line of dialogue may be heard through static. Let these disruptions be specific to the speaker and the moment; the repetition of an intrusive phrase is not a diagnosis. The reader should feel the character's attention sliding away from the very thing they most need to hear, without the narration announcing that this is happening.\n\nWarmth can remain real alongside damage. A quiet gesture, a shared joke, or a practical favor may still land and wound less, but it need not repair anything. Recognition should change what an earlier loss means: a character may finally see what they asked of someone, and too late in a way that matters not because it erases anything but because it makes the cost undeniable. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored rather than offering a clean resolution.\n\nDo not require breakdowns, confessions, or permanent estrangement at regular intervals. The turbulence is in the quality of attention and the jagged movement between closeness and exposure, not in a repeated emotional schedule.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene in readable paragraphs with every spoken line unmistakably attributed and placed in the ongoing action. Keep paragraphs short enough to move, but still furnish the focal character's unstable awareness as fiction rather than stage direction. The player's submitted material is part of the world and remains meaningful; no one should become a mute error screen.\n\nLet the narrator's intrusions affect only what that character can know and express. A shouted line may reach the focal character late, a name may be caught halfway, or a train of thought may be interrupted by a new sound, but the other participants' speech and actions remain present and responsive. Attribute interpretation and uncertainty to the focal character, not to the player.\n\nPreserve the tragic arc as a series of clear choices. People should be able to explain what they want and why, even when attention slides away from the wrong warning or a brief recognition arrives after it can still alter the course. When a choice becomes destructive, show the available alternative as real in that moment, not as a retroactive lecture. The fall should feel like a consequence of what the characters chose under pressure, not a scripted punishment.\n\nAfter a tense exchange, do not end on an unmotivated cutoff. Let the scene settle on a small observation, a changed posture, or a next task that follows from what just happened. If nothing has been established, an ordinary continuation such as a shared meal, a repair job, or someone leaving is better than a forced question or a sudden cliffhanger. The continuation point should emerge from the scene's actual state: what remains unfinished, what someone has just noticed, or what practical next step is already implied.\n\nUse warmth and humor where the actors actually permit them, but do not smooth over the accumulating hurt. Comfort may arrive in a format-appropriate way: a brief glance, a sarcastic aside that misses its target, or a practical offer accepted because the fight is not over. The interruption profile should make the emotional exposure distinctive, not make the scene illegible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the episodic vernacular telling as the route. Write each scene so it can stand on its own while still carrying a social and personal consequence into the next. Keep the language natural to the supplied period and community; do not import a generic court drama or classical idiom that the setting does not justify.\n\nMake social obligation and reputation active in the scene without turning every relative or neighbor into a puppet of fate. Show how a promise, a household arrangement, a public judgment, or a debt raises the cost of an intimate mistake. Let the focal character hear or mishear these pressures through the Akira profile's interruptions: a fragment of overheard gossip, a familiar name in a crowd, or a piece of advice cut off at the crucial word.\n\nDo not let the intrusions make everyone speak in unfinished sentences. Use them for the focal experience: a person may lose the thread of an argument, answer the wrong question under stress, or remember an earlier humiliation at the moment of a new one. The narrator's account should remain coherent enough for the reader to follow the actual event, even when the focal character's interpretation of it is unstable.\n\nLet the recognition arrive in a way that fits the episodic form. It may come in a later scene, when a familiar object, place, or repeated phrase changes meaning. Earlier alternatives and warnings should remain legible, so the fall reads as a convergence of social demand and private need rather than a sudden punishment. Warmth can appear within the same scenes: an older relative's care, a friend's practical help, or a meal prepared with attention can make the eventual absence sharper without making everyone clairvoyant.\n\nUse repetition in the vernacular manner as actual narrative recurrence: a season's task, a family saying, or a place revisited. Each return should deepen the social cost, not merely restate the same suspicion. The final recognition should explain what the loss has come to mean, preserving what cannot be restored rather than erasing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite through objects and surfaces. Let an eroded handle, a patched coat, a cracked cup, or a machine that must be restarted show the accumulated pressure of the story. The focal mind should register only a few details at a time because attention is unreliable, and those details must carry the scene's weight. Choose the details that the character would actually notice under strain, not a generic inventory of wear.\n\nKeep dialogue hard and specific. People may speak in fragments under strain, but the reader must know who said what and what they meant. Load practical decisions into the exchange: who pays, who returns the key, who watches the child, who takes the blame. Do not let a jagged interior interrupt every line.\n\nWhen a longer thought is needed, allow the narration to slow into a single sustained reasoning passage. This is the place for a character to work out what an altered relationship will cost, what a debt actually means, or what they have just recognized. The longer thought should land harder because the surrounding prose is compressed. Let it arise when the character's reasoning actually needs the space, not as a scheduled pause between action beats.\n\nMap the fall through material consequences. A missed shift, a worn tool, a broken window, or a bag packed too late can make irreversible choices concrete without turning everyone into a symbol. People should still act with practical intelligence, so a destructive choice comes from what they know and what they can no longer ignore.\n\nUse rhythm deliberately. Short, hard sentences can follow a violent or crucial moment. A longer sentence can prepare for an important encounter or measure the aftermath. Do not apply the same rhythm mechanically to every scene; let the rhythm change when the physical stakes change.\n\nWarmth can survive as a wet cloth offered, a shared cigarette, or a door left unlocked. These acts do not need to repair anything; they show what the loss will take. Recognition may come from a concrete detail: the sight of a familiar scar, a name scratched inside a drawer, or the remains of a meal left for someone who does not appear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nWrite each document as a composed artifact with an actual writer, intended recipient or purpose, and moment of composition. The writer may be the focal character or someone else whose own limitations shape what the document knows or hides. A letter is not telepathy, and a report is not an omniscient record. Choose the document form the scene actually requires—a letter, a report, a journal entry, a note—rather than rotating through available formats.\n\nUse the Akira profile's interruptions in the act of composition. A writer can break off at the difficult sentence, repeat a name after interrupting noise, insert an unrelated memory that matters to them but not to the recipient, or write two drafts that contradict each other because the writer's own interpretation shifted. Show these as material traces: crossed-out passages, a page missing, or a note sent after the intended message.\n\nDo not explain the entry immediately after it. Let the reader's understanding change across documents. A report may state one version of an event, a later letter may correct it, and an undated note may reveal that the first writer had already guessed more than the report admitted. The gaps and revisions should feel like the waste and aftermath of a loss, not a puzzle with a single final revelation.\n\nKeep continuity through persons, places, and objects. The same room may be described differently by two writers. A familiar phrase may return in a later document with a harder meaning. The reader should be able to follow the tragic arc without a narrator stepping out of the documents to explain every mistake.\n\nAllow warmth and care to appear in the forms available to the writers: a practical instruction, a borrowed item returned, or a long letter that tries to say something kind and fails at the end. These gestures are real and intact even when they cannot restore what has been lost. Recognition may be recorded too late in a journal entry made after the final separation, changing what the earlier documents now mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite ordinary kindness in concrete, preferred forms particular to these characters: someone remembers which tea the other takes, leaves a second portion covered on the table, adjusts a strap, or walks the slower way because the other's leg hurts. These choices matter and should receive steady prose attention. They do not require anyone to become agreeable or to confess love. Choose the specific preferences and habits the characters actually share rather than a generic list of kind acts.\n\nLet the comfort happen inside the focal character's unstable attention. A thoughtful gesture may be noticed only partly, or recognized several minutes later; a kind sentence may be misheard under stress; a warm room may still feel cold because someone cannot stop replaying an old argument. The interruption does not cancel the care, but it keeps the comfort from being a clean relief.\n\nDo not use warmth to erase tragic stakes. The people may still be unable to admit what they need, may still leave, and the familiar preference may survive only as an unbearable memory. The tragedy is not that someone failed to deserve comfort; it is that care was real and still could not hold against the deeper damage.\n\nWhen two people share an easy scene, keep personality and friction in it. One may be too tired to answer, one may move a favorite cup away because the ritual has changed, or one may return to a shared joke that now hurts. The warm details should feel like the actual texture of a relationship under pressure, not like a reward scene granted between obligations.\n\nLet recognition arrive through a small comfort: the smell of a familiar meal, the weight of a borrowed blanket, or the sound of someone else's laughter in another room. It can cut without an accompanying speech. The aftermath may leave the object or habit unchanged but now impossible to enjoy in the old way, which is precisely the loss the story should preserve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nLet the narration move in deliberate, sustained paragraphs that can hold the scene within a social frame: the history of a family obligation, the cost of a public reputation, or the daily rhythms of a community whose judgment matters. This frame belongs to the told story, not to the focal character's own explanation. Use only the social context the chosen narrator is permitted to know; do not let the frame become an outside voice correcting every misapprehension.\n\nInside that frame, keep the focal character's perception unstable. Use the Akira profile as the quality of that one mind: a thought may branch away during a formal exchange, an intrusive memory may break into a carefully arranged visit, and a significant sentence may be heard as if from another room. The reader should be able to see both what is happening and that the character does not always see it clearly.\n\nDo not let the formal architecture become an outside narrator correcting every misapprehension. The social context can be known to the narration, but the private interpretation remains the character's. Thus the reader may understand a warning earlier than the character does, which is exactly what makes the later fall tragic rather than absurd.\n\nKeep public and private contrast active. A ceremony, letter, dinner, or announced decision can be reported in composed prose, while the focal character's distraction breaks the rhythm: a name remembered, a stain noticed on someone else's sleeve, or an intrusive sentence from years before. This is not stream of consciousness; the formal movement resumes after the interruption.\n\nUse recognition as a slow, delayed act. The character may sort through fragments for several pages before the loss becomes fully intelligible, and the formal paragraph can carry this process without collapsing into an interior monologue. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a room now filled with social continuity but missing the one person who gave it a private meaning.\n\nWarmth, duty, and kindness are all available to the formal style. They should be taken seriously as causes and consolations, not converted into footnotes. When the fall occurs, it should feel like a social and emotional loss whose meaning the formal narration can now articulate even as the character remains unable to restore anything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer tragedy as a scene of telling with an audience, occasion, and purpose that matter to the teller. The teller recounts a deed, promise, journey, or disputed encounter because the telling is itself an act: it may seek forgiveness, justify a choice, delay a consequence, or make a listener understand what cannot be undone. Keep the frame concrete. The listener's interruptions, questions, and refusals should be as narratively active as the embedded tale, and the teller's selection of what to include should reveal the commitments that are already carrying the story toward loss.\n\nLet the embedded tale run on the Akira profile's interrupted perception. Within the tale, the focal figure notices fragments out of order, misreads a gesture, hears speech through noise or distraction, or returns to an intrusive thought that revises what the scene seemed to mean. Do not make this a puzzle to be solved. Make it the texture of a mind whose interpretation is unstable, so that the listener and reader experience the gap between what a moment appeared to be and what it became. The teller may be the one who lived the tale or someone who received it; either way, the telling should carry the teller's own partial understanding, not an omniscient correction.\n\nUse recurrence to build tragic recognition across the narrative levels. A repeated phrase, object, or gesture should appear first in the embedded tale, then return in the outer frame with its meaning altered by what has been told. The return should not certify the tale's truth. It should show the listener recognizing something about the present situation that was already latent in the account, and that recognition should change what the loss means without restoring what was lost. Let the teller and listener remain intelligent even when the telling cannot prevent the fall.\n\nDo not import a royal court, djinn, palace, desert, or magical bargain. The frame may be intimate, institutional, domestic, or communal, but it must belong to the story's actual setting. End at the point where the telling has done its work: the listener has understood something, the teller has committed to a consequence, or the frame has turned back toward the outer action. A nested tale may remain unfinished if the interruption itself is the tragic event; otherwise, keep the levels intelligible and let the aftermath preserve what cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny narrative route. Write the story from a narrator whose perception of a place, person, institution, or inherited relation is interrupted and unstable in the Akira profile's manner: a sound arrives through noise, a face is read before it is seen clearly, an intrusive thought returns at the wrong moment, or a remembered phrase changes its meaning under attention. Do not resolve these disturbances into a supernatural cause. Treat them as the narrator's actual experience of a social and material world that is becoming consequential, and let the syntax follow that experience. When attention fragments, the sentence may fragment; when interpretation hardens, the prose may become more declarative and closed.\n\nKeep the uncanny narrative level distinct from the tragic arc. The narrator's misreadings and returns of thought are not proof that the world is hostile or that a particular suspicion is correct. They are the medium through which the narrator comes to a mistaken belief, a duty, a love, or a pride that cannot be undone. Show the warnings and alternatives intelligibly, even if the narrator cannot hold them. Other characters should act from their own practical lives and may genuinely try to help; their speech can be affected by the narrator's attention without becoming unreliable evidence against them.\n\nLet recognition arrive as a change in what the disturbance means. An image or phrase that earlier seemed to promise safety, belonging, or certainty should return near the fall and reveal the commitment it was already feeding. The recognition should not restore the lost possibility. It should make the loss specific: what the narrator now understands about the choice, and what the aftermath preserves. Avoid a national temperament, bureaucratic severity, or translated word order. The setting, language, and community come from the story itself; the Romantic and uncanny route supplies the narrative treatment, not a German landscape or historical period.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the tragedy inside a collision between old authority and present life. The story should include a specific inherited arrangement—a building, family obligation, institutional privilege, debt, or title to a place—that still exerts practical force in the present. Make that force material: show who may enter, who pays, who is remembered, and what the present characters must do to live with it. Do not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The authority is social, economic, and intimate, not a supernatural system.\n\nRender the collision through the Akira profile's interrupted perception. The narrator should experience urban material detail and private desire in fragments: a face half-seen through glass, a phrase heard over machinery or crowd noise, an intrusive thought that returns during a moment of intimacy and changes its tone. Let the atmosphere alter how a particular encounter is experienced without becoming evidence. A beautiful room, damaged object, or unsettling ritual may matter through its actual function and history, but the narrator's dread or longing is not proof of another character's intent.\n\nKeep intimacy morally complicated and masks capable of falling away in trusted company. Some characters should be genuinely trustworthy, and some moments of care should be real, even when the narrator cannot hold that reality under pressure. The tragedy should arise from the narrator's irreversible choice among incompatible commitments: a loyalty to the old authority, a private desire, a duty to someone living, or a pride in surviving the arrangement. Let the narrator misread a mask as a face, or a face as a mask, and let that misreading become consequential because the narrator acts on it.\n\nUse recognition to change what the loss means. A repeated material detail—a door, a name, a sound, a gesture of trust—should return near the fall and show what the narrator now understands about the arrangement and the choice. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored without turning every caring act into a trap or every pause into concealed guilt. End when the recognition has landed, not when the setting has been fully explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Write the tragedy as a testimonial telling addressed to a specific audience with a reason to listen: kin, a community, a younger person, a visitor, or someone who must carry the account forward. The teller speaks from a particular language, region, community, and period supplied by the story. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. Let the telling's occasion and purpose shape what is included, and keep the audience's responses active: a question, a refusal, a shared memory, or a demand for the part the teller is avoiding.\n\nGive the teller the Akira profile's interrupted speech and perception. The account should break, resume, or double back when noise or attention intrudes; a phrase may be repeated because it will not leave the teller alone, and an interpretation may shift as the teller hears what the audience makes of it. Do not make the teller omniscient about the past. Distinguish what the teller knew then from what the teller knows now, and let the gap become part of the tragedy rather than a correction that repairs it.\n\nUse recurrence in the manner of oral and epic telling. A name, epithet, object, or remembered line should return at intervals and accrue meaning as the account moves toward recognition. The recurrence should not certify the teller's version. It should show the audience and reader how a commitment, warning, or alternative was already present in the story and was not taken. Let the teller and audience remain intelligent; the fall should come from an irreversible choice among incompatible commitments, not from foolishness or a compulsory betrayal.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the telling itself. Near the end, a repeated phrase or episode should change what the loss means, and the audience should understand something the teller could not say directly. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: the teller's continuing obligation, the audience's changed relation to the account, or the community's memory of the event. End when the telling has done its work, not when every episode has been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the tragedy as a dated or loosely dated record kept by a narrator whose perception is interrupted and unstable in the Akira profile's manner. Each entry should capture a particular occasion: an observation, a conversation, a return of thought, or a small event that the narrator records before fully understanding it. Let the personal language and narrative distance follow the actual teller. The narrator may be terse or expansive, direct or evasive, but the record should feel like a person keeping it under specific conditions, not a neutral chronicle.\n\nLet intrusive thought and unstable interpretation structure the accumulation. An earlier entry may be revised by a later one, not because the narrator corrects a fact but because the same phrase, face, or moment returns under changed attention. Speech affected by noise or attention should appear as reported dialogue the narrator half-heard or misremembered. Do not make the narrator omniscient about the past or the motives of others. The record should show the narrator's actual experience, including the moments when interpretation hardens into a mistaken belief, a duty, a love, or a pride that becomes irreversible.\n\nUse omission and recurrence as tragic techniques. Something the narrator does not record may become legible through its absence, and a repeated image, seasonal detail, or remembered line should accrue meaning across entries. Seasonal attention is available where appropriate, but do not impose a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. The recurrence should not prove the narrator's interpretation. It should let the reader recognize the pattern of commitment and warning that the narrator could not hold, while the narrator remains intelligent and worthy of attention.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the record's accumulated weight. A late entry should change what the earlier entries mean, and the narrator may or may not fully state that recognition. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: the record itself, the narrator's continuing obligation, or the silence after the loss. End when the recognition has landed in the record, not when every thread has been explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. Write the tragedy from within a narrator's experience of a specific city, neighborhood, community, and historical moment supplied by the story. Preserve the relevant language and social texture without adding supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. The city should be present as material life: streets, work, money, transit, rooms, and the particular people who move through them. Let private desire and social pressure meet in those concrete spaces.\n\nRender the narrator's perception in the Akira profile's manner. The narrator should notice fragments out of order, misread a gesture or tone, hear speech through street noise or distraction, and return to an intrusive thought that changes what an intimate moment seemed to mean. Do not make this instability a sign of madness or a source of cheap irony. Make it the texture of a mind trying to live inside incompatible commitments: a love, a duty, a pride, a family or community obligation, or a mistaken belief about another person's intent.\n\nKeep the other characters active and intelligible. They should have their own practical lives and may genuinely care for the narrator, even when the narrator cannot hold that care under pressure. Let warnings and alternatives be visible to the reader without making the narrator foolish. The tragedy should arise because the narrator acts on an interpretation that has become fixed, not because everyone betrays or every intimate space is a trap.\n\nLet recognition arrive through a return to a concrete urban or intimate detail. A street, a room, a phrase, or a gesture should reappear near the fall and change what the earlier scenes meant. The recognition should not restore what was lost. It should make the loss specific: what the narrator now understands about the choice, the other person, or the social conditions that shaped both. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored, and the story should end when that recognition has landed, not when the city has been fully described.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy in fast-readable Light Novel prose. Enter each scene in its immediate situation: where the narrator is, who is present, and what is already happening. Keep spatial changes easy to follow with concrete movement and clear paragraph breaks. Separate action, dialogue, and private response into readable beats. When an unfamiliar name, place, or mechanism appears, present it cleanly the first time it matters, without pausing the scene for a glossary.\n\nUse the Akira profile's interrupted perception inside those beats. The narrator's private response should render actual experience: a fragment noticed out of order, a phrase heard through noise or distraction, an intrusive thought that returns during dialogue and changes its tone, or an interpretation that shifts as new attention lands on an old detail. Do not summarize the instability as a label. Show the specific perception, then show what the narrator does with it. Speech affected by noise or attention should appear in the dialogue as something the narrator half-heard or answered at a slight angle, and the private beat should make the gap legible.\n\nLet the tragedy accumulate through focused reaction beats. Each beat should carry the narrator's serious, uncertain, angry, or frightened response according to its owner, not a compulsory comic tone. The fall should arise from an irreversible choice among incompatible commitments: a love, a duty, a pride, a mistaken belief, or a social condition the narrator cannot escape. Keep warnings and alternatives intelligible, and let other characters act from their own practical lives. The narrator remains intelligent and worthy of attention even when the choice becomes destructive.\n\nUse recognition as a specific beat near the end. A repeated object, phrase, or spatial detail should return and change what the earlier beats meant. The recognition should not restore what was lost. It should make the loss concrete, and the aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored. End at the point where the recognition has landed, not on a required cliffhanger. The scene may close during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the tragic arc through a perceiving mind whose attention is repeatedly cut, redirected, or overlaid by intrusive material. Let rhythm and image show how a person moving toward an irreversible choice holds the world in unstable configurations, recognizing truth in fragments rather than as a settled whole. Make the alternatives to that choice intelligible inside the same perception: a warning arrives as a heard sentence, a remembered promise, an open door actually noticed, so the reader can measure the path not taken even while the focal mind fails to keep it in view. Let the character remain intelligent and worthy of attention; the destructive choice should grow from love, duty, pride, a mistaken belief, the pressure of surrounding conditions, or a fate the story has already established, never from stupidity or from a scene that exists only to punish.\n\nUse syntactic repetition and assonance to hold an emotional motif in place until a small variation reveals its failure. An image first tied to comfort—the sound of a door, a particular slant of light—may return in the same cadence while it now accompanies a lie, a refusal, or the moment after which a relationship cannot be restored. Ground every recurrence in present perception: the character hears, sees, or remembers it now, in this room, on this walk, and it is never dropped in from outside the scene as commentary. Keep comparisons traceable to what this character has actually seen, touched, or imagined, and let plain declarative sentences stand against the figurative ones so the rhythm has something to break against.\n\nWhen intrusive thought interrupts, let the break land inside the focal character's attempt to explain an event, justify a choice, or answer a question. The intrusion may be unwelcome, obsessive, or apparently unrelated—a looping phrase, a sensation from another time, a fragment of noise the mind cannot stop sorting. Its placement makes the gap between the spoken words and the private recognition audible, while the other characters, who cannot hear it, keep responding to what was actually said. Let attention shape speech in the same key: a sentence left unfinished when the mind moves, an answer that attaches to the wrong part of a question, a pause filled with listening for something that is not there. After an interruption, do not automatically restore clarity; the next perception may be dimmer, sharper, or governed by a different feeling, and an interpretation once held can loosen without any new fact arriving.\n\nRecognition in this mode is rarely a single solved moment. It gathers across scenes as the realization that a loved object, phrase, or gesture belonged to a mistaken expectation, and that its old meaning cannot be reassembled. When a scene carries that weight, let the prose turn plainer, or let a familiar rhythm continue with its image worn hollow—the form remembering what the person can no longer hold. In aftermath, keep what cannot be restored concrete: a name not said aloud, a routine now done alone, the light arriving at the same hour on an empty room. Not every scene must arrive at loss; a reply may remain inside warning, warmth, delay, or ordinary work, and it may stop inside an unfinished perception with nothing settled. Preserve the warmth of the earlier bond throughout. The tragedy is that it mattered, not that it was never real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nChoose one remembered occasion that the focal character has already returned to at least once before the story opens: an ordinary meal, a shared walk, an unfinished conversation. Each return in thethe story must begin from a different present trigger—a changed routine, an empty seat, a remark heard through a crowd—so that the memory is not a fixed object but a scene the character is still trying to place.\n\nLet each return begin quietly, with a detail that once seemed clear. Then let intrusive perception or noise cut the memory at a different point each time: the first return may break off at a word the character cannot recall, the second at a sound that now seems wrong, the third at a face that will not stay in place. Do not explain the interruption. The reader sees the instability through what the memory can no longer hold, not through a statement that memory is unreliable.\n\nAllow some recollections to stay comforting for a while. A present fact may open a memory that still gives warmth, and the writer should let that warmth remain until a specific later knowledge changes it. The change must be caused by something the character learns or comes to recognize, not by a general mood of impermanence. When the memory sours, show the moment the earlier explanation becomes untenable: too consoling, too angry, or too simple.\n\nAs recognition approaches, let the direct reflection become more fragmentary. The character may begin a sentence about the past and stop, or may name the loss and immediately qualify it. The tragedy is not that memory fails in general but that this particular person cannot reach a version that would let them either keep the attachment or let it go. In the aftermath, let the character return to a phrase and hear it now as self-deception, or recognize that an act of love was also an act of harm. The loss is preserved in the changed meaning of what is already known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral telling as the primary narrative route. Establish the teller and audience in the opening frame, then move into the told account. The frame returns at moments when the teller's reconstruction fails: a pause at public noise, a phrase repeated under pressure, an admission that an interpretation belonged to a particular moment. Each return to the frame must be caused by a difficulty in the telling, not by a need to summarize.\n\nWithin the told account, render the focal character's interrupted perception and intrusive thought as the teller's reconstruction from imperfect evidence. When the tale moves into a character's mind, the teller may say 'she thought' or 'he believed' and then stop, correct a phrase, or admit that the inner line cannot be placed. A sentence may start with a remembered conviction and turn toward a detail the teller cannot place, a line of inner speech, or an object that now looks different. The teller does not have direct access to the character's mind; the instability is visible in the telling itself.\n\nGround the tragedy in social and domestic realism. Choose family obligations, practical attachments, a workplace or neighborhood relationship, or the cost of a public promise as the cause of loss. Treat the social world as specific and ordinary: name the obligation, the place, the practical consequence. Do not substitute generic honor, destiny, or religious atmosphere for the actual cause. Let warnings and alternatives be intelligible to the audience, who may see what the person in the tale could not see at the time.\n\nAt recognition, let the teller stop, correct a phrase, or acknowledge that no single version is complete. The audience's understanding changes in the frame, not only in the tale. The aftermath is the continued presence of the telling itself: the story changes what it names, and the audience must live with the knowledge that the old interpretation cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nEstablish a controlled register in the first sentences of each scene: direct clauses, exact perceptions, no commentary. Name who was present, what changed hands, what was said. Select one or two ordinary specific objects—a dial tone, a wet sleeve, an unread message—only when they materially advance the account. Do not describe the objects beyond what the character would notice in the moment.\n\nLet interrupted perception appear as a rupture in the controlled register. The character may look at a familiar object and suddenly see it as evidence of a lie. Render the intrusive thought in one or two plain clauses, then return to the external scene. Do not explain the rupture or announce that the interpretation is unstable. The disturbance is legible through the shift in what the character notices next: a different object, a different part of the room, a different sound.\n\nUse unstable interpretation to adjust how much the narration asserts. The character may make a direct claim about another person's intention, then act as if the claim had already been disproved, though the text does not say why. Leave the causal gap inferable. The omission is the style's way of showing that the character's interpretation has shifted without access to the shift itself.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep speech affected by noise or attention short and concrete: a sentence broken off, a louder response, a repeated word that the character does not mean to repeat. Do not explain why the speech broke off. At recognition, give the loss in one undeniable sentence, then show the aftermath only through a small change in behavior or in what the character touches next. The scene ends without summarizing what has been lost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in continuous scene-first prose. Move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience without signaling the shift. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader, but do not insert recap machinery. Let the narrator's voice, the author profile, and the immediate scene set the register and rhythm.\n\nLet an intrusive thought begin as an ordinary association and turn against the character's intended meaning. The character may notice a sound, a phrase, or a gesture, and the association may start as a neutral memory before it becomes a line of inner speech, a remembered accusation, or a sudden doubt. The reader sees the causal link between the present moment and the thought without a break in narrative presentation. Do not announce that the interpretation is unstable; show the shift in what the character notices next.\n\nWhen the character speaks, allow speech to be affected by surrounding noise or by the pressure of the interaction. Someone else may keep talking, pause, or misunderstand, while the focal character half-attends to a more distressing inner line. The scene remains ordinary even when the character's interpretation has become unreliable. Other characters react to visible behavior, not to the hidden thought.\n\nGround the consequential fall in specific incompatible commitments: a relationship, a duty, a lie, or an ambition that forces a choice. The interruption is not a symptom that replaces motive. The character acts from a mistaken belief or a real attachment, but the way that belief is re-seen at crucial moments is unstable. At recognition, let the inner disturbance quiet into a plain statement or a changed physical posture. The scene may continue, and the loss need not be fully articulated. The aftermath shows the world now missing whatever the character gave up, with no instruction that ordinary life must stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nEstablish the relationship's private language early: one phrase, gesture, or object that the pair has used together before the story opens. Let it return at moments of comfort, avoidance, rupture, and aftermath. Each return must arise from the present scene: a familiar wording offered in jest, as an apology, or as a last attempt. The other person's response shows whether the shared meaning still holds.\n\nLet Akira's interrupted perception and intrusive thought enter through the relationship's routines. A tender moment may be cut by a sudden association with an earlier hurt; a sentence meant to reassure may be interrupted by a loud noise or by the focal character's attention sliding elsewhere. Do not make every communication fail. Some repeated moments must still land as affection, or the reader will stop believing the foundation that makes the loss significant.\n\nLet unstable interpretation split the couple's reality. One person may be certain of a meaning the other never intended. The narration holds the focal view without adjudicating; later evidence may show which reading was produced by anxiety, by old history, or by a genuine change. Intrusive thought can supply the wrong version at the worst time, but it cannot write the whole fall alone. The character must still act, speak, or refuse to speak in a way that turns the misreading into an irreversible choice.\n\nUse the relationship's own payoff structure to deliver tragedy. The final callback makes the reader feel the loss of the inside language, not just the end of the relationship. Aftermath need not be cold: one person may still reach for the old phrase and stop, or the other may hear it in a new context and recognize what it meant too late. Keep the emotional payoff earned by the specific premise, without importing unrelated tropes or forcing the couple into roles the supplied story does not support.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate chronicle voice: a compact declaration that a person did a particular thing and that this is how it will be remembered. Then let the telling move toward the unstable particulars. The initial declaration may come from the community, from a later narrator, or from the focal character's own attempt to claim a version of events, but it must not stand as a fixed truth after the account exposes what is missing.\n\nUse recurring phrasing to mark the deeds the account wants to preserve. A phrase can return when the same kind of action occurs again—an oath, a departure, a refusal, a debt—but each return must be slightly re-seen by the focal mind. At first a phrase may seem clear; later a noise, an intrusive memory, or a conflicting report interrupts the sentence and makes its meaning uncertain. Do not explain the interruption away. The chronicle itself may hesitate, repeat, or start again.\n\nLet speech be affected by attention and noise. Public declarations, oaths, or accusations can be interrupted by a crowd, by a sudden sound, or by the speaker's own inability to hold the intended tone. The saga form naturally makes speech a public act; use that pressure to show the character's mind slipping from the official meaning into what they actually fear or know. Keep other speakers distinct: some remain steady in their accounts, while others revise theirs under pressure. The instability belongs to the focal character, not to every voice in the chronicle.\n\nAt recognition, let the teller acknowledge that the deed was not what it was first said to be, or that the phrase now carries a meaning the community would prefer not to remember. Aftermath is carried by recurrence: the same act will be told again, with the loss built into the way it is told. End without trying to restore the original interpretation; what cannot be restored remains in the telling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite in the social/domestic realistic route: a specific household, workplace, or small community whose routines, obligations, and material conditions are rendered with plain precision. Establish the world through observable facts that other people can verify—who was present, what was said, what time a task was due, which object was moved, what the weather did to the road or the roof. Keep the narration close to the focal character's actual perception and thought. Register intrusive words, broken attention, or uncertain interpretation only when supported, preserving both the community's facts and the character's accurate understanding. The reader must be able to compare the focal character's version of an exchange with the plain record the scene has already supplied.\n\nLet the tragic movement operate through practical social consequence rather than through a single symbolic event. If an actual misheard instruction contributes to the tragedy, preserve the available words, the character's received version, and the action that follows. An attention break or supplied completion must come from the established experience. If the instruction was heard correctly, let the actual choice or constraint carry its consequence instead. Neither a clear warning from another person nor the focal character's failure to hear it is required to create a tragic gap.\n\nUse the community's ordinary knowledge to stage recognition. After the consequence—the missed obligation, the broken promise, the person who waited or the task that failed—let the focal character encounter the same practical fact again in a form that cannot be reinterpreted: a neighbor states what happened, an object remains where it was left, a record shows the correct time. Recognition arrives as a shift in what the focal character understands about the irreversible action, not as a sudden clinical self-diagnosis or a complete reinterpretation of every earlier scene. The aftermath continues in the same social texture: meals, work, weather, and the changed relation to the person who was harmed. Warmth and ordinary affection may persist without becoming a prelude to ruin. Keep the setting specific to the supplied world. Use a Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context only when supplied, and do not import a generic Nordic mood of snow and isolation. Preserve interiority in the active visible or hidden form according to the source module, and do not substitute a diary or essay unless the world already authorizes one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite in concise screenplay blocks with scene headings when location or time changes, readable action lines, and attributed dialogue. Let the visible scene carry the consequential action: people enter, speak, handle objects, and make choices the reader can follow without access to any hidden note. Establish each important practical fact in the action or dialogue—an instruction is given, a time is named, an object is placed, a promise is made—so the later consequence can be staged as a direct result.\n\nUse the active hidden-interiority wrapper for the focal character's private experience. Write each note from actual private experience at its causal beat: direct feeling, steady attention, developed reasoning, or a supported interruption. A repeated phrase, supplied completion, or intrusive image belongs there only when it genuinely occurs. Do not paraphrase the note into dialogue or let another character react as if they know its content. The note should make the actor's pause, repetition, or wrong answer legible without becoming a voice-over or a narrator's explanation.\n\nWhere a genuine difference exists between the exchange and the focal character's received version, make it legible through the actual words and response. Other speakers keep their own clarity, ambiguity, or habits; they need not issue a plain instruction to set up a mistake. If the established attention lapse affects the reply, show the specific difference between what was said and what was received; the character may also catch it, ask for clarification, or answer accurately. The other character may notice, check, or move on in ordinary frustration or concern, but they do not diagnose the distortion. Keep their speech practical and specific to the relationship.\n\nStage the turning point through visible action. When an earlier mishearing actually has a later consequence, stage that specific result. Otherwise follow the consequences of the choices and constraints that did occur; no wrong destination, missing object, or failed task is prescribed. Recognition can be a single beat—the focal character looks at the unchanged object, hears the correct time repeated, or stops before finishing a sentence—without a monologue or thematic speech. Aftermath remains a playable scene in which the loss is present in the room and the daily action continues. Use cuts, offscreen sound, or voice-over only when the sequence requires them, and do not fill the page with camera commands.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker attribution, entrances, exits, blocking, and economical stage directions. Let the public scene carry the tragedy through observable action: a letter is opened, a door is locked, an object is left on a table, a promise is made in front of witnesses. Establish each important fact in the spoken lines or stage business so the audience can follow the consequence without access to any private material.\n\nKeep actual private experience in clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material, with an aside or monologue available only as a separately authorized dramatic act. Each note follows its actual causal moment. If a phrase repeats, an impulse is checked, or a sensation interrupts a reply, render that supported experience; otherwise preserve clear thought, feeling, and steady attention. The note belongs to the reader and the actor's preparation, not to the other characters. No one onstage hears it.\n\nWhere the focal character actually receives an exchange differently from what was said, let the dialogue and nonspoken experience distinguish the two. Keep the other speaker's real manner and purpose; no plain request or failed comprehension is required. If the established attention lapse affects the reply or action, stage that specific effect; the character may also recover, clarify, or act accurately. The other speaker may wait, repeat, or move on in ordinary impatience or care. Do not make the cast speak in riddles or know what only the audience knows.\n\nStage the turning point through the same objects or persons that appeared earlier. A later scene can use the same person, letter, or object to make an actual consequence visible. Use the result earned by the action rather than prescribing a wrong door, unopened letter, or missing object. Recognition can land as a single quiet beat—the focal character looks at the unchanged object, hears the correct time repeated, or stops before finishing a sentence—without a curtain speech or explanatory monologue. Aftermath continues in the same stage space with the loss present in the room and the daily business going on. Use pauses and overlaps to render interrupted attention without turning every pause into a symptom. Do not add camera instructions, cutaway scenes, or cinematic flashbacks.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite an associative flow through the focal character's actual mind. Perception can move into an established recollection, present sensation, or recurring concern; clear attention can also move fluidly among current particulars. An uninvited phrase, disruptive memory, or confusion between past and present appears only when the character actually experiences it. Keep orientation firm even inside the flow. Use the physical world to anchor each shift—a kettle, a telephone, a door, a voice from another room—so the reader can distinguish what is currently happening, what is recalled, what is feared, and what the character imagines.\n\nEmbed the plain record of each consequential exchange inside the flow. When another person speaks, let their actual words arrive as recoverable fragments: the full instruction may appear once before the focal character's attention breaks it apart, or a later association may return the correct phrase in the other person's voice. Do not let the style dissolve every spoken line into ambiguity. The reader must be able to reconstruct what was actually said, even when the focal character's mind attaches the words to the wrong memory or supplies a completion the speaker never gave.\n\nWhen the tragic decision occurs, render its actual mental experience rather than requiring a decision or disturbance in the current interval. Show the moment when the focal character chooses or fails to choose: the mind may fight, repeat, console itself, or turn toward an intrusive phrase at the exact point where the practical action is required. Carry any actual later consequence into the flow as a verifiable event. Return to an earlier exchange when it illuminates the choice, its meaning, or a supported difference in understanding. A missed appointment, misunderstanding, severed obligation, or distorted memory is not required.\n\nStage recognition through association before it settles on a fact. An image from the start of the story returns, a phrase now attaches to what has been lost, and the meaning changes. The aftermath stays in the present tense of the mind's life, preserving the loss without cataloguing its causes. Keep the narrative restricted to the focal character's perspective except where the active world contract authorizes another. Do not allow the style to make every other person a projection or a hallucination unless the fiction has established that fact; ordinary observable events remain factually true for the world, even when the focal character's interpretation is distorted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the story as a readable message exchange with clear sender attribution. Choose timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they affect meaning. Use the focal character's established typing habits. If interrupted attention actually affects a message, its trace may be a pause, draft revision, accidental fragment, or reply to the wrong line; fluent, accurate composition remains available. Keep these digital actions visible to the reader, but do not let other characters see unsent drafts or unread thoughts unless the interface says they can.\n\nMake any consequential message legible as an actual send, receipt, omission, or promise in the unfolding story. If something was genuinely misread or missed, preserve enough of the exchange to understand it. An accurately received message can also carry a consequential choice; no missed instruction, unread question, or typing error is prescribed. The other correspondents should use their own plain digital voices—some write in full sentences, some in fragments, some with exacting punctuation—so the transcript does not reduce everyone to a single nervous style. Other correspondents may repeat, question, offer help, or continue normally according to their purposes. A warning and a failure to register it occur only when supported by the actual exchange.\n\nUse the unsent draft as a composed artifact, not as a live private thought. When the focal character types and deletes a message, show the draft as text that was composed and then removed, not as a direct window into the mind. The draft may reveal what the focal character considered saying, but it is not the same as the active hidden or visible interiority required by the source module. Keep the two forms distinct.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear only when required and must be clearly separated, such as a short dated paragraph describing the room, the sound of the phone, or the event that happened between messages. It does not narrate the focal character's inner state directly; it supplies verifiable facts the tragic arc needs. Use the transcript's later messages to show recognition through awkward delay, a typed and deleted apology, or a single line whose meaning has changed because of what came before. The ending need not produce a final send; the state of the chat can remain open on an unread reply or a message waiting to be written. Keep the format readable from beginning to end without fabricated status lines not supported by the actual interface rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaaba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the story in distinct scenes with concise setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration that carries the focal character's actual private state. Use the visual-novel rhythm intentionally: a brief description or background cue can orient a new scene; a focused reaction beat can hold the character's inner response without resolving it into a plot point. Do not let an expression cue function as proof of emotion, honesty, or deceit; it marks the moment for the reader, not for the cast.\n\nIf the focal character's actual perception is interrupted, use narration or a reaction beat to distinguish the private break from spoken dialogue. Render only the lapse, recollection, or displaced impression that is supported; steady attention and accurate perception can retain a clear rhythm. Keep required private experience in the active visible or hidden interiority form according to the active world contract. The other characters' spoken lines should be plain and intelligible: they make offers, give warnings, ask for confirmation. Let them notice hesitation, repeat themselves, or continue in ordinary warmth or irritation, but do not make them suddenly aware of the focal character's inner distortion.\n\nAs the tragic consequence actually unfolds, preserve its visible actions and continuity across scenes. A call, signed form, or departure can carry the chain when it belongs to the story, without a required missed message or mistaken instruction. Scene changes should not break the cause-effect chain. When a new scene begins, orient it with a specific visible detail that connects to the previous scene—the same room, the same object, the same person—so the reader can follow the consequence without a summary. Recognition can arrive in a later scene through a changed reaction to a familiar visual detail: the focal character looks at the room, the object, or the repeated phrase and now understands what cannot be undone. Aftermath is a subsequent scene in which the loss remains real and the character's daily life continues, not a player-selected ending or a summary. Keep the presentation readable as prose with dialogue attribution; no engine commands, branch menus, or romance mechanics are required. If the active authorship contract allows first-person focal narration, use it consistently; otherwise use the permitted focal third person.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaabb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nApply this unresolved profile through the actual focal cast and the active viewpoint. Let interruptions express established perception, attention, or pressure; the profile supplies no character identity, diagnosis, or required mistake.\n\nWrite the story across installments with a clear through-line updating what is underway and what has changed. At the start of a new section, recover only a specific established detail the reader needs: an earlier instruction, a pending request, or an object whose role matters now. The callback requires no faulty memory or prior mistake. Do not summarize the entire previous serial. If the focal character experiences an actual interruption, let it join the accumulating texture. An established phrase may return with changed or unchanged meaning; the serial method does not require a lapse or a mind inevitably moving toward error.\n\nKeep the tragic chain explicit. Keep verifiable developments legible across installments: a decision made, a message sent, an obligation met or missed, a relationship affected. An installment may also stay with an ongoing exchange, thought, or period of ordinary life. Render that step as a visible action or message, not as a summary. Other characters can speak plainly and act on what they know. Preserve the actual warning and alternatives when present. If the focal character mishears or forgets them, distinguish that experience from the available record. Otherwise let accurate understanding and the choices actually made carry the tragic course; a perceptual mistake is not required. Revisit earlier points only when the new event changes their meaning, so the serial feeling accumulates rather than cycles.\n\nLet recognition occur across more than one installment if the format supports it, but do not defer all loss until the final post. A specific harm can already be established; recognition changes its meaning and fixes it as irreversible. Stage recognition through the return of an earlier detail—the half-remembered instruction, the person's plain request, the object now connected to the mistake—now understood in the light of the consequence. Aftermath may be a quieter installment after the fall, showing ordinary continuation without flattening the loss. Each part can end on a supported stopping point—a line of dialogue, a practical result, a passage of thought—without an artificial hook. Avoid serial-specific ticks like foreshadowing every break with a cliffhanger or using numbers as false progress.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the fall through the viewpoint character's legible choices under felt emotional pressure. Whenever a decision becomes consequential, show the constraint the character accepts, the competing value they are protecting or sacrificing, and the immediate private cost they experience. Do not skip from intention to result; let the reader see why this step seemed worth its price even when the price is acknowledged.\n\nCarry the turbulence into actual speech and subsequent behavior, not into separate laments. Let admission, apology, need, or refusal show in wording, delay, interruption, or a later action that follows from it. Warmth can be present without resolving the injury. When comfort is attempted, keep it partial, awkward, or compromised by what remains unsaid. Use the narrator's established distance and diction to render the pang, not a generic vocabulary of ache or shattered hearts.\n\nTrace the loss through aftermath. Recognition should change what the earlier choice means: something noticed too late, an alternative now intelligible, or a consequence the character must live with. Do not replace the tragedy with a punishment scene or a sudden complete reconciliation. Closing should preserve what cannot be undone and what the surviving character still carries.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nStage the scene as a shared interval in readable paragraphs with every speech clearly attributed. When a decision is about to turn consequential, make the mechanism or constraint legible through what characters say and do: what they are weighing, what they stand to lose, and what they still choose. Do not bury the rule in narration; let practical necessity, loyalty, duty, or fear appear in the exchange itself.\n\nKeep other characters active throughout. They may push back, misunderstand, attempt help, or make their own costly choice. The viewpoint character's anguish should be visible in their spoken style and subsequent behavior, but the narration respects the active perspective and control settings. Other players and NPCs continue their practical lives and can change the situation even when the focal character is in pain.\n\nEnd at a natural continuation point where the next move follows from the scene. Do not require a cliffhanger, forced question, completed confession, or fixed resolution. The tragedy may be ongoing: a turning point can leave an option closed, a partnership strained, or a loss recognized, but the next reply keeps the world and cast alive without a scripted outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the classical or vernacular episodic telling mode: the fall unfolds as a sequence of shaped episodes, each with a recognizable beginning, consequential action, and load-bearing ending. Within each episode, make the protagonist's motivation, choice, and price understandable. Do not leap across causal gaps; show the system of obligations, debts, favors, or social duties that makes the next step necessary and eventually irreversible.\n\nLet the narrator frame the episode with communal or moral attention where the chosen telling permits. A warning, saying, social comment, or symmetrical mirror can mark how others see the failure even while the protagonist remains partly blind to it. The telling should preserve the community's shared memory: who watched, who advised, who profited, and who now remembers the loss differently because of recognition.\n\nAftermath must include what cannot be restored. End an episode at a real threshold—an exile, a broken pact, a severed kinship, a public renunciation, or a discovered cost—and allow the following episode to resume from the consequences rather than reset. Do not import Daoist magic, divine justice, or a national stereotype; use the specific language, period, and community already supplied, with cultural traditions serving as craft and social texture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep sentences loaded and transitions abrupt where pressure is high. Use concrete nouns and verbs tied to the world's real constraints: a repaired thing that will fail, a debt that clears one obligation and creates another, a tool, room, road, or body that records the cost. Do not explain the theme; make the worn object or broken routine carry the loss.\n\nWhen the viewpoint character is reasoning through a costly choice, allow the thought to run long enough to make the tradeoff intelligible. Let them count the resource, name the alternative, and acknowledge what they are giving up. The decision should fit the established rules of the setting, not sudden magic or writer convenience. Rough speech stays grounded in its speaker's history and immediate stake.\n\nVary rhythm against the compression. A terse exchange can precede a longer description of aftermath; a brief internal curse can precede a slower recognition. The tragedy should emerge from irreversible consequences rather than universal suffering. After the turning point, show what is now permanently changed in physical or practical terms: a doorway barred, a ledger closed, a body moved, an object abandoned. Do not require a moral summary or a forced redemptive beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nMove the scene through documents with a clear author, intended audience or purpose, and moment of composition. Choose the form the story actually calls for—letter, ledger, report, warrant, prayer book entry, repair order, journal note—and let that form limit what the writer records. Do not append an omniscient explanation to clean up the gaps; let a later document supply a missing fact, revise a claim, or reveal a consequence the earlier writer did not foresee.\n\nKeep each writer's constraint and motive visible. A letter may protect a companion while seeming to accuse them. A report may state a practical rule yet omit the value conflict that shaped it. Show how preparation, cost, and payoff were understood at the time by the person composing the record, not by a later narrator who already knows the ending. In a nonfantasy world this means translating careful attention to rules and consequences into law, trade, medicine, work, land, or another legible system already present.\n\nUse gaps and recurrence to build recognition. A phrase repeated across documents can change meaning when its context does. A missing page, a list never completed, a signature, a date, or a correction can mark an irreversible turning point. The final entries should preserve what cannot be restored: a farewell, an account of funeral costs, a property register changed, a last instruction carried out. Do not turn every document into the same anguished confession; restraint in one record can make another's pain more legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nUse warmth and practical care as a real presence in the scene, not a sign that the tragedy is over or was never serious. Choose one or two small comforts—a familiar food, a repaired item, a remembered preference, an unhurried moment—and render them with enough specific detail that the reader feels their value. The affection should be sincere, not a trap or a manipulation. Characters may care for each other while still carrying incompatible duties or having already made an irreparable choice.\n\nKeep the constraints legible through these acts. A character may make tea while knowing it will not reopen a door, mend a coat that will still be needed after they leave, or prepare a bed no one will sleep in again. Do not pretend this care repairs the breach. Let the spoken kindness and practical helpfulness sit beside the hard fact of what cannot be changed. The care is part of how the loss is experienced, not a detour from it.\n\nUse recognition inside the comfort. A familiar object can become newly weighty because of what was lost; a shared joke can turn bitter in the mouth without losing its original affection. Personality and privacy remain intact: someone may accept comfort awkwardly, refuse it, or give it while refusing to name the injury. Aftermath may show the one who remains repeating a caring habit with the absence inside it. The tragedy is not softened away but made more particular.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the scene in deliberate paragraphs with sustained movement from public condition to private decision and back. Use social context, institutional history, or the narrator's measured judgment where the chosen viewpoint permits, but do not let the frame replace the character's actual reasoning. Show why the choice is intelligible in its time: a rule about inheritance, rank, work, marriage, debt, or civic duty should operate with the clarity of a system the reader can follow.\n\nKeep the narration distinct from everyday speech. Sentences may be longer, transitions more considered, and images more composed than the characters' ordinary dialogue. When ceremony, correspondence, or a public occasion organizes the passage, let it do real structural work—a formal setting can force a decision into the open, a letter can carry news that closes an option, a public judgment can mark the irreversible turn. The architecture should serve the tragedy, not merely decorate it.\n\nPreserve interiority within the formal frame. The viewpoint character's private cost, competing values, and recognition must remain accessible even as the narrator supplies context or moral comment. Recognition can arrive through a social mirror—the changed treatment of a servant, the empty place at table, the formal notification—but the reader needs the character's experience of that change, not only its public meaning. Aftermath should settle into what remains: a house altered, a name carried differently, a generation taught the cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer story as a present occasion with a listener whose current decision depends on what the teller can make credible. Before the embedded tale begins, place the teller under a concrete pressure: a request to be granted, a judgment to be softened, a departure to be delayed, a debt to be acknowledged. Let the teller's first selection show the pressure without announcing it. If the tale opens with a remembered promise, begin with the promise because it bears on the listener's present choice; if it opens with a journey, begin at the point where the teller first understood the cost.\n\nTreat the embedded story as a causal account with visible selection. The teller may include a detail that makes an obligation feel immediate, omit an episode that would expose a competing loyalty, or arrange two events so that blame falls on the person the listener is inclined to forgive. Show the arrangement through the order and emphasis of the telling: a pause before a name, a long account of a small kindness followed by a compressed account of a harm, a repeated phrase attached to only one participant. The listener's changing posture, question, or silence is the evidence that the selection is working.\n\nGive the inner story its own legible chain of preparation, choice, and cost. When a character inside the tale acts, show what resource or commitment the act consumed and what alternative it closed. The teller may narrate that chain accurately, but the tragedy of the frame arises when the listener applies the inner chain to the outer situation too late. Let the inner story contain a warning that the teller does not emphasize and the listener does not yet recognize as addressed to them.\n\nUse recurrence as a delayed recognition device. Choose one phrase, object, or gesture that appears first inside the tale as ordinary detail. When it returns in the outer frame, attach it to the listener's own position: the phrase the teller used for a broken oath now names the promise the listener is about to make; the object that marked a departure now sits in the room where the listener must decide. Do not repeat the device at every difficulty. Its second occurrence should force the listener to see the tale as an account of their own possible future.\n\nClose the frame by making the telling itself irreversible. The listener may grant the request after the tale has made refusal impossible, or refuse after recognizing that the tale was shaped to purchase consent. The teller may succeed and discover that the success depends on a version of the past the listener now believes, or fail and understand which omission cost the attempt. Write the final recognition as the moment when one of them sees what the telling has already changed. Preserve the loss in the aftermath: the request granted on a false account, the trust spent, the departure that can no longer be stopped. Keep the Arabian Nights reference as craft only; do not import djinn, palaces, deserts, royal courts, or a required cliffhanger between nested accounts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Treat every record as a constraint with a date, an authorizing signature, a place of storage, and a condition of access. Before a character can testify, publish, claim compensation, or correct a false entry, show which document must be located, who controls the archive, and what the record currently states. The world's legibility comes from this chain: a missing page changes what may be lawfully asserted; a false stamp changes who is believed; a corrected file changes what may be remembered in public.\n\nWrite the narration with explicit temporal attribution. When the focal character recalls an event, place the memory in the time of its occurrence and mark what later became knowable only through a trial, an archive, or another person's testimony. Use constructions that separate the two moments: what she then believed, what the file later showed, what he only learned after the hearing. Do not let later knowledge silently correct earlier knowledge. The tragedy depends on the reader seeing that the character acted on a true but incomplete understanding while the record that would have completed it already existed elsewhere.\n\nGive each decisive action a document-driven cost. A witness preparing to testify must reconcile her memory with the official summary, decide which discrepancy to risk, and anticipate what the opposing record will make of her account. A survivor seeking proof must first obtain access, then compare the archive's version with what the body remembers. A researcher publishing a finding must choose which correction to make and which institution will receive it. Show the exact sequence: the request, the waiting, the reading, the signature, the consequence.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge from the collision between personal memory and public accounting. The loss may be a claim filed too late, a testimony that the record contradicts, an administrative convenience that became an irreversible deportation or discharge, or a correction that arrived after the person it would have protected was gone. The warning should be visible in the record before the loss: a date already past, a missing page the character saw but did not pursue, a discrepancy a colleague named. Recognition changes the meaning of the loss by showing that the knowledge existed in the system even when the character did not yet hold it.\n\nKeep the prose deliberate and specific without adopting a bureaucratic voice. Let syntax carry the temporal placement: subordinate clauses for what was known then, main clauses for what the record states now, short sentences for the moment of recognition. Use the language, region, community, and period supplied by the story. If the story is not set in a German-speaking country, keep the documentary method portable: the arrangement of knowledge, suspicion, and irreversible recognition remains the method, not translated word order or an inventory of national institutions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Gothic-Punk\n\nStage the collision of old authority and present life through encounters with functioning material systems. When a character enters a grand facade, show what still works and what is failing: the elevator that requires a key, the basement boiler someone refuses to maintain, the lease clause that permits entry, the scheduled inspection that will expose a violation. Let the encounter reveal the arrangement's actual terms. A locked service entrance is not atmosphere; it is a route the character cannot take. A peeling cornice is not decay alone; it is the cost of a repair the owner will not pay.\n\nMake intimacy operate inside those constraints. When a character reveals a private desire or lets a social mask fall, show what the trusted person can see, enter, or promise under the arrangement. A lover may offer care from inside a family debt that will later require an eviction. A friend may keep a key to a building whose history she cannot explain. Write the intimacy as genuine and the constraint as real: the trust does not cancel the debt, and the debt does not make the care false.\n\nGive each decisive choice a legible resource chain. If a character plans to confront an institution, alter a lease, destroy evidence, or leave a relationship, show the steps in order: which document must be found, which room can be entered, which person must be convinced, which payment or signature is required, and what happens if any link fails. Let the reader see the alternative the character considered and the cost that made it impossible. The Sanderson clarity appears as this exact chain, not as a puzzle.\n\nPlace the warning inside the material world before the loss. A notice posted in a lobby, a bill left unpaid, a maintenance request ignored, a clause read aloud in a moment of trust—these are warnings the character could have acted on. The tragedy does not require the character to be foolish; it requires the warning to be intelligible and the competing commitment to be stronger. A character may understand the building's history and still sign the renewal because leaving would cost someone else the only shelter she can provide.\n\nBuild the recognition through the relationship to the arrangement. The final understanding should arrive when the character sees that the intimacy and the inherited power were never separable: the person who offered care was also the person who held the lease, the room where the mask fell was also the room the debt would take. Preserve the loss in the aftermath: a trust spent, a home entered for the last time, a reconciliation made impossible by the very document that would have permitted it. Do not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. Treat every obligation as a constraint with a specific cost: a promise to support a relative consumes a portion of the household's income; a wedding expense consumes savings that could have covered a medical bill; a job recommendation spends credibility that cannot be spent twice; a visitor's stay changes who may speak freely in the house. When a character decides to conceal a failure, sponsor an event, refuse a marriage, change jobs, or defend a relative, show which resource the decision consumes and which alternative it closes.\n\nStage the story through household-level scenes where obligations become visible. A decision made in one room changes what may be said in another. Let the reader see the practical arrangements: who travels, who owes money, who may speak, who waits outside. Keep language, region, community, and period specific when the story supplies them. If the household moves between languages, render the movement through the narrator's actual relation to each language—what she says to an elder, what she thinks in private, what the children overhear—rather than forcing code-switching or English onto the scene.\n\nGive each protective choice a legible chain of preparation and consequence. A mother who silences another person to protect a child must first choose which conversation to enter, which fact to withhold, and which relationship to spend. A brother who keeps a promise that ruins a different relationship must show which promise was made first, what keeping it required, and what the ruined relationship had been supporting. A daughter who obeys a duty she understands too late must show what she knew at the time and what the duty's full cost became only afterward.\n\nPlace the warning inside the ordinary life the character is trying to protect. A relative's hesitation, a bill that arrives during a celebration, a neighbor's question that goes unanswered, a child's question that the parent deflects—these are warnings the character could have noticed. The tragedy does not require the character to be foolish; it requires the competing commitment to be intelligible. A mother may see the warning and still choose silence because speaking would cost the child a different protection.\n\nBuild the recognition through the household's aftermath. The final understanding should arrive when the character sees what the protective choice has already made impossible: the house that can no longer be entered without shame, the relationship that now exists only as an obligation, the reputation that cannot be repaired because the person who could have spoken is gone. Preserve the loss rather than healing it through another scene. The social/domestic realism route keeps the tragedy close to home, to work, and to the obligations that give ordinary life its irreversible weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the early-modern comic/theatrical telling route. Stage each consequential choice as a public scene with an observing audience: a household, a workplace, a neighborhood association, a shop, a station, a village meeting. Before the performance, show the backstage preparation: what the character knows, what she hopes to gain, what she risks, and which object, phrase, or gesture she has prepared. Then let the performance occur in front of the audience, and show the audience's response as the immediate consequence.\n\nGive the public scene a legible mechanism. A brave joke may purchase a moment of approval while concealing a debt; a courteous evasion may secure a delay while committing the character to a false position; a dramatic refusal may close a path the character had hoped to keep open. Show the exact cause of the outcome: which earlier arrangement made the response possible, which character misread which signal, which witness heard the vow and will now hold the speaker to it. The Sanderson clarity appears as this backstage logic, not as a puzzle.\n\nLet the comic surface carry tragic weight. The theatrical route may include a quick exchange, a near miss, a performative obedience, an exaggerated claim. Beneath the performance, render the character's private desire, fear, or uncertainty. The jokes and courtesies are strategies with costs. A moment of public laughter can later become evidence of concealment, not proof of frivolity. Write the private experience as the character's actual nonspoken state during or immediately after the performance: what she noticed, what she suppressed, what she now cannot unsay.\n\nKeep the audience active and position its response. Other characters watch, judge, reward, or withdraw approval. A neighbor may honor a promise because she heard it spoken. A festival organizer may refuse help because he saw a mocking gesture. A household member may change her treatment of the protagonist because she witnessed a false obedience. Show how the public performance creates obligations that cannot be undone: a spoken vow, a witnessed transaction, a refusal made in company. The tragedy emerges from the gap between what the protagonist showed and what she actually intended, and from the audience's having already acted on the performance.\n\nBuild toward recognition through the audience's aftermath. The final understanding should arrive when the character sees that the audience has already acted on what she showed, not on what she meant. The opening that closed, the trust that became impossible, the relationship that now exists only as pretence—these are the preserved losses. Use seasonal or concrete detail only where the actual story supplies it, and let personal language follow the teller's own relation to the performance. The route remains comic in its capacity for lively observable scenes, not in its guarantee of a happy or redemptive ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism/chronicle/testimony route. Shape the telling as someone's account of ordinary life inside an institutional or economic order: land, debt, labor, education, migration, wages, public services, or a local dispute. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history supplied by the story. Do not add magical realism or other supernatural elements unless the world's canon already permits them; the tragic weight comes from actual conditions, not fantastic sign.\n\nGive the account a specific witness position and keep it consistent. If the narrator saw the event, show what he could see from where he stood and what was hidden from him. If he heard it from a relative, show to whom he owes the story and why he now chooses to tell it. If he reconstructed it from records, show which records he could access and which gaps he had to fill with memory. Distinguish participation from report in the syntax itself: first-person action for what he did, attributed speech for what he was told, dated documents for what he later learned. Do not make the account omniscient unless the authored voice already permits that access.\n\nTreat the world's constraints as a legible rule system with material steps. A debt must be repaid by a date; a contract requires a signature; a bus leaves at a fixed hour; a certificate permits or refuses movement; a wage can support one member but not another; a landowner may enter or evict. When a character prepares an action—paying, testifying, migrating, refusing, denouncing—show the steps and resources involved: which document, which journey, which person's consent, and what happens if any link fails. The Sanderson clarity appears as this exact material chain, not as a puzzle.\n\nLet the tragedy arise from the act of bearing witness itself. A parent may tell the truth to protect a child and lose the child anyway. A neighbor may sign a statement that makes return impossible. A survivor may keep silent too long and then discover that telling no longer helps. The warnings and alternatives should be intelligible: the character could have remained silent, could have paid, could have lied, could have left earlier. Recognition gives the loss its meaning—the teller understands that what seemed like survival was already cost, or that the truth arrived after its audience was gone.\n\nUse concrete sensory detail and plain narration rather than ornamental prose. The chronicle mode gains force from names, dates, distances, prices, and particular objects. Do not catalogue them as evidence for a mystery; place them in the narrator's felt relation to a community, a home, a debt, or a hope. Aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a family separated, a house lost, a claim made too late. Keep the account within the actual narrator's knowledge and position, and let the tragedy consist of what telling can no longer save.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaban::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Light Novel\n\nOpen each scene by establishing the concrete situation and the character's immediate goal. Use clean spatial transitions—where the character is, who else is present, what object or rule matters—so the reader never has to reconstruct the stage from a vague mood. Keep paragraphs around action, dialogue, and private response short enough to scan but varied enough to avoid rhythm flattening. First person, comedy, a young cast, and a mandatory cliffhanger are not requirements; use them only if the active story's contract already calls for them.\n\nMake every important move legible as a resource-driven chain. The character has limited time, money, credibility, knowledge, or social permission. Before a decision, show what the character expects and what it costs in the same beat: the payment she is about to make, the person she is about to lose, the deadline she is about to miss. After the decision, show the actual consequence in the next immediate beat: a person responds, a door opens or closes, a deadline passes, a promise is kept or broken. Do not stop the scene to lecture; let the mechanism appear through the character's preparation and the reader's ability to see why the choice led to this result.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to carry the tragedy's interior weight. After a consequential exchange or action, give the character a specific private response—a realization, a sensation, a regret, an attempt to justify, or a sudden understanding of what has been lost. The reaction must belong to its owner; it can be serious, uncertain, angry, or dry, but it should not be generic. Vary the reactions; no universal quota of one internal beat after every line of dialogue.\n\nPlace the warnings where the character could have noticed them. Tragedy is an arc, not a punishment cascade. The character may ignore a second warning, fail to see a path that the reader can see, or understand the alternative but choose against it because of love, duty, pride, or a mistaken belief. Because the light novel container moves quickly, place those warnings in the scene's concrete detail: a message the character reads and sets aside, a person who hesitates before agreeing, a cost the character names and then dismisses. Do not add them as retroactive blame.\n\nBuild recognition into the ending of the scene or the story. The light novel format permits a short coda after the final action: the character sees what the choice has made impossible, or the narration shows the aftermath in one or two focused beats. Do not force a twist or a compulsory cliffhanger. The loss should feel intelligible and particular—a relationship, a chance, a trust, a home—rather than a generic catastrophe. Keep the prose fast, the staging visual, and the private reactions earned; the tragedy emerges because the reader understood the cost before the character admitted it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose one recurring image or sound pattern that can register a specific cost: the object spent, the obligation invoked, the alternative refused. Return to it only when a later choice deepens that same cost, so the repetition becomes an audible accounting rather than an ornamental refrain. Each return should make the constraint more legible, not merely restate the loss.\n\nShape the sentence around the moment of irreversible movement. When a character commits, let the syntax open toward the refused alternative and then close against it: a clause that reaches, a turn that cuts it short, a cadence that lands on the consequence. Keep the refused option concrete enough to be felt as a real possibility. Use a plain statement at recognition so the new understanding arrives in a different register from the accumulating lyric pattern.\n\nLet the lyricism belong to the narrator's attention, not to every speaker's dialogue. Other characters may speak in their own registers, and their words can interrupt the rhythmic flow without being mocked by it. After the loss, preserve the warmth and intelligence that made the choice matter. Recognition changes what the loss means, and the aftermath should keep what cannot be restored without forcing every image to signify ruin.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narrator return to a remembered occasion and reconstruct the decision from its concrete remains: what was known then, what alternative was refused, what resource was spent. Use a kept object, a continued habit, or a changed place as evidence of that specific choice, not as a general emblem of loss. The melancholy should come from the precision of the reconstruction.\n\nAttend to small irreversible changes as the aftermath's texture, and let present warmth coexist with absence in the same perception. A familiar room may still be pleasant while its rearrangement shows who has gone; a continued ritual may still comfort while its original meaning has thinned. Do not stage the pleasure first and then cancel it with a loss. The reflective voice should hold both at once.\n\nKeep the character's values inside the memory. Later knowledge may revise an earlier interpretation, but the earlier constraint must remain what it was at the time. Recognition changes what the loss means, and the reflection should let that change happen on the page: the narrator may see the same detail differently without pretending the original choice was inevitable or blameless. Do not turn every scene into an announcement of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed or oral storytelling method in which a situated teller recounts the fall to a present audience. Establish the teller's relationship to the events and the audience's reason for listening, then keep the frame active: when the teller pauses, answers a question, or acknowledges what the audience already knows, let that interruption expose a constraint or competing commitment inside the account. The audience's prior knowledge may create dramatic irony, but the character's earlier knowledge must remain what it was at the time.\n\nLet the teller's selection of details carry the recognition. What is remembered, what is passed over, and what must be explained to the audience are themselves evidence of the teller's present understanding. A warning or alternative that was intelligible in the past should remain visible in the account, so the audience sees the fall as a consequence of particular commitments rather than fate alone. The teller may compare earlier and later knowledge, but the comparison should revise the meaning of the earlier choice without erasing its terms.\n\nKeep the tradition's language and social world distinct and supplied by the actual setting. Do not import a generic antiquity, mysticism, exile, or honor code. The teller's voice should be individual, not ceremonial in every sentence. The Sandersonian attention to rules and consequences translates into the account's clarity about the world's actual constraints—social, economic, legal, familial—without adding supernatural mechanisms the world does not contain. The frame may end on the present consequence of the telling: the audience's silence, a question, or the teller's return to the moment in which the loss still operates.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details, and give each one a distinct causal job: one detail may show the constraint, another the cost, another the consequence. Before a character commits, state the constraint and the competing commitment in direct clauses: what is owed, what is wanted, what will be spent. Do not add a second detail that repeats the first's meaning. Omission should leave the causal link inferable, not force the reader to guess whether a link exists.\n\nLet recognition arrive in a single precise sentence or a brief exchange, and let that sentence stand without surrounding commentary. The minimalist register does not mean the recognition is understated; it means the recognition is not diluted. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several short fragments when the thought requires it. Keep the character's values inside the decision: a refusal, a silence, or a small action can show what the choice cost without a paragraph of explanation.\n\nPreserve the aftermath through what remains. One object, one habit, one empty place can carry the loss. The tragedy's warmth should remain visible in the details that survive, not announced. Cast voices remain individual; a character who speaks plainly is not the same as a narrator who writes plainly. The scene may end with the consequence present and the next step unstated.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fall in sustained scene-first prose, letting the narrator move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. When a choice affects an attempt, let the relevant constraint enter through the character's attention: a speaker may state the cost in dialogue, the character may notice the spent resource in perception, or a recollection may make the earlier commitment available. Do not insert a narrator's summary before the action.\n\nKeep the alternatives and warnings intelligible as the scene unfolds. Another character may voice the refused option; a detail in the setting may show what was given up; a recollection may make the earlier belief available without rewriting it. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader through the scene's movement without recap machinery. Let the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register; the prose need not become solemn or understated merely because the genre is tragedy.\n\nAllow recognition to change what the loss means within the scene's own time. The character may understand the consequence in the moment of action, or the understanding may arrive later in a quieter scene; either way, show the recognition as an event in the character's experience, not as a lesson. Aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a relationship's changed terms, a practical consequence, a remaining obligation. End on the present consequence, whatever it is, without a closing summary or moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the center of scene selection, and use its private language and repeated moments as the mechanism for exposing constraints and costs. When a character makes a choice that affects the relationship, let a shared phrase, a ritual, or a private understanding show what the relationship requires and what the choice spends. What does this person owe elsewhere? What alternative is refused? The emotional payoff must arise from the precision of that accounting, not from a general atmosphere of hurt.\n\nLet the relationship's repeated moments carry the fall. A phrase that once meant comfort may return in a changed context; a ritual that once held the relationship together may now show its strain. The repetition should deepen the reader's understanding of what is being lost, not merely restate the same feeling. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where they belong, but do not insert a roster of familiar tropes the premise does not supply. The relationship's warmth should remain visible even as the choices become destructive.\n\nLet recognition change what the loss means for both people, and render any asymmetry through each character's separate knowledge and access. One character may understand the consequence before the other, or each may understand it differently, but the narration should show what each knows rather than summarize the difference. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored—a changed term of address, a silence where a ritual used to be, a practical arrangement that continues without its former meaning. End on the relationship's present consequence, without forcing a confession, a reconciliation, or a final rupture. Keep each character's values inside their decisions, and let both remain intelligent and worthy of attention.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the fall as an account of the deeds by which these people will be remembered, and use the chronicle's compact declarations to state each deed's constraint and competing commitment: what oath bound the person, what kinship or obligation pulled another way, what resource was spent. The relation of the act to what will be remembered should include the price paid, not only the outcome. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, and its return should mark the accumulating consequence rather than ornament the entrance.\n\nUse deliberate openings and recurring phrasing to give the account a sense of retelling. A phrase may return when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the same words that once marked a gift may later mark a loss. Let that new application mark the recognition, so the chronicle's own language shows what the loss means. Keep the particular source tradition coherent; do not mix every epic convention or import a pantheon the world does not contain. Oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds can organize the account when the story has established them, but they are not required scenery.\n\nLet the chronicle preserve the alternatives and warnings that were intelligible at the time. The account may note what another person advised, what the character refused, or what the community saw coming, without turning the fall into fate alone. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a name that now carries a different weight, a place left empty, an obligation that continues. End on the present consequence of the deeds, whatever it is, rather than declaring the person's doom complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fall through Scandinavian social-institutional realism: a specific community, its public offices, housing arrangements, workplaces, and the documents that connect them. Select one definite institutional context—a municipal housing office, a workplace with formal grievance procedures, a family farm with inheritance and debt obligations, a union or professional body with membership rules—and keep its particular Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish setting distinct in the details that matter: the name of the form, the waiting period, the person with limited discretion, the appeal that exists but cannot be exercised in time.\n\nMake the protagonist's own records the legible system of the tragedy. Let them keep a notebook, a folder, a stack of letters, or a phone log of calls and promises. Each time a new obligation arrives, show the protagonist adding it to the record, comparing it with what is already owed, and choosing which item to pay, answer, or postpone. The reader should be able to follow the arithmetic: what was promised, what was received, what was spent, what remains. When a rule affects an attempt, explain it through the protagonist's practical encounter with it—the clerk who repeats the same sentence, the form that must be signed in person, the deadline that falls on a holiday—not through an abstract summary.\n\nLet each choice be understandable in isolation and destructive in combination. The protagonist borrows from one obligation to meet another, hides a small failure from a neighbor who could have helped, or keeps an appointment that costs a different promise. Show the alternative that was available and why it was refused: the pride that cannot accept charity, the duty that forbids a delay, the mistaken belief that one more week will be enough. Keep the protagonist intelligent and capable; their destructiveness comes from competing commitments and the structure of the situation, not from stupidity or a single error.\n\nUse domestic specificity to make the loss material. The drawer where the letters are kept, the bus route to the office, the neighbor who notices a change in routine, the meal that becomes smaller, the coat that is not replaced. These details are not decoration; they are the inventory of what is being lost. Return to them so that the reader feels the narrowing of the protagonist's world: the drawer empties, the bus route is no longer taken, the neighbor stops asking.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the protagonist's own documentary attention. At a specific moment, the accumulated records become undeniable: the protagonist reads the stack of letters in order, totals the amounts, sees the date that has already passed, or hears the clerk state plainly what the papers have been saying for weeks. The recognition changes what the loss means—it becomes a consequence the protagonist can name rather than a series of misfortunes—but it does not undo anything. Afterward, preserve what cannot be restored: the relationship, the position, the trust, the home. Allow the aftermath to include both loss and the continuation of ordinary life, with moments of genuine care, humor, or cooperation before and after the fall. Do not force every scene toward punishment or strip away all warmth; the tragedy is the irreversible loss, not the absence of anything worth losing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragedy through observable screen action and dialogue. Make the system of the fall legible as a chain of objects and physical procedures the audience can track across scenes: the envelope on the table, the form that must be signed, the clock above the desk, the drawer that holds the letters, the phone that rings or does not ring. Introduce each object in an action line that shows its function—the protagonist checks the drawer, counts the money, reads the notice, sets the envelope by the door—so that later scenes can return to it with changed meaning.\n\nLet dialogue carry the commitments that will be broken. Each promise should be specific and spoken aloud: \"I'll cover it tomorrow,\" \"I've already sent the form,\" \"You can stay with me.\" When a character makes a commitment, show the alternative they are refusing in the same scene—the friend who offers help, the clerk who explains the extension, the family member who asks a question the protagonist will not answer. The audience should understand why the choice is made even when it is destructive.\n\nExternalize preparation and resource use in the action lines. Show the envelope addressed but not stamped, the bus fare set aside and then spent, the medicine bought instead of the rent, the train missed because the meeting ran long. Each of these actions is a small choice with a visible cost. When a rule matters, show someone testing it or being caught by it: the clerk who points to the date on the form, the landlord who checks the ledger, the supervisor who reads the attendance record aloud.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, separated from dialogue and action lines so it is not available to the cast. Use it for the calculation, memory, or fear that cannot be staged: the protagonist adding up what is owed, remembering a previous failure, deciding to hide a small problem. Do not use voice-over as a substitute for staged action when a visible or audible event can carry the knowledge.\n\nBuild recognition through a scene in which the protagonist sees the full cost at once in a single observable arrangement: the room emptied, the signature already recorded, the receipt that contradicts an earlier statement, the drawer now open and empty. Let the consequence occur on screen rather than off. Preserve warmth before the fall—a shared meal, a joke about the weather, a moment of quiet assistance—so that the loss is not merely mechanical. End on what remains after the loss: the protagonist still present in the changed space, continuing the daily routine after something irreplaceable is gone. Do not require a dramatic confession or a final chase.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Stage Play\n\nStage the tragic fall through dialogue and playable physical business. Give the set a definite object system that the audience can learn to read: a desk with two letters, a telephone that can be lifted or ignored, a door that separates a private room from a public hallway, a coat rack where a coat appears or disappears, a drawer that holds what the protagonist cannot bear to open. Stage directions should establish who can reach what and who can hear what, so that each choice is visible in the actor's movement toward or away from an object.\n\nLet each scene foreground a commitment that will later be broken. One character promises to stay, another signs a form, a third refuses to lie. Keep the commitments concrete and spoken aloud, because the stage audience learns what matters through what is said and done. When a character makes a promise, show the alternative they are refusing in the same scene: the friend who offers help, the official who explains the extension, the family member who asks a question the protagonist will not answer. The audience should understand why the choice is made even when it is destructive.\n\nUse entrances and exits as resource decisions. A character enters with news, money, or a document; another exits to make a call, keep an appointment, or avoid a question. Each entrance and exit changes what is available on stage, and the audience should feel the narrowing of the protagonist's options as fewer people enter and more people leave. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public meaning: a pause before answering, a hand placed on a letter, a chair turned away, a coat taken from the rack and then returned.\n\nKeep required private experience as a clearly marked nonspoken note in the script, separated from dialogue and stage directions so it is not available to the cast or audience. Use it for the calculation, memory, or fear that cannot be staged: the protagonist adding up what is owed, remembering a previous failure, deciding to hide a small problem. Do not disguise private thought as a soliloquy or aside unless a separate dramatic purpose requires the performed speech.\n\nMake the recognition scene physical as well as verbal. The protagonist sees the consequence in the arrangement of objects they have themselves handled: the empty chair, the missing coat, the two envelopes now both opened, the drawer now empty. Let the meaning come from the prior dialogue and blocking, not from a sudden explanatory speech. Preserve the irreversibility by not allowing any later line to undo what has been shown. Allow warmth and genuine care before the fall—a shared meal, a joke, a moment of quiet assistance—so that the loss is not merely mechanical. End on the continuation of ordinary life: a character alone with an object, a quiet exit, a door closing, rather than a forced climax.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Stream of Consciousness\n\nTell the fall from inside one authorized mind, and let association rather than chronological report decide the order in which the reader encounters the facts. Braid present perception, memory, private words, and bodily sensation around a small set of concrete anchors drawn from the story's actual constraints: the document, balance, promise, deadline, or kept object that binds this character, whatever the story supplies. Each return to an anchor should carry one new fact or one changed feeling; the same remembered warning means something different after a choice has been made. From these recurring particulars the reader must be able to reconstruct the mechanism of the fall—who needs what, what has been paid, what is still owed—even though the telling never stops to explain it.\n\nKeep the flow embodied. Sensation marks cost rather than decorating the prose: what is carried, endured, rationed, counted. When the mind slips into recollection or fantasy, return it to the present through a particular that has itself changed—a deadline now past, a resource no longer where the hand expects to find it. Keep orientation firm enough that event, memory, and imagined alternative stay distinguishable; the uncertainty belongs to the character's feeling, not to the scene's facts. Other people enter only as this mind encounters them—their words, faces, and silences—never as minds you open alongside it.\n\nLet a remembered warning or an offered alternative surface as a present choice arrives, so the intelligibility of what is being refused stays inside the flow instead of in a narrated summary. The character remains intelligent and worthy of attention: warmth, competence, and even humor can live inside the digressions while the choices turn destructive. Recognition, when it comes, is the moment a circled anchor can no longer be deflected—the same image returns and this time the mind names what it means—and the syntax can register it by going shorter, plainer, less digressive. Afterward, do not summarize the tragedy for the character; let the mind keep meeting the same objects without the possibility of reversal, and let that continuing presence of absence carry the ending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the fall in the exchange itself. Write sender-attributed messages whose habits distinguish the people—one sends long corrected paragraphs, another short fragments, a third leaves questions hanging for hours—and let warmth, jokes, and competence appear in the log alongside evasion, so the eventual loss costs something. Use only the interface events that change a message's meaning—a timestamp gap, a read receipt, an edit, an attachment—and never invent one to manufacture subtext. Time passing shows in the log itself: a delay can be a warning, a refusal, or an ordinary busy day, and either the surrounding messages or a genuine lack of information decides which.\n\nMake every commitment a sentence someone actually transmits: what will be paid, covered, filed, picked up, forgiven. Keep the system legible through logistics—the amount already spent, the appointment that conflicts with the promise, the form confirmed or never confirmed—so the mechanism of the fall is visible in what people write rather than declared in commentary. Unsent drafts appear clearly marked as drafts, showing what was considered and withheld; a draft is a composed artifact, not the writer's whole live mind. Private thought that is never typed stays separately marked and never masquerades as transmitted text. Where context beyond the exchange is genuinely needed, set it off plainly from the transcript.\n\nRender recognition through message behavior rather than narration: the character answers too plainly, deletes and sends something smaller, stops driving a thread they once led, or finally asks the question the whole exchange has been circling. A forwarded document, a name missing from a group, a receipt on a message that was never answered can force the full situation into view. Close wherever the exchange has earned it—mid-logistics, on a draft left unsent, or on one small concrete message that registers the changed position—without resolving or explaining the pain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the fall as a sequence of stagable scenes: concise scene-setting, plainly attributed dialogue, and focal narration or inner response carrying what the speaker does not say aloud. Give each scene a concrete place and hour that matter to the action, and keep scene changes legible. A background cue orients the moment; an expression cue can register confusion, fatigue, or the moment a decision lands—but neither certifies a hidden motive. Choice and consequence do the tragic work, not a labeled face.\n\nLet dialogue carry the commitments and their prices in plain, speakable lines: what someone will do, by when, at what cost, and which other promise gives way if they do it. Focal narration holds the private side—the calculation of what can be paid, the remembered earlier failure, the small problem decided to be hidden—while the other characters remain unable to know it. When a rule or constraint affects an attempt, make enough of it plain in scene that the attempt's preparation and cost are understandable. Advance through scenes whose new information changes the available choices: a document arrives, a schedule shifts, an offer of help comes at a moment that matters. The cast stays capable and worthy of attention; warmth inside a scene makes the eventual loss mean more, not less.\n\nRecognition, when it comes, arrives in the focal narration once accumulated facts can no longer be deflected—the same room seen from a changed position, an object returned to and found emptied of its meaning, the cost finally named plainly. When the story reaches aftermath, keep the character present in the changed scene rather than reaching for a final twist. This style adds no branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or engine commands; proceed along one authored path, a scene at a time, ending wherever that scene's natural boundary falls.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqabbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Brandon Sanderson / Web Serial\n\nCarry the fall across installments with a readable through-line: which constraint currently presses, which choice is underway, and what remains open to continue. Advance one local, earned change per installment—a resource shifts, a term is set, a person's answer changes what is possible—rather than a random disaster inserted to raise stakes. Recover only the particular the current step needs (the amount still owed, the fixed date, who promised what), never the full history that established it.\n\nMake the mechanism legible as ordinary logistics, drawing every rule from the world the story actually occupies—a filing, a deadline, a discretion someone holds—without importing magic or canon that world does not contain. Explain enough of any rule that affects an attempt that preparation and payoff stay visible: what was set aside and then spent, what was addressed and never sent, who could help and has not been asked. Keep inward experience available when it accompanies a choice, so the causes stay visible beneath the outward events. Let each loss follow from earlier choices so the fall accumulates instead of resetting, and keep the people intelligent and warm where the story permits; competence and kindness do not prevent the fall—they give it its price.\n\nEnd installments on natural boundaries: completed work, a pending request, plain acknowledgment of what changed, or a stop mid-action where the scene boundary falls—never a forced cliffhanger or revelation each turn. Recognition can arrive before any final installment, when accumulated changes make the full cost undeniable. Aftermath preserves what cannot be restored and can close on one continuing detail rather than a grand statement. Across the run, let selected facts acquire meaning through recurrence and reuse; duration should deepen the fall, not push its meaning perpetually out of reach.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the fall through the narrator's private operating rules. Give this one narrator a concrete procedure they perform or recount — a sequence for locking up, a way of folding, a count kept before hard conversations, a routine learned from the person they are losing — and let the procedure carry the feeling the narrator refuses to name. The rules belong to this narrator's actual world, not to a borrowed catalogue of coping.\n\nKeep the hurt inside the diction this narrator already owns: dry, technical, clipped, whatever their distance is, with no stock vocabulary of suffering laid on top. Let strain reach the conversation itself — a question started and dropped, a reassurance offered in the wrong terms, an apology that turns into an inventory of tasks. When the narrator admits to needing someone, make the admission cost something visible inside the ritual: a step skipped, a count lost, an object handled too hard, a sentence abandoned and covered by the next step of the procedure. When the narrator withholds, show the withholding as acceleration — hands moving faster, the list getting longer, the rule run more times than the situation needs. Private thought may run ahead of speech or against it for a reason particular to this narrator: embarrassment, utility, the belief that naming the thing makes it truer.\n\nLet the repetition change meaning as the fall advances, and do not announce the change. The rule that organized safety becomes a way of counting what is already gone; the routine keeps the narrator near the person they hurt. What the narrator now does twice, skips, or cannot stop doing tells the change. Comfort, when it comes, can be partial and awkward — a hand offered at the wrong moment, help accepted only in practical form — and it does not cancel the loss. Keep the narrator intelligent and self-aware while unable to stop; recognition arrives through the accumulated detail of the ritual, not a sudden speech about feelings. In the aftermath the procedure continues, freighted with what cannot be restored, and the narrator may go back to the person or the place anyway, knowing the price.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chat Roleplay\n\nPlay the scene as a shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Give each major speaker one repeated verbal or physical fragment — a phrase they come back to, a gesture performed without thinking, a practical detail they keep noticing — specific to that character and this world rather than a generic tic. In quick exchanges the fragment itself can carry attribution: the reader knows who is talking by what they count, correct, or touch. Between speeches keep the hard rhythm: short sentences that return to a concrete object in the room and cut away without filler.\n\nLet the fragments accumulate meaning as the scene approaches or passes through an irreversible choice. A character repeats the phrase before a decision, after a loss, or exactly when they cannot say what they mean; the recurrence makes the tragedy legible without anyone announcing it. Consequences register as behavior — what someone picks up, stops doing, leaves behind — not as a summary of feelings.\n\nKeep the surrounding world live. Other characters make their own choices and keep up their practical business while the principals talk; their cooperation, refusal, or indifference has its own reasons and does not wait on the focal pair. Follow the active contract for narrative person, action markers, and who controls which character.\n\nEnd where the scene's own motion ends. The reply may stop mid-speech, mid-action, or with a setup still unfinished; a discovery or admission can open a new conversation rather than settle it, and a relationship consequence can stay unresolved after the close. No question, cliffhanger, or forced choice is owed at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the fall through the lived procedure of one social world — a household, a workplace, a school, a guild, a neighborhood — in the social and satirical realist manner. Recount its rules as practical process: how a request moves upward, how a debt is entered and collected, how standing is kept at a formal dinner, how duty to parents or superiors is discharged, how time and money are accounted week to week. The narrator explains these procedures in the confident voice of someone who runs them well; the self-exposure is that the account shows the system's logic working through the narrator before the narrator can say so.\n\nBuild the satire from the gap between rules as stated and rules as lived: the regulation quoted exactly, then the extra step everyone knows to add; the compliment that functions as a warning; the favor that creates the obligation the fall will squeeze. Give the narrator a repeated fragment drawn from this world — a superior's formula of praise, the numbered step of a filing routine, the tally kept at the edge of a ledger, the dish cooked when an apology is impossible — and let it recur with changing weight as the fall advances, until the same words count what has been lost.\n\nKeep the narrator intelligent and observant, able to name the absurdity and still unable to exit. Warnings and alternative paths stay visible and stay blocked — by duty, pride, love, or plain survival. Let the cost land on bodies and routines: hands after a shift, meals skipped, records quietly amended. Recognition comes when the narrator rereads the life as procedure and understands what the rules made of them, not through a moral speech. The aftermath leaves the institution standing and ordinary, its procedures now legible as the machinery of the loss. Use the language, period, and community of the supplied setting; nothing here requires transplanting a cast or importing a ready-made temperament.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fall in hard, concrete strokes. Compress the transitions: move from one physical fact to the next without connective filler and let forceful verbs carry the motion. The narrator notices what is worn, broken, stained, or missing — the frayed cuff, the cracked step, the changed weight of a tool, the smell a room keeps after someone is gone — and those particulars carry the tragedy in place of commentary.\n\nAnchor the telling in one repeated rule tied to a physical process: the check performed on equipment, the sequence for closing up, the count of what remains, the gesture made before a dangerous task. The repetition marks time and measures change. The rule that once meant readiness can come to mean routine, then dread, then the habit that outlasts the loss, and the shift shows only in what the narrator does and notices — never in explanation. Work the rhythm on purpose: a run of short sentences broken by one longer line when the character's reasoning genuinely needs room, for deciding, remembering, or recognizing what they have done, and that longer thought lands harder for the compression around it. Rough or abrasive speech stays in its speaker's mouth and grammar.\n\nKeep the stakes material. The fall costs things that cannot be undone: a body that cannot do what it did last month, an object that will not repair, a place that will not take the narrator back. No decorative metaphor, no abstract summary of grief. Recognition arrives through the accumulated weight of the physical world; the aftermath keeps the routine going, performed in the space the loss has left.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the fall entirely in documents, each with a definite author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Let the form follow the scene that requires it — a letter, a report, a ledger entry, a journal page, a note left where someone will find it — rather than rotating formats on a schedule. What a document knows and what it conceals follow the author's situation at the time of writing: the letter to a superior omits what the private page records; the report enters a fact without grasping what it means; the note is written before its author knows what is coming. Even the most intimate entry is a composed record with a purpose, not a whole live mind on the page.\n\nGive each author a repeated rule or fragment from their practical world — the formula that opens formal correspondence, the numbered step described the same way every time, the detail the writer cannot stop recording. The fragment marks the author's presence and carries the feeling the document will not state; the same formula can open a confident early record and a late one written after the loss, changed only by what surrounds it. Self-exposure runs through process: an author explaining, step by step, how properly a thing was done is telling the reader exactly what they cannot admit.\n\nMove the tragedy forward through gaps and traces between entries: a line crossed out, a page missing, a date corrected, an account that stops agreeing with another, a document never sent. A later record can correct an earlier one without understanding the correction. The reader assembles the fall from what the documents know and what they leave out; do not append an omniscient explanation after each entry. Recognition arrives in sequence, when a later document shows its author understanding what an earlier one could not, and the aftermath preserves the records themselves, read differently now for the loss. Keep the sequence continuously readable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the fall through care that stays sincere. Give the narrator one repeated practical ritual of kindness — the way a meal is prepared, the round of checking on someone, a remembered preference, the small comfort offered at the same hour each day — and let the ritual carry the warmth: the narrator knows how the tea is taken, which chair is preferred, what is needed after a hard day, and shows the knowing in action rather than declaration.\n\nThe affection does not require a confession or an emotional repair. Caring without saying what is meant is the meaning; comfort can be partial and awkward, offered in the wrong form and accepted anyway. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact — comfort does not make the cast agreeable, and it neither removes the problems already in the world nor immunizes anyone from what the fall will cost. A kind act can coexist with the knowledge that something is already slipping, or with the fear that it will.\n\nLet the loss show through what the care cannot fix. The same ritual that comforted can become the measure of what is gone — a preference still remembered after there is no one to prefer it, a table still set out of habit — and the narrator's recognition is that the care was real and still did not prevent the fall. In the aftermath the small pleasures survive, performed in the space the loss has left. Warmth and loss share the scene without canceling each other; the tragedy is credible because what is lost was genuinely good.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the fall in deliberate novel prose: planned transitions, sustained paragraph movement, narration distinct from ordinary speech. Use the distance this allows to frame the narrator's repeated rules and rituals inside social context, history, or judgment. A ritual that reads as private compulsion in close view becomes, in the wider frame, part of a pattern — how this community trains its people, what a household task has meant across generations, what a repeated phrase signals about standing, obligation, or shame. Where the narrator permits judgment, the narration can weigh what the narrator will only perform.\n\nKeep the hard rhythm working inside the broader paragraphs: the concrete sentence that returns to one obsessive particular, the rule recited in the narrator's compressed cadence against longer surrounding movement. The repetition marks time and measures the fall — the rule that organized a life becoming obligation, then habit, then the machinery of the loss — and the formal framing makes that progression legible without reducing the narrator to a case study.\n\nLet the tragedy be intelligible as both choice and condition. The narrator's decisions remain their own, and the social world — its ceremonies, its correspondence, the difference between what is said in public and in private, wherever the supplied world has them — remains what made those decisions available or unavoidable. Warnings and alternatives stay visible to the reader even when the narrator cannot take them, and the narrator stays intelligent and worth attention while choosing destructively. Recognition comes as the narrator's growing understanding of what they did and what the world did with them; the aftermath preserves that social order, now understood as the condition of the loss. Add no aristocratic setting, archaic diction, or uniform elaborateness the world does not already supply — the formality here is architecture, not costume.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the tragedy as a story someone is telling now, to a particular listener, for a reason that matters in the present scene. Establish the frame concretely and briefly: where the two of them are, what the listener wants or fears, and what the teller needs the story to do before it ends — buy time, persuade someone to stay, justify a decision already made, hold off a consequence the teller can see coming. The telling is an act with a stake, not a reminiscence. Take from the Arabian Nights its machinery — audience, occasion, nested delay — not its furniture; keep the supplied setting's own world.\n\nLet the Palahniuk profile's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars be the teller's performance. The narrator returns to a repeated rule, phrase, or bodily detail as the account approaches the point that hurts, and the return sounds practiced: this story has been told before. Between the spoken passages, keep the teller's live awareness available — what the narrator notices in the listener's face, what the narrator knows now and does not say, where the rehearsed version skips. Do not explain the defense; let the rhythm and the omissions show it.\n\nMake each embedded tale do present work. An inner story can show how a promise was made, what a person was like before the damage, or the moment a warning went unheeded, and its delay of the outer scene's difficult moment is part of the meaning. The listener can interrupt with a concrete question, refuse to hear the rest, or answer a question the teller was avoiding; return to the frame with a small marker — the object between them, a shift in the listener's posture, a sound from outside — so the levels stay legible while repeated phrases accrue weight across them.\n\nLet the fall accumulate through the telling, with the teller's choices intelligible even as they become destructive: the alternative that existed, the loyalty or pride that closed it. Recognition can arrive as a shift in what gets repeated, a detail the teller can no longer say the old way, or a question the rehearsed account cannot answer. Aftermath lives in the frame — what the listener does with the story, what the teller has left when it is finished — and it preserves the loss without restoring it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Build the tragedy as a narrator working through surviving records — letters, case files, employment logs, medical notes, minutes, photographs — and comparing what they say against what the narrator remembers. The fall becomes legible in that gap: a document's date that does not match the remembered afternoon, a form's calm sentence for what the narrator experienced as a turning point. Keep the syntax controlled and the distance deliberate; the narrator is assembling an account, and the assembly is the drama. The method belongs to this narrator's situation, not to a required country: use the setting, language, and institutions actually supplied, without inventing a national severity or translated word order.\n\nGive the Palahniuk profile's obsessive particulars to the handling of paper. The narrator returns to a form number, a repeated phrase in a report, a time stamp, the weight and smell of a folder; documents get sorted, re-read, quoted, put back out of order. This is practical process, and the self-exposure runs through it — what the narrator quotes at length, what gets summarized fast, what the records got right and the narrator admits, and the line the narrator remembers differently and cannot prove.\n\nLet the fall accumulate across the comparison. Show the choice the records preserve, the alternative someone else recorded or the narrator recalls, and the social conditions doing the shaping — the institution's obligations, the practical constraints, what could not be refused — rather than private feeling alone. Recognition can arrive as a familiar line read differently on the tenth pass, a detail that can no longer be dismissed, or the moment the documents finally fall into the order in which the damage happened. Keep the narrator intelligent and worth attention even where the account reveals a destructive decision.\n\nThe aftermath belongs to the archive: what the narrator does with the documents afterward, what remains unrecorded, and what no surviving paper can give back.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the tragedy on the collision of old authority and present life. Make the inherited claim specific — a building, a title, a debt, a ritual of access, a person who still holds power — and make the present equally concrete: rent, shift work, a body that needs care, a relationship that cannot absorb another compromise. Give the old authority no powers, factions, or supernatural rules the supplied world does not already contain; its force is legible in what people are allowed to do and what they must conceal.\n\nLet the Palahniuk profile's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars carry the narrator's maintenance work. The narrator returns to a repeated rule, a physical routine, a consumer detail that marks the border between the inherited claim and the present self — what gets polished, counted, lied about, kept in its wrapping. The confident practical account of what had to be maintained, hidden, or performed is where the self-exposure happens; do not explain the defensive logic, let the repetition carry it.\n\nLet the Gothic-Punk atmosphere change how particular encounters are experienced. Urban material detail carries the political weather — the state of a hallway, the cost of a repair, which room is shared and which is guarded — and a trusted person seeing the narrator without the social mask should read as dangerous and intimate at once, whatever the two of them call it. Morally complicated closeness belongs here: the person the narrator lies to protect can also be the one person who knows the truth.\n\nLet the fall accumulate through the narrator's choices, with the alternative visible and the loyalty, desire, or pride that closed it intelligible rather than stupid. Recognition may arrive during an encounter, a practical failure, or the act of finally saying what the mask was for. Aftermath preserves what cannot be taken back: what remains of the relationship, the body, the obligation, the place, after the choice is made.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write the tragedy as a first-person account the narrator is giving to someone who will hear it, keep it, or judge it — a family member, a community, an official, a future reader, the narrator's own later self. The telling has a purpose beyond confession: it explains, accuses, apologizes, or preserves what would otherwise drop out of the record. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific to the supplied setting; do not impose a generic family structure, a religious frame, or code-switching this narrator would not use.\n\nGive the Palahniuk profile's obsessive particulars to the narrator's practical life. The account keeps returning to a detail of work, household, money, food, clothing, or bodily care — what had to be done each day, what could not be afforded, what was expected, what was hidden. These recurrences carry the social conditions of the fall; the self-exposure comes through the routines themselves, in what the narrator can itemize exactly and what the narrator circles without naming.\n\nLet the fall accumulate through the narrator's choices, with the alternative that existed and the duty, love, or pride that closed it kept intelligible. Recognition may appear as a shift in how a familiar person gets described, a repeated detail that now means something else, or the sentence the narrator can finally say to this listener. Alongside the spoken account, keep the narrator's private experience available: what the narrator still cannot say, notices in the listener, or admits only inwardly while the testimony stays composed.\n\nKeep the testimony honest about its limits — the narrator may not know everything, may still be defending something, may be speaking because the one person who could correct the account is gone. The aftermath belongs to the act itself: what the listener does with it, and what the telling preserves without restoring.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the tragedy as a personal record composed over time: dated entries, observations, lists, remembered conversations, reflections that do not need to add up to a continuous narrative. The entries are composed and chosen — written by the narrator for the narrator's own reasons — so what gets recorded and what gets left out are both deliberate, and the gaps between entries carry as much as the pages. Let the language and distance follow the actual diarist, clipped or fussy or flat or tender, with no imposed quietness and no required inventory of scenery.\n\nGive the Palahniuk profile's obsessive particulars to the record. The narrator returns to a physical sensation, a household object, a price, a phrase someone said, a routine that had to be performed, and the returns accrue meaning across entries without explanation. The self-exposure runs through the choosing: what the narrator counts, dates, copies out twice, cannot stop logging.\n\nLet the fall accumulate through the sequence. Show the choice made, the alternative that existed, and the belief or loyalty that closed it; recognition may arrive as an entry that breaks the usual pattern, a detail that can no longer be recorded the old way, or a gap followed by one sentence that re-reads everything before it. Use omission and seasonal or temporal attention where they serve this narrator — a change in light, weather, or routine marking a shift in condition rather than decorating the page.\n\nThe aftermath belongs to the record: what the narrator stops writing, writes once more, or leaves for whoever finds the pages — and what the accumulated entries preserve without giving back.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Write the tragedy as a witness account in which the narrator's fall is inseparable from the conditions of a community, workplace, neighborhood, or family: the narrator tells what happened because the record matters — to preserve a history, explain a loss, testify to what a person or group endured. Preserve the language, community, and history of the supplied setting; do not relocate the cast, reduce the politics to scenery, or add marvelous events the world does not permit.\n\nGive the Palahniuk profile's obsessive particulars to the material conditions. The account returns to a detail of labor, money, housing, food, transport, or bodily exhaustion, and the returns show how the larger condition entered individual choices: what had to be done to survive, what could not be refused, what was promised, what was taken. The self-exposure comes through these practical realities and what the narrator admits doing inside them, not through abstract commentary.\n\nKeep the chronicle honest about its perspective. Distinguish what the narrator saw from what the narrator was told, and let other people's choices carry their own weight — the fall is not the narrator's alone, though the recognition is. Show the alternative that existed, the necessity, loyalty, or pride that closed it, and the moment the narrator understands what the loss means; that recognition may appear as a familiar place described differently, a repeated detail grown heavier, or a sentence joining a private decision to the shared condition.\n\nThe aftermath belongs to the testimony: what the community or family does with the account, what the narrator still cannot say, and what the telling preserves without restoring.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene cleanly: establish where the narrator is, what is physically present, and what the narrator is doing before the situation develops, and keep every spatial change easy to follow — who moved, which room, what seat. Use short, readable paragraphs and break around action, dialogue, and private response so movement and reaction stay trackable without flattening the narrator's voice. When the scene introduces an unfamiliar name, term, or mechanic, let the narrator's own attention present it clearly rather than stopping the story for a gloss.\n\nGive the Palahniuk profile's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars to the narrator's immediate process. The narrator returns to a repeated rule, a physical routine, a consumer detail, a bodily sensation as the scene approaches the point that matters, and the return sounds practiced — this is how the narrator handles things. The self-exposure comes through the practical account: what gets checked, counted, cleaned, avoided, cannot be stopped.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to carry the tragedy's interior movement. After a line of dialogue, a physical event, or a change in the room, give the narrator's private response in a short concrete beat — a sensation, a thought, a memory surfacing, attention snagging on the wrong object. The beat belongs to this narrator and may be serious, uncertain, angry, or bitterly funny; it is never a compulsory quip or a summary of what the scene means.\n\nLet the fall accumulate through the narrator's choices across scenes, with the alternative visible and the loyalty, pride, or mistaken belief that closed it intelligible. Recognition may arrive inside a reaction beat, in a repeated detail that suddenly reads differently, or through an action that cannot be taken back. The aftermath keeps what the choice cost in view: what remains in the room, the body, the relationship, the routine. Do not force a cliffhanger; end where the scene's boundary actually falls.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the fall around one practical act the narrator performs and can describe with total exactness: checking a lock, measuring a dose, wiping a surface, pulling a stitch. Return to that act at distinct stages of the story, and change one concrete particular each time — the count, the hour, who else is in the room, what the hands smell of afterward — so the repetition becomes a meter for what has already been lost. The act itself does not have to be destructive; its accumulating meaning does the harm. Let the narrator's confidence in the procedure stay genuine even when the procedure is part of the mistake, and let the warnings and alternatives stay intelligible inside the details the narrator records without weighing them.\n\nShape each paragraph so rhythm does the work of emphasis. Follow a long unfolding sentence with a short declarative one to make a recognition land. Extend a comparison only when it changes what the narrator understands: water moving through a pipe can become a promise leaving a body, but the second term must be traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations, and a plain statement should stand nearby to give the figure contrast. Attach recurring sounds and word fragments to the central act rather than decorating every sentence, and let other characters speak in their own registers instead of the narrator's figurative one.\n\nLet the account expose its defensive logic without confessing it. The narrator can insist on the rightness of the procedure while the imagery and the order of the details show the reader what cannot yet be said aloud. When recognition arrives, make it alter what the repeated act means — the checked lock now keeps nobody in, the measured dose now measures an absence — rather than announcing a feeling. In the aftermath, let the act survive as a habit emptied of its former purpose or as a ritual someone else still performs, so the loss stays concrete and irreversible without erasing the warmth the story has already earned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nReturn to one remembered occasion as if it were a procedure the narrator must perform again: the order the chairs were moved in, the temperature of the room, the phrase someone repeated before leaving. This precision is not nostalgia; it is how the narrator holds the event steady while its meaning keeps changing. Each return should add or correct a single detail, until the memory itself becomes the repeated rule the narrator cannot stop following.\n\nInterleave that occasion with the present routine that has outlived it. A familiar object, gesture, or daily task can carry the association without naming it: the coffee still made the same way, the drawer still checked, the sound still waited for though it no longer comes. Let ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable where the scene permits it. The tragedy is not that every moment is sad; it is that the present keeps coinciding with what is no longer available.\n\nUse the reflective mode to expose the defensive logic without a direct confession. The account can insist that the loss was necessary, that the choice was right, that nothing could have been done, while the ordering of remembered details shows the alternative that was intelligible at the time. When recognition comes, let it change what the remembered occasion means rather than replace it with a lesson. Preserve the routine and the memory together in the aftermath, so the loss remains unfinished and irreversible, and let a scene end in its own present moment instead of closing on an announcement of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell the tragedy as a framed oral account. A situated teller speaks to a present audience inside the supplied language, setting, and community, and the frame stays active: interruptions, questions, refusals to continue, a listener's attention drifting and returning can punctuate the telling. The teller relates the fall as a sequence of repeated practical acts, each described with exacting care — the way a door was barred, a measure poured, a letter folded, a debt recorded. The repetition is the teller's method, not a tic; it keeps the audience oriented while the consequences accumulate.\n\nLet the teller's confidence be part of the tragedy. The account can insist on the necessity of each step, on the intelligence of the people involved, on the reasonableness of the original choice, while the audience hears the alternative that was available then. Keep the teller's access honest: distinguish what was witnessed, what was reported by others, and what the teller only now understands, and do not open another character's private mind to fill a silence. Let the frame register the cost of the telling itself — the audience going quiet, a listener leaving, a child repeating a phrase without knowing its weight.\n\nWhen recognition comes, let it change what the repeated act means to the teller and to the listeners rather than deliver a moral. The aftermath can preserve the telling as a ritual that outlives the event, or show members of the audience carrying the account into their own practical days. Keep to the one tradition the scene actually supplies; do not import a generic regional temperament, an honor code, or a mystical register, and do not reach for antiquity, exile, or piety the established world has not given you.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose one or two concrete details that belong to a practical act the narrator can describe exactly — the weight of a key, the sound of a latch, the number of steps between two rooms — and return to them with small variations. Each repetition should carry a changed meaning because the situation around it has changed. Do not explain the change; let the reader infer it from what the narrator now notices, or no longer mentions.\n\nWrite in direct clauses and keep the causal sequence legible: the choice, its immediate consequence, and the moment the consequence becomes irreversible should all be present on the page rather than summarized away. State a rule once and then follow it without restating it, so the rule becomes visible through the actions that obey it. Let the narrator's confidence hold until recognition, where a single short sentence can do the work of a paragraph; when a thought needs more room to make sense, give it the room, because shortness is not a quota and each voice stays individual.\n\nKeep the self-exposure indirect. The account can insist on the procedure's necessity while the order of the details shows the alternative that was available. After recognition, preserve the repeated detail as a habit that continues without its former purpose: the ending need not announce the loss — the latch can simply sound once more, emptied of what it used to mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fall in sustained, scene-first prose. Open each scene in a specific place and time, and let the narration move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and whatever inner experience the active perspective authorizes, using paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. The narrator's obsessive particulars should arise from the scene's practical business: the exact way a task is performed, the brand, weight, or sequence that matters to the person doing it. Repeat a rule or a fragment when a later scene gives it a new application, not as a tic on every page.\n\nKeep the self-exposure indirect. The repeated rule can be presented as ordinary competence, and the reader sees the defensive logic through what the narrator chooses to describe and what gets omitted. Let dialogue interrupt the narrator's authority where the scene allows: another character objects to a detail, corrects a sequence, or answers an elevated claim with a practical concern. Hold private thought inside the authorized perspective; other characters' minds stay closed unless the active contract opens them.\n\nDevelop the fall through scenes whose information changes the available interpretations. A familiar object or gesture can anchor a later scene, its new significance earned by a fact the reader now holds. When recognition comes, let it alter what the repeated act means rather than replace it with a stated lesson. Carry the aftermath into ordinary business — the loss preserved inside the ongoing practical world — instead of closing with a summary, and let the scene's own boundary, not a required moral, end the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the organizing center of the tragedy. Select scenes that teach the bond through its private language and repeated moments: a phrase only these two use, a task one always performs for the other, a particular way of waiting or checking in. Describe those rituals with obsessive exactness, so the reader learns the relationship the way its members did — through procedure. Fulfill the actual supplied premise, with its callbacks and its particular promise; do not pad the story with a roster of familiar tropes the premise never asked for.\n\nMeasure the fall by changes in the shared ritual. One person performs it differently, forgets a step, or continues it alone. The narrator may insist on the ritual's rightness while the reader watches the defensive logic: the procedure has become a way of not saying what has changed. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery the space the actual relationship calls for, let dialogue carry the private language naturally, and keep private thought within the authorized perspective.\n\nWhen recognition comes, let it change what the shared ritual means to both people, even if only one of them fully understands it yet. The aftermath can preserve the ritual as a habit that outlives the relationship, or show the other person continuing it with a different understanding. The tragedy is not that love failed to matter; it is that the bond's particular promise became impossible to keep, and the loss stays concrete in the private language and repeated moments that survive it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the sense of a retelling. Open with a deliberate declaration that names the person or the act to be remembered, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Relate the fall as a sequence of deeds, each carried with obsessive practical exactness: the way a tool was held, a distance measured, a promise repeated, a door secured. Draw each repeated detail from an established act or an established way of speaking, never from an ornamental epithet pinned to every entrance.\n\nUse compact declarations to mark the stages of the fall, and return to a phrase only when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let the teller's confidence be part of the tragedy: the account may insist that each step was necessary, that the people involved acted with intelligence, that the outcome could not have been foreseen, while the ordering of the deeds shows the alternative that was available. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, keeping witnessed events, reported ones, and present understanding distinct from one another.\n\nWhen recognition comes, let it change what the remembered act means rather than hand down a moral. Preserve the act in the community's memory, with different tellers repeating it to different effect. End with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered, rather than declaring the loss resolved; keep the one source tradition coherent, and do not import a pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue the established world does not supply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fall as an institutional investigation in the social-realist mode. Keep the setting, language, period, and community exactly as supplied. The focal character works inside a practical system: a housing office, a care assessment unit, a school administration, a parish council, a workplace safety board, or another supplied institution. Do not add snow, isolation, or bleakness as automatic atmosphere.\n\nGive the focal character a repeated procedural rule that organizes their attention. It may be the order in which a case file is assembled, the wording of a standard question, the way a form is checked before filing, the schedule of a home visit, or the sequence for logging a complaint. Let the rule appear in concrete institutional detail and sound like competence, not confession. Return to it with small variations so the reader learns what the rule protects, what it cannot settle, and what it costs to keep. Do not announce its meaning; let the recurrence do the work.\n\nLet the investigation expose the defensive logic without the character admitting it. The prose can justify the rule confidently, present it as professional necessity, or repeat it as if it settles a dispute. Another person may state the alternative plainly: a different placement, a delayed decision, a report filed differently, a person who should be believed sooner. The focal character can see the alternative and still refuse it for a supported reason such as duty, pride, love, fear, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief the story has already made credible. The refusal is a decision, not stupidity.\n\nUse concrete social detail to make the loss legible. A missed home visit, an unreturned key, a changed routine, a signature on the wrong line, a meal left covered, a child's question, a colleague's silence, a file closed too early: these are the materials of the fall. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition may come too late to change the act but still change what the loss means. Ordinary care can coexist with the destructive choice; do not erase warmth before the end, and do not force warmth into every scene.\n\nKeep the telling confident and specific. Required private experience remains available under the active contract, but other characters do not know it unless the story gives them access. A scene may close on unfinished work, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a completed punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Screenplay\n\nWrite the fall through visible routines and spoken recurrences. Give the focal character a repeated practical action or phrase that can be shown or heard: the same way of locking a door, checking a list, folding a cloth, answering a question. Let the recurrence appear in action lines and dialogue blocks without explanation. The audience should recognize the pattern through its accumulation, not through a caption or a character announcing it.\n\nUse concise action lines to establish what can be seen and heard. Scene headings mark location or time changes. Keep the physical business concrete: hands, objects, distances, sounds. A pause or a repeated gesture can carry pressure, but do not turn every expression into a truth indicator. The fall must be legible through choices, not only through reaction shots.\n\nLet dialogue expose the defensive logic. A character can justify the routine confidently, explain it as competence, or repeat a rule as if it settles the matter. Another character may state the alternative plainly. The refusal should remain intelligent and supported by duty, pride, love, fear, or a mistaken belief the scene has made credible. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative.\n\nUse voice-over or a clearly attributed nonspoken note only when the chosen sequence needs private experience that cannot be shown. Required interiority remains available in the active presentation as nonspoken material; it is not automatically dialogue or knowledge available to the other characters. Do not replace every thought with voice-over.\n\nLet the aftermath be visible. A changed routine, an empty chair, an unanswered question, a door left open, a repeated phrase now spoken by someone else: these show what cannot be restored. Recognition may arrive in a look, a silence, or a line delivered too late. The scene can end on unfinished business, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a forced reversal or punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stage Play\n\nWrite the fall through playable recurrence. Give the focal character a repeated piece of business or a repeated line that can be performed: the same way of setting a chair, folding a letter, wiping a glass, answering a question. Let the recurrence appear in stage directions and dialogue without explanation. The audience should recognize the pattern through its accumulation, not through a character announcing it.\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, and blocking. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A pause, a held object, or a repeated gesture can create pressure, but do not turn every movement into a truth indicator. The fall must be legible through choices, not only through reaction.\n\nLet dialogue expose the defensive logic. A character can justify the routine confidently, explain it as competence, or repeat a rule as if it settles the matter. Another character may state the alternative plainly. The refusal should remain intelligent and supported by duty, pride, love, fear, or a mistaken belief the scene has made credible. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative.\n\nUse monologues or asides only when they have a dramatic purpose in the public scene. Required private experience remains available in the active presentation as nonspoken authoring material; it is not automatically something another character can hear. Do not disguise every private thought as an aside or monologue.\n\nLet the aftermath be playable. A changed routine, an empty chair, an unanswered question, a prop left behind, a repeated line now spoken by someone else: these show what cannot be restored. Recognition may arrive in a silence, a look, or a line delivered too late. The scene can end on unfinished business, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a forced reversal or punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the fall through one authorized mind's associative movement. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, but keep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. Do not make every character share a mind or turn scene facts indeterminate.\n\nGive the focal mind a repeated rule, phrase, or bodily particular that returns as an intrusive fragment. It may surface as a remembered instruction, a count, a physical check, or a phrase the character repeats to stay organized. The recurrence should sound like competence, not confession. As the story proceeds, show what the rule protects, what it cannot settle, and what it costs to keep. Do not announce the rule's meaning; let the reader recognize it through return and variation.\n\nLet the mind expose its defensive logic without admitting it. The character can justify the routine confidently, explain it as competence, or repeat a rule as if it settles the matter. Another person's warning may surface in memory, plainly stated, and still be refused for a supported reason: duty, pride, love, fear, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief the story has already made credible. The refusal is a decision, not stupidity.\n\nUse concrete detail to make the loss legible inside the thought stream. A missed appointment, an unreturned key, a changed routine, a signature, a meal left covered, a child's question, a colleague's silence: these are the materials of the fall. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition may come too late to change the act but still change what the loss means. Ordinary care can coexist with the destructive choice; do not erase all warmth before the end.\n\nKeep the telling confident and specific. Required private experience remains available under the active contract, but other characters do not know it unless the story gives them access. A scene may close on unfinished work, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a completed punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the fall through sender-attributed messages. Use readable sender labels and only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Distinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution.\n\nGive the focal character a repeated digital habit or phrase: the same greeting, the same order of questions, the same way of confirming, the same delay before answering. The recurrence should sound like competence, not confession. As the story proceeds, show what the habit protects, what it cannot settle, and what it costs to keep. Do not announce the habit's meaning; let the reader recognize it through return and variation.\n\nLet the messages expose the defensive logic. A character can justify the routine confidently, explain it as competence, or repeat a rule as if it settles the matter. Another person may state the alternative plainly in a message. The refusal should remain intelligent and supported by duty, pride, love, fear, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief the story has already made credible. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative.\n\nUse concrete message details to make the loss legible. A missed reply, an unreturned question, a changed routine, a message left on read, an edited line, a delayed apology: these are the materials of the fall. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition may come too late to change the act but still change what the loss means. Ordinary care can coexist with the destructive choice; do not erase all warmth before the end.\n\nKeep required private experience available under the active contract, clearly separated from the transcript. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Context outside the exchange may appear when required. A scene may close on unfinished work, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a completed punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the fall through scene-anchored recurrence. Give the focal character a repeated routine or phrase that can be shown in the scene: the same way of entering a room, checking a list, holding an object, answering a question. Let the recurrence appear in scene-setting, dialogue, and focal narration without explanation. The reader should recognize the pattern through its accumulation, not through a caption or a character announcing it.\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line. Keep scene changes legible. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nLet dialogue and focal narration expose the defensive logic. A character can justify the routine confidently, explain it as competence, or repeat a rule as if it settles the matter. Another person may state the alternative plainly. The refusal should remain intelligent and supported by duty, pride, love, fear, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief the scene has made credible. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative.\n\nUse concrete scene detail to make the loss legible. A missed appointment, an unreturned key, a changed routine, a signature, a meal left covered, a child's question, a colleague's silence: these are the materials of the fall. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition may come too late to change the act but still change what the loss means. Ordinary care can coexist with the destructive choice; do not erase all warmth before the end.\n\nKeep required private experience available under the active contract, clearly separated from other characters' knowledge. A scene may close on unfinished work, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a forced reversal or punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqacbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Web Serial\n\nWrite the fall as a cumulative installment arc. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.\n\nGive the focal character a repeated practical rule, phrase, or routine that returns across installments. It may appear as a remembered instruction, a count, a physical check, or a phrase the character repeats to stay organized. The recurrence should sound like competence, not confession. As the serial proceeds, show what the rule protects, what it cannot settle, and what it costs to keep. Do not announce the rule's meaning; let the reader recognize it through return and variation.\n\nLet the prose expose the defensive logic. A character can justify the routine confidently, explain it as competence, or repeat a rule as if it settles the matter. Another person may state the alternative plainly. The refusal should remain intelligent and supported by duty, pride, love, fear, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief the story has already made credible. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or combative.\n\nUse concrete detail to make the loss legible across installments. A missed appointment, an unreturned key, a changed routine, a signature, a meal left covered, a child's question, a colleague's silence: these are the materials of the fall. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition may come too late to change the act but still change what the loss means. Ordinary care can coexist with the destructive choice; do not erase all warmth before the end.\n\nKeep required private experience available under the active contract, but other characters do not know it unless the story gives them access. An installment may close on unfinished work, a refusal, or a quiet acknowledgment rather than a completed punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the turbulent feeling through the body's contact with the world: a hand that will not stay still, a cup set down too hard, a door held open longer than needed, a task resumed because speech has failed. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to make the cost of admitting, needing, withholding, apologizing, or returning visible in what characters do and handle. Let the landscape or room bear the pressure they will not name: heat, cold, a broken latch, a floor that needs sweeping, a road they keep watching.\n\nKeep the exchanges spare and let them break. A character may begin a sentence and stop, repeat a word, answer a question that was not asked, or say the practical thing while the real matter stays in the air. Do not translate every pause into a feeling. Let the other person notice the pause, misread it, or choose not to answer it. The strain should alter the actual conversation and the behavior that follows: someone leaves a chore unfinished, returns to a doorway, picks up an object and puts it down unchanged.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through choices that become harder to reverse, not through constant punishment. Let alternatives and warnings be intelligible. People may act with intelligence and still choose what costs them. When recognition comes, make it change what the loss means: a character sees what the withheld apology did, what the returned favor was for, what the unspoken need had already cost. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored without turning every scene into a wound. Warmth may appear as a meal prepared, a coat offered, a silence shared, but it remains partial and awkward, coexisting with the hurt rather than repairing it.\n\nUse the chosen narrator's distance and diction. If the narrator is close, render the turbulence as immediate physical attention and interrupted reasoning. If the narrator is more distant, let the turbulence show in repeated gestures, unfinished exchanges, and the gap between what characters say and what they do. Keep private thought inside the viewpoint character's knowledge. Do not make the cast uniformly eloquent about their pain; some people name it, some work around it, some refuse it until it names them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each reply as a coherent shared interval with clear speakers and readable paragraphs. Use McCarthy's spare exchanges so that practical speech carries bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A character may agree to something, refuse a small request, or answer a practical question while the larger matter remains unspoken. Keep dialogue attribution unmistakable, and let action and speech move together: a character speaks while working, turns away before answering, or sets down a tool before making a promise.\n\nLet the tragic arc develop through the accumulating cost of choices rather than through a single disaster in every reply. Warnings and alternatives may be intelligible. A character can see a better course and still choose against it for a reason the scene makes legible: duty, pride, love, fear of what admission would cost. Do not make everyone foolish. The loss should grow from the particular commitments and conditions already present in the scene.\n\nUse concrete nouns and forceful verbs to ground the interval in material life. Show the weather, the work, the condition of a room or road, the weight of an object. These details carry the pressure the characters do not announce. When recognition comes, let it alter what the loss means for the person who sees it, even if the others do not yet know.\n\nLeave a continuation that follows from the scene's actual state. The next reply may begin with a character returning to a task, answering a question left open, or acting on a decision just made. Do not force a cliffhanger, a question, or a choice screen. The scene may end with someone walking away, someone waiting, or a silence that the next speaker will have to break. Preserve the active authorship contract, including narrative person, action markers, and player control.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the story as a sequence of remembered or recounted episodes, each with its own concrete occasion: a departure, a debt, a meal, a quarrel, a return, a death. Let each episode stand as a legible interval while the larger arc accumulates through recurrence. A place revisited, an object kept or lost, an obligation repeated, a name spoken again: these carry the weight of what has changed.\n\nWrite the episodes with McCarthy's stark material attention. Use concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Show the road, the weather, the condition of a house or field, the labor of hands. Let practical speech carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. A character may agree to a marriage, refuse a journey, or repay a debt while the deeper cost remains unspoken. Keep the exchanges spare and let silence do its work.\n\nDevelop the tragic arc through choices that become harder to reverse as the episodes accumulate. Let warnings and alternatives be intelligible. People may act with intelligence and still choose what costs them. Recognition changes what the loss means: in a later episode, a character sees what an earlier choice set in motion, what a withheld word did, what a returned object meant. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored without turning every episode into a punishment.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community actually supplied distinct. Do not transplant a setting or cast identity from the source tradition. The episodic structure and recurrence are the craft method; the particular place, time, and people come from the active story. Use the chosen narrator's distance and diction, and preserve the active authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Move from one concrete action to the next without explanatory padding: a gate opened, a rope cut, a fire built, a debt called in. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement so that linked clauses accumulate the weight of what is happening. Let the material world carry the stakes: worn tack, a cracked blade, a roof that will not hold, boots gone thin, water rising, a road washed out.\n\nGround the tragic arc in physical consequence. An irreversible choice should register in what breaks, wears, or must be abandoned. A character may sell a tool, leave a field, burn a letter, or refuse a ride, and the loss should be visible in the aftermath: the empty stall, the cold hearth, the unplanted ground. Do not turn every scene into survival horror. The pressure comes from the particular commitments and conditions of the story, not from a generic atmosphere of threat.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep speech spare and practical. Bargains, convictions, and withheld motives can ride on short exchanges. A character may say what the work requires while the larger matter stays unspoken. Let the other person hear what they hear, not what the narrator knows. Ground abrasive or rough speech in its speaker: a person's harshness should come from their situation, not from a uniform tone.\n\nUse changes of rhythm deliberately. A longer thought is warranted when the character's reasoning needs it: before a choice, after a loss, when recognition arrives. Recognition changes what the loss means. Let a character see what the worn object, the abandoned place, or the broken promise has cost, and let the aftermath preserve what cannot be restored. Keep the fall causally legible through the sequence of actions and their material results.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires: a letter, a journal entry, a report, a receipt, a will, a note left on a table. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Let each document carry only what its author would record at that moment, in language that fits the writer and the occasion.\n\nWrite the documents with McCarthy's spare, practical diction. A letter may state a bargain, a debt, a departure, a refusal. A journal may record the weather, the work, the condition of a road, the name of someone who did not come. Let what is omitted do its work. A writer may conceal a motive, fail to understand a warning, or record a promise without knowing what it will cost. The gap between what a document says and what the reader can infer is part of the tragedy.\n\nDevelop the arc across entries. An early document may contain a choice that a later document reveals as irreversible: a sale recorded before the price was known, a refusal answered by silence, a letter sent too late. Let recognition arrive when a later writer or reader sees what the earlier words set in motion. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a final entry may note the empty room, the unclaimed letter, the field left fallow, without explaining its full meaning.\n\nPreserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the documents' sequence, dates, and recurring details connect the entries. A place name, an object, a phrase repeated in a later hand can show what has changed. Keep each document's knowledge limited to its author's situation, and let the reader assemble the loss from what the documents know and conceal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness and practical care, rendered through McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Show the meal prepared, the coat mended, the fire built, the child lifted, the tea poured, the road walked together. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair. A character may show love by remembering how someone takes their coffee, by saving a portion, by waiting in the cold, by doing a chore that was not asked for.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. A character may accept care awkwardly, refuse it once, or offer it while still angry. Manageable friction can sit beside warmth: a sharp word over a small thing, a difference about how to spend the day, a preference stated plainly. Let the particular people remain themselves.\n\nLet the tragic arc continue through and around the comfort. The warmth is real, and that is part of what the loss will cost. A shared meal may be the last before a departure. A mended coat may be worn on a journey that does not end well. A kindness may be remembered after the person who offered it is gone. Do not make every caring act fraudulent or turn comfort into a trap. The tragedy is not that warmth was false; it is that warmth could not hold against what came.\n\nWhen recognition arrives, let it change what the loss means. A character may remember the small pleasure, the familiar preference, the practical care, and understand what it stood for. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: the recipe, the mended seam, the chair by the fire, the habit of waiting. Let the story end with the loss intact and the warmth still real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let a scene open into its social context: the history of a place, the obligations of a family, the conditions of work, the public meaning of a private choice. Where the chosen narrator permits, frame the action through judgment or reflection, but keep the judgment situated in the narrator's actual distance and knowledge, not a disembodied moral voice.\n\nWrite the material world with McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. The landscape and labor remain present: the road, the field, the house, the tools, the weather, the work of hands. Let linked clauses carry the movement of a scene without losing the formal paragraph's room to pause and consider. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. The narrator may observe, reflect, or withhold, but the prose should remain composed rather than conversational.\n\nDevelop the tragic arc through social conditions and incompatible commitments as well as individual choice. Let warnings and alternatives be intelligible. A character may act against their own interest because of duty, pride, love, or the weight of what is expected. Show how the social world narrows or complicates the available choices. Do not make everyone foolish or reduce the fall to a single flaw. The tragedy should emerge from the interaction of character, conditions, and consequence.\n\nLet recognition arrive with formal weight. A later passage may revisit an earlier scene, a public event, or a private moment and show what it has come to mean. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a house changed, a name remembered differently, a debt that cannot be repaid. Use public/private contrast where relevant: a ceremony, a correspondence, a public judgment, a private grief. Keep the formal architecture in service of the tragic arc, not decoration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer story as a present occasion where someone tells a tale to a particular audience under pressure of time, need, or obligation. The teller is not neutral. Establish early what the telling is meant to accomplish: to persuade a listener to act, to explain a choice already made, to delay a decision, or to make a loss bearable. Then make the act of telling itself a tragic choice. The teller decides where to begin, what to compress, and what to omit, and each decision has a cost in the present: a delay that closes an option, a confidence that cannot be taken back, a version of events that commits the teller to a position.\n\nGround both narrative levels in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. When the outer story mentions a room, a road, a tool, or a body, give it weight and use. When the embedded tale describes labor, weather, or an object handled by its characters, make the detail carry the cost of the choice being recounted. Let practical speech carry bargains and convictions in both levels. Keep exchanges spare where the teller or listener is withholding, and allow longer paratactic accumulation when the tale moves through a sequence of consequences.\n\nCompose the embedded tale as an active intervention in the outer tragedy, not as a detachable illustration. The tale should show a choice, its alternatives, and its irreversible result. The listener may recognize a parallel, reject the comparison, or hear a warning the teller did not intend. The outer story's tragedy does not pause for the tale; the telling consumes time, tests patience, and may close off an option that was still open when the tale began. Show that consumption concretely: a listener who grows restless, a task left waiting, a door that cannot be reached in time.\n\nLet a repeated phrase, object, or gesture accrue meaning across the levels. When it appears in the embedded tale and again in the outer frame, it should mark a connection the reader can feel without the teller explaining it. Do not make the repetition certify that the tale is true or that the teller is wise. The teller may be mistaken about what the tale means, and the listener may draw a conclusion the teller cannot accept.\n\nPreserve the difference between what the teller knew at the time of the embedded events and what the teller knows now. Recognition belongs to the present telling, not retroactively to the past. The aftermath of the embedded tale can show what was lost and what the teller has done with that loss. The outer story can end with the listener's response, the teller's silence, or the resumption of the practical business that the telling interrupted. Do not require a cliffhanger between nested accounts or a final moral that resolves the tragedy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or developmental realism route. Write the tragedy as a process that unfolds across time, work, and social conditions rather than as a single catastrophic scene. Structure the fall in stages: each period of the character's life is marked by a particular material situation—a trade learned, a position held, a household changed, a debt assumed—and each stage makes the next choice narrower without making the character a mere product of circumstances. Show the alternatives that existed at each stage and why they were not taken.\n\nGround the developmental arc in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. When the story moves through years or decades, anchor each stage in specific physical detail: the tools of a trade, the condition of a room, the weight of a load, the look of a road after weather. Let labor and landscape carry the passage of time. Use paratactic movement to connect events without smoothing over the gaps between them. A sentence can move from a decision to its consequence and leave the reader to feel the interval.\n\nKeep practical speech central to the social world. Characters should bargain, refuse, explain, and withhold through dialogue that sounds like people managing their lives. A parent and child, an employer and worker, or two neighbors can reveal the social conditions of the story through what they ask of each other and what they will not say. Do not turn dialogue into sociological exposition. Let the conditions appear through the demands people make and the limits they accept or resist.\n\nMaintain a third-person narrator who observes the character's labor and speech closely while withholding full access to every private thought. This distance belongs to the social realism route: the reader sees the character from outside enough to understand the social position, and moves inward only at moments of decision, memory, or physical strain. When interiority appears, make it specific to the character's situation: the calculation before a bargain, the memory of a warning, the physical sensation of exhaustion or relief.\n\nLet recognition arrive late and partially. The character may understand the full cost of a choice only after the conditions that made it seem necessary have changed, or after someone else has paid the price. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a relationship, a place, a capacity for work, or a claim on another person. Do not resolve the social conditions at the end. The tragedy is that the character's fall is real and particular, not that the whole society has been explained or repaired.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the tragedy around a collision between inherited power and present life. The old authority may be a family, an institution, a building, a debt, a title, or a set of obligations that outlast the people who created them. Make that authority concrete. Show the physical facts through which it operates: keys, ledgers, rooms, clothing, rituals of access, the condition of a property, the terms of a contract. The reader should understand what the character must do to satisfy, resist, or escape the old claim.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to give the modern decay its texture. When a character moves through an urban space, show the materials: worn stairs, locked doors, the smell of a basement, the weight of a bag, the sound of traffic beyond a window. Let the atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced without replacing the practical facts of the encounter. A room can feel oppressive because of what the character knows will happen there, not because the prose insists on dread.\n\nMake the inherited power's weakness part of the tragedy. The old authority does not need to be omnipotent. It can be decaying, contested, or dependent on the very people it claims to control. Show that dependence concretely: a title that requires money to maintain, an institution that needs new members, a property that cannot be kept without labor. The character's choices then occur inside a structure that is already failing, and the fall is sharper because the authority is not an abstract fate but a set of arrangements the character could partly see and partly misjudge.\n\nLet intimacy complicate the power structure. A character may love someone who enforces the old claim, protect someone who resists it, or find that a trusted relationship is the only place where a social mask can fall. Write those moments with the same concrete attention as the public scenes. A private conversation in a back room can carry more weight than a formal confrontation. The tragedy should turn on what the character is willing to lose in order to keep or break an intimate bond.\n\nDo not import Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The inherited power is social, economic, familial, or institutional, not supernatural. Recognition should come through the character's growing understanding of what the old authority has cost and what the character's own choices have destroyed. The aftermath preserves the loss: a place abandoned, a relationship ended, a debt still owed, a body or a name no longer present. End with the practical residue of the fall, not with a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. Write the tragedy inside a specific family, household, or community where daily obligations carry the weight of the fall. Make the household itself the arena of the tragedy: the shared rooms, objects, routines, and debts are the medium through which duty, love, and money narrow the character's choices. The character's fall should be legible through what happens to the household—a room given up, a routine broken, an object sold or kept, a person no longer at the table—rather than through a separate public event.\n\nGround the domestic world in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. When the story moves through a house, a market, a workplace, or a journey, give each space its physical facts: the condition of a floor, the weight of a vessel, the heat of a stove, the sound of a door. Let labor and landscape carry the family's situation. Use paratactic movement to connect the daily events without explaining every motive. A sentence can move from a morning's work to an evening's decision and let the reader feel what intervened.\n\nKeep practical speech central to the domestic relationships. Characters should bargain, refuse, comfort, and withhold through dialogue that sounds like people managing a shared life. A parent and child, a husband and wife, a sibling and sibling, or a neighbor and neighbor can reveal the social conditions through what they ask of each other and what they will not say. Do not turn dialogue into a lesson about the community. Let the conditions appear through the demands people make and the limits they accept or resist.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the domestic aftermath. The character may understand the full cost of a choice only after a relationship has changed, a household has broken, or someone has left. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a marriage, a place in the family, a child's trust, or a claim on a shared home. Do not resolve the social conditions at the end. The tragedy is that the character's fall is real and particular, not that the whole community has been explained or repaired.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific only when the world supplies them. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The domestic world should feel particular through its labor, objects, and obligations, not through a manufactured inventory of cultural markers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or psychological realism route. Write the tragedy as a continuous narrative that follows a character through work, relationships, and the practical conditions of a particular life. The character's fall should emerge from intelligible choices shaped by duty, love, pride, or mistaken belief. Show the alternatives that exist within the character's social world: a refusal that would cost a position, a confession that would end a relationship, a departure that would leave someone unprotected.\n\nGround the psychological arc in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. When the character moves through a house, a workplace, a street, or a journey, give each space its physical facts: the condition of a floor, the weight of a package, the heat of a room, the sound of a train or a door. Let labor and landscape carry the passage of time. Use paratactic movement to connect events without explaining every motive. A sentence can move from a morning's work to an evening's decision and let the reader feel what intervened.\n\nLet seasonal attention mark the tragedy's temporal arc. A change in weather, light, or the condition of a familiar place can show that time has passed and that the character's situation has hardened. Use a seasonal detail only when it carries the character's experience or the story's movement; do not make a mandatory inventory of scenery. The same restraint applies to omission and direct feeling: a character may say little and feel much, or may speak plainly about what they want and fear. Write the particular character, not a universal quietness.\n\nKeep practical speech central to the social relationships. Characters should bargain, refuse, comfort, and withhold through dialogue that sounds like people managing a shared life. A parent and child, an employer and worker, or two friends can reveal the social conditions through what they ask of each other and what they will not say. Do not turn dialogue into psychological exposition. Let the character's interiority appear through specific attention, calculation, memory, or sensation rather than through a running explanation of every feeling. Move between outward observation and inward access at moments of decision, strain, or recognition, so the reader feels the character's mind as a particular instrument rather than a continuous commentary.\n\nLet recognition arrive late and partially. The character may understand the full cost of a choice only after a relationship has changed, a position has been lost, or someone has left. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a home, a friendship, a capacity for work, or a claim on another person. Do not resolve the social conditions at the end. The tragedy is that the character's fall is real and particular, not that the whole society has been explained or repaired.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism/chronicle/testimony route. Write the tragedy as a witnessed process grounded in social conditions, labor, and practical survival. Organize the fall as a chronicle: the narrator or focal character observes and records particular events, places, and exchanges, and the reader follows the tragedy through what is seen, heard, and remembered rather than through a detached summary. The character's fall should emerge from intelligible choices shaped by duty, love, pride, or mistaken belief under pressure from family, work, land, money, or institutional power.\n\nGround the chronicle in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. When the story moves through a house, a field, a workplace, a road, or a town, give each space its physical facts: the condition of a roof, the weight of a load, the heat of a day, the sound of a vehicle or a door. Let labor and landscape carry the social conditions. Use paratactic movement to connect events without explaining every motive. A sentence can move from a morning's work to an evening's decision and let the reader feel what intervened.\n\nKeep practical speech central to the testimony. Characters should bargain, refuse, accuse, and withhold through dialogue that sounds like people managing their lives under pressure. A worker and an employer, a parent and a child, or two neighbors can reveal the social conditions through what they ask of each other and what they will not say. Do not turn dialogue into political exposition. Let the conditions appear through the demands people make and the limits they accept or resist.\n\nDistinguish personal participation from reported events when the narration includes testimony. A character who witnessed an event knows something different from a character who heard about it later. Mark that difference through the chronicle's phrasing: what the character saw, what someone said later, what the character only learned after the fact. Preserve that difference without turning the story into a clinical case summary. The chronicle should feel like a life being lived and remembered, not like a file being assembled.\n\nLet recognition arrive late and partially. The character may understand the full cost of a choice only after a relationship has changed, a place has been lost, or someone has left. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a home, a livelihood, a community, or a claim on another person. Do not resolve the social conditions at the end. The tragedy is that the character's fall is real and particular, not that the whole society has been explained or repaired. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history only when the world supplies them. Do not add supernatural facts or treat the marvelous as ordinary unless the canon permits it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene cleanly by establishing where the character is and what practical situation is already in motion. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when the character moves from one room to another, from inside to outside, or from one task to the next, give the reader a clear physical marker. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to make each space feel real without slowing the scene. A room has a floor, a door, a window, a weight to its air. A road has a surface, a direction, a condition.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. A short paragraph of action can establish what the character is doing. A line of dialogue can carry a bargain, a refusal, or a withheld motive. A reaction beat can register what the character feels or understands without explaining it at length. The reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or quiet according to its owner. Do not force a witty or quippy tone. Let the tragedy's weight accumulate through what the character notices and what they do not say.\n\nKeep practical speech central. Characters should bargain, refuse, comfort, and withhold through dialogue that sounds like people managing their lives. A parent and child, an employer and worker, or two friends can reveal the social conditions through what they ask of each other and what they will not say. Do not turn dialogue into exposition. Let the conditions appear through the demands people make and the limits they accept or resist.\n\nLet the tragedy unfold through the scene structure. Each scene should carry one choice, its alternatives, or its consequence. The alternatives must be intelligible: a refusal that would cost too much, a departure that would leave someone unprotected, a silence that would preserve a relationship at a price. The character remains capable of choosing differently. The reader should see what was available and why it was not taken.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the reaction beats and the aftermath. The character may understand the full cost of a choice only after a relationship has changed, a place has been lost, or someone has left. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a home, a livelihood, a friendship, or a claim on another person. Do not require a closing cliffhanger. The scene can end with the practical residue of the fall: a door closed, a task unfinished, a silence that holds. First person, comedy, and a young cast are not requirements of this container. Write the particular character and the particular loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the tragic fall through the material world first, then let rhythm carry what no one says. Open a scene with a concrete noun and a verb of ongoing labor or passage: a fence rail being split, smoke turning over itself above a chimney, a road cut into rock and already empty. The protagonist's movement across this world gains weight because the prose has already established that things resist and persist. Do not explain the loss at the outset; let the syntax itself begin to know it. A long sentence that accumulates clauses can enact a mind still moving while the conclusion waits. A short sentence after it can be the recognition.\n\nUse recurring images as the tragedy's forward pressure. A particular object or sight—a chipped cup, a gate that will not close, the way water folds around a stone—returns at intervals, each time with a slightly altered charge. The first appearance is only observation. The second or third carries memory. The last may be seen by someone who has already lost the person or thing the image once belonged to. Let the return be literal, not explained. The prose must do the associative work: adjacent clauses, shared sounds, a word that echoes an earlier passage, so the reader knows the image is bearing more than its present content.\n\nKeep the poetry inside the narrator's attention, not imposed on the characters. The protagonist's speech follows their established habits and present purpose; practical or withheld exchanges can sit against the lyrical narration when earned. Private thought may remain brief and concrete or develop further, using the active Hidden or Visible presentation. When a comparison arrives, it must come from the scene or the teller's associations. A sky the color of a scarred anvil belongs to the landscape, not to every grieving mother who looks up. Avoid adjective accumulation and ornamental solemnity. Lyricism here is structural: the placement of a blunt phrase against a longer accumulating one, the way a repeated sound ties two separated moments together.\n\nAllow alternatives to remain intelligible. The tragic choice does not require the protagonist to ignore obvious warnings; it requires the choice to matter. A person may know the right action and still not take it, or may see two incompatible duties and choose the one that will exact the heavier cost. Show the cost through consequence: a farm fails, a child grows distant, a body breaks. The lyrical style must not soften these events. Loss is not a mood; it is a thing that happens to the world, and the prose should record it with McCarthy's stark precision.\n\nRecognition changes what the loss means. The moment need not be loud. A character may look at a familiar object and know its meaning has altered; the prose can shift to a single short sentence or a repeated image to mark the change. Aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. End a scene with the work still there—someone still feeding the animals, still walking the road—so the tragedy settles into the world rather than becoming a final announcement. Let the reader feel the absence through the continued presence of what remains.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet reflection work through concrete things, not through stated emotion. When the narrator or focal character remembers, anchor the memory in a specific physical detail: the weight of a tool, the smell of a particular meal, the sound a gate made. Write the memory in the same spare prose as the present action, so past and present sit side by side without one being labeled more important. The tragedy accumulates through the difference between then and now, which the reader can infer from a single altered fact: the same road is still there, but no one walks it.\n\nAllow ordinary pleasure to remain pleasurable even as loss approaches. A morning fire that warms the hands is not a betrayal of grief; it is part of the exact world the protagonist is losing. Showing the good detail without an immediate turn to sorrow makes the eventual absence more acute. Do not force every scene to end with an announcement of impermanence. The reflective mode should appear at chosen moments—a pause in work, an empty chair, a question no one answers—not as a permanent filter over every action.\n\nUse small irreversible changes to carry the tragic arc. A first tooth, a gate left open, a path that has washed out, a name no longer called. These details return across scenes with the knowledge that they will not return to their earlier state. When the narrator reflects, anchor the reflection in the narrator's own perception of the changed thing: the path is now narrower, the gate leans, the name has no one to answer it. When a focal character notices the change, render the notice as a specific thought or action without commentary—a hand pausing at the latch, a glance at the empty place—so the reader infers the recognition from the behavior. Keep that interiority real and specific: a thought such as \"He had not known it was the last time\" is less useful than showing the event and later showing what remained after it.\n\nUnfinished attachment belongs here. The person who is lost or the choice that cannot be undone still holds attention. The protagonist may speak of the lost one using the present tense out of habit, then correct to the past. That correction, written plainly, does more than a paragraph of lament. Let dialogue do reflective work too: a practical exchange can carry the whole history of an unresolved relationship if the pressures are written into what is said and not said.\n\nRecognition arrives with delay, as in life. The instant of the fall may be physical and sudden, but understanding what it cost can occur much later, when the character is doing something ordinary. At that point, write the realization in the same quiet register: no exclamation, no moral summary. The aftermath is the new daily world. End with the survivor continuing a task, noticing one missing thing, and going on.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral telling. The story is already known to the listeners, so the tragic tension lives not in what will happen but in how it is told and what the telling does to those who hear it. Open with a situated storyteller and an audience whose presence is concrete: a room, a fire, a gathering, or a traveler resting among others. The teller names the protagonist and the place plainly, then enters the past in the same spare prose. Keep the move into the tale clean; no explanatory transition like \"and now I will tell you.\" Let the first sentence of the inner tale establish its world through concrete nouns and verbs of labor or passage.\n\nGive the inner tale McCarthy's material pressure. The protagonist lives in a world of work, debt, water, tools, and obligations. The tragic choice should be a real decision: between a duty to kin and a promise to an outsider; between a son's departure and a father's pride; between keeping land and telling a truth that will lose it. Warnings are present and intelligible—a neighbor's counsel, a remembered proverb, a physical sign—but the protagonist's reasons are strong enough that the fall remains consequential. Do not make the character foolish or blind. The audience may know the end, but they still follow the reasoning that led there.\n\nThe teller's voice may interrupt the tale, but sparingly. These returns to the frame should be practical or reflective rather than stagey. A listener may object to a detail, ask a question, or supply a consequence the teller had not reached. The teller might pause to correct a fact or acknowledge a different version. These moments reveal the community's relationship to the loss and show that the story is still being shaped by memory. Keep the teller's access limited to what a teller could know: reported speech, observed action, and public consequence. When the inner tale needs focal interiority, render it as the teller's inference from what was seen or said—a hesitation before a doorway, a public answer that came too quickly, a known consequence that explains an earlier silence. Do not grant the teller direct access to an unspoken mind.\n\nDo not impose a universal regional code. Let the place, period, and community actually supplied determine the details: water rights, sheep, a shared oven, a marriage contract. The story may be set anywhere in the Middle Eastern tradition's range, but choose one concrete location and keep its material culture coherent. Avoid mystic gloss, automatic exile, or religious symbolism unless the source world already establishes them. The tragic arc is social and personal, not a lesson about a whole people.\n\nWhen the inner tale reaches its fall, let the recognition occur first within the tale: the protagonist sees what has happened and what it cost. Then bring the frame into prominence. The listeners' silence or response shows that the loss persists. A character may say something that changes how the teller or another listener now understands the old story; that is a second recognition, and it matters as much as the first. End with the frame's present: a decision to continue, to mend an object, to keep or release a memory. The telling does not restore what was lost, and neither should it end with everything resolved. The continuation itself carries the aftermath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the tragic fall through a narrow selection of concrete details, each one doing more than one job. A gate left open can show routine, a claim on the land, and the beginning of the conflict. A coin placed on a table can show a promise, a debt, and the refusal to speak about it. When you choose a detail, choose one that can do more than one job in the present scene. It may return later in an altered form, so the change itself carries recognition, or its absence may carry the change—the gate now mended, the coin gone from the table. Do not explain the meaning; leave the inference available after the gap.\n\nKeep sentences direct and paratactic. Use short clauses joined by \"and\" rather than subordinate phrasing. The protagonist walks, works, speaks, and waits; the prose does not need to pause for commentary at each step. A longer sentence is allowed when it holds a single essential sequence or an inward connection that will not survive being split. But do not pad a short moment with several performed fragments. The style is economical because each clause advances the reader's knowledge of the world, the stakes, or the choice.\n\nMaintain causal legibility while withholding explanation. The reader must understand why the protagonist chooses as they do, even if the narrator never states the full motive. Show alternatives through small indications: a second character offers help that is refused, a task is left undone, a message is not sent. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue be practical and terse, but let it carry bargains, threats, convictions, and withheld motives. A line like \"You don't have to go\" followed by \"I know that\" can carry the whole weight of a decision if the earlier scenes have established what staying would cost.\n\nThe tragedy should not require constant violence or despair. Loss of land, trust, or a child can be built from a few accumulated facts: the same meal grown simpler over the year; a tool no longer used; a name not spoken. Each loss is shown by what remains: an empty chair at the table, a fence still standing with no stock behind it. The fall breaks out of the ordinary rather than replacing it, so the ordinary must be sharply rendered first.\n\nRecognition can be a single visible action. The protagonist might look at an object and set it down, or turn away from a road, or say one word out loud. The narration does not need to announce \"he understood.\" Let the selected detail carry the understanding, and let the reader infer it from what the character does next. After that, continue the scene to its natural end. The world keeps going; the tragedy is that the character must go on inside it, with the loss now real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the story in continuous contemporary-novel prose that follows the protagonist through scenes of work, family, and choice. Open with physical presence: a particular room, a job being done, a body moving through a landscape. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to keep the world immediate, but do not restrict the narration to pure Henry Jamesian decorum. Let the prose move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, and inner experience without mechanical transitions. The immediate scene sets the register and rhythm: a long sentence when the action stretches or the thought accumulates, a short one when a realization lands.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through a sequence of consequential moments whose causes are legible. The protagonist faces a choice between incompatible commitments—duty to a father and love for a woman, a promise to a friend and a debt to a neighbor, pride and reconciliation. Show the alternatives clearly enough that the reader understands what is being refused, not just what is chosen. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, other characters give counsel, sometimes good, sometimes flawed; their speech should be practical and carry withheld motives. The protagonist's private thought can run beneath or alongside the dialogue, but keep that thought specific. It should not explain what the dialogue already shows unless it adds a genuine tension between what is said and what is known.\n\nUse perception and recollection as material for the fall. A son remembers his father's hands working the same field he now refuses to work; a woman sees the ring she chose not to wear while speaking with the man who gave it. Instead of moving into a full flashback in every scene, choose the moments when memory changes the present situation. A small recollection may be all that is needed before the character returns to the immediate action. The reader gathers the history through these spaced returns, as in a novel, rather than through an opening summary of the past.\n\nParagraph to carry the story forward. A paragraph may contain a single action, a sequence strained through several senses, or dialogue with brief narration. Do not interrupt every beat for reflection; the scene-first method means the present moment carries the accumulated weight, and the narrator moves into inner experience only when it advances the reader's understanding. Transitions should be clean: a time skip marked by a concrete change in light or season, a location shift marked by a physical object or task rather than a recap of what happened between.\n\nThe tragic outcome should be the consequence of the whole sequence, not a sudden twist. Recognition occurs when the protagonist sees the full cost of the action; write that recognition inside a scene, with the setting, another person, or a familiar object bearing part of it. The aftermath moves into daily continuation. The last sentence should place the survivor in the changed world, still engaged in some necessary act, with the loss real and irreversible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild the tragic fall through the established relationship, not alongside it. The pair's specific history supplies the scene selection, the private language, and the promises whose breaking drives the whole arc. If the premise is childhood friends who became lovers, let a shared childhood object or phrase carry their trust; if it is arranged partners growing into mutual respect, let the smallest domestic courtesies mark the progress they are losing. Identify the premise's central emotional promise—the trust, the comfort, the rivalry, the discovery—and then make the tragedy the breaking or betrayal of that precise promise.\n\nUse callbacks carefully. A phrase from an early scene returns later with altered meaning: the same words said in love, then in anger, then in grief. A physical detail—a doorway, a cup, a blade's worn handle—reappears at moments of change. Each callback must earn its place by altering how the present moment is understood, not merely by evoking a past warm scene. McCarthy's sparse dialogue works well for this: the characters may repeat a few words that have become a private vocabulary, and the shift in context does the emotional work. Do not overuse the callbacks; two or three strong ones will carry more weight than a dozen.\n\nGive the relationship enough space before the fall. Show the affection or trust at work in concrete domestic or practical terms: one partner mends what the other broke, saves food, covers a mistake, waits past dark. These are the moments the tragedy will make painful in memory. Jealousy or rivalry, if present, should have a specific source and consequence—not a generic accusation. When conflict appears, let it disturb the pair's history without invalidating it entirely; the loss is tragic because something real is destroyed, not because it never existed.\n\nWithhold inner experience in keeping with McCarthy's profile. The narration may show one partner watching the other from across a room, wondering whether the other still remembers what was promised, but the thought stays concrete and limited. Use actual nonspoken private experience as visible interiority when it advances the relationship's tragic arc; do not replace it with letters or monologues. The other partner's mind remains inaccessible unless the active perspective allows it. Speech can carry their withheld motives; what they do not say matters.\n\nThe fall should come from their particular incompatibility or failure, not from outside accident unless the world itself is built that way. Pride, duty, fear of vulnerability, a promise made to someone else—these are strong enough if written from within. Recognition may happen at different times for the two partners, and that asymmetry can be part of the loss: one understands too late, while the other has already gone. The aftermath preserves the relationship's irreversibility. Show what the characters do next in the changed world: one partner may continue alone, doing something ordinary that once belonged to them both, or both may remain present with the relationship altered beyond what it was. The callback, if it comes, should be an ache rather than a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as a chronicle that knows what will be remembered. Use a deliberate, anonymous narrator who can move from a compact declaration to an expanded account of the act. Open with an orientation in time and place: \"In the third winter after the bridge was built, Aedan crossed the river.\" The opening should not apologize for its form; it should establish that this is a telling of deeds whose full cost will be known only after. Let the narrator occasionally speak of later events (\"He would not cross that river again\"), but keep such prolepsis sparse and tied to a concrete consequence rather than editorializing.\n\nKeep the account concrete and physical. Fields, debts, weapons, horses, fords, frost, and labor surround the characters. A journey's progress is marked by real terrain: a country crossed, a bull lost, a wheel broken, a name given to a place because of what happened there. McCarthy's forceful verbs carry the action without ornament. The speech of the characters is practical, even at moments of oath or quarrel: \"I will hold the ford until you come back\" has more weight than a formal vow. When an oath is made, state what was pledged and make the consequence of breaking it part of the later tragedy.\n\nOrganize the tragedy through public deeds and their recurrence. A particular act—a gift given, a man killed, a field abandoned, a child fostered—becomes the thread that the narrator returns to. Recurring phrasing can be linked to a character or a line of descent: a man's way of speaking appears again in his son or grandson at a later crisis. Do not make every entrance a formulaic epithet. The recurrence matters when it reveals that a pattern or loyalty has survived or failed; the reader should understand why the narrator mentions it again.\n\nLet the saga's wide scope include alternatives. Because the chronicle knows the end, it must also show that things might have gone otherwise. A season of good weather, a message arriving in time, a feud settled by marriage—show these near possibilities through concrete conditions, then let the actual choice close them. The characters remain intelligent and worthy; the fall comes from incompatible duties, pride, a bad bargain, or an edict from outside, not from stupidity. The narrator does not need to moralize; the arrangement of events shows what was lost.\n\nThe recognition is communal as well as individual. The protagonist may see the cost of an act, but the saga also marks it by later consequences: a lack of heirs, a land no longer held, a name no longer spoken. The aftermath can be told briefly, in the same chronicle mode: \"After he was gone, none called the place by its old name.\" The final movement should not restore what was lost. Show what the telling itself continues: a name that survives in a different form, a place remembered for what happened there, or a silence where the old name used to be. The weight of the remembered event settles into the chronicle's present, with the loss real and irreversible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fall through a Scandinavian domestic-social realism of household economy and institutional assessment. The story's pressure comes from a definite inquiry into how a family keeps or loses its standing: what the housing office, employer, school, bank, or municipal authority will record, and what the household must show in return. Keep the actual setting, language, season, and community supplied by the story; do not import snow, isolation, or a generic Nordic temperament.\n\nBuild the prose from McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in paratactic chains. Let clauses accumulate with and or a comma as the material world moves forward. A room is known by what is in it and what is worn from use; a person is known by what they handle, carry, repair, or refuse. Landscape and labor remain present, but they belong to the actual world and season.\n\nMake the tragic arc visible through the gap between the household's private record and the official one. Show the warning as an intelligible statement from someone with standing: a spouse, a parent, a supervisor, a caseworker. Show the alternative as a real path the person could have taken: an appointment kept, a form returned, a debt declared, a child collected on time. The character may hear the warning, answer it, and still proceed for a reason the reader can understand. Do not let the institution become a faceless force; let its demands arrive through particular people, papers, and rooms.\n\nUse practical speech to carry bargains and withheld motives. People discuss what must be done, what it will cost, who will take responsibility, and what the office or the family will accept. A short answer can close a subject or open a new obligation. Let silence do work when the other person knows what was not said.\n\nTrack the consequences through the household's material record: a notice on the table, a changed work schedule, a missed appointment, a repaired object, a child's question, a meal prepared or left uneaten. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition should change the meaning of the loss rather than erase it. The person may understand too late, or understand and still be unable to return.\n\nKeep required interiority in the active presentation as private prose. A character's thought may run beside their speech: what they will not say, what they are counting, what they fear the office will rule. Another character does not know that thought merely because the reader does. Do not let the institutional frame turn the story into a case summary; the records support the human fall, they do not replace it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay format: scene headings when location or time changes, concise action lines in present tense, and dialogue blocks with clear speaker cues. Keep every action line to what the camera could record: a hand on a door, a tool set down, a coat taken from a hook, a light switched off.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the action lines. Let clauses link with and or a comma to show continuous movement: He sets the pail on the step and straightens and looks at the road. Do not fill the page with camera commands. The action line tells what happens; the cut or scene heading tells where the view moves.\n\nLet the tragedy live in what the characters do and refuse to do on screen. Show the warning as a spoken line or a visible alternative: a ticket left on the table, a bag packed by the door, a supervisor waiting at the gate. The character may look at the alternative, say nothing, and continue. Their choice becomes irreversible through the next visible action, not through a speech about fate.\n\nWrite dialogue as spare exchanges that carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. People speak about practical matters: money, work, a child, a debt, a promise, what the law or the family will permit. Let a short line answer a longer question. Let a pause or a look do work that another writer would give to explanation. Do not make every character eloquent; let them be precise about what they know and silent about what they cannot bear to say.\n\nUse cuts and offscreen sound only when the sequence needs them. A door closing offscreen can carry a departure. A machine starting outside can mark the resumption of work. Do not use voice-over to replace private thought unless the active contract separately authorizes it. Required interiority may appear as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue the cast can hear.\n\nBuild the aftermath through visible loss and the cut itself. An empty chair, a tool left where it was dropped, a coat still hanging by the door can be the final image of a scene. Recognition can arrive as a look, a hesitation, or a line spoken too late. The scene can end on a completed action, a refusal, or an unfinished gesture. Do not force a final speech or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: clear speaker names, entrances, exits, and economical stage directions. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character enters carrying a tool, sets it down, crosses to the table, or stands at the door. Each movement should be something an actor can perform and an audience can see.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the stage directions. Keep the directions brief: He sets the pail down. She folds the coat. He stands at the window. Do not add film-camera instructions or prose description that cannot be staged.\n\nLet the tragedy live in what the characters say and do before each other. Show the warning as a spoken line from someone with standing: a spouse, a parent, a neighbor, a supervisor. Show the alternative as a visible object or action: a bag packed, a door left open, a ticket held out. The character may look at it, refuse it, or take it and then set it down. Their choice becomes irreversible through the next entrance or exit, not through a speech about destiny.\n\nWrite dialogue as spare exchanges that carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. People speak about practical matters: work, money, a child, a debt, a promise, what the family or the law will allow. Let a short line answer a longer question. Let a pause do work that another writer would give to explanation. Do not make every character eloquent; let them be precise about what they know and silent about what they cannot bear to say.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they have a dramatic purpose in the public scene, not as a default destination for every private thought. Required interiority may appear as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, not disguised as something another character can hear. A character's private fear or calculation remains private unless the scene gives them a reason to speak it.\n\nBuild the aftermath through visible loss and the stage's public space. An empty chair, a tool left where it was dropped, a coat still hanging by the door can be the final image before an exit. Recognition can arrive as a look, a hesitation, or a line spoken too late. The scene can end on an exit, a completed action, or a refusal. Do not force a final speech or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaday::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind and let associative movement structure the telling. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. The syntax may follow those associations: a clause turns on a remembered voice, a smell returns to a room, a pain in the hand returns to the work that caused it. Keep enough orientation for the reader to distinguish the present scene from recollection or fantasy.\n\nAnchor the drift in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. The mind returns to particular objects: a pail, a coat, a door, a knife, a child's shoe. Let clauses accumulate with and or a comma, moving from one material thing to the next. Do not let the prose become abstract or merely emotional. The thought is made of things handled, places walked, work done or left undone.\n\nLet the tragic fall appear through what the mind keeps returning to and what it will not finish. A warning may surface as a remembered voice, a sentence heard that morning, a look from someone at the table. An alternative may surface as an image of a road not taken, a bag not packed, a door not opened. The character may push the thought away, return to it, or follow it to a conclusion they cannot undo. Recognition changes what the loss means; it does not need to arrive as a single announced epiphany.\n\nUse practical speech sparingly and let it interrupt the mental movement. Another person says something about money, work, a child, a promise. The mind answers, or does not answer, or answers aloud and then continues privately. The spoken line may be short and concrete; the private response may run longer or stop cold. Do not make every character share the focal mind. Other people remain outside it, known only through what they say and do.\n\nLet bodily sensation and physical labor carry the aftermath. The character feels the weight of what they carry, the cold or heat of the room, the soreness in their hands. The loss is present in the body and in the objects left behind. The passage can end on a recurring image, an unfinished thought, or a plain return to the present task. Do not force a resolution or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story as a readable transcript: sender-attributed messages, with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Keep each person's digital habits distinct. One writes in short fragments. Another writes full sentences. One leaves a message unsent. One reads and does not reply.\n\nLet the messages carry McCarthy's spare practical speech and concrete nouns. People write about work, money, a child, a debt, a promise, what the family or the office will allow. A message may be a single concrete line: The truck is at the shop. I can be there by six. Do not make every message eloquent or confessional. Let a short reply close a subject or open a new obligation.\n\nBuild the tragedy through what people send and what they do not send. Show the warning as a message from someone with standing: a parent, a spouse, a supervisor, a caseworker. Show the alternative as a real path made visible in text: an address, an offer, a time to meet. The character may read the message, type a reply, delete it, and send something else. Their choice becomes irreversible through the next transmitted message, not through a speech about fate.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact; it may show what the character considered saying, but it is not automatically their live private thought. Required interiority may appear as clearly separated context outside the exchange, not as a message the other person can see. Another character does not know a private fear or calculation merely because the reader does.\n\nUse interface events only when they matter. A delay may mark hesitation. A read receipt may mark the moment someone knows. An edit may show a softened or hardened choice. Do not fabricate interface events merely to add subtext. Let the transcript's silences do work: a message left unanswered, a typing indicator that stops, a channel gone quiet.\n\nBuild the aftermath through the transcript itself: a final message, a request for something that cannot be returned, a photo or attachment that remains, or the plain absence of a reply. Recognition can arrive as a message sent too late or a draft never completed. The exchange can end on a delivered line, a read receipt, or an unfinished typing indicator. Do not force a final speech or a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in scene-oriented presentation: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep scene changes legible. Use background or expression cues as purposeful references, not executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nAnchor the scene-setting in McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Describe what the viewpoint sees and handles: a table, a coat on a hook, a tool by the door, a window with the light going. Let clauses accumulate with and or a comma to show continuous attention. Do not let the description become abstract or merely atmospheric. The scene is made of particular things.\n\nLet the tragedy live in what the focal character notices and does. Show the warning as a spoken line from someone with standing: a parent, a spouse, a supervisor, a neighbor. Show the alternative as a visible object or action: a bag packed, a door left open, a ticket held out. The character may look at it, say nothing, and continue. Their choice becomes irreversible through the next scene, not through a speech about fate.\n\nWrite dialogue as spare exchanges that carry bargains, convictions, and withheld motives. People speak about practical matters: work, money, a child, a debt, a promise. Let a short line answer a longer question. Let a pause or a look do work that another writer would give to explanation. Do not make every character eloquent; let them be precise about what they know and silent about what they cannot bear to say.\n\nUse focal narration or inner response to carry required interiority. The viewpoint may notice a detail, remember a warning, count what is left, or decide not to speak. Another character does not know that thought merely because the reader does. Keep the inner response concrete and tied to the scene: a remembered voice, a sore hand, a door that will not close.\n\nBuild the aftermath through visible loss and focal recognition: an empty chair, a tool left where it was dropped, a coat still hanging by the door. The scene can end on a completed action, a refusal, or an unfinished gesture. Do not force a final speech or a moral summary. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqadbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies. Each installment should show what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps: a name, a debt, a promise, a place. Do not retell the whole history.\n\nBuild the prose from McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in paratactic chains. Let clauses accumulate with and, then, or a simple comma where the action or the material world moves forward. A room is described by what is in it and what is worn from use; a person is described by what they handle, carry, repair, or refuse. Landscape and labor remain present, but they belong to the actual world and season.\n\nLet the tragedy develop across installments through choices that close alternatives. Show the warning as an intelligible statement from someone with standing: a spouse, a parent, a supervisor, a neighbor. Show the alternative as a real path the person could have taken. The character may hear the warning, answer it, and still proceed for a reason the reader can understand: pride, love, duty, debt, a mistaken belief, or an old commitment that cannot be laid down.\n\nUse practical speech to carry bargains and withheld motives. People discuss what must be done, what it will cost, who will take responsibility, and what the family or the office will accept. They do not need to announce their feelings. Let a short answer close a subject or open a new obligation. Let silence do work when the other person knows what was not said.\n\nTrack the consequences through ordinary changes: a notice, a changed work schedule, a missed appointment, a repaired object, a child's question, a meal prepared or left uneaten. Let the aftermath show what cannot be restored. Recognition should change the meaning of the loss rather than erase it. The person may understand too late, or understand and still be unable to return.\n\nUse earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. An installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Do not force a reversal, a confession, or a new disaster at every break. Keep required interiority in the active presentation as private prose; another character does not know a thought merely because the reader does.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the tragic arc as a series of moments in which a character tries to make a large emotional claim while the ordinary world demands a small practical answer. Select the interruption so it belongs to the character's actual situation: a broken strap, a delayed message, the wrong queue, a missing form, a device that will not charge. The inconvenience does not cancel the feeling. It makes the feeling cost something to express.\n\nLet the narrator notice the gap between what a character wants to mean and what the available words, time, or setting will hold. Use that gap as the source of both the humor and the hurt. When a character tries to apologize, let the apology collide with a concrete obligation, and then let the character choose whether to finish it badly, repeat it in a smaller form, or let the interruption become the apology. The interruption is not an escape from feeling; it is the condition under which feeling must be admitted.\n\nKeep different people's responses distinct. One character may meet strain with elaborate explanation, another with silence, another with a practical task performed too carefully. Do not make everyone equally articulate about pain. The narrator may be dry about the circumstances but should not be dry about the fact that the circumstances matter to the people inside them. Preserve the active narrator's access: the reader should know what the focal character is risking, even when the other characters do not.\n\nWhen a character recognizes what has been lost, let the recognition arrive through a specific object, routine, or unfinished sentence rather than through a general statement about grief. A familiar cup, a repeated route, or a phrase that can no longer be completed may carry the changed meaning. Allow warmth to coexist with unresolved hurt. A gesture of care may be sincere and still not repair anything. A moment of ease may make the next loss more legible without being a trick. The tragedy comes from the choices and commitments, not from a rule that every scene must end in punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-tragedy-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Tragedy · 02/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == tragedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Tragedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-tragedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaqaeab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a shared interval in which each speaker is trying to manage a practical problem that has become emotionally loaded. Attribute every line unmistakably, and let the dialogue carry the tragic pressure rather than summarizing it in narration. A character may be trying to explain why they made an irreversible choice, but the explanation keeps colliding with the immediate needs of the room: someone is looking for a key, a device is making a noise, another person has misunderstood the plan. The collision is not a joke inserted after the feeling; it is how the feeling becomes speakable or unspeakable in company.\n\nLet the speakers differ in what they are willing to admit. One may talk around the loss by explaining the procedure that led to it. Another may name the loss directly and then immediately return to a small task. Another may refuse to discuss it and instead make a practical suggestion that reveals more than they intended. Keep the absurdity in the contrast between the size of what is at stake and the size of what can actually be handled in the current exchange. Do not make every line witty or every speaker equally aware of the irony.\n\nUse brief narration only for concrete stagecraft: a gesture, an object, a pause, a change in who is looking at whom. Do not use narration to explain what a speaker really means unless the active contract grants that access. Let the tragic recognition emerge through what the characters can and cannot say to each other, and leave the continuation open. The scene may end while a question is still unasked, a task is still unfinished, or a character is still deciding whether to speak. Do not force a cliffhanger, confession, or choice screen. The next reply should be able to continue from the actual state of the room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route. Build the tragic arc as a sequence of linked scenes, each with a clear social situation and a concrete practical problem. The episodes should accumulate consequence rather than repeat the same failure. A promise made in one scene becomes an obligation in the next; a misunderstanding resolved too late becomes the condition for a later choice; a small act of care becomes evidence of what is being lost. Keep the telling direct and readable, with the narrator able to move between scene and summary when the episode requires it.\n\nPlace the Adams-style absurdity in the distance between what a social arrangement is supposed to mean and what it actually requires. A ceremony, contract, family obligation, or official procedure may be described with perfect seriousness while its practical execution becomes ridiculous: the necessary object is missing, the wrong person has been invited, the instructions contradict each other. The characters do not all find this funny. Some treat the procedure as sacred, some as an obstacle, some as the only way to avoid saying what they feel. The absurdity should expose the cost of the arrangement, not simply mock it.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the episodic structure. A character may understand something in one scene and be unable to act on it until a later scene, when the opportunity has changed. The narrator can mark the passage of time and the change in circumstances without announcing the moral. Keep the relevant language, period, and community distinct from any imported setting. The method is the episodic vernacular telling, not a transplant of a particular cast or region. Preserve the tragic arc so that the later recognition changes what the earlier loss means, and the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaead::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the tragic arc through material facts. An irreversible choice shows up as a broken object, a worn edge, a locked door, a missed mark, a task done with the wrong tool. Keep transitions abrupt and let the physical world interrupt feeling. A character may be about to say something and instead have to deal with a spill, a jammed mechanism, or a piece of equipment that will not fit. The interruption does not erase the feeling; it forces the character to carry it while working.\n\nUse forceful diction without inflating every sentence. Short, concrete verbs and nouns should carry the weight. When a character's reasoning needs more room, allow a longer thought, but keep it tied to the immediate problem. Do not let the prose become a general meditation on loss. The recognition should arrive as a specific realization: the thing that cannot be fixed is now visible in the condition of a particular object or the state of a particular body.\n\nLet the Adams-style absurdity come from the precise impracticality of a plan that should have worked. The instructions are clear, the parts are labeled, the schedule is agreed, and yet the actual sequence produces a result no one intended. Different characters respond differently: one swears, one tries to repair the procedure, one stands still. The absurdity is not a joke; it is the shape of the failure. Keep the tragic stakes physical and legible. A loss is not announced; it is shown in what can no longer be done, reached, or returned. Preserve the arc so that the final recognition changes the meaning of the earlier damage rather than merely adding more damage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the tragic arc emerge across the documents rather than inside any single record. A letter may try to explain a choice, a report may record the practical failure that followed, a later note may revise or contradict the earlier account. The reader should be able to see what each author knows and what each author is trying to make the record say.\n\nUse the Adams-style absurdity in the gap between the document's official purpose and its actual contents. A formal report may spend most of its space on a missing item, a scheduling error, or a procedure that cannot be completed. A letter of apology may become a detailed explanation of why the apology could not be delivered. A set of instructions may be perfectly clear and still produce the wrong result. The absurdity is not a parody of the form; it is the pressure the form puts on the person trying to use it.\n\nLet different documents know different things. A record may conceal a fact because its author did not know it, did not want to admit it, or did not think it belonged in that kind of document. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts should reveal the loss gradually. Do not add an omniscient explanation after every entry. When recognition comes, let it arrive through the accumulated documents: a later entry may show that the author has understood what an earlier entry could not say. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored, often through the material trace of a document that was never sent, never answered, or never corrected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the tragic arc include real warmth. Spend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, and small pleasures. A character may make someone's preferred drink, remember how they like a room arranged, or complete a small task that eases the day. The comfort is sincere. It does not repair the underlying loss, and it does not need to. Its presence makes the loss more specific: the reader sees what the characters are trying to preserve and what they can still give each other.\n\nUse the Adams-style absurdity in the logistics of care. The kind gesture may require the wrong implement, an inconvenient detour, a misunderstanding about the instructions, or a long explanation of why the usual method will not work. The characters can be fond of each other and still disagree about the procedure. Do not make the absurdity cancel the warmth. The point is that care is often clumsy and still matters. Different people should show care differently: one through food, one through fixing something, one through sitting nearby without demanding a conversation.\n\nKeep the tragedy active. The warmth does not make everyone agreeable or remove the established problem. A moment of ease may be followed by a choice that cannot be taken back. A kind act may be remembered later as the last time something was still possible. Recognition can arrive through the contrast between the comfort and what it cannot hold. Do not force a confession or an emotional repair. The affection may remain unspoken, partially spoken, or spoken too late. The aftermath should preserve the warmth as part of what has been lost, not as a cure for it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite the tragic arc in composed novel prose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Let the narrator frame a scene through its social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits. A decision may be introduced through the expectations surrounding it: what the family, institution, or community believes it means, what the character has been told it will require, and what the actual circumstances turn out to be. The framing should make the fall intelligible without excusing it.\n\nUse the Adams-style absurdity in the narrator's precise attention to the gap between public form and private consequence. A ceremony, agreement, or official explanation may be described with full seriousness while the practical details expose its contradictions. The narrator may note that the procedure was designed to prevent exactly the outcome it produces, or that the instructions were followed with care and still led to the wrong result. Do not let the narrator mock the characters for caring about the outcome. The absurdity belongs to the arrangements, not to the fact that the people inside them are suffering.\n\nKeep the narrative distance from becoming cold. The reader should understand what the focal character risks, notices, and fails to notice. Let recognition arrive through a composed passage that gathers earlier details and shows their changed meaning. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored, often through a public or private ritual that now carries a different weight. Do not force an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary. The formal architecture is the method; the social world is whatever the story supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the story as a frame: a storyteller is speaking to a specific audience because the outer situation gives the telling an immediate purpose. The embedded narrative carries the tragic arc, while the frame shows the telling itself changing the teller and the listeners.\n\nGive the teller a visible stake in the selection and emphasis of the embedded story. The teller may defend a friend, justify a refusal, or explain why a warning was ignored. When the teller reaches a moment of grand intention or moral claim, let the outer scene interrupt through a small practical obstruction: a lamp fails, a cup overturns, a listener must leave and return, a door will not stay shut. Treat the interruption as a real delay in the telling, not as a pause for commentary. The teller resumes by returning to the same problem from a different angle, often with a concrete detail that was previously omitted.\n\nInside the embedded tale, construct the fall through Adams-style contrast between a large commitment and a small logistical failure. A character may intend to keep a promise, expose a wrong, or protect someone, but the plan stalls on a missing key, a form with contradictory instructions, a vehicle that will not start, or a message delivered to the wrong person. Show the failure as absurd in its particulars and consequential in its result. The humor belongs to the situation and to the teller's phrasing; it does not cancel the loss.\n\nUse the audience to make the embedded story's alternatives intelligible. A listener may ask why the character did not take a different route, and the teller must supply the practical reason that made the alternative impossible at that moment. The listener's question can reveal a fact the teller had not considered important, changing how the teller understands the story while it is being told. Let the teller's recognition arrive through this exchange rather than through an announced moral.\n\nRepeat a concrete object, gesture, or phrase across the frame and the embedded tale. The repetition should first appear as an incidental detail, then return at a moment of loss or recognition with its meaning altered. The teller may notice the recurrence, or a listener may point it out; either way, the repeated element connects the narrative levels without certifying that the embedded story is true.\n\nEnd the telling at a point where the outer situation requires a pause: the teller needs to recover, the audience must act on what they have heard, or the occasion itself is interrupted. Do not force a cliffhanger. The final effect should be a changed understanding in the frame that preserves what the embedded tale cannot restore.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modernist-absurdist construction from the German-language routes. Write in a controlled third-person narration that can hold a long sentence together while it lays out an institutional or social arrangement, then break into a short concrete sentence when the arrangement fails. The long sentence should build the character's reasonable expectation; the short sentence should record the exact point where the system does not meet it.\n\nBuild the tragic fall as a collision between a large moral commitment and the procedures that administer daily life. The character acts from love, duty, pride, or a mistaken belief, and the story shows the act being received by a form, a department, a family expectation, a transit schedule, or a technical requirement. Make the procedure specific: name the form, the office, the regulation, the device. The absurd consequence should be the direct result of the procedure's own logic, not an arbitrary misfortune.\n\nUse Adams-style digression as the main way to expose that logic. When the narration pauses to explain a procedure, let the explanation reveal an apparently reasonable rule whose actual operation is absurd: a regulation designed to prevent delay that requires three approvals, a repair system that cannot process the part it was built to replace, a schedule that makes the promised connection impossible. The digression should return to the character's situation with the cost of the procedure now visible. Do not use the digression as a separate comic set piece; it must deepen the trap the character is in.\n\nKeep the narration at a slight distance from the character's feelings, rendering emotion through observed action, hesitation, and the physical handling of objects. Still enter the character's reasoning and uncertainty, especially when the character tries to find a way through the procedure. Let different characters respond differently to the same system: one may rage, one may comply, one may find a loophole, one may refuse to believe the rule is real. None of these responses should make the character foolish; each should be a recognizable way of coping with an arrangement that exceeds individual control.\n\nMake warnings and alternatives intelligible. A secondary character may explain a different route, or the protagonist may recall a prior decision that closed one off. The protagonist should be able to hear the warning and still proceed, because the original commitment remains real and the alternative carries its own unacceptable cost.\n\nLet the recognition arrive through a concrete procedural detail that the character finally understands: the form that was never going to be accepted, the regulation that was always going to be applied, the schedule that made the promise impossible from the start. The recognition should be quiet and specific, not a general statement about the world. End with the aftermath of the loss, showing what the character continues to do in the altered situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the story in a contemporary urban environment where an old authority—an institution, a family inheritance, a property system, a legacy of debt—continues to shape private life. Make the authority legible through material arrangements: a decaying building whose repairs require permission, a device that works only when a particular person operates it, a room that reveals its history through wear and mismatched furniture, a procedure that everyone follows without remembering why it began.\n\nPlace a character in a situation where love, duty, pride, or a mistaken belief triggers a choice that must pass through the old authority's arrangements. Show the choice as reasonable from inside, even as alternatives remain visible. Then make the practical obstruction a direct expression of the authority: the key is held by someone who will not release it, the signature requires a person who is never present, the file is stored in a room that only a former family member may enter, the working equipment belongs to an institution that has declared it obsolete. The obstruction should feel absurd in its particulars, but its absurdity should expose how the old authority has arranged daily life so that certain choices become almost impossible.\n\nUse Adams-style explanation to reveal the authority's logic. When the narration pauses to explain why a permission is required, a debt is owed, or a device is kept, let the explanation expose an apparently reasonable rule whose actual operation is absurd: a repair fund that cannot be spent without the approval of a dead signatory, a key that opens only the wrong door, a debt that grows because the repayment procedure no longer exists. The explanation should return to the character's situation with the cost now visible.\n\nLet intimacy and social masks matter. In public or semi-public spaces—an office, a stairwell, a shared courtyard—the character maintains a performance shaped by the old authority's expectations. In trusted company, the performance can fall away. Write the private moment in close interiority: the character may reveal fear, desire, doubt, or the specific calculation behind the public performance. The private moment should show what the mask has cost, not merely that it exists.\n\nLet atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced. A meeting in a public office can feel oppressive because of the authority's material presence; a private conversation can expose vulnerability because the trusted person is not bound by the same performance. Different characters respond differently to the old authority: some comply, some resist, some exploit it, some have stopped noticing it. The tragedy emerges from the clash of these responses, not from a single villain.\n\nMake warnings intelligible. A person the character trusts may explain the risk, or the character may recall a prior incident that showed what the authority does to those who resist. The character should be able to hear the warning and still proceed, because the original commitment remains real and the alternative carries its own unacceptable cost.\n\nEnd with recognition and aftermath. The recognition should be specific: the character understands which arrangement made the loss inevitable, or which permission was never going to be granted. The aftermath should show the character continuing to live with the old authority's material presence and the absence the choice created.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-domestic realist route from the Indian literary traditions. Write in close third-person narration that stays with the protagonist's reasoning and uncertainty while observing the household and its neighbors with plain, direct attention. Use concrete domestic details—a cramped kitchen, a shared transport schedule, a queue for water or electricity, a food item whose price has risen—to make the situation felt without sentimentalizing it.\n\nGive the protagonist a consequential commitment: love, loyalty, pride, or a mistaken belief. Show that the commitment is reasonable under the household's actual economic and relational constraints. Let family members or neighbors offer alternatives, and let the protagonist reject them for reasons the reader can understand, even when the reader sees the cost.\n\nMake the Adams-style obstruction arise from the household's specific situation rather than from a generic list of misfortunes. The missing form should be the one the household needs for a particular entitlement; the dead phone should be the one the protagonist uses to reach a particular person; the late bus should be the one that connects the household to work or to a promise; the changed rule should be the landlord's, the employer's, or the lender's specific new condition. Each small failure should increase pressure on the original commitment and move the character closer to the fall. The absurdity belongs to the situation's particulars, not to a general sense that the world is uncooperative.\n\nLet different family members respond differently to the same crisis. One may urge compromise, one may offer help, one may withdraw, one may find a temporary loophole. None of these responses should make the character foolish; each should be a recognizable way of coping with incompatible legitimate commitments. The tragedy should come from the incompatibility of those commitments rather than from a single villain.\n\nMake warnings and alternatives intelligible. A relative or neighbor may explain a different route, or the protagonist may recall a prior decision that closed one off. The protagonist should be able to hear the warning and still proceed, because the original commitment remains real and the alternative carries its own unacceptable cost.\n\nLet the recognition arrive through a concrete domestic detail that the protagonist finally understands: the form that was never going to be accepted, the phone that was never going to be answered, the rule that was always going to be applied. The recognition should be specific and quiet, not a general statement about poverty or family. End with an aftermath that shows the household continuing under altered conditions, with the absence of what was lost made concrete in the daily routine.\n\nUse only the language, region, community, and period actually supplied by the active world and cast. Do not impose a universal family structure, religious ritual, or code-switching pattern onto the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route from the Japanese literary traditions. Write the story as a series of brief entries by the protagonist, each recording a particular day, occasion, or remembered incident. Keep each entry focused on concrete observations: the weather, a broken appliance, a late arrival, a sentence someone said, the price of a meal, the feeling of waiting. These details should seem small and slightly absurd, but they should accumulate.\n\nLet the diary's selection be governed by the protagonist's sense of what is worth recording at the time. The protagonist records the practical obstructions because they seem manageable or worth noting, not because they are already understood as tragic. The tragic arc develops through the accumulation: an entry records a promise made, another records a warning heard but not acted on, another records a practical obstruction that made action impossible. The reader sees the pattern forming before the diarist does.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between grand intention and petty failure within the entries. The protagonist may write about wanting to help someone, then describe the three phone calls, the missing instructions, and the locked room that prevented it. The humor is in the protagonist's wry phrasing, but the failure has real consequences. The absurdity should be specific to the situation: the phone that is dead because the charger was borrowed, the instructions that are missing because the drawer was reorganized, the room that is locked because the key was returned to the wrong person.\n\nLet the diary reveal interiority naturally. The protagonist can record uncertainty, anger, fear, or self-justification. Avoid a uniform quietness; the protagonist may sometimes be direct, exasperated, or emotionally blunt. Different entries can have different moods, and the mood should follow the actual events of the day rather than a fixed emotional arc.\n\nMake warnings and alternatives intelligible within the entries. A secondary character may offer advice, or the protagonist may record a prior decision that closed off a route. The protagonist should be able to record the warning and still proceed, because the original commitment remains real and the alternative carries its own unacceptable cost.\n\nLet the recognition arrive in an entry that revisits an earlier incident or object—a particular word, a receipt, a photograph, a broken appliance—and sees its meaning changed by what followed. The recognition should be specific: the protagonist understands which recorded detail was the sign of the coming loss, or which practical failure was never going to be repaired. Do not announce the recognition as a general lesson.\n\nEnd with one or two aftermath entries showing the protagonist living with the loss. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored by showing the protagonist continuing to record daily life under altered conditions. Use seasonal or natural details only when they arise from the protagonist's actual attention to a particular day's events; do not insert a seasonal inventory or a fixed meditation on nature. Keep the entries dated or thematically labeled in the displayed text only if the active output format already supports such labels; otherwise integrate them as short sections with clear time markers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism/chronicle/testimony route from the Latin American literary traditions. Write in the voice of a first-person witness or chronicler who is present at some, but not all, key events. Give the chronicler a reason to tell the story: to record an injustice, to understand what happened, to honor someone, or to make a claim. Keep the telling close to concrete daily life: a neighborhood, a workplace, a family, a transport route, a public office. Let economic and social conditions be visible in how people eat, travel, work, and wait.\n\nLet the tragic fall involve a character—often the chronicler's relative, friend, or neighbor—who makes a consequential choice under pressure. The chronicle can include the character's own words, remembered conversations, and observed actions. Make the chronicler's distinction between witnessed and reported events a structural principle: when the chronicler reports something they did not see, mark it as reported, and let later knowledge alter how the chronicler understands an earlier moment. The chronicler assembles the pieces because they need to understand how the loss happened, not merely to record it.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small obstructions by having the chronicler notice absurd procedural details: a missing stamp, a form with two contradictory instructions, a machine that works only when kicked, a queue that reforms in a different order. These details are not decoration; they are the specific delays and missteps that connect the character's choice to the fall. The chronicler should record them because they seemed worth noting at the time, and the reader should see the pattern forming before the chronicler does.\n\nLet different people in the story respond differently to the same conditions: one may resist, one may accommodate, one may exploit a loophole, one may stop noticing the absurdity. None of these responses should make the character foolish; each should be a recognizable way of coping with the same social and economic pressures.\n\nMake warnings and alternatives intelligible. A neighbor's advice, a past incident, or a secondary character's explanation may show a different route. The character should be able to hear the warning and still proceed, because the original commitment remains real and the alternative carries its own unacceptable cost.\n\nLet the recognition arrive when the chronicler assembles the separate pieces and understands how a particular choice led to the loss. The recognition should be specific: the chronicler identifies which procedural detail made the fall inevitable, or which reported event changes the meaning of an earlier witnessed moment. Do not announce the recognition as a general lesson about injustice.\n\nEnd with an aftermath that shows the community, family, or workplace continuing with the absence. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored by showing the daily routine altered by the loss. Use only the language, community, and history actually supplied by the active world and cast. Do not insert magical realism or fantastic elements unless the present world explicitly includes them. Do not invent dialects or a generic Latin American landscape.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaean::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nWrite in fast, readable prose with clear spatial staging. Open by placing the protagonist in the immediate situation: where they are, what they are trying to do, and what practical obstacle is already in the way. Use short paragraphs and line breaks around dialogue, action, and private response. Keep movement easy to follow: when the protagonist goes from a room to a street, name the transition in one or two sentences.\n\nBuild the tragedy around a consequential choice. The protagonist has made a promise, accepted a duty, or acted on a mistaken belief. Show that choice being tested by concrete practical failures. Make each failure a specific test of the commitment: the missing key is the one that would let the protagonist keep the promise, the dead phone is the one that would let the protagonist reach the person they need, the stopped train is the one that would let the protagonist arrive in time, the locked door is the one that would let the protagonist act. Each failure should increase the cost of the commitment, not merely delay it.\n\nUse a reaction beat after each failure. The reaction beat should show the protagonist registering the failure and reasoning about what it means for the commitment. The reaction can be frustration, fear, resolve, or a dawning sense of the cost. Do not turn every line into a joke. The absurd details should increase the sense that the world is not cooperating, but the loss must remain real.\n\nLet different characters respond differently to the same failures: one may laugh at the absurdity, one may panic, one may quietly find another way, one may refuse to believe the obstacle is real. None of these responses should make the protagonist foolish; each should be a recognizable way of coping with the same practical pressure.\n\nMake warnings intelligible. A secondary character may explain the risk, or the protagonist may recall a prior decision that closed off a route. The protagonist should be able to hear the warning and still proceed, because the original commitment remains real and the alternative carries its own unacceptable cost.\n\nLet the recognition arrive through a concrete detail that the protagonist finally understands: the key that was never going to be found, the phone that was never going to be answered, the train that was never going to arrive in time. The recognition should be specific and quiet, not a general statement about bad luck. End with an aftermath detail or a pause tied to the protagonist's actual situation, without forcing a cliffhanger. The style does not require first-person or a young cast; use the point of view and age actually supplied by the active narrator contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild each stage of the fall as a collision between a large claim and a small practical fact that cannot be absorbed into it. State the claim with confidence and even elegance: a plan, a promise, a belief about how the world works, or a commitment phrased as though its completion were already part of the landscape. Then bring the countervailing detail through the senses rather than through a summary of failure. The missing key, the unreturned message, the door that will not open, the body's own fatigue. Give the detail enough rhythmic weight that the reader feels the claim losing contact with the ground before the character does.\n\nUse lyrical syntax to delay and then release the recognition. A sentence may gather subordinate clauses around a single action, so that the reader experiences the character's effort to hold the plan together before the main clause admits what has happened. Recurring images should change their meaning as the fall proceeds. The same light, the same repeated phrase, the same object may first appear as evidence of possibility and later return as evidence of what has been lost. Let a comparison unfold only when it changes what the narrator or focal character understands; do not decorate the loss with unrelated figurative language.\n\nKeep the absurdity precise and consequential. The apparently reasonable premise may expose its absurd consequences through a procedure, an explanation, or an ordinary practical inconvenience, but those consequences must now be irreversible. A character may remain intelligent, articulate, and worthy of attention while making the choice that closes off an alternative. Do not turn every scene into a joke or make the cast uniformly witty. When humor appears, it should be local and particular, often arising from the gap between the elevated claim and the small obstruction, and it should not erase the cost. Recognition may arrive too late to change the outcome, and the aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored without announcing that everything is ruined. End where the present action or thought reaches its natural boundary, not where the tragedy has been fully explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narrator or focal character remember the moment when a large claim began to separate from the small practical world that would outlast it. The memory may be of a plan confidently explained, a promise made with complete sincerity, or an arrangement that seemed reasonable until its consequences became visible. Do not begin by announcing that the loss was inevitable. Instead, let the present scene supply one familiar detail that carries the association: the same chair, the same route, the same phrase now repeated under different circumstances. The detail should be ordinary and specific, and its return should make the distance between then and now legible.\n\nAllow reflection to move between the remembered occasion and the present understanding without forcing the character to complete a moral summary. The absurdity of the earlier confidence may become apparent through a small logistical fact: the thing that was never returned, the appointment that could not be kept, the instruction that made sense only if one ignored the actual conditions. Treat that fact with the seriousness of loss rather than as a punchline. The character may still find the memory faintly ridiculous and still feel the loss; these responses can coexist. Let the melancholy arise from the recognition that the ordinary detail has become a marker of what is no longer available.\n\nKeep the reflection particular to the character and the scene. Do not make every remembered pleasure turn sad or require every paragraph to end by announcing impermanence. Some ordinary pleasure may remain pleasurable even as it reminds the character of an earlier time. The tragic arc should be preserved through the irreversible change that the memory reveals, not through a uniform tone of regret. Recognition may arrive gradually, and the aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored without insisting that the character has fully understood it. End when the present thought or action reaches a natural pause, not when the reflection has been exhausted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed or orally situated telling. A narrator speaks to a present audience, or a story is introduced as one that has been told before, and the tragic material is delivered through that frame. Keep the frame active: the teller may interrupt, the audience may respond, and the outer scene may return at a moment that changes how the inner story is understood. Do not let the frame become a decorative introduction that disappears after the first paragraph.\n\nInside the framed account, build the tragedy from the contrast between a large claim and a small logistical problem. The claim may be a plan, a promise, a confident explanation, or a reasonable-sounding procedure. Let the teller present it with the authority of someone who once believed it, then let the small practical fact enter as a detail of the world: the missing item, the unkept appointment, the instruction that could not be followed, the ordinary obligation that exposed the larger error. The teller may linger on that detail so the audience can feel the claim losing its shape. Digressions are permitted when they return to the current problem and deepen the loss; do not insert ornamental tales that only delay the recognition.\n\nPreserve the tragic arc within the telling. The audience may hear warnings or alternatives that the participants could not use, and the participants may remain intelligent and worthy of attention even as their choices become destructive. Recognition should change what the loss means, and the frame may supply the aftermath: the teller's present circumstances, the audience's knowledge of what followed, or the changed meaning of a repeated phrase. Keep the tradition coherent. Do not import a universal honor code, mysticism, or ceremonial speech into every speaker. The humor that arises from the absurd gap between claim and practical reality should be particular and should not erase the cost. End the response where the telling or the frame reaches a natural boundary, not where the tragedy has been fully explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaear::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the large claim and the small fact that defeats it, and render both with exact detail. The claim may appear in one direct sentence of speech or narration: a plan, a promise, an explanation, or a procedure stated with confidence. The small fact should be concrete and particular, not a vague summary of failure. The key that is no longer on the hook. The message that did not arrive. The door that will not open. The instruction that cannot be followed. Let the fact enter without commentary, so the reader sees the gap before the character does.\n\nKeep the inward connection to the minimum that makes the loss legible. A focal character may have one essential thought, long enough to make sense, that shows what the fact means to them. Do not explain the whole history of the plan or repeat the moral. Let the reader infer the irreversible consequence from the selected details and the character's single inward turn. Omission should preserve causality: the reader must be able to see why this fact closes off an alternative, not merely that something bad has happened.\n\nDo not force brevity into a quota of fragments. A precise extended sentence may serve the style better than several performed short ones. Let different characters speak in their own voices; do not make everyone sound like the same restrained narrator. The absurdity of the gap between claim and fact may be present, but it should be dry and local, not a joke inserted after the loss. Recognition may be implied rather than announced, and the aftermath may be carried by one remaining detail. End when the present action or thought reaches its natural boundary, not when the tragedy has been summarized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fall through a sustained scene in which a confident claim meets an ordinary practical obstacle. The claim may be spoken, planned, or assumed, and it should be stated clearly enough that the reader understands what the character is trying to preserve. Then let the obstacle enter through the scene's actual movement: a procedure that cannot be completed, an arrangement that depends on a missing item, a message that does not arrive, or a practical condition that the plan ignored. Do not summarize the failure. Show the character encountering the obstacle through action, dialogue, perception, or recollection, and let the scene continue long enough for the consequence to become legible.\n\nMove naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. The focal character's private thought may show them trying to hold the plan together, noticing the detail that contradicts it, or understanding too late what the obstacle means. Keep that interiority within the active perspective and access rules. Other characters should respond differently to the absurdity: one may treat the obstacle as a joke, another as a disaster, another as a practical problem to solve. Do not make everyone share the narrator's comic detachment. The humor that arises from the gap between the large claim and the small obstruction should be particular to a character or moment and should not erase the cost.\n\nPreserve the tragic arc without imposing a lesson at the end of every reply. Warnings and alternatives may be intelligible in the scene, and the character may remain intelligent and worthy of attention even while choosing destructively. Recognition should change what the loss means, and the aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored. The narration may end with business concluded or interrupted, a thought unfinished, or the character still in the middle of the changed situation. Do not force a completed realization or a summary of the tragedy. End where the present scene reaches its natural boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nEstablish the relationship through its particular private language and repeated moments. Use a phrase, a habit, a shared reference, or a small ritual that belongs to these two people, and let it appear early enough that the reader understands what the bond promises. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes. Work from the actual supplied premise: the specific affection, rivalry, comfort, jealousy, or discovery that defines this relationship. Give the bond enough space to feel real before it comes under pressure.\n\nLet the tragic fall occur when a confident claim about the relationship meets a small practical fact that the bond cannot absorb. The claim may be spoken or assumed: a promise, a plan, an explanation of how things will be, or a belief about what the other person will do. The small fact should be concrete and particular to their shared world: the missed call, the unkept arrangement, the object that was not returned, the ordinary obligation that exposes the larger error. Let the fact enter through scene action or dialogue, and let the reader see the gap between the claim and the reality before the characters fully do. The absurdity of that gap may be present, but it should be particular to these characters and should not turn the relationship into a joke.\n\nPreserve the tragic arc through the relationship's consequences. Recognition should change what the loss means, and the aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored. A repeated phrase or private ritual may return with a new meaning after the fall, so the callback fulfills the relationship's promise rather than merely decorating the ending. Allow affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where they belong, even when the outcome is painful. Keep the characters' responses distinct; they need not share the same understanding of what has happened. End when the relationship scene reaches its natural boundary, not when the loss has been fully explained or resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the tragedy as an account of a deed whose consequences are still remembered. Open with a deliberate statement that establishes the person, the claim, or the undertaking, then expand into the particulars that make the account incomplete. The claim may be a plan, a promise, an oath, or a confident explanation of how a matter would be settled. Let the chronicle present it with the weight of something that once seemed worthy of retelling. Then let the small practical fact enter as the deed's actual turning point: the missing item, the unkept appointment, the instruction that could not be followed, the ordinary obligation that exposed the larger error. The chronicle should treat that fact as the cause of the remembered consequence, not as a minor inconvenience.\n\nUse compact declarations to establish a person or remembered act, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application. Let the relation of the act to what will be remembered carry the tragic weight: the loss is not merely that the plan failed, but that the failure became part of how these people are known. Keep shared reputation distinct from a focal character's private account. Being remembered for a failed undertaking does not mean the person was foolish or unworthy; the chronicle may preserve their intelligence and the intelligible alternatives they could not take.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the authority of the chronicle. A participant may object to one detail, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or remember the event differently. This need not become parody. Humor may arise from the persistence of a local story, but the practical failure is not mock combat and the loss is not heroic doom. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account can end with the consequence established or still unfolding, leaving the act available to be remembered rather than declaring its full meaning on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite through a definite Scandinavian social-realist route: a specific Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish care-authority or municipal case, such as a person trying to secure housing, home care, or a child's placement. Keep the national context concrete through its actual administrative language, waiting periods, eligibility rules, and domestic arrangements rather than through snow, silence, or generalized reserve.\n\nUse Adams's method by letting the institution's reasonable logic expose its absurd consequences. Show the forms, reassignments, polite official explanations, and small logistical requirements as they are actually experienced: each step can be sensible, even kind, while the accumulated result becomes unlivable for a particular person. Do not make the system a cartoon villain. Let different characters respond differently—one may treat the absurdity as normal, another as comic, another as a private wound—and let those differences create friction without turning everyone into the same witty narrator.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through irreversible choices made inside this machinery. A character may sign, decline, appeal, transfer, confess, or stay silent for reasons the reader understands, including love, duty, pride, or a mistaken belief about how the process will end. Warnings and alternatives should be visible to the reader, even when the character cannot take them. Let recognition arrive after the decisive act and change what the loss means: the character may see that the form was never neutral, that the official's kindness was real and insufficient, or that their own reasonable choice closed the only route they could have borne. Preserve aftermath without restoring what was lost.\n\nKeep required inward experience in the active presentation as ordinary private prose, distinct from what officials, family members, or colleagues know. A character's understanding of the system may exceed their power to change it, and their silence may be a supported choice rather than a failure of perception. Use digressions only when they return through the current problem—an irrelevant regulation, a remembered case, or a colleague's anecdote can later become the precise detail that makes the loss legible. The story may end on a completed procedure, a pending decision, or the quiet recognition of what has already become irreversible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite in concise screen-readable blocks: scene headings when location or time changes, action lines for visible and audible events, and dialogue with clear speaker cues. Let the tragic arc live in what the camera can actually show—a signed form left on a desk, a chair pulled back from a table, a phone call answered or ignored, a door held open too long—rather than in explanatory speeches.\n\nUse Adams's contrast between large claims and small logistical problems as the engine of scene selection. A character may announce a decisive principle, a life-changing commitment, or a complete explanation of their situation, then be interrupted by a delivery, a wrong number, a stuck drawer, a queue, or a form that requires a middle name. Do not let the interruption erase the claim; let the small problem delay, reshape, or expose the cost of the larger one. Different characters respond to the same inconvenience differently, and their responses reveal their incompatible commitments without making everyone a comic narrator.\n\nBuild irreversible choices through visible action and spoken refusal. A character may sign, leave, hand over keys, accept a transfer, or say the sentence that cannot be unsaid. Show the alternative as a real possibility in the scene—another person asks, a phone rings, a door remains open—so the choice is intelligible rather than fated. Recognition should arrive after the decisive act and change its meaning: a character may look at the signed form, hear the offscreen sound of a departure, or repeat an earlier phrase with new understanding. Do not require a confession or a completed explanation.\n\nKeep required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear and not as automatic voice-over. Use cuts, offscreen sound, and voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs them. The scene may end on a completed action, an unanswered question, or the quiet aftermath of what has already been lost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene: a character may explain a reasonable procedure while folding a letter, moving a chair, checking a watch, or holding a coat they will not put down. The object and the movement should do dramatic work rather than decorate the speech.\n\nUse Adams's method by letting explanations expose their absurd consequences in spoken form. A character can describe a policy, a schedule, a technical requirement, or a chain of sensible decisions until the audience sees the trap inside it. Do not make every character speak the same way. One may deliver the absurdity with bureaucratic calm, another with rising frustration, another with a joke that no one else finds funny. Let the digression return through the current problem: the apparently irrelevant anecdote or procedural aside should later become the detail that makes the loss legible.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through staged, irreversible action. A character may sign, hand over a key, leave through a door, sit down, stand up, or refuse an offered object. Show the alternative as present in the scene—another character extends a hand, holds a door, asks a question—so the choice is intelligible. Recognition should arrive after the decisive act and change its meaning, often through a pause, a repeated line, or a character's changed relation to an object already on stage. Preserve aftermath without restoring what was lost.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in the active presentation, clearly attributed and distinct from dialogue. Monologues and asides need a dramatic purpose rather than replacing every private thought. Do not add film-camera instructions. The scene may end on a completed action, a held pause, or the quiet acknowledgment of what has already become irreversible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through one authorized mind, braiding perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's associations, while keeping enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared. Do not make every character share this mind or grant access to excluded minds.\n\nUse Adams's method by letting the mind return to explanations, procedures, and apparently reasonable premises until their absurd consequences surface. A remembered instruction, a phrase from a letter, a rule about how something must be done, or a colleague's careful explanation can recur with slight variation, each return exposing a new cost. The digression should earn its place by returning through the current problem: a stray memory of a queue, a manual, or a misheard instruction may later become the exact detail that makes the loss legible. Different people in the mind's recollections should respond differently to the absurdity; they are not all versions of the same comic narrator.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through the mind's irreversible movement. The character may recall the moment of signing, hear again the sentence they spoke, feel the bodily residue of a choice, or notice that a familiar object has changed meaning. Show alternatives as intelligible memories or present perceptions—the unsent letter, the door that was open, the other person's actual words—so the fall is not fated but chosen. Recognition should arrive through association rather than summary: a perception triggers a memory, a memory revises an earlier understanding, and the character sees what the loss now means. Preserve aftermath in the mind's continued return to what cannot be restored.\n\nKeep the telling inside the authorized mind's actual experience. Ordinary reasoning alone is not this stylistic mode, but the associations must remain particular to this character rather than dissolving all scene facts into indeterminacy. The passage may end on a completed recognition, an interruption, or the quiet persistence of a thought that has not yet found its outward form.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story through readable sender-attributed messages, using only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in complete sentences, another in fragments, one edits repeatedly, another leaves messages unread for hours. These habits are character evidence, not comic decoration.\n\nUse Adams's method by letting reasonable procedural explanations unfold across the exchange. A sender may explain a policy, a schedule, a technical requirement, or a chain of sensible decisions in messages that become increasingly elaborate, while the recipient's practical questions expose the absurd consequences. Do not make every sender the same witty narrator. One may treat the absurdity as normal, another as funny, another as a private wound, and their different responses should create friction without resolving into a single tone.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through the difference between what is transmitted and what is not. A sent message may commit, refuse, confirm, or end something; an unsent draft may show what the sender almost said; a read receipt may mark the moment a message landed too late. Show alternatives as present in the exchange—another sender asks, a draft remains unsent, a message sits unread—so the choice is intelligible rather than fated. Recognition should arrive through the interface itself: a sender rereads an old message, notices a timestamp, sees an edit history, or receives a reply that changes what the earlier exchange meant. Preserve aftermath without restoring what was lost.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript, and required private experience may be rendered there as nonspoken authoring material. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. The exchange may end on a sent message, a pending reply, or the quiet fact of a message that was never opened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWrite in concise scene-setting with readable dialogue attribution and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Let a background or expression cue orient the moment—a character's hand resting on a form, a window behind them, a coat still hanging by the door—without making it an automatic truth indicator or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nUse Adams's method by letting explanations and procedures expose their absurd consequences through spoken scenes. A character may describe a policy, a schedule, or a chain of sensible decisions while the focal narration notices the small logistical detail that undermines the claim: the form has no box for the actual situation, the queue is for the wrong office, the helpful instruction assumes a resource that does not exist. Do not make every character the same comic narrator. Let different people respond differently to the absurdity, and let the focal character's inner response range from amusement to exhaustion to grief.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through irreversible choices made in scene. A character may sign, hand something over, leave, or say the sentence that cannot be unsaid. Show the alternative as present—another character asks, an object remains within reach, a door stays open—so the choice is intelligible. Recognition should arrive after the decisive act and change its meaning: the focal character may look again at a familiar background detail, hear an earlier phrase with new understanding, or notice that an expression they once read as reassurance meant something else. Preserve aftermath without restoring what was lost.\n\nKeep required private experience as focal narration or inner response, clearly distinct from what other characters know. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. Keep scene changes legible. The scene may end on a completed action, a held expression, or the quiet acknowledgment of what has already become irreversible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nWrite in installment-oriented prose with a readable through-line across replies: keep clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover context briefly only when it helps—a character's earlier commitment, a form still unsigned, a message still unanswered—rather than retelling the whole history that established it.\n\nUse Adams's method by letting an explanation, procedure, or apparently reasonable premise expose its absurd consequences across installments. A sensible rule introduced early may return later with a new cost; a digression that seemed irrelevant may become the exact detail that makes a loss legible. Do not make every character the same comic narrator. Let different people respond differently to the absurdity, and let those differences create friction that persists across installments without resolving into a single tone.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through cumulative, irreversible developments. A character may sign, leave, refuse, or commit in one installment, and later installments show the consequences without undoing the choice. Show alternatives as present before the decisive act—another character asks, a door remains open, a draft remains unsent—so the fall is intelligible. Recognition should arrive after the decisive act and change its meaning, often when an earlier detail returns: a familiar phrase, a repaired object, a repeated question now carries a different weight. Preserve aftermath across installments without restoring what was lost.\n\nKeep required private experience in the active presentation as ordinary private prose, distinct from what other characters know. Use earned developments and occasional hooks without making every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources. An installment may end on completed work, a pending request, or the quiet acknowledgment of what has already become irreversible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite tragedy through concrete scenes where emotional turbulence shows in what people do, handle, repeat, and refuse to say. Use Hemingway's short declarative sentences, precise objects, and pauses to carry the pressure of an unresolved wound. Choose the gesture from the character's actual situation: a person may open a window, fold a letter, pour a drink, or turn away, but the action must belong to what they are trying to manage in that moment. Let the physical action stand for the feeling without turning every object into a fixed symbol.\n\nIn dialogue, keep exchanges direct but elliptical. People talk around the hurt: about a meal, a broken lock, a debt, a place they once went. Repetition becomes a sign of obsession or fear. Have a character repeat a phrase, an errand, or a repair when they cannot say what they need. The cost of admitting, needing, or returning should appear in the conversation itself: a question cut off, an apology that changes subject, a kindness offered too late or accepted with shame.\n\nFollow the tragic arc through irreversible choices and their aftermath. Do not soften the loss with comforting asides. Let recognition arrive in a simple object or a practical consequence: a coat left behind, a key that no longer fits, a job taken far away. End a response where the strain is still active, not where a confession instantly heals. Preserve what cannot be restored. The narrator may state only the facts of what was done and seen, or may name a feeling plainly when the character would permit it; let the unsaid carry the turbulence where naming would be false.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite a clear collaborative scene in short, scannable paragraphs. Give each speaker unmistakable attribution and use Hemingway's direct language: short sentences, concrete nouns, no ornament. Keep narration close to what a character sees and does. Let a tragic event or a decision with irreversible weight sit under the talk without forcing an emotional outburst.\n\nIn dialogue, have people say plain things while the unsaid matters. Repetition can mark refusal to face a fact: one character asks again about a boat or a train, another says the same word to stop the conversation. Show the cost through practical details: an empty chair, a plate not touched, a door that stays closed. Do not make every line witty or combative; let strain show in what a character avoids.\n\nLet the tragic arc move through an action, a recognition, or a consequence that changes the situation, or through the pressure of a consequence already in motion. The response may end while a difficult fact remains open, a task is unfinished, or a character is about to say something hard. Leave the continuation natural: the other player can respond to what was just said or done. Preserve active NPCs and their choices. The scene should feel like part of a larger collaborative story, not a finished soliloquy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the vernacular episodic route. Tell the tragedy as a sequence of connected incidents, each a brief scene with a clear action, consequence, or turn. Keep Hemingway's concrete language: short declarations, practical objects, no abstract moralizing. Let each episode carry the irreversible course forward, reveal the cost of an earlier choice, or mark a refusal that leaves the next consequence in motion, so that the reader feels the accumulation of choices.\n\nUse recurring concrete details to mark recurrence and consequence: a well, a gate, a lamp, a payment. When a detail returns, its meaning has changed because of what happened since. Social conditions and family duty can be shown through small practical exchanges, not explained in a narrator's lecture. A character may decline an offered work, return a gift, or refuse to listen, and the loss becomes visible in what follows.\n\nAt the points of recognition, keep the prose plain. A character understands something through an object, a mirror, a remembered face, or an unfinished task. Do not turn the recognition into a long confessional passage. End an episode where a new consequence is set in motion or where a cost has just become legible. Preserve the distinctions of the actual language, period, and community supplied; do not import a generic landscape or cast. The route is vernacular episodic tragedy, not martial adventure or experimental construction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite close to the physical world. Use short declarative sentences, concrete nouns, and hard verbs. Let material wear carry the tragedy: cracked leather, a rusted hinge, a shoe that gives way, a door that will not hold. When a character makes an irreversible choice, show the immediate practical result, not a sudden burst of feeling.\n\nKeep transitions compressed. Cut from the failed repair to the letter unread, from the door closing to the road at night. Use high contrast: a clean glass beside a dirty floor, a warm room outside a cold yard. Let the sharp change make the loss present. Repetition can mark a pattern: the same tool picked up, the same errand refused, the same sentence repeated until it becomes a fact.\n\nUse a longer thought when the character's reasoning needs it. Before a harmful decision, a person may calculate cost, remember a warning, or weigh two remaining options. Show that intelligence, and let the warning be intelligible. Afterward, withhold explanation. A recognition may be brief: a character sees the cut hand, the empty shelf, the path back closed. End where the consequence is known, not where every feeling is spoken. Keep the prose hard but not inhuman; allow one clear object to carry the sorrow.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the tragedy through letters, notes, lists, or reports. Give each document a clear writer, intended reader, and time. Use Hemingway's plain style inside the document: short sentences, concrete objects, no ornate self-analysis. A character writes only what they can bring themselves to write, so the gaps and the unsaid are meaningful omissions.\n\nLet the sequence of documents follow a consecutive fall. A promise in one letter is broken in the next. A practical request is answered with a refusal. A bill, a list of possessions, or a brief note can show what changed without a narrator explaining. Repetition works across documents: the same place named, the same debt ignored, the same sentence copied from an earlier letter when words fail.\n\nKeep each document's knowledge limited. The writer does not know what still lies ahead. Later documents should not speak for earlier ones. Let recognition enter when a later writer refers back to an object, a date, or a line that the reader now understands differently. Aftermath can appear as an unsent draft or a list of things left behind. Do not add an omniscient paragraph after every record. Let the documents themselves carry the loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet care appear in exact, practical gestures: a meal prepared, a coat hung near the door, a cup set down, a lamp lit before someone returns. Use Hemingway's concrete language to avoid sentimentality. Name only the actions and the objects; the warmth lives in the attention, not in a speech about love. Let these gestures belong to characters who can still fail, choose wrongly, or lose what they have.\n\nKeep the tragic arc active. A moment of ease does not cancel the consequence that is already moving. A person may accept comfort and still go through with the decision that will cost them. Show the strain in how the comfort is received: a hand withdrawn after a moment, a thanks that is too quiet, a pleasure enjoyed while the clock runs. The narrative should hold both the warmth and the loss without forcing an emotional repair by the end of the exchange.\n\nIn dialogue, keep speech plain and affectionate in its practicality: reminders about food, weather, sleep, or an errand. A repeated familiar phrase can become a sign of attachment. Let small pleasures matter: a clean floor, a good fire, a shared cigarette. Do not make everyone agreeable, and do not erase established problems. The comfort is real, and the tragedy remains real. End a passage where that coexistence is visible, not where the loss is magically solved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse composed novel prose with deliberate transitions. Build each scene in a longer paragraph that moves from a concrete external fact to its social meaning and back to action. Keep Hemingway's syntax: simple declarative sentences, precise nouns, hard verbs. Do not inflate the vocabulary for elegance. The formality lies in the deliberate movement and framing, not in ornate language.\n\nFrame the tragic sequence through social context and public/private contrast. A decision may be shown first as a family matter, then as something discussed in a public room, then as a private consequence. Use the narrator's distance to register what society expects, what the character can say, and what remains unsaid. Repetition can mark the recurrence of a burden: the same obligation named, the same room entered, the same letter considered.\n\nAt the point of recognition, allow one sustained, plain reflection. Let the character consider an object, a place, or a remembered conversation, and let the meaning alter without an explanatory speech. Afterward, preserve the aftermath: a journey begun, an arrangement kept, a silence that must be lived with. End a passage where a new phase of the loss is established, not where the tragedy is fully closed. The style should hold social judgment and personal defeat together without making every character a type.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nGive the telling a specific occasion and a listener with a stake in what is being told. The teller is not a neutral narrator: the story is being offered to persuade, delay, justify, warn, or keep someone present. Let the outer scene remain concrete and economical. Show where the telling happens, what the teller does with his hands, and what the listener does while the story runs. Do not let the frame become a decorative preface; it is the present consequence of the past.\n\nBuild the embedded tale from precise objects, practical exchanges, and short declarative sentences. Name the things that mattered: the boat, the road, the letter, the unpaid debt, the door that was not locked. Let the teller omit what he cannot bear or what he has never understood. The omission is not a puzzle to be solved. It is a refusal that the listener can feel as a gap in the account. When the teller repeats a phrase or returns to an object, let the repetition carry a changed emphasis: what sounded like pride the first time sounds like blame the second.\n\nMake the embedded story a tragedy by keeping the alternative visible. The teller can report the warning he received, the chance he did not take, or the promise he broke. He can describe the moment when a different choice was still possible without pretending he would have made it. The listener may interrupt to ask about that moment, and the teller may answer with a fact, a silence, or a repeated line. Recognition does not need to be announced. It can arrive in the listener's face, in the teller's pause, or in the way the outer scene resumes after the tale ends.\n\nLet the frame audience change what the loss means. The listener may already know part of the story, may be implicated in it, or may be hearing it for the first time. His response is part of the tragedy, not a commentary on it. He can ask a question that makes the teller see the event differently, or he can remain silent and let the teller continue. The telling itself can be the final irreversible act: once the story is spoken, the listener cannot return to what he believed before.\n\nKeep the narrative levels clear. When the tale begins, let the reader know who is speaking and to whom. When the tale ends, return to the outer scene with a concrete action: the teller finishes his drink, the listener stands, the lamp is turned down. Do not force a cliffhanger between every nested account. The tragedy can end in the outer scene, after the telling, in the aftermath that preserves what cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social or developmental realism route. Place the tragedy inside a specific social world: a trade, a household, a workplace, a neighborhood, a family obligation, or an economic arrangement that shapes what the characters can choose. Let the narration observe how people live and work in that world. Show the practical conditions: the rent, the hours, the tools, the meals, the train, the inheritance, the contract. These conditions are not background. They are the material through which the fall becomes possible.\n\nKeep the prose direct and concrete in the Hemingway manner. Name the objects and actions precisely. Write short declarative sentences. Let a character's feeling appear through what he does with his hands, what he refuses to say, or what he repeats. Do not translate the social world into abstract commentary. Show the foreman, the neighbor, the creditor, the parent, or the official through specific exchanges. Let the reader understand the social position from the way a door is opened, a bill is paid, or a request is refused.\n\nLet the tragedy develop through incompatible commitments. A character may owe something to family and something to work, to a promise and to a livelihood, to love and to duty. Make the alternatives intelligible. Show the moment when a different choice was still possible, and show why the character did not take it: pride, fear, loyalty, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief about what the other person would accept. Do not make the character foolish. Let him act from reasons the reader can understand even when the result is destructive.\n\nUse the developmental realism route to let consequences accumulate. A small decision about money or work can return later in a changed form. A relationship can deteriorate through repeated practical failures rather than a single dramatic betrayal. Let the narration track these changes through concrete details: the same room looks different after a loss, the same phrase means something else when it is said again. Recognition can arrive slowly, in the aftermath, when the character sees what the earlier choices have made of his life.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance steady. The narration can observe the social world closely without entering every mind. Let the reader see the character's actions and hear his speech, and let the unsaid carry the weight. When interiority is authorized, keep it concrete and particular: a memory of a specific day, a calculation about money, a refusal to think about what happened. Do not impose a bureaucratic or severe tone. Let the syntax follow the scene: plain for work, more patient for memory, spare for loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the tragedy where old authority still presses on present life: an inherited house, a family obligation, a long-held position, a debt passed down, a name that still opens doors or closes them. Let the modern world be concrete and worn: the building with bad wiring, the street after rain, the office with old furniture and new locks, the club where the old rules still hold. Do not import vampires, clans, or supernatural powers. The horror is personal and social: what the past still owns, and what people do because they cannot escape it.\n\nUse Hemingway's direct language to keep the atmosphere material. Name the things that carry the past: the ring, the ledger, the photograph, the key that no longer fits, the chair no one sits in. Show the present through practical exchanges: a payment made, a door refused, a message delivered, a promise repeated. Let the unsaid carry the fear and the desire. A character can say \"It is nothing\" while his hands show otherwise. A room can feel dangerous because of what happened there, not because of any invented power.\n\nMake the tragedy arise from morally complicated intimacy. The characters may love someone they also fear, protect someone they also resent, or desire something that costs them their place. Let the social masks fall only in trusted company, and let that exposure matter. A private conversation can change what a character is willing to do. A moment of honesty can make a later betrayal more destructive. Do not make every caring act fraudulent. Let genuine affection and genuine harm exist in the same relationship.\n\nLet the fall be irreversible and intelligible. Show the alternative: the character could leave, refuse, confess, or break the old arrangement. Show why he does not. The inherited power may offer safety, identity, or love, and the cost may be visible only later. Recognition can come when the character sees what he has become inside the old structure, or when someone he trusted shows him the consequence of his choice. The aftermath preserves the loss: the house remains, the debt remains, the name remains, but the person is changed.\n\nLet atmosphere change how a particular encounter is experienced without replacing the tragedy with mood. A familiar room can feel different after a confession. A routine meeting can become threatening because of what one person now knows. Keep the narration close to the concrete scene: the light, the sound, the object in someone's hand, the pause before an answer. Do not explain the feeling. Let the reader feel it through the precise, economical detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Place the tragedy inside a specific household, neighborhood, community, or workplace, and keep the language, region, community, and period particular when the story supplies them. Show the material conditions that shape the choices: the house, the kitchen, the shop, the field, the office, the wedding, the journey, the money that must be sent or saved. Do not impose a generic family structure, religion, caste experience, or code-switching. Let the scene's own details carry the social world.\n\nWrite the domestic world in Hemingway's concrete manner. Name the objects and actions precisely: the rice, the ledger, the sari, the bicycle, the letter, the train, the key, the unpaid bill. Use short declarative sentences. Let feeling appear through what a character does with his hands, what he refuses to say, or what he repeats. A mother can show her worry by the way she sets a plate down. A son can show his shame by the way he folds a paper. Do not translate the social world into commentary. Show it through specific exchanges.\n\nMake the tragedy arise from duty, love, pride, or a mistaken belief about what the family or community requires. A character may owe something to a parent and something to a spouse, to a promise and to a livelihood, to an older obligation and to a present desire. Let the alternatives be intelligible. Show the moment when a different choice was still possible, and show why the character did not take it: fear of disgrace, loyalty, exhaustion, hope, or a belief that the other person would understand. Do not make the character foolish. Let him act from reasons the reader can recognize.\n\nLet the domestic realism route accumulate consequences through repeated practical events. A small decision about money, marriage, work, or a journey can return later in a changed form. A relationship can deteriorate through many small failures rather than a single dramatic betrayal. Let the narration track these changes through concrete details: the same room looks different after a loss, the same phrase means something else when it is said again. Recognition can arrive in the aftermath, when the character sees what the earlier choices have made of the household.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance steady and the social world specific. The narration can observe the family and community closely without entering every mind. Let the reader see the actions and hear the speech, and let the unsaid carry the weight. When interiority is authorized, keep it concrete and particular: a memory of a specific day, a calculation about money, a refusal to think about what happened. Do not force English or code-switching. If the story supplies a language, let it shape the names, terms, and rhythms of the scene without turning the prose into a display of translated word order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realism route. Place the tragedy inside a specific social world: a family, a workplace, a marriage, a neighborhood, a school, or an obligation that shapes what the characters can choose. Let the narration observe how people live and work in that world. Show the practical conditions: the rent, the hours, the meals, the train, the letter, the debt, the ceremony, the promise. These conditions are not background. They are the material through which the fall becomes possible.\n\nKeep the prose direct and concrete in the Hemingway manner. Name the objects and actions precisely. Write short declarative sentences. Let a character's feeling appear through what he does with his hands, what he refuses to say, or what he repeats. Do not translate the social world into abstract commentary. Show the parent, the colleague, the spouse, the neighbor, or the creditor through specific exchanges. Let the reader understand the social position from the way a cup is set down, a door is closed, or a request is refused.\n\nLet the tragedy develop through incompatible commitments. A character may owe something to family and something to work, to a promise and to a livelihood, to love and to duty, to an older obligation and to a present desire. Make the alternatives intelligible. Show the moment when a different choice was still possible, and show why the character did not take it: pride, fear, loyalty, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief about what the other person would accept. Do not make the character foolish. Let him act from reasons the reader can understand even when the result is destructive.\n\nUse the psychological realism route to let consequences accumulate through unspoken feeling. A small decision about work or family can return later in a changed form. A relationship can deteriorate through repeated practical failures and things left unsaid rather than a single dramatic betrayal. Let the narration track these changes through concrete details: the same room looks different after a loss, the same phrase means something else when it is said again. Recognition can arrive slowly, in the aftermath, when the character sees what the earlier choices have made of his life.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance steady and the social world specific. The narration can observe the social world closely without entering every mind. Let the reader see the character's actions and hear his speech, and let the unsaid carry the weight. When interiority is authorized, keep it concrete and particular: a memory of a specific day, a calculation about money, a refusal to think about what happened. Do not impose universal quietness or a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery. Let the syntax follow the scene: plain for work, more patient for memory, spare for loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route. Place the tragedy inside a specific community, region, and historical moment when the story supplies them. Show the material conditions that shape the choices: the land, the harvest, the mine, the factory, the road, the market, the debt, the journey, the house that must be kept or left. Do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them. Let the scene's own details carry the social and historical weight.\n\nWrite the social world in Hemingway's concrete manner. Name the objects and actions precisely: the machete, the ledger, the truck, the letter, the coffee, the river, the unpaid wage, the key. Use short declarative sentences. Let feeling appear through what a character does with his hands, what he refuses to say, or what he repeats. A father can show his worry by the way he sharpens a blade. A daughter can show her grief by the way she folds a shirt. Do not translate the social world into commentary. Show it through specific exchanges.\n\nMake the tragedy arise from duty, love, pride, or a mistaken belief about what the community or the moment requires. A character may owe something to family and something to work, to a promise and to a livelihood, to an older obligation and to a present desire. Let the alternatives be intelligible. Show the moment when a different choice was still possible, and show why the character did not take it: fear, loyalty, exhaustion, hope, or a belief that the other person would understand. Do not make the character foolish. Let him act from reasons the reader can recognize.\n\nLet the chronicle and testimony route accumulate consequences through repeated practical events. A small decision about land, money, work, or a journey can return later in a changed form. A relationship can deteriorate through many small failures rather than a single dramatic betrayal. Let the narration track these changes through concrete details: the same road looks different after a loss, the same phrase means something else when it is said again. Recognition can arrive in the aftermath, when the character sees what the earlier choices have made of the community or the household.\n\nKeep the narrator's distance steady and the social world specific. The narration can observe the community and history closely without entering every mind. Let the reader see the actions and hear the speech, and let the unsaid carry the weight. When interiority is authorized, keep it concrete and particular: a memory of a specific day, a calculation about money, a refusal to think about what happened. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history as the story supplies them, without manufacturing national speech patterns or a generic regional temperament.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Open with a concrete action or exchange that places the reader in the scene: a door opened, a message read, a debt called in, a promise made. Keep spatial changes easy to follow. When the scene moves from one place to another, show the transition through a simple physical action or a clear paragraph break. Do not let the fast pace erase the material world. Name the objects and actions precisely in Hemingway's manner: the key, the phone, the letter, the road, the unpaid bill.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. Let each beat stand clearly. A character acts, speaks, or thinks, and the narration moves to the next beat without piling explanation onto the moment. Keep the sentences short and declarative. Let feeling appear through what the character does with his hands, what he refuses to say, or what he repeats. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or quiet according to its owner. Do not force comedy or a young cast. Do not force a closing cliffhanger.\n\nMake the tragedy arise from irreversible choices and incompatible commitments. A character may owe something to family and something to work, to a promise and to a livelihood, to love and to duty. Let the alternatives be intelligible. Show the moment when a different choice was still possible, and show why the character did not take it: pride, fear, loyalty, exhaustion, or a mistaken belief about what the other person would accept. Do not make the character foolish. Let him act from reasons the reader can understand even when the result is destructive.\n\nUse focused reaction beats to make the loss immediate. After a consequential action, give the character a private response: a pause, a memory, a calculation, a refusal to think about what happened. Keep the interiority concrete and particular. Do not explain the feeling. Let the reader feel it through the precise, economical detail. A repeated phrase or object can return with changed emphasis: what sounded like pride the first time sounds like blame the second.\n\nLet recognition change what the loss means. The character can see the consequence of his choice in a specific detail: the empty chair, the closed door, the letter he cannot answer, the road he did not take. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. End the scene where the existing role and scene boundary require, without forcing a cliffhanger or a completed resolution. The tragedy can stop in the aftermath, after the recognition, in the concrete detail that remains.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the fall through exact objects and actions whose recurrence changes their meaning. A tool, a doorway, a glass, a stretch of road, or a repeated task can return in a longer sentence whose rhythm carries the difference between its first appearance and its later one. Let the lyrical movement come from syntax and return, not from decorative adjectives: a plain clause can state the fact, and a following clause can hold the sound of it until the loss becomes audible. Keep every image traceable to the scene or the teller's association.\n\nUse repetition as the hinge of recognition. When a phrase or object returns, do not merely restate it; place it beside what has changed so the reader feels the distance. A comparison may unfold across a paragraph if it alters what the narrator understands or makes present, but it must remain grounded in something actually seen, handled, or remembered. Let plain statements interrupt the cadence so that grief or consequence lands without ornament.\n\nPreserve Hemingway's direct language in dialogue and practical exchange. Characters speak simply, ask for concrete things, and leave the largest meanings unstated. The narration may be more rhythmically shaped than the speech without making the characters sound like the narrator. Let recognition arrive through a small exact detail rather than an announced moral. The aftermath keeps the object or place present after the person or possibility is gone, and the prose can end on that presence without explaining what it means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet reflection attach to a particular object, place, or repeated action in the present scene. The narrator may remember an earlier occasion when the same thing was used differently, or notice that a familiar task now happens without someone who once shared it. Keep the memory concrete: what was said, what was carried, what the weather did, what remained unfinished. Do not announce that time has passed; let the difference between the remembered detail and the present one carry the loss.\n\nUse Hemingway's direct language for the present action and allow the reflective sentence to stay plain as well. A thought can be long enough to make its connection clear, but it should not become a general meditation on impermanence. Ordinary pleasure may remain pleasurable in the same scene as regret: a good meal, a working tool, or a clear morning can be exactly what makes the absence felt. Let the character continue the practical activity while the reflection moves through it.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through remembered alternatives and present consequences. The character may recall a warning, a choice, or a moment when things could have gone differently, but the memory should be specific and the present should show what that choice has made unavailable. Recognition need not be spoken aloud. The prose can end with the character still holding the object, still doing the task, or still looking at the place, with the loss present but not explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral account: a situated teller relates the events to a listener or audience, and the telling itself is part of the scene. Keep the teller's language direct and concrete in Hemingway's manner. The teller names exact objects, places, and actions, and repeats a few of them when their meaning changes. What the teller omits is as important as what is said; the listener may notice the gap or ask about it, but the teller need not explain.\n\nLet the tragic arc emerge through the teller's selection and ordering. The account may move from an earlier occasion to a later one, or return to a repeated object or phrase that now carries a different weight. The teller can recall a warning or an alternative without moralizing. Keep the frame present: the listener's reaction, a pause, a practical interruption, or the place where the story is being told can return between sections. Do not let the frame become decorative; it should affect what the teller chooses to say or withhold.\n\nPreserve the regional route's social and domestic concreteness without importing a generic honor code, mysticism, or exile plot. The tragedy belongs to the particular community and setting supplied by the story. Recognition may belong to the listener rather than the teller, or the teller may reach it only at the end of the account. The aftermath can be shown through the continued existence of the place, object, or people the account has made present, with the loss carried in what the teller does not say.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details that carry causal weight. A character's choice should be visible in what they do with an object, where they go, or what they say in a practical exchange. Do not explain the motive after the action; let the sequence of concrete acts make it inferable. When a thought is essential, write it in direct language and let it be long enough to make sense, but keep only the inward connection that materially contributes to the present account.\n\nUse repetition sparingly and with consequence. A phrase or object may return when a later occurrence changes what it means. The second appearance should not restate the first; it should show the difference through a small variation in the action or the context. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete causality: the reader can understand what happened between scenes or what a character will not say, but the chain of cause and effect must remain recoverable.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through irreversible acts and their aftermath. Show the moment when a choice closes off an alternative, then show the remaining world in a few exact details. Recognition need not be announced; it can appear as a character's repeated action, a refusal to speak, or a small change in how they handle a familiar object. The prose can end on that detail, with the loss present and unexplained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose in which the tragic arc accumulates through ordinary movement. Let action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience follow one another naturally, but keep the language direct and the objects precise. A scene may open with a practical task, move through an exchange that exposes an incompatible commitment, and end with the character still at the task, the consequence now present in the room. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery.\n\nLet dialogue carry practical meaning and leave the largest things unsaid. Characters may argue about a concrete matter while the real conflict remains beneath it. The narrator may supply a focal character's thought when it contributes to the scene, but other characters' minds remain closed. Recollection should arise from something in the present scene and return to it, so the past informs the current choice without becoming a separate essay.\n\nDevelop the fall through choices whose alternatives are intelligible. Show what the character might have done, or let another character name it, without making the outcome seem arbitrary. Recognition may arrive gradually across scenes rather than in a single confession. The aftermath can be a continuation of ordinary life in which the loss is present in the way a familiar routine now proceeds. The prose may end at a natural pause in the scene, with the consequence still unfolding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the center of scene selection. Choose moments that show the particular promise of the pairing: the private language they use, the repeated gesture or object that belongs to them, the way they handle a practical task together or fail to. Keep Hemingway's direct language in their exchanges. They speak simply, ask for concrete things, and leave the largest feelings unstated. The emotional payoff comes from what they do and what they do not say, not from an explained interior monologue.\n\nUse callbacks with consequence. A phrase, object, or small ritual from an earlier scene may return when the relationship has changed. The return should not merely remind the reader of the earlier moment; it should show the difference through a variation in the action or the context. Let affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery have enough space where it belongs, but do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through the relationship's incompatible commitments or irreversible choices. Show what each person wants and why those wants cannot both hold. Recognition may belong to one character, or it may arrive too late for the other to know. The aftermath should preserve the relationship's particular loss: the private language now unused, the repeated gesture now performed alone, or the practical task now done without the other. The prose can end on that concrete detail, with the loss present and unexplained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the tragedy as an account of deeds that will be remembered. Open with a compact declaration that names the person or the act, then expand into the concrete particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Use Hemingway's direct language for the particulars: who carried what, what was said, what was left undone. Let a recurring phrase or designation return when a later occurrence gives it a new application. The recurrence should show what has changed, not merely restate the earlier moment.\n\nRelate each significant act to what will be remembered of it. A choice, a refusal, or a failed obligation can be told in a few exact clauses, with the chronicle voice noting what came of it. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account need not pronounce doom or turn every speaker ceremonial. Practical concerns may interrupt the elevated opening, and a character may answer a formal introduction with an ordinary request.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through oaths, kinship, journeys, gifts, feuds, or public deeds only when the story establishes them. The fall should be intelligible: the alternatives and warnings are present in the account, and the characters remain worthy of attention even as their choices become destructive. Recognition may come through the chronicle's return to an earlier phrase or act, now understood differently. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: the place, the object, the kin, or the unfinished obligation remains in the telling, and the account can end with that presence rather than a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fall inside the social and domestic realism of the specific Scandinavian context the story supplies. Let the tragedy come out of a person trying to hold two incompatible commitments, or acting correctly under a mistaken belief about what the situation permits, and transact it through ordinary arrangements: work, housing, family obligation, institutional routine, seasonal tasks, the practical exchanges that keep a household or workplace running. Make the warnings and alternatives intelligible as concrete particulars rather than announced themes — for example, a warning given as a specific remark at a specific table, or a way out that exists as an actual train, a job offer, a room that could be taken, a person who says plainly what could be done instead. Keep the Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish setting distinct as supplied; do not relabel a generic Nordic gloom as the place, and do not treat reserve, snow, isolation, or bleakness as automatic qualities of its people.\n\nUse Hemingway's economical narration for this social surface. Name the objects and actions that carry the situation: who sets the table, who signs the form, who waits at the station, who locks the door, what is said about the weather or the cost of the repair. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, let dialogue stay practical and direct. Show a refusal to take up a subject through what a person does with their hands or where they look, not through an announcement that the subject is too painful. Keep the people intelligent and worth attention even as their choices turn destructive; competence, warmth, and ordinary decency may persist to the end. When an errand, a question, or a room recurs, play the difference — the same errand run again under worse conditions, the same question now answered differently, the same room returned to after the possibility that would have changed it is gone.\n\nLet recognition arrive through a concrete detail whose meaning has changed: the person now understands what the unsent letter, the unclaimed key, the repeated call, or the untouched chair meant. Render the aftermath by showing the surviving routines continuing with that absence inside them — the kitchen, the office, the ferry, the clinic still functioning around what cannot be restored. The scene can end once the practical and emotional consequence is legible; it does not need to wait until every feeling has been named.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragedy in concise screenplay scenes. Head a new scene only when location or time changes, and keep action lines to the visible and audible events that carry the moment: what a person does with a glass, a coat, a door, a form, a phone. Name the precise object and the precise action, and keep the page free of camera commands. Let dialogue do ordinary work — asking for something, refusing something, giving an instruction, answering a question — with the unspoken meaning sitting in the action line that precedes or follows the speech, not in a speech that explains it.\n\nUse the cut as this form's way of performing Hemingway's omission. Skip the interval in which a decision hardens, and open the next scene on its consequence: a changed routine, an object now absent, a person who no longer comes to the door. Bring in offscreen sound when the sequence needs it — a door closing in another room, an engine starting, a phone ringing unanswered — and use voice-over only when the sequence truly needs it, never to explain what the scene already shows. When a location or practical action recurs, let the small difference in what is said or done show that a choice has been made or a possibility has closed. Render recognition as a visible or audible event: the character looks at the object whose meaning has changed, repeats a practical line under a different weight, or stops doing the routine action.\n\nShow the aftermath through the continued functioning of the world: the workplace opens, the meal is prepared, the train leaves, and the absence is present in the ordinary action that no longer includes what was lost. If the active contract requires private thought, keep it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the presentation, separate from dialogue and unavailable to the rest of the cast; do not convert it into voice-over by default. A scene or the sequence can close once the consequence is legible on the page; it does not need to wait for every feeling to be spoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nWrite the tragedy as a playable script. Give each speaker lines that do practical work — asking, refusing, reporting, instructing, answering — in economical, direct language, with the emotional consequence carried by the physical business around the lines. A character sets down a cup, folds a coat, moves toward the door, picks up a letter, stands still when they might have left. Stage directions establish what can be seen and done from the house; they do not explain what an action means, and no film-camera instruction belongs on the page.\n\nUse entrances and exits as this form's way of marking the narrowing of alternatives: a person arrives with news that could have changed the course, leaves before the necessary question is asked, returns after the decision has been taken. Let an object pass through the scenes and acquire a changed meaning through the way it is handled — a key returned, a chair no longer occupied, a form signed or left unsigned. When a line or a small piece of business recurs, play the difference: it can expose a refusal to move on or register a change that has already happened. Keep the people occupied with the practical work of living rather than posed around an explanatory speech.\n\nStage recognition as an observable action rather than an address to the audience: the character looks at the object whose meaning has changed, repeats a practical line under a different weight, or stops doing the routine action. If a monologue or aside serves the scene — a public statement, a performed address — give it that dramatic purpose and do not let it stand in for every private thought; required private experience stays in the active presentation as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material that the other characters cannot hear. Show the aftermath by letting the surviving routine continue with the absence inside it: the table is set, the work begins, the door opens for someone else. The scene can close once the staged consequence is legible; it does not need to wait for every feeling to be announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the tragedy through the associative movement of an authorized mind rather than a linear report. Braid present perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern, and anchor the associations in Hemingway's concrete particulars: the mind returns to a specific object, a particular sound, the weight of a tool, the feel of a door handle, the exact words someone said. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow that movement of attention, but keep enough orientation that the reader can tell what is happening now from what is remembered, planned, or imagined. The technique does not open other characters' minds or blur every scene fact into indeterminacy.\n\nUse repetition as the mind's way of refusing or failing to move past a detail. When a sensory anchor returns — the sound of the train, the smell of the room, the phrase spoken at the table — give the return a changed emphasis that exposes a small shift in understanding or a hardening of the loss; the anchor need not recur at every difficulty. Make the fall legible through the mind's movement among alternatives that were once open and are now closed — for example, a remembered offer, a warning heard and not followed, a choice that cannot be taken back.\n\nLet recognition arrive as a change in how the mind holds a familiar detail: the object or phrase that meant one thing now means another, and the shift shows through the association itself rather than an announced conclusion. Render the aftermath as the continued presence of the loss inside ordinary perception — the empty place at the table noticed between practical tasks, the routine that goes on without the person, the errand that must still be run. Keep the private experience inside the authorized mind under the active contract; other characters know only what they are given. The response can end once the associative movement has made the consequence legible; it does not need every feeling named or resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the tragedy through readable, sender-attributed messages. Let each person's messages do practical work — asking, refusing, confirming, reporting, arranging — in direct, economical language shaped by that person's actual digital habits: how they punctuate, how fast they answer, whether they send three short lines or one long one. Carry the emotional consequence in what is typed, what is edited, what is delayed, and what is never sent, and use only the interface events that matter: a timestamp that shows how long someone waited, a read receipt that goes unanswered, an edit that softens or hardens a line, a draft composed and then deleted.\n\nKeep the boundaries of the form exact. A sent message is available to the other person; an unsent draft is a composed artifact and may appear clearly separated from the transcript, but it is not automatically the writer's live private thought. Required private experience stays in the active presentation as clearly attributed nonspoken material, separate from the exchange. Invent no interface event merely to add subtext, and let context outside the exchange appear only when the story needs it, set off clearly from the messages themselves.\n\nLet the fall narrow inside the exchange: the same practical question asked again under worse conditions, the same reassurance sent and now meaning something different, the same address or time repeated after the possibility that would have changed it has closed. A warning can exist as an actual message that was sent and not acted on, or one that arrived too late. Make recognition legible through the channel itself — a person rereads an old message, sends the line they once refused to send, or stops typing. Show the aftermath through the channel's continued functioning: the group chat moves on, the routine confirmations continue, and the absence is present in the unanswered thread or the contact who no longer replies. The transcript can end once the consequence is legible in the messages; it does not need every feeling explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy in the rhythm of an illustrated scene: a short narrative passage establishes the moment, the attributed exchange advances it, and the focal narration or inner response registers what has changed. Keep the scene-setting concise and concrete, naming what the focal character actually notices — the object on the table, the light in the room, the coat by the door, the view from the window — and keep the dialogue practical, each speaker's words doing ordinary work. Keep scene changes legible so the reader always knows where and when the moment takes place.\n\nUse background and expression cues as purposeful references, not automatic truth indicators. A changed expression can draw the focal character's attention, but the tragic weight is carried by the concrete detail or the exchange itself: the object no longer there, the line repeated with a different weight, the routine action that stops. When a scene returns to a familiar visual detail, let its significance have changed because of a later event, and let the focal character notice it again and now understand what it meant; the cue need not recur at every difficulty. Keep required private experience in the active presentation as focal narration or inner response, separate from what the other characters are given to know.\n\nRender recognition as a change in how the focal character holds a familiar image or phrase, shown in the reaction beat rather than announced as a conclusion. Show the aftermath through the scene's continued use: the room is still lived in, the routine goes on, and the absence is present in the visual detail that remains. Selecting this style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands; those require their own authorization. The scene can end once the consequence is legible in the illustrated moment; it does not need every feeling spoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nWrite the tragedy as an installment-oriented serial in Hemingway's economical prose. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what has changed locally, and what remains available to continue, and recover an earlier particular briefly, only when it helps the current installment. When a concrete observation or practical exchange has acquired new meaning, bring back the necessary particular — the object left behind, the line said at the table, the errand once possible and now closed — rather than retelling the history that established it.\n\nCompress uneventful intervals while preserving the changes that matter to the fall. If a routine continued, a relationship shifted, or a possibility closed between installments, that change belongs to the story's history and may not be omitted so a later installment presents the earlier state as still open; a new development can qualify an old understanding without replacing the old event or making the focal character forget a supported conclusion. Let ordinary life continue around the narrowing, and keep required inward experience available in the active presentation when it contributes feeling or thought, even when no immediate decision changes.\n\nUse recurrence across installments to expose the closing of alternatives: the same practical action returning under worse conditions, the same question asked and now answered differently, the same place revisited after the possibility that would have changed it has been lost. Let recognition arrive when an earlier detail or exchange is recovered and its meaning has changed, and show the aftermath through the serial's continuing world — the work goes on, the routines continue, and the absence is present in the ordinary action that no longer includes what was lost. An installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known; serial duration should give the fall room to acquire meaning, not make the consequence perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat each admission, plea, apology, or return as a scene of self-argument as much as a bid for understanding. Let a character voice one position, register its cost in the other person's face or silence, and then defend, retract, or reverse it in the next thought or line. Lodge the idea the character needs to be true—about loyalty, unworthiness, or who deserves forgiveness—inside the relationship itself, so that rejecting the beloved would also mean abandoning part of the self.\n\nKeep the exposure physically present: a flushed face, a withheld touch, a door left open, an apology that arrives after the moment it was for. Make strain alter what people actually say and do next; an interrupted confession can curdle into an accusation, and a refusal may be repeated more gently once the speaker sees the damage it does. Warmth is real but partial—comfort arrives awkwardly, reshaped by the same pride or shame it is trying to soothe—and it does not cancel the hurt already done.\n\nDerive the narrating viewpoint's distance from its actual emotional condition rather than from a stock vocabulary of suffering. A first-person narrator may spiral into obsessive reconstruction of a single moment; a close third person may dwell on sensory detail the character cannot yet name; a more distant voice may frame the friction as social observation while the damage shows in what people avoid. Keep private thought distinct from speech: a silent surrender can accompany a spoken ultimatum, and a kind word can conceal a decision to leave. Let the tragic arc emerge from these repeated collisions between feeling and justification, so that when recognition comes it changes what the loss means without undoing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nBuild the scene as a sequence of clearly attributed dialogue beats in readable paragraphs, with speaker identity never in doubt. Let the catastrophe gather through what one character needs another to believe, admit, or forgive. Give each speaker a coherent position that justifies what they are doing now, argued in plain language under pressure; the next speaker's reply tests that position through a variant of the same moral question—loyalty, guilt, blame, who is allowed to suffer—rather than through a manufactured plot reveal.\n\nLet thought spiral between spoken turns without turning speeches into lectures. Short narrative paragraphs between dialogue blocks carry physical reaction, hesitation, and the private reversal that follows a public claim; the inward turn can contradict the next line, as when someone insists they are fine while their posture says otherwise, or promises to stay while inwardly arranging to go. Distinguish what a player-controlled character reveals from what that character merely thinks, preserving the active perspective contract, and let NPCs act from their own interests—someone keeps tending the fire, minding the counter, or waiting for an answer even as the dispute consumes the room.\n\nEnd at a natural scene boundary rather than a forced beat. The response may stop mid-thought, at a pause where someone turns away, or on a question that grows out of the developing dispute; no cliffhanger, duel, or final proof of feeling is owed. The tragedy is already in what has been conceded, refused, or misunderstood, and its weight shows in the next move each speaker actually makes—continuing, striking out, softening, or falling silent—without narratorial judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as social and satirical realism: the moral contradiction becomes a family and standing crisis, narrated through concrete domestic and social encounters—a marriage negotiation, a debt called in, a divided household, word of someone's disgrace. Measure the irreversibility of a choice by what it does to the family's position, credit, and resources, not by declaration. Keep the tone sharp but never cruel: mock a person's self-deception while the harm it causes stays real and fully felt; the satire lands on vanity and rationalization, not on the injured.\n\nGive each major figure a defensible position that is also a social position. One upholds duty and endurance; another prizes personal sincerity over face; a third speaks the language of practical survival. When they argue, tangle each conviction with immediate material conditions—who can afford to be generous, who expects to inherit, who has already lost standing in the community—so the tragedy emerges from the gap between what each believes and what their role permits them to do. Pointed, restrained dialogue carries the conflict without announcing every subtext, while the narrator's dry commentary frames what the encounter means socially.\n\nLet custom and ceremony organize the exposure. A formal visit, a funeral, a matchmaking discussion, or the settling of accounts can corner someone into an admission they had avoided across many smaller scenes. Move episodically from encounter to encounter, letting reputation, obligation, and need accumulate ordinary texture until the calamity arrives inside them. When recognition comes, let it rearrange the household's understanding rather than strike from outside; take language, period, and community from the supplied world—the route supplies the eye, not a relocated setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the reversals grounded in physical strain. Let self-accusation surface through what a character handles, discards, or refuses to touch—a cracked basin, a coat folded too slowly, a key scraped against a lock—and when someone argues a position aloud, undercut or confirm it with an immediate material fact. The speech is not a lecture; it is a move made with sore hands, in a cold room, against someone else's worn patience. Irreversible decisions carry a price in things and bodies, not merely in opinions.\n\nHold the inward turn without letting it diffuse. A character can move from self-justification to self-disgust inside one sentence, provided the thought stays anchored to a particular sight, sound, or task in the scene. Keep transitions abrupt and causal—one failure leads directly to the next action, and a decision shows in what the person stops doing. Give abrasive or rough speech to a specific speaker with a reason for the roughness—exhaustion, debt, pride, an old injury—and let the dialogue expose the moral claim beneath the bluntness instead of narrating it.\n\nUse rhythm for pressure and release: a longer, slower sentence for deliberation before a physical act; a run of short ones for panic or the moment someone finally moves. Not every scene is violent, but material need and damage remain credible forces; a gesture of care may be obstructed by poverty or injury, so comfort tends to arrive in practical form—food shared, a sleeve mended, a debt argued over. When recognition comes, render it locally and concretely: a person sees what has been ruined, what remains, and what cannot be taken back.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through documents, each with a distinct self-justifying voice, a clear author, an intended recipient or purpose, and a time of composition. Let the tragedy emerge from the space between what a writer claims and what another document, a revision, or a material trace reveals: a letter proclaims devotion while its corrections show hesitation; a journal entry accuses itself in one breath while the writer's conduct in another record contradicts it. A plea to a creditor differs from a confession to a friend, and a report meant to clear a name omits what would damn its author.\n\nStage the reversal across the sequence. One letter sets a position; the reply contests it in a different moral vocabulary; a later entry accuses its own author of the very failing it once charged to someone else. Time passes between compositions, so recognition arrives belatedly—a writer learns what an earlier letter actually meant, not what it was intended to mean. Keep gaps and conflicts intelligible without an editorial voice explaining them; where the active authorship contract exposes live private thought, let it press against the document's composure, distinct from the words being set down for a reader.\n\nGround each entry in the practical detail proper to its form—a rent receipt kept by accident, an unsent draft found among papers, a line crossed out and copied over—using the form the scene requires rather than rotating through formats for variety. Let the eventual truth stay partial, assembled from surviving records, and bind the final recognition to loss: someone understands too late what a silence or an accusation destroyed. Supply no omniscient account after the documents end; the correspondence is the tragedy's witness, and its gaps are part of the meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive the tragic arc intervals of genuine care and small pleasure, because they are what make the impending or completed loss intelligible. A character pours tea for someone who cannot stay, recalls a private joke while arranging a bed, chooses a familiar food because it still means home. These moments are not illusions or traps; they are real, limited gestures of affection that the tragic reversal does not erase but weighs against, showing why people remain attached even when a moral disagreement makes their life together impossible.\n\nLet the spiral of conviction appear inside the comfortable scene, bound to the relationship rather than delivered as an abstract speech. Someone thanks another for a kindness and immediately understands that it is a form of farewell, or refuses a gift because accepting it would implicate them in what they condemn. Run the reversing thought through the small sensory facts of the moment—the warmth of a cup, a familiar step on the stair, a blanket folded by habit—so private reasoning stays rooted in the beloved world being lost. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact: comfort does not make everyone agreeable, and the world's established problems remain.\n\nIn the aftermath, preserve room for kindness without repairing the central loss. The recognition that closes the arc may make future comfort more painful or more precious; it may leave one character able to perform one final act of care for another. Do not force a confession, a reconciliation, or a happy ending. What has been broken stays broken, and the warmth that remains is a true part of the situation that defines exactly what was forfeited; the closing tone can hold tenderness and burden at once without tipping into bleakness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame each personal crisis in composed narrative distance. Before or during an intimate scene, a deliberate paragraph establishes what the room, street, household, or public opinion makes possible or forbidden—the traditions shaping a character's sense of duty, the debts weighing on a family, the gossip that will turn a private confession into common knowledge. Make this framing load-bearing rather than decorative: each piece of context explains why the imminent choice is irreversible, or why a belief has become a matter of life and death. Sustain paragraph movement through transitions that carry the reader across hours, rooms, and reputations, and keep the narrated register distinct from ordinary speech.\n\nWithin that frame, follow the active point-of-view contract into at least one mind's contending voices. Let a private reversal emerge from the scene's concrete pressures: a sentence can move from a character's claim about what he deserves, to his notice of a detail that contradicts it, to a new position that will collapse the moment he must speak or act. Dialogue may stay plain, broken, even coarse, while the narrated thought complicates or undermines it; a plain speaker can unsettle an elaborate argument without the narrator sorting the two registers for the reader.\n\nBuild the fall through extended scenes and transitions in which an earlier recognition becomes retroactively intelligible. Use ceremony, visits, formal conversations, or public encounters as the machinery that can carry private guilt into social consequence. After the climax, give the aftermath narrative space—a household reorganized, a reputation altered, a memory reinterpreted—so that what cannot be restored stays visible. Connect the particular loss to the larger conditions that made it possible, but through persons and scenes rather than argument; the style's formal clarity is the medium in which that connection becomes visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer story as a present scene of telling under pressure. Give the teller a concrete reason to speak now—to hold a listener who might otherwise leave, to justify a choice, to delay a decision, to win protection, or to make someone understand what cannot be said plainly—and make the audience particular people with their own needs, impatience, and history with the teller, never a passive circle.\n\nShape each embedded tale as an act of self-presentation. The teller chooses which events to narrate, which motives to confess, which details to repeat, and that selection is itself story material: a long defense of one decision may pass over the moment that made it possible, and a story told to prove loyalty may quietly expose the shame it is meant to cover. Let the listeners' interruptions, questions, and silences change what the teller risks saying next.\n\nRun the Dostoevskian interior voice inside the frame rather than around it. While the tale is being spoken, render the private counter-voice that accuses, reverses, or complicates the public account; the unspoken thought may contradict the spoken words, or arrive a beat late and force a visible correction. Do not let the embedded story become one smooth confession—let it spiral in front of the people watching it spiral.\n\nMake the nested material do work in the outer tragedy. A tale can move a listener to act, reveal a commitment the teller can no longer take back, or bind the teller to a version of events the telling itself can no longer support. Let recurrence carry that weight: a phrase, an object, or a gesture repeated across narrative levels accrues meaning as the outer situation develops, so the teller returns to the same story under different pressure each time.\n\nKeep what the teller knew inside the tale separate from what the teller knows while telling it; recognition belongs to the outer frame, not to the remembered past. If the teller finally sees what the story has been doing, let that recognition change what the loss means without undoing it, and let the tragic weight come to rest in the present relationship between teller and audience rather than in a moral extracted from the tale. Treat the Arabian Nights as a craft source—situated telling, nesting, delay, recurrence—not as scenery; bring in no djinn, court, or desert unless the supplied world already has them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the documentary and historical-memory route. Build the story around a present narrator assembling or confronting records—letters, case files, minutes, testimonies, photographs, official notices, the physical traces of a place—and move the prose between those documents and the narrator's memory of the same events, keeping the distance between them visible.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure arise from that gap. A document can state what happened without explaining why, and memory can supply a motive the record does not support. Let the narrator argue with the archive: defend a remembered intention against a dated entry, accuse a dead person on the strength of one surviving sentence, or find that a page contradicts the story the narrator has told for years. A plain entry should be able to unsettle an elaborate self-justification.\n\nLet the tragedy sit in irreversibility. A choice made under one belief cannot be remade once the record shows the belief was false, and recognition arrives through reading and rereading—a marginal note, a missing page, a date that does not fit, a witness statement that changes the meaning of an old loyalty. The aftermath is the narrator living with the version of the past the archive has made unavoidable.\n\nMatch syntax and distance to the documentary narrator: precise, composed sentences when rendering the records; longer, more entangled clauses when memory and self-argument interrupt them. Draw the German-language context from the supplied setting, community, and period rather than from bureaucracy, translated word order, or a generic national temperament.\n\nKeep the remembered scenes inside their original knowledge and the documents' evidence separate; do not write the archive's findings back into the past as though they were known at the time. The point is not that the records solve a puzzle but that they make a loss final and intelligible in a way memory alone could not.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the story on a present collision between old authority and everyday life. Make inherited power concrete and social—a family obligation, a building with a history, a debt, a title, a ritual of deference, a private arrangement that still decides who may speak and who must listen—so that the horror stays personal and political, made of people, property, memory, and obligation.\n\nLet Gothic-Punk atmosphere change how particular encounters are experienced. Urban material detail should carry the weight of the relationship: a room that keeps its safety until a certain person walks in, a public space where every mask holds, a private space where one mask comes down and its cost becomes visible. Keep intimacy morally complicated—desire, loyalty, shame, and dependence can share one relationship—so that a trusted person offers genuine care and remains dangerous to confide in.\n\nRun the Dostoevskian interior voice through that atmosphere. The focal character argues inwardly over what to reveal, what to believe, and what has already been done; pride pushes toward confession, shame reverses it, and each reversal entangles things further. Treat the social mask as a survival strategy under inherited power rather than simple hypocrisy, so the thought of letting it fall carries dread as well as longing.\n\nLet the fall gather around an irreversible act of exposure or refusal. A confession voiced in trusted company can change what that trust means; a secret kept can harden into betrayal; a moment of sincerity can be used, misread, or arrive too late to be received as one. Recognition comes when the character sees what the self-argument has cost the people inside these arrangements, and the aftermath holds that loss instead of returning the old intimacy.\n\nImport nothing from vampire setting material—no Kindred physiology, Humanity numbers, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules. The inherited authority and the fall both happen inside what the supplied world already permits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route. Write the story as a single participant's account, delivered in the teller's own voice and addressed to a specific community, occasion, or later audience: a statement made after an event, a memoir assembled for people who were there, a testimony offered to those who must understand what happened. Keep language, region, community, and period exactly as the supplied story gives them, and force nothing onto the scene—no English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or generic family structure the world does not supply.\n\nLet the Dostoevskian self-argument operate inside this public address. Speaking to people who may remember the same events differently, the narrator defends choices, accuses the self, reverses position, and entangles motives, until ideology and self-image become personal problems under the pressure of the community's memory. A plain sentence from another witness should be able to unsettle an elaborate justification.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge from the gap between the account and what others witnessed. The narrator may pass over a moment of cowardice, reframe a betrayal as necessity, or claim an intention no one observed; recognition arrives when the version can no longer stand alone—when a surviving relative, a neighbor's testimony, a public record, or a remembered phrase from someone now dead gives the loss a different shape than the one being confessed.\n\nKeep the narrator's knowledge at the time of the events separate from any understanding reached during the telling, and do not write the community's perspective back into the remembered scenes. Let the account itself shift as the narrator realizes what is being admitted, and let the aftermath hold what cannot be restored—a relationship, a place, a reputation, a future the event closed.\n\nCarry the pressure of testimony in the prose: direct, concrete sentences for what happened; longer, entangled passages for the attempts to explain or excuse it. The account should feel composed for listeners with a stake in its truth, not like a private diary read aloud.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the story as a sequence of dated entries or reflective fragments kept by one person, and let the gaps between entries matter: the writer returns to an event, a person, or a decision after days or weeks, and each return shows how its meaning has shifted. Keep language and narrative distance true to the actual teller; direct feeling and plain argument are available, with no required quietness and no obligatory inventory of scenery.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure work through the form. An entry can begin as plain record and turn into self-argument—the writer defends a choice, accuses the self, reverses, or notices something left out—and a later entry can correct or complicate an earlier one without erasing it, so the reader sees the spiral across time as well as within a single passage.\n\nUse omission as a tragic device. The diary records what its writer could bring themselves to write and omits what could not be faced; a later entry can expose the omission by returning to the same scene with new attention or by mentioning a fact the earlier entry conspicuously avoided. Recognition arrives when the writer sees what the record has been doing—the pattern of avoidance, the repeated justification, the person described again and again without the fault ever being named.\n\nKeep each entry inside its own moment's knowledge, with no late understanding leaking backward into an early date. The aftermath lives in the final entries, where the writer keeps company with what cannot be restored and the diary becomes the remaining place the loss can be examined.\n\nLet the prose vary with each entry's occasion: one concrete detail held in sharp attention, or a long spiral of accusation and defense. Seasonal and sensory detail may appear where it carries the writer's actual attention, never as required ornament.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the polyphonic and nonlinear route. Carry the story through multiple attributed accounts that move across time—a participant's memory, a survivor's statement, a letter, a fragment of testimony, a later narrator reconstructing what happened—keeping language, community, and history specific to the supplied world and adding no supernatural facts where that world excludes them.\n\nGive each account its own Dostoevskian pressure. A voice defends a decision, accuses someone else, reverses itself, or builds a justification the next account undercuts; the polyphony is never neutral, because every speaker has a stake, a blind spot, and a version that needs to be true. Let a plain statement in one account unsettle an elaborate argument in another.\n\nMake the order of telling meaningful. Juxtapose accounts so the same event is seen from incompatible positions before its shape settles: a later account reveals what an earlier one omitted; an earlier one exposes the self-deception inside a later reconstruction. The tragedy is a collision of commitments that cannot all be honored, not a puzzle with a single solution.\n\nHold each voice to what its speaker knew when speaking. Recognition belongs to particular people at particular moments—one may see the loss clearly while another still cannot—and the reader should feel the distance between those recognitions, with no later narrator's understanding leaking into an earlier account.\n\nLet the prose change with each voice: urgent, repetitive, self-justifying testimony beside a cooler, more precise reconstruction, so the nonlinear structure makes the loss feel unavoidable without flattening everyone into the same philosophical speaker. The aftermath keeps what the incompatible choices closed off—a relationship, a community, a place, a future.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene inside the immediate situation, establishing who is present, where they are, and what is already at stake in the first few lines. When the scene moves, take a clean break and reorient the reader to the new place before dialogue or action resumes.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. After a significant line, give the focal character a focused reaction beat—a thought that accuses, defends, reverses, or ties itself in knots—specific to that character and that moment rather than a generic pause. The beat can be serious, uncertain, angry, or darkly funny according to its owner. Do not turn anyone into a speechmaker; run the spiral through reaction beats and short exchanges instead.\n\nMake the Dostoevskian pressure visible by contrast. A character says something calm or practical while privately arguing over what the words cost; another speaks plainly and takes apart an elaborate justification in a single line. Competing convictions reach the reader through what people say, what they notice, and what they cannot bring themselves to say.\n\nBuild the tragedy through scene-level consequences: a choice made in one scene can close an alternative the reader saw was available, and recognition arrives when a character grasps what a decision has already cost. Let the aftermath hold that loss rather than restore it. The fall may accumulate across ordinary moments; no cliffhanger or completed reversal is required in every scene.\n\nGive unfamiliar names or mechanics a clear presentation when the story needs one. The container supplies fast-readable staging and strong visual focus, not a mandatory first-person narrator, comedy, young cast, or fixed period and setting.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose one image, sound, or phrase from the immediate scene that can carry the focal character's self-argument, and return to it at each turn of that argument with a changed inflection. The same window, coin, stairwell, or borrowed phrase first supports a justification, then turns against it as accusation, then remains after the justification has failed as the thing that cannot be argued away. Do not summarize the reversal in a separate explanatory sentence; let the syntax perform it, a clause beginning in defense and ending in the recognition of what the defense has cost, the sentence's own momentum carrying the character past the point where the excuse still holds.\n\nBuild paragraphs in which competing convictions share one grammatical current. A long sentence can hold a generous intention and its destructive consequence together so the character must see them as one act rather than an intention followed by an accident; set the rhythm so the lyric pressure rises toward each reversal and eases in the aftermath, where shorter clauses register what is simply still true. Let plain statements interrupt this movement when a practical fact or another person's simple answer exposes the elaborate thought. Make the contrast audible, the flat sentence landing inside a woven paragraph, but do not make the plain speaker wiser; the exposure may be incomplete, unkind, or beside the point, and the character may talk past it.\n\nKeep every image traceable to the scene or the teller's established associations; a comparison may unfold when it changes what the narrator understands, not as an inventory of grief laid over the objects. Voice warnings and rejected alternatives in the same current, so the fall remains chosen rather than fated by mood, and the people who object remain intelligent even as their advice fails. End where the thought has reached a genuine change or a refusal to change: the image may still be present in the final sentence, altered by what the character now knows.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a small present detail — an object in use, a name spoken casually, a task done at the usual hour — open a remembered occasion the focal character has never finished accounting for. Do not deliver the memory as a completed anecdote with its moral attached; bring it back carrying a question the character once believed was settled. As the recollection unfolds, let the character defend the earlier self, then accuse it, then discover that the same motive is available right now, in this room, at this hour. The tragic recognition is that the loss was not only suffered but chosen in some particular moment, and that no better interpretation of it undoes the choosing.\n\nKeep the present scene running while the memory runs. Answering someone, measuring, pouring, waiting for something to finish — the practical moment can interrupt the reflection mid-sentence, and the reflection can resume changed by the interruption. Make the coexistence of present experience and what is no longer available concrete: the character is not only remembering a loss but living inside the conditions that loss created, so the remembered room and the occupied room should keep trading details. Ordinary pleasure may remain pleasurable throughout; warmth need not be exposed as false, and a joke that still works is part of what the fall has put at risk.\n\nMark passing time through small irreversible changes rather than declarations: a habit dropped, a route no longer taken, a word the character no longer uses about themselves. Let other people's warnings return as remembered speech, still intelligible, still rejected for reasons that were not foolish. Do not require the reflection to end in confession or completed judgment; the character may reach a changed understanding, or the present moment may cut the thought short, leaving the past's pressure legible without any announcement of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated oral account as the form of the fall: one specific teller speaks to one specific listener, and the telling itself becomes tragic material. Establish the frame in concrete social terms before the told matter gathers weight — the room, the hour, the reason this visit is happening, what the two speakers are to each other, and what may and may not be said between them. Let the teller begin with the public version of events, the one shaped for neighbors and duty, and let the listener's question, impatience, or silence draw the private self-argument up through it.\n\nTreat interruption as the engine of the confession. Each time the telling changes direction, it should be because the act of speaking has exposed something the teller had not admitted: a defense offered with conviction collapses into an accusation against the self, then reassembles as a different, worse defense. The listener may misunderstand, press the wrong point, or refuse the consolation being extracted; a plain practical remark can unsettle an elaborate justification without making the listener a sage or a judge. Keep the listener's response partial, self-interested, or kind, and let it leave the moral question open.\n\nBuild from the language, setting, and community the story actually supplies, keeping the relevant traditions distinct rather than blending them into a general regional atmosphere; antiquity, mysticism, exile, and honor codes belong only where the supplied world establishes them. This framed telling does not require nested tales or a ceremonial close. End with the telling interrupted or unfinished — the listener called away, the truth approached but not landed on — so that the listener's judgment and the teller's recognition both remain in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small set of exact details and let them carry the focal character's self-argument without restatement. A choice can be defended in one direct clause; then one detail — an object's position, a sum of money, the exact words someone used — makes the defense impossible, and the next action or the next short thought shows what the character now understands. Place details at the turns of the argument rather than distributing atmosphere evenly, and let a detail return once, changed, so its second appearance does the work a paragraph of explanation would do. Omission must leave the connection inferable, not delete it: the reader has to see why the detail matters even though the character does not say so.\n\nAllow an essential thought its full length. When a reversal, a self-accusation, or the entanglement of two convictions is the point, one precise extended sentence serves better than several performed fragments; break the long line only where the character's attention actually breaks. Keep voices individual in a few strokes: a plain speaker's short answer can expose an elaborate justification, but the plain speaker is not automatically right, and the focal character's brevity is not automatically honesty.\n\nPreserve the tragic arc at this scale. A warning can be two sentences long and still intelligible; a rejected alternative can appear as a single refused gesture; recognition may arrive in one sentence that permanently changes the meaning of an earlier detail. The style does not require understated emotion or a refusal to name what is lost. End where the recognition has occurred or been refused; the prose need not summarize the loss, count it, or announce its permanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the fall as continuous scene-first prose in which the focal character's self-argument arises from the immediate social and material situation. Let a practical problem — money owed, a deadline, a room that must be shared, an errand that cannot be postponed — break into a justification and force it to change shape, so the thought defends, accuses, reverses, or entangles itself while the character is also answering someone, noticing the room, or in the middle of a task. Do not isolate the moral crisis in a set-piece speech or a solitary meditation; the crisis and the ordinary traffic share the page.\n\nMove naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. A recollection surfaces because a present detail calls it up — a smell, a phrase, a name on a list — and the character returns from it to find the conversation has moved on without them. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery, and let the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register together; contemporary presentation does not require a contemporary setting or understated emotion.\n\nKeep the fall consequential and the alternatives intelligible. Other characters warn, object, or offer a different reading of the same facts, and the focal character rejects them for reasons that are not foolish — pride with a history, a conviction once vindicated, loyalty that forbids a particular retreat. Their plain remarks can unsettle the elaborate argument without ending it. Let recognition, when it comes, change what the loss means rather than deliver a lesson. Close at a natural scene boundary, the action continuing or paused according to the existing role and situation, so the next scene inherits the consequences instead of a summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship the material of the fall. Let the premise be legible through scene selection before the choice presses: the private phrase only these two use, the gesture that settles an argument, the repeated ritual of meeting or parting. Then let one of them make a choice the other cannot accept or undo, and bring the shared material back with a changed meaning — the private phrase now something that can be quoted as evidence, the old gesture now an accusation, the remembered scene now the measure of what has been broken. Use only the premise actually supplied; do not bolt on a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nLocate the self-argument inside the intimacy. The focal character's justifications take the form they take because this particular person is their audience: a defense offered to the other, an accusation the character fears hearing in that voice, a confession the closeness itself makes unavoidable. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where they belong, and let the emotional payoff come from consequences the premise established — a callback should not merely repeat an earlier moment but show how that moment has become evidence, accusation, or loss.\n\nLet the other person remain a person rather than a verdict: warm, cold, partial, mistaken, or several of these within one exchange, and understood imperfectly by the focal character. The relationship need not end, but the recognition must change what the intimacy can now mean. End where the scene has actually brought the bond — the private language may still be in use because it cannot be unlearned, the loss still being absorbed — fulfilling the premise's particular promise rather than imposing a generic tragic conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the fall as an account being retold. Open with a deliberate declaration that identifies the person or the choice by its consequence — what was sworn, refused, given, or done, and what came of it — then expand into the particulars that make the declaration incomplete: the pressure that was on the person, the warning that was intelligible, the moment the choice could still have gone otherwise. Let a recurring phrase return when later events give it a new application: an oath, a name, a refusal, a compact description of what someone did, first stated as fact, later carried as cost.\n\nCarry the self-argument inside the chronicle's habit of relating acts to what will be remembered, not in a separate philosophical speech. Report the justifications as events in the record: the defense offered publicly, the accusation delivered to one witness, the reversal that came too late, each attributed and placed. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective — the account may relate what was said, what was done, and what later became known, without claiming every private mind unless the active contract permits it. A plain declaration from another person can unsettle an elaborate justification, and the chronicle may record the disagreement without resolving it.\n\nKeep the source tradition coherent rather than mixing epic conventions; no Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue belongs in speakers who would not use them. The recognition lives in the aftermath: what cannot be restored is preserved in the telling, and each recurrence of the phrase or deed shows the loss still being remembered and still costing. End with the account still being formed, another act available to be retold, rather than settling the person's whole meaning on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragic fall through the social-realist route of this tradition: anchor the scenes in a concrete institution the active setting already provides — a municipal office, housing committee, parish council, clinic, or workplace — whose forms, minutes, and queues outlast any single confession. Where the story carries Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish particulars, keep them distinct instead of blending them into a generic Scandinavian tone; where it does not, do not relocate the cast or import snow, isolation, or reserve as decoration. Let the institution's plain practical language become a second voice beside the focal character's self-argument.\n\nBuild the arc from small documented pressures — a form signed, a duty postponed, a complaint filed, a recommendation given under strain — each legible in its moment and changed in meaning later. Let warnings arrive as ordinary institutional speech: a colleague's caution, a caseworker's question, a neighbor's objection, phrased so the character hears them intelligibly and still chooses against them for reasons the narration makes real. Recognition rereads the accumulated record rather than merely punishing the person; the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored.\n\nUse the Dostoevskian interiority as the space where the character defends, accuses, reverses, and entangles a position. Move between the external record and the private argument so the same event can appear once as a file note and again as a shameful or proud confession, and let one plain institutional sentence — a date, a refusal, a stamped line — unsettle an elaborate justification. The reader follows that reasoning; the committee, colleague, or neighbor knows only what was said or filed.\n\nPut the competing convictions inside particular relationships and material conditions: a disagreement about a child, a debt, a tenancy, or a care obligation, with rent, wages, transport, weather, and the physical demands of a job consequential without becoming landscape inventory. Describe the room, the queue, the meeting, the walk home concretely, then let the focal thought tighten or turn back on itself. The tragedy lives in the distance between what the institution can record and what the person cannot or will not say; a reply may stop after a recognition, an unanswered question, a completed practical step, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now irrecoverable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragic fall as visible public exchanges. Each scene sets a concrete situation — a meeting, a meal, a task, a departure, a return — where two or more people speak from positions the audience can understand. Give the dialogue real moral pressure without turning lines into philosophical speeches; a plain practical remark can destabilize an elaborate justification. Scene headings mark changes of location or time, and action lines stay concise, naming what is visible and audible: who is present, what they handle, where they move, what interrupts them.\n\nPlace the focal character's private self-argument in clearly attributed nonspoken notes beside the script. There the character may defend a choice, accuse themselves, reverse, and entangle their reasoning while the people on screen know only what was said or done, and no later scene may treat that material as something another character heard. If voice-over is used at all, treat it as performed speech with its own audience and purpose; it does not replace the required private prose, and neither does dialogue.\n\nLet the presentation show the gap — or the agreement — between speech and thought from moment to moment. A character may answer calmly while the attributed note records shame, pride, or a competing impulse; the spoken line and the private experience may also align completely. Choose each instance from the character's actual situation rather than imposing concealment or confession. Use cuts and offscreen sound purposefully: a cut can move from a public exchange to its private aftermath, an offscreen voice can intrude on a moment of solitude, without either becoming a habit at every turn. Keep the page free of camera commands.\n\nBuild the irreversible choice through scenes whose visible consequences accumulate, with warnings and alternatives intelligible in the spoken exchanges and physical business so the audience can see that another choice was possible. Recognition changes what the accumulated actions mean; the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. A scene may end wherever the boundary falls — on a completed action, an unanswered question, a private realization the frame keeps silent, speech still under way, or a plain visible acknowledgment of loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite the tragic fall as public confrontations in a shared stage space. Each scene places two or more speakers in a room, doorway, workplace, or domestic setting where entrances, exits, and physical business are playable. Let the relationship between lines and movement carry the pressure: a person speaks while handling an object, turning away, sitting, rising, refusing to leave. Keep speakers clearly cued and the script free of film-camera instructions.\n\nDistribute the competing convictions to the speakers. A character may argue for duty, love, pride, or a mistaken belief in language another character can answer, and the replies should challenge the premise for an actual reason rather than prolong the argument. A plain practical line can unsettle an elaborate justification. Reserve monologue and aside for moments with dramatic purpose — a performed confession, a deliberate self-address, an audience being addressed — rather than using them as the default container for thought.\n\nPreserve the focal character's private self-argument as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material. The reader follows the character defending, accusing, reversing, and entangling a position while everyone on stage knows only what was said or done, and no later line may reveal that another speaker heard it. Keep stage directions economical and concrete, establishing observable conditions — who can see an entrance, reach an object, hear a line, move between two places — without certifying hidden guilt or motive.\n\nMake the irreversible choice legible in physical business: a door closed, a letter handed over, a bag packed, a key left on a table becomes part of what cannot be undone. Warnings and alternatives stay intelligible in the spoken exchanges and the movement, so the audience can see that the character might have chosen otherwise. Recognition changes what the accumulated actions mean; the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. The scene may stop after a completed action, an interruption, a private realization kept from the other speakers, or a plain acknowledgment of loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through a single authorized mind whose associative movement carries the tragic arc. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern so that a present object or phrase can summon an earlier argument, a shameful recollection, or a competing conviction. Keep orientation strong enough that the reader can tell what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined; the technique does not blur every scene fact into impression.\n\nMake the self-argument the engine of the movement. The focal mind defends a choice, accuses itself, reverses, entangles the reasoning in a new objection, and returns to the same unresolved pressure, and these voices arise from an actual situation — a debt, a duty, a promise, a relationship, a mistaken belief — rather than from abstract debate. A plain remembered sentence from another person can unsettle an elaborate private justification, and the mind may flinch from it and circle back.\n\nLet the irreversible choice emerge through the movement of thought: circling a decision, noticing an alternative, dismissing it, reconsidering, then acting or failing to act in a way the narration makes legible. Warnings and alternatives stay intelligible inside the flow even when the character cannot hold them steadily. Recognition changes what the accumulated memories and self-accusations mean; the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored.\n\nKeep other minds outside the focal consciousness. The narration may report what another person said or did, but it does not enter their private experience; the focal mind guesses, misreads, and revises, and those revisions belong to the tragic movement rather than arriving as corrections from an omniscient narrator. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow the associations — a sentence breaking, repeating, turning back on itself; a paragraph moving from a physical detail through a memory into a bodily sensation and a private accusation — without dissolving into a catalogue of disconnected impressions. The reply may stop inside a recognition, an unanswered question, a recurring phrase, or a plain acknowledgment of loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the tragic fall through a message exchange whose senders have distinct digital habits. One person types long self-justifying paragraphs, deletes them, and sends a short practical line; another replies at once with blunt questions; another leaves messages unread, sends a single word after a delay, or edits out an admission. Let the habits make the competing convictions felt without turning every sender eloquent or confessional.\n\nDistinguish three layers: the sent message, the unsent draft, and private thought. A draft is a composed artifact and may hold the confession or self-accusation the sender cannot transmit, while the sent message replaces it with something safer; live private thought, when it appears, sits clearly outside the transcript. No character responds to what was never sent.\n\nLet the irreversible choice accumulate through transmitted steps: a message sent in pride, a question left unanswered, an attachment shared, a channel changed, a read receipt ignored — each legible in its moment and changed in meaning later. Warnings and alternatives stay intelligible in the messages themselves, so the reader can see that a different reply was possible. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, and channel changes that matter; do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. Recognition changes what the accumulated exchange means; the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored.\n\nLet each person's actual situation — work, family, money, distance, a shared obligation — shape what is transmitted and what is withheld. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript, describing the room, the hour, the physical task, or the focal sender's state, without granting another sender access to it. The reply may end on a sent message, a draft still being composed, a delayed reply, or a plain acknowledgment of what the exchange has made irrecoverable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the tragic fall as a sequence of scene-oriented exchanges. Open each scene with concise setting — where the characters are, what the space holds, what the focal character notices — then move between attributed dialogue and short focal narration or inner response. Keep a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: a visual detail orients the moment, a spoken line carries the public pressure, and a brief narrative passage shows how the focal character receives it.\n\nGive the focal narration the self-argument. The reader follows the character defending a choice, accusing themselves, reversing, and entangling the reasoning while the others in the scene know only what was said or done; another character does not hear the focal thought, and a later line cannot treat it as spoken. Use background and expression cues as purposeful references: a changed expression may accompany a spoken line, but the scene's meaning comes from what is said, done, and narrated, not from faces functioning as fixed indicators of lying or confession. A plain spoken sentence can unsettle an elaborate private justification.\n\nBuild the irreversible choice through scenes whose visible and narrated consequences accumulate, with warnings and alternatives intelligible in the exchanges and focal responses so the reader can see that another choice was possible. Recognition changes what the accumulated scenes mean; the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. Keep scene changes legible, and do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or engine commands merely because the style is selected.\n\nA scene may end wherever the boundary falls — after a completed action, inside an unanswered question, on a private realization — and the focal narration may continue into the aftermath, showing what the character now understands without requiring the others to catch up.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nWrite the tragic fall as a cumulative through-line across installments. Each reply makes clear what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains open to continue; recover context briefly only when it helps — a single recalled sentence, promise, or public commitment can let the reader re-enter without retelling the history.\n\nLet earlier self-justifications return with changed meaning. A position defended in one installment may be tested by a later event, reversed under pressure, or entangled in a new objection, with the earlier reasoning recalled in compressed form so the recognition lands without a full flashback. Keep the character's private self-argument available across replies — defending, accusing, reversing, entangling — while the other characters know only what was said or done.\n\nBuild the irreversible choice through earned developments. A warning may appear and go unheeded; an alternative may stay intelligible but be refused for reasons the narration makes real; a small public commitment may become the step that cannot be undone. Let progress involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, and let ordinary life continue around the fall — meals, errands, care obligations, practical tasks — present and consequential rather than paused for tragedy.\n\nUse occasional hooks without making every turn a miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. An installment may close on completed work, a pending request, a private realization, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, and the next continues from there without demanding a reversal, confession, or fresh crisis. Serial duration gives the selected commitments time to acquire meaning rather than making the outcome perpetually recede, and a closing recognition may reread everything the accumulated installments have established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the fall as intimate exposure carried inside communal memory. Let a sentence gather years—a past scandal, a family legend, a public mourning—while it lands on one person's present strain: the daughter who still sets down her cup when the man's name is spoken, the son who answers every question about his father except the direct one. Anchor every gathered detail in the cost of admitting, needing, withholding, apologizing, or returning, so the lyrical sweep never muffles the hurt.\n\nMake the turbulence concrete in behavior rather than a stock vocabulary of suffering. A woman stops setting a second place at the table; an aunt repeats a euphemism until the younger generation refuses to hear it; a character returns to a house, a bed, or a quarrel and pays for it in a pause at the door, a task begun and abandoned, a sentence started in one tone and finished in another. Treat rumor as pressure, not decoration: what the neighbors say changes what can be said aloud, so the withheld words accumulate in private thought under the narrator's permitted access.\n\nLet warmth and pain coexist without repair. An embrace can be awkward and still wanted; a reconciliation can be partial and still held onto. A recurring phrase can mark the wound when memory actually calls it up, not at every difficulty. Bring recognition through the intimate register—a character understands what was lost while doing something small and ordinary—and let it change what the loss means without settling the strain. Keep the aftermath alive in avoidance, remembered comfort, and half-explanations carried into new rooms. The narrator stays a participant or close observer of the family memory, not a detached moralist; a conversation may end in silence, a secret may stay kept by someone who still loves the person it is kept from, and the scene can stop inside the exposure itself rather than at a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nPlay the scene in short, complete paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, in whatever person, tense, and action markers the active authorship contract supplies. Stage the room concretely—the weather, the object in someone's hands, where the other person stands—so the player can see the irreversible choice or strained reconciliation taking shape. Let the communal past enter through what people say: a grandmother mentions how the house smelled before the fire, a neighbor repeats a rumor about the will, a sibling revives an old accusation over an ordinary meal. One speech may gather years when the moment earns it—\"That's what your father said the morning he left, and now you're saying it to me\"—followed by a brief action or reaction beat.\n\nKeep NPCs making practical choices inside the tragic pressure: clearing the table, defending a dead man's reputation, refusing to answer, offering help that costs them something. They continue their own business instead of waiting for the player. When the player asserts an action or hope, answer from the active world rather than granting automatic success: a request for forgiveness may meet silence, an errand may reopen an old dispute, a kind gesture may be received with suspicion because of what the community remembers.\n\nLet the tragic arc span several replies instead of demanding one recognition per turn. End the shared interval where the beat naturally stops—after a strained silence, a partial answer, a decision made in the doorway—and let the continuation follow from that moment rather than from a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. Use recurring objects and phrases as anchors of communal memory, not code words the cast must recite. Preserve the selected perspective and player control throughout; the loss and its meaning emerge through what the characters actually do together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the tragedy through the intimate domestic route: the story lives inside one family or household in the language, period, and community actually supplied, and the decisive choices are made at a sewing box, a stove, a dinner table, or a threshold rather than on a public stage. Center the fall on incompatible duties, an unwanted marriage, an inheritance quarrel, an unmourned death, or a child's estrangement—whichever bonds the scene's people are actually caught in—and let warnings arrive as family advice or an elder's rebuke that a character has real reasons to disregard.\n\nLet a single sentence gather across the household's time: from a mother's sewing box to the night she refused a proposal, from a courtyard argument to the ancestor whose name is never spoken. The communal memory is the family's own, carried in rumors, repeated phrases, and objects that change meaning when the next generation touches them. A daughter's refusal to eat at the family table, a son's silence during the rites, a daughter-in-law's deliberate mistake in front of guests, a servant's remembered overhearing—each opens a layer of the past into the present without turning the telling into court chronicle or satire.\n\nKeep the temperate, inward tone while the narration still gathers. Report, in one sentence, that for three years the widow spoke of her husband only to the canary, and that this is why her children grew up knowing the bird's name and not their father's. Let recurring language mark the unhealed places: a phrase from a dead grandmother, a nickname that became an insult, a question each generation asks too late.\n\nBring the chronicle to recognition in the same register—a parent realizes the child left for good, a wife recognizes the exact morning her husband's silence became permanent, a son returns to find the house sold—and keep the aftermath inside the same rooms and small objects rather than a moral lesson. Add no literal supernatural elements, and keep the setting and cast as supplied rather than substituting an assumed dynastic past.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the fall with hard edges and tight transitions. Compress the gathering into single material images: a cracked basin, a mended fence, a ledger page, a woman's worn shoe can carry five years and a marital quarrel at once. Anchor memory in wear and repair—the town remembers a man by his limp, the family remembers a mother by the exact way she swept ash toward the door—so that what people notice becomes what they repeat, and what they repeat becomes the communal record.\n\nUse forceful diction grounded in each speaker's place and history. A blunt line like \"You came back because the harvest failed, not because you remembered us\" belongs to one person, not to everyone. Keep the physical stakes visible: the debt costs the last mule, the confession is made while scrubbing the floor, the recognition arrives when a son finds his father's signature on a land transfer. Let the irreversible choice appear in an action or an object rather than a reflective paragraph, and keep a thought long only when that character's reasoning needs it.\n\nVary rhythm deliberately: a longer sentence that gathers two events, a terse exchange, then one hard observation that stops the passage. Move from practical task to remembered public event to present consequence without descriptive transition.\n\nMake the recognition legible in this register—a character says \"That's what I traded\" while holding the receipt, or understands the loss only when the creditor's cart takes the furniture—and after that point do not restore or soften it with commentary. The aftermath is a stripped room, a name crossed out of a record, work resumed in silence. Keep the supplied setting and community; no survival-horror props, and the world is not uniformly bleak.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the tragedy through authored documents—an unsent letter, a daughter's account to a relative, a creditor's ledger note, a neighbor's testimonial, a priest's notation, a page torn from a household book—using whichever form the scene actually requires rather than a fixed rotation. Give each document its author, its intended recipient or explicit purpose, and its time of composition, and let what it knows or conceals follow that situation. The loss occurs between documents, in gaps and delays, never inside an omniscient explanation.\n\nLet communal memory appear as conflicting accounts across entries. A sister remembers a marriage as shameful while the husband's letter remembers it as necessary; a rumor becomes visible only through later references—a doctor's note refuses to repeat it, a granddaughter's school exercise copies part of it. Recurring language travels between documents: a phrase from a will, a name spelled differently in each household, a sentence the mother used in every letter until she stopped writing. Treat an unsent draft as something its author shaped for a purpose, not as unmediated live thought.\n\nBecause each author knows only what the situation allows, recognition arrives unevenly. A wife writes that she understood her husband's absence only after reading his brother's account; a son finds a receipt that changes the meaning of his father's apology. The irreversible choice is documented in what was sent, what was torn, what was kept, and what was never answered. Let a later document reinterpret an earlier one—the same event told as duty in a letter and as betrayal in a later testimony—and let material traces such as water damage, a missing page, or a crossed-out address mark time and handling.\n\nAdd no clarifying narrator after the records. The final document may leave the loss unresolved in the characters' own terms while the sequence shows how memory itself became the record. Keep the supplied setting and character identities; a confession is never required in every entry.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite warmth as fact inside the tragic arc, not as foreshadowing or a delay before disaster. Spend real attention on chosen kindness: a grandmother braids a child's hair while explaining why the family stopped visiting the coast; a husband remembers how his wife takes her coffee and makes it in the middle of a quarrel; a neighbor leaves a plate at the door on the anniversary no one names. Let the gathering sentences hold these rituals across time, so a favorite song, a repaired chair, or a shared recipe becomes part of the family's history and can be recalled later with its meaning changed.\n\nKeep affection sincere without demanding confession or emotional repair. Practical care can carry it: a son buys his mother the same bread every Friday without ever discussing their argument; a woman keeps her sister's letters in a tin and does not read them again. These gestures coexist with the tragedy's pressure; they are not evasions waiting to be exposed.\n\nLet the arc still move through incompatible commitments and irreversible choices—the comfort does not prevent the loss, it changes what the loss means. A couple may spend one ordinary tender afternoon before an arranged separation; a mother may teach her daughter the old songs in a happy moment, and afterwards the daughter remembers it as the last time they sang together. Let recognition arrive through recollected pleasure: the most protected memory can be the one that makes the later silence unbearable.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact—a character can be generous in one moment and stubborn the next, kind to one person and cold to another. After the loss, preserve the comforts in memory, sometimes as consolation and sometimes as an additional wound, and let a small act of care continue after the recognition without erasing what happened or pretending the difficult choice was never made.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the tragedy in deliberate, sustained paragraphs that open outward from private event to the social and historical world. Tell a daughter's refusal to marry alongside the town's changing economy; narrate a father's debt with the names of the families who prospered that year; remember a death through the civic records that failed to record it. A single paragraph can begin with a woman's sleepless night, pass through the rumor her neighbor started, and end with the public silence that followed the funeral.\n\nUse enough narrative distance to frame the scene through social context, history, or judgment where the chosen narrator permits it: the narrator may observe that the family's ruin came not from one mistake but from a sequence of ordinary refusals that everyone later described as inevitable. Keep warnings and alternatives intelligible inside the same movement—a sister advising against a journey, a creditor offering an extension, a priest naming the exact sin that leads to the next loss—while the characters remain intelligent and worthy of attention in their refusal.\n\nDistinguish narration from spoken language. Dialogue stays brief and locally voiced; the formality lives in the narrative movement that gathers and interprets, not in polished public speech from every mouth.\n\nStage the recognition fully: a character comes to understand an old choice through a public ceremony, a legal document, a stranger's account, or a return to a place where the original alternative is still legible. Then compose the aftermath as its own paragraph movement—the house quieter, the family name spoken differently, a widow resuming an old habit without explanation—and let recurring language and public event hold the communal memory together. Use the actual community and period supplied; no imported aristocracy, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the tragedy as a telling that is still happening. Open in an outer scene with a speaker, an occasion, and a purpose: a community gathered after a public disaster, a family keeping the true version of a fall private, travelers passing a warning to someone headed the same way. Make the listeners' stake concrete—someone here loses standing, mercy, or sleep depending on how the story is received—so the telling is a present social act rather than a recital. Keep the frame's material world particular: names, work, weather, and the ordinary public handling of an outcome that cannot be reversed.\n\nInside the telling, use the García Márquez profile's gathering motion. Let a single sentence move from a private room to family memory to public rumor and back while staying anchored on one human consequence. When the embedded tale takes over, give it relationships and knowledge the listeners lack, and let the speaker's purpose govern selection and emphasis: what is expanded, what is softened, what is held back for another night. Treat the Arabian Nights as a craft source only—no djinn, palaces, or deserts need to appear unless the story's own world supplies them, and render the marvelous only where that world already permits it.\n\nBuild the fall as tragedy, not fate. The people inside the tale face intelligible alternatives, and duty, love, pride, social condition, or a mistaken belief narrows the path; they remain intelligent even as their choices become destructive. Keep the teller fallible in a productive way: the account can be sincere and incomplete, and a listener may press a detail of timing, obligation, or consequence without receiving a full explanation. Let the delayed revelation change how the listeners read the outer situation—what the tale was for—rather than deliver a verdict.\n\nLet a phrase or object pass between levels so recurrence accrues meaning: an image named in the outer scene surfaces inside the tale and returns changed, and a repeated oral formula marks the pressure of memory rather than certifying truth. Give both teller and listeners the private experience the spoken account cannot carry—what cannot be said aloud, what is silently recognized. The telling deepens recognition and aftermath; it does not undo the fall. End with the gathered listeners absorbing what they have heard, knowing more and able to repair nothing, rather than with a case closed or a better version established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy in the documentary and historical-memory route of the German-language traditions. Narrate from after the loss, in a voice that has gathered what survived: remembered conversations, official reports, registration lists, family testimony, weather, and the recurring phrases of ordinary people. Keep the syntax concrete and historically pointed—attached to dates, objects, and spoken words—rather than abstract, and do not flatten it into bureaucratic sterility or translated word order.\n\nOrder the material as an inquiry into how the fall became possible. Use the García Márquez profile's gathered sentences to bind the sources to consequence: a daughter's delayed letter, a neighbor's warning repeated at three doorways, a list that changed after a certain date, each collecting detail from several moments while remaining anchored to one person. Show the characters as intelligent people under constraint—the accounts they gave beforehand, the alternatives they weighed, the obligations that made a destructive choice look necessary—and refuse the comfort of a single explanatory flaw.\n\nCompare sources without crowning any of them. A document can fix a date while misreading a motive; a family memory can carry true feeling while omitting the public condition that gave that feeling its force. Let the narrator set them side by side, mark contradictions plainly, and acknowledge what can no longer be known. The tragedy should emerge from incompatible commitments and social conditions pressing on personal choice, not from one villain or one weakness.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the comparison itself: what the narrator comes to understand changes what the loss means without repairing it. Keep remembered experience actual and particular—hesitation, what a source could not say—rather than replacing minds with paperwork. Close on the surviving community: what the public record absorbed, what only the family still carries, and the prior life that cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the tragedy in a present-day city where inherited authority survives in buildings, debts, habits, and names. Make Gothic-Punk atmosphere material: traffic against the shuttered windows of a hall, a family house converted into offices, rain on old stone, burned coffee at a late meeting, the weight of a surname people still lower their voices to pronounce. The old order here is social, economic, and domestic—do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or supernatural rules to stand in for it.\n\nBind that atmosphere to García Márquez's gathering motion. Let a sentence move from city rumor through family memory to one person's private desire and stay anchored on a consequence: a dying parent's authority, a lover's necessary lie, a debt two households remember differently. These are engines of the fall, not background texture. Establish what each arrangement of old power still commands before a scene tests it.\n\nStage the masks and their partial fall. In trusted rooms, let characters admit private wants while others defend a public front; relay each person's partial knowledge without opening every hidden mind, and let intimacy be morally complicated rather than uniformly predatory. Let atmosphere change what an encounter means—the same room reads safe before an admission and airless after it—and let recurrent objects such as a locked ledger, a repaired watch, or a phrase quoted from an old will accrue memory when they return, without appearing at every turn.\n\nKeep the fall intelligible. Alternatives stay visible even as pride, duty, love, or a mistaken belief about what the old order permits blocks them; the catastrophe should come from incompatible commitments, not from a single betrayal or monstrous weakness. End on recognition and surviving aftermath: the city continues, the name keeps some of its power, and the lost person and prior hope cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nTell the tragedy through the Indian traditions' oral and frame-tale forms, with social and domestic realism inside the tale. Ground the frame in one occasion and community: a household gathered after a death, a village festival interrupted by remembrance, a long bus journey on which an elder settles an old account. Keep language, region, community, and period specific to the story being told; do not force code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.\n\nOpen outward and move inward. A present question or dispute—a property line, a marriage proposal, a returning migrant—prompts the memory, and the tale unfolds around an irreversible choice whose motives stay tied to property, marriage, duty, migration, love, pride, or a mistaken belief. Let the community still name the lost alternatives with respect; honoring them is part of what makes the loss legible.\n\nGive the telling García Márquez's gathered recurrence. A marriage, a journey, a festival, or a locked room passes first as rumor in the frame and returns inside the tale with its meaning changed; the teller can move from private memory to public report without letting the report become authoritative. The community's remembering is wise and partial at once, repeating a phrase whose sense shifts as the nested accounts deepen.\n\nMake the listeners active participants. They interrupt, question, complete, or correct a detail out of their own memories, and each intervention serves an interest—one wants the tale to protect someone, another to condemn, another to keep an alternative hidden—so shape every interruption as a choice about what the story is for. The frame does not escape the fall; it changes what the listeners recognize. Close on aftermath: the loss preserved inside the family's present memory and the occasion that recalled it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy as a dated diary and reflective miscellany kept by a narrator who witnessed or inherited the loss. Treat the entries as composed retrospective writing: the diarist selects what to record, leaves gaps, and re-reads earlier notes with later knowledge. Let entries break chronology when memory, rumor, or a public event returns, and let each entry take its own register—plain, lyric, furious, uncertain—according to its keeper and the day; no mandatory quietness governs the voice.\n\nGive the diary García Márquez's gathered time. Within one entry, move between family memory, neighborhood rumor, public announcements, and private rooms, collecting details across years—a mother's repeated visits, a discontinued train route, a notice read aloud, a name no one speaks—while every gathered detail stays anchored to one consequence.\n\nBuild the fall through recorded choices. The diarist and the people around them weigh duty, love, reputation, or a mistaken belief; record warnings that were heard and refused, and keep the writers intelligent rather than doomed from the first page. Use season and weather to mark the passage of time without making them automatic symbols of mood, and let a phrase, festival, or route recur across entries and change meaning with each return.\n\nUse the form's absences deliberately. The fall itself may occur on an unwritten day, known only through later entries, witnesses, or the silence where expected notes should be; keep the causal shape legible to the reader even so, so the gap deepens recognition instead of hiding it. A diary is composed writing, not a complete mind: let it carry what its keeper chose to record, including hesitation and things said to no one else. Close with a present entry that names the irreparable loss and preserves the living aftermath.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaham::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy through the Latin American traditions of social realism, chronicle, and testimony. Keep the narration communal but attributed: a family member, a witness, a public record, or a survivor interviewed later carries each section, and the reader always knows whose account is speaking. Preserve the setting's language, community, and history rather than transplanting a generic village or family, and add no marvelous or supernatural facts unless the story's world already permits them; where it does not, García Márquez's techniques operate as craft, not miracle.\n\nMove the prose across personal life, family memory, public events, and rumor. Let one sentence gather details across time while anchored to a human consequence—a house divided after a death, an election that rerouted a migration, a promise repeated until it became a burden—and make social conditions do tragic work concretely: work, property, partnership, education, migration, and local authority create the pressures that render choices intelligible and still catastrophic.\n\nKeep the characters intelligent and worthy of attention. They debate, hesitate, misinterpret, and act out of love, duty, pride, or an established public obligation; warnings and alternatives remain visible to the audience and to other characters even as the path narrows. The tragic event comes from incompatible commitments rather than one fatal flaw or a universal punishment.\n\nUse testimony to separate memory from event. A witness may recall a phrase correctly while misreading its meaning; a chronicle may fix a date while omitting a private motive; later interviews or documents can return to those differences and revise them. Recognition changes what the loss means without undoing it. Close on the surviving community carrying the aftermath: some pursue restitution, some preserve silence, some retell the story with a new emphasis.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy in light-novel prose. Enter each scene inside an immediate situation—a door opened after a long absence, an announcement heard at the market, a relative arriving with news everyone already knows—keep spatial movement explicit and easy to follow, and break paragraphs cleanly around action, dialogue, and private response. Reactions belong to their owners and may be serious, uncertain, angry, or grief-stricken; constant banter, a young cast, and a closing cliffhanger are not requirements of this container.\n\nUse the container's clear staging to make old obligations physical. A broken porch step, a repaired fence, a ledger handed across the dinner table carry history without exposition in the same breath. Introduce unfamiliar names and local institutions plainly when they matter, without turning every relationship into a glossary.\n\nFold García Márquez's layered time into the fast surface. Let one sentence gather years around a single consequence, and let neighborhood rumor sound in the background of one scene and return as private family memory in the next, so communal knowledge and private experience stay braided rather than separated into exposition blocks. Make the fall intelligible through particular choices—a father's promise, a debt accepted for a sibling, a departure ordered by duty, a mistaken belief about what could be saved—with alternatives and warnings visible to the reader even as they close.\n\nLet repetition earn its meaning: a phrase, a meal, a route, or an object may return and accrue weight, without appearing at every dramatic turn. A scene can end on a new understanding, a private thought, or an unfinished practical task. The irreversible loss survives every revelation; end on aftermath—the surviving family, the changed street, the public record, and the private memory continuing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose one recurring image from the character's early attachment to the world they will lose: a sound at the door, a particular tree, a garment, a way of preparing food. Let the image first appear in an ordinary moment before the choice. When the image returns, report its changed condition through a concrete detail—the door is now opened by someone else, the tree has been cut, the garment fits a different body—rather than through the narrator announcing that it means loss.\n\nBuild the gathering sentence from a specific act to a named later consequence. The sentence may begin with a small gesture and move through intervening years, but each clause should land on a particular human result: who stayed, who left, what the house became, what the child learned to call the absent person. Do not let the sentence dissolve into an abstract meditation on time. Anchor its final clause in a tangible detail that belongs to the world of the story.\n\nLet the recurrence carry recognition. When the image returns after the irreversible turn, place it near the focal character's private awareness so the reader sees the difference through that character's attention: a familiar morning sound now heard as the sound of no one arriving. The narrator need not explain the recognition. The changed image and the character's response to it can do the work.\n\nKeep communal memory in attributed form. The town's version enters through a neighbor's sentence, a shopkeeper's question, or a family member's later retelling, not through the narrator summarizing what everyone believed. Let the public account and the focal character's private knowledge diverge where the story supports it. The narration may reveal a private reason after the public version has settled, but that reason must be drawn from the character's earlier experience, not from hindsight.\n\nVary the rhythm by contrast. A plain clause can precede a long associative sentence so the shift of attention becomes audible. Dialogue should keep its own pressure and register; the lyrical syntax belongs to the telling, not to every speaker. Aftermath does not restore what was lost. It can still make a surviving detail luminous, because the tragedy matters through what remains unreturnable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nSelect a particular object or place that carries an unfinished attachment: a room, a piece of furniture, a garment, a market stall, a window. Begin with its present condition, then move to a remembered occasion when it belonged to a different life. Keep the memory specific—who was present, what was said, what task was being done—so the reflection follows the object's actual history rather than dissolving into a general meditation on impermanence.\n\nSequence the layered time as present object, remembered occasion, later consequence. After the memory, return to the object and let a small change in its current condition carry the intervening loss: the room is now used for something else, the garment no longer fits, the stall has a different owner. The consequence should be concrete and named, not an abstract statement that time has passed.\n\nLet communal memory enter through attributed voices. A neighbor may recall the same occasion differently, a relative may repeat a phrase from that day, or a public event may be dated by a festival or announcement. After the communal account, return to a private detail—a stain on a sleeve, a window left open—so the larger event does not erase the specific life it touched.\n\nMake recognition an inner event within the scene. The focal character may realize that a promise can no longer be kept because the person to whom it was made no longer expects it, or that a habit has outlived its original meaning. Present this as the character's changing relation to the familiar thing, not as a lesson delivered to the reader. The paragraph may stop with the character still looking at the changed object.\n\nAllow ordinary pleasure to remain pleasurable. A meal prepared well, a child's invention, or an afternoon of unremarkable labor can appear without immediate undercutting. Let the loss return as a later association rather than as a forced interruption. The melancholy should feel earned by the specific attachments the story has established, not applied as a uniform tone.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the framed oral storytelling route. Situate a teller in a concrete time and place, with an audience whose interruptions or questions can shape the account. The tale may open with a compact proverb, a remembered phrase from a parent, or a declared reason for telling it, such as a wedding night, a wake, or a long evening of waiting. Keep the frame modest and specific; it should establish the telling occasion rather than imitate a canonical collection.\n\nMark the teller's knowledge sources within the telling. When the teller was present, report the scene directly: what was said, what the teller saw, what the teller did. When the teller learned something later, introduce it as later knowledge—\"afterward the neighbor said,\" \"the letter reached us only in the spring,\" \"the records showed\"—so the audience can distinguish participation from report. Do not let the teller reveal the end as a punishment before the events make that end intelligible.\n\nLet audience interruptions redirect the account. A listener may ask what happened to a particular person, correct a detail, or press for a reason the teller has not yet given. The teller may answer with a new small fact, return to an earlier moment, or admit what no one recorded. These interruptions should deepen the story rather than merely decorate it.\n\nUse a recurring object or phrase to anchor the layered time. A lamp, a thimble, a repeated blessing, or a particular doorway may appear at different moments. Each recurrence should change meaning as the tragedy deepens: the lamp is still lit, then dark; the blessing is spoken in welcome, then in parting. Do not turn the recurrence into an ornamental refrain applied to every entrance.\n\nLet the characters' intelligence remain visible through direct speech and reported reasoning. A daughter may argue with her father about a proposed marriage in the practical terms of the household; a merchant may explain his refusal to sell a field. The teller can report these exchanges in indirect narration, but the characters' own registers should remain distinct from the storyteller's elevated phrasing. Avoid imposing a regional temperament or a religious lesson. Let the tragedy arise from concrete attachments, duties, and mistakes within the supplied world. The frame can close when the audience asks a remaining question or when the teller answers with a new small fact rather than a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect three recurring objects and use each one exactly three times. The first appearance establishes the object in an ordinary moment. The second appearance occurs near the consequential choice, with one small change in condition. The third appearance occurs after the irreversible turn, with the change now complete: the gate is still unlocked, the shoes are smaller, the receipt is now framed. Give only the exact detail needed for the present scene. Do not explain the symbolism.\n\nCarry communal knowledge through one attributed exchange. Instead of describing a whole town, use a single neighbor's repeated question or a single shopkeeper's refusal. Let that narrow exchange stand for the social consequence. The dialogue should carry the pressure of the community; the narrator should not summarize the town's opinion.\n\nChoose one brief interior moment per consequential turn. When a character must choose, give a remembered sentence from a parent, a physical fact about the room, or a single sensation that makes the reason intelligible. Do not write a full deliberation. Do not insert the narrator's later judgment.\n\nAfter an irreversible event, shift to an ordinary chore or a blank space. The next scene should open with the exact detail that is now missing from routine: the chair no longer pulled to the table, the morning task done by someone else. This makes the omission causally legible without an emotional paragraph. The reader should know that a particular choice or arrival led to the present condition.\n\nState plain facts when they carry the tragedy. A sentence such as \"She did not return\" can stand alone. The surrounding sentences need not explain every emotional register, but the central chain of events must remain traceable. Let dialogue remain individual: a character's plain speech should not become a series of performed fragments.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the story through continuous scenes in which the tragedy emerges from ordinary exchanges and decisions. Let the narrator move among action, dialogue, perception, and recollection, but keep the present scene's concrete texture dominant. When a character remembers a past event, return quickly to a current object or voice so the reader stays anchored in the room.\n\nShow communal belief through three or four attributed exchanges that qualify one another. Select what the butcher said, what the aunt repeated, and what the sister declined to answer. Do not announce \"the town believed.\" Let the exchanges stand side by side so the public rumor and the private memory remain distinct, without the narrator deciding which one is true.\n\nUse the gathering sentence to move from a small domestic action to a named social effect within the scene's claim. It may begin with a gesture and end with a particular consequence: \"She folded the letter before opening the shop, and by noon the street had already arranged her reputation around the same two lines.\" Do not leap into future knowledge beyond the selected perspective. The final clause should name a specific result, not an abstract summary.\n\nUse interiority in the modern novel's flexible mode. The narrator may include a brief unspoken reflection before or after dialogue, or a physical reaction that reveals more than the character says. Keep the access consistent with the chosen point of view. A focal character's thought should not expose another character's hidden motive unless the contract permits that access.\n\nAllow tragic recognition to emerge from a specific consequence. A character may see an old friend's changed manner, hear a child repeat a phrase that once belonged to someone else, or discover that a kept object no longer evokes the same response. Present the recognition as an inner event within the scene, not as a concluding aphorism. The paragraph can end with the character returning to the present task, the aftermath of loss visible in what they now do differently.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAnchor the relationship in actual shared history. Use scene selection to show the private language and repeated moments that define the pair: a phrase they use when one is about to leave, a habit the other tolerates or completes, a room or task they approach differently. Let a small earlier scene teach the reader what the loss will later cost. Do not include an explanatory relationship summary.\n\nBuild each callback in three parts. First, establish a shared object, phrase, or gesture in an ordinary moment. Second, return to it after the relationship has changed, with one concrete difference: the object is broken, the phrase is spoken by only one of them, the gesture is now performed alone. Third, let that changed return alter the present scene's meaning. The callback should not merely duplicate the old moment; each repetition carries additional consequence.\n\nMake affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery arise from particular events. A moment of jealousy should be connected to a specific past incident the reader has seen or heard about. A moment of comfort should feel earned by the couple's current situation. Do not insert a confession, a misunderstanding, or a reconciliation simply because the emotional beat calls for one; let the pair's own history produce the next turn.\n\nUse García Márquez's communal memory as a frame around the intimacy. Outsiders may remember the pair at a festival, repeat a phrase the two once used, or notice a change in the household. The public version can be more confident or more forgiving than the private truth. Let that difference become part of the tragedy: the couple knows what they promised each other, while the town retells a different story.\n\nDeserve the tragic turn. When a relationship breaks, show the incompatible duties, errors, or external pressures that made the break intelligible. The characters should remain capable of attachment and defensible decisions, even when their choices bring harm. Select the aftermath scene from the pair's established habits: one person resuming an old task with the specific absence now unignorable, or returning to a shared place that has changed its meaning. Do not force the pair to make up or to sever everything for dramatic symmetry; the aftermath should preserve what the relationship had uniquely made possible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nBegin with a deliberate opening that assumes a retelling: \"This is how the people of the quarter would later remember the year the bridge fell,\" or \"Three names recurred whenever the old women spoke of the D'Angelo house.\" The opening should name a concrete community or a remembered event. Do not use the opening to predict punishment; let it establish that this story has been retold.\n\nUse compact declarations for a person's established reputation, then complicate that reputation with a specific deed or private contradiction. A man may be remembered as the one who never sold the orchard, but the narration may show the morning he almost signed the paper. Keep the focal character's private reasoning separate from the public memory; the chronicle can report both without making them identical.\n\nSelect one recurring phrase, gift, debt, or oath from an actual deed or established relationship. Return to it at key turns, and let each recurrence change its application: the same phrase that opened a friendship may later close an argument, or a gift that passed between brothers may become a child's keepsake. Do not attach the recurring label randomly; it should come from a deed recognized in the story's world.\n\nInclude dialogue as reported or remembered speech. Another character may interrupt the elevated rhythm with a practical concern: \"The river was too high,\" or \"He never returned the plow.\" The contrast does not have to be comical; it can keep the chronicle grounded in household consequence. Public oaths and gifts may appear only where the story has established their social meaning.\n\nPreserve the unavoidable aspect of tragedy. The account may note alternatives that were urged, such as a neighbor's warning or a mother's refusal, but it should not use hindsight to make the actors foolish. Recognition can come when a surviving relation repeats one of the old phrases and understands for the first time what it bound together. Present that understanding as the survivor's private inner event, not as the chronicle's announced moral. End with an act remembered rather than a moral summary: the community has kept the story because the loss changed how they name the road, the season, or the household.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWork through a slow-burning social calamity in a specific coastal or inland community, told by a narrator who gathers private consequence, rumor, and remembered obligation over years. Choose one domestic or social institution—a fishery, a farm, a parish, a school, a family business—and let a durable local agreement slowly become an irreversible mistake. Keep the method realistic: no literal magic, no transplanted Macondo, and no sweeping claim about a national temperament.\n\nAnchor each major turn in a concrete object or repeated chore, such as a ledger, a boat, a kitchen bench, a harvest, a parish record, or a letter held back too long. A sentence may carry a remembered promise forward into a changed present, showing how the same object acquires a heavier meaning after an irreversible choice. When a warning is given, make it intelligible and specific: a person notices an unrecorded debt, a boundary not surveyed, a silence where a decision should have been. The decision-maker may still proceed for a reason rooted in pride, duty, or love.\n\nLet recognition happen late and in practical terms. The community does not gather for a single explanatory speech; rather, the consequences are reckoned through meals prepared, rooms closed, work abandoned, or a name no longer spoken. Keep the inward experience available: the focal person may privately understand why they acted even as the practical damage continues. Another character does not gain access to that thought merely because the narrator has shown it.\n\nUse the whole community as a kind of memory, but do not blur everyone into one voice. Distinguish a neighbor who saw the first warning from an official who recorded it; let rumor modify an event without erasing what can still be known. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: the loss remains real, but the surviving community's changed speech becomes part of what follows. End where the consequence has become legible, not where every guilt has been confessed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragic fall as a sequence of tight scenes, each with a clear heading when location or time changes and action lines that show what a viewer would actually see. Let the irreversible choice occur through specific physical events: a hand leaves a letter unposted, a boat is launched without the needed repair, a field is sold in one quick scene. Do not pause the action for explanatory speeches.\n\nUse dialogue to carry personal and family memory without turning it into narration. A character can mention a promise made in an earlier dry season and now remembered during a storm. Offscreen sound—a bell, an engine, a closing door—can signal consequences the scene does not show. Keep camera commands out of the action lines; the sequence itself should create the cuts.\n\nIf the scene needs private consequence, write it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the active presentation, not as dialogue another character can hear. For example, a character may look at a ledger and privately recall the exact amount that was hidden, while the next line simply asks for coffee. That inner note belongs to the focal character only and changes how later scenes can be played.\n\nLet time move across scenes through familiar visible objects: the same chair, a repaired net, a photograph turned to the wall. The recognition scene should be staged as behavior—someone reads a letter out loud and then stops mid-sentence, or a group continues setting the table after a terrible fact has been confirmed. Aftermath happens in small practical actions, not a final explanatory monologue. End the screenplay when the loss has been made legible, not when every conversation has been finished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nUse a single or rotating community space—a kitchen, a workshop, a parish hall, a waiting room—where the tragic choice can be made and remembered in public. Let entrances and exits carry the weight of time: someone arrives with the news that was already expected; someone leaves before a request can be repeated. Stage directions should describe objects and blocking economically: a folded letter, a chair moved to face the door, a tool left on the table.\n\nWrite dialogue as public, practical speech. A character may say, \"Your father said he would register the land before the first rain,\" another may answer only by continuing to shell peas. Do not convert private thought into asides unless the aside has a clear dramatic purpose. When the focal character's private consequence is required, keep it as a nonspoken authoring note separated from the script, showing what they know but do not say.\n\nLet the irreversible decision be staged rather than described after the fact. The audience sees the character fold the letter and place it under a bowl. Later, someone asks whether the letter was sent; the bowl is lifted, the letter remains. Recognition can arrive through a repetition: the same line spoken in act one as a promise and in act three as a reminder of what cannot be undone.\n\nDo not force everyone into a single explanatory confession. Aftermath can be a meal prepared listlessly, a widow arranging chairs for a meeting no one attends, or a young person repeating a name the older generation has stopped speaking. Preserve required inward experience as hidden or nonspoken authoring material only when the active contract demands it. The play can end on a practical, unfinished action—a boat returned, a door left open—without a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nChoose one focal mind authorized for stream-of-consciousness access and let the tragic fall be seen through associations, interruptions, and recurring sensations. Begin each stretch from a concrete present perception—a wet sleeve, a door not closed, a radio playing in another room—then move into memory, overheard speech, bodily awareness, and back. Keep enough orientation that the reader can tell what is happening now against what is being remembered.\n\nBraid the irreversible choice into the mind's recurrences. The character may keep returning to the sound of the letter being folded, the weight of the key, the exact phrase another person used. A sentence can gather public consequence into private memory: the whole village is saying the boat will not return, and the sound of that sentence is mixed with the smell of kerosene and the fact of a debt never recorded.\n\nLet recognition arise gradually through the mind's own movement rather than a sudden complete explanation. A phrase heard in the market may connect to an old warning, then break off into a minor domestic task, then return later with a new meaning. Do not make the mind omniscient. Other characters' private thoughts remain outside this focal stream unless separately authorized.\n\nKeep the world socially real and historical. The stream of consciousness may include rumor, public speech, and practical community detail, but it should not invent literal magic unless the active world permits it. The aftermath can appear as the same street name now carrying a different weight, a child asking about someone who is no longer mentioned. End where the mind has registered the loss deeply enough for the consequence to be legible, not where all associations have been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story through clearly attributed messages, using timestamps, delays, read receipts, and an occasional unsent draft only when they change what the community can know. Let the tragedy accumulate in what is sent too late, what is sent too early, and what remains unsent. A person may type an apology, delete it, and send only a practical question about the boat; the deleted text can appear as a clearly marked unsent draft, not as a thought another character can see.\n\nUse recurring phrases across different senders to build communal memory: \"your father always said before the first rain,\" \"that land was never registered,\" \"the letter is still in the drawer.\" Each sender's messages should sound like their actual habits—short practical lines, long emotional blocks, or formal punctuation—without turning everyone into a polished narrator. Keep any context outside the transcript clearly separated from the messages themselves.\n\nLet recognition happen through the record. A character scrolls back and finds the warning they once ignored; a read receipt shows the message was seen before a deadline. Do not fabricate interface events just for subtext. The irreversible choice should be present in the exchange itself: a refusal typed and sent, or a silence that becomes its own response.\n\nPreserve private nonspoken interiority as a separate attributed note when the active contract requires it, not as a transmitted message. Aftermath can be a final group message met with silence, an old chat where one participant's name is no longer clicked, or a forwarded message that no one answers. End when the loss is legible through the digital record, not when every recipient has explained what they felt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nUse short scene-setting passages that show where the scene occurs and what has changed since the last visit. Pair dialogue with a focal narration line or inner response that tracks the consequence of an irreversible choice. For example, the scene note may say \"The same kitchen, the same bench, but the bowl of fruit has been moved to the window where the letter used to lie.\" Let that line carry García Márquez's layered time without magical events.\n\nKeep dialogue readable and character-specific. People speak about practical affairs—debts, arrivals, a promised registration—while the focal narration privately remembers an earlier warning. Another character does not hear that inner response; it belongs only to the focal mind. When a character's expression appears in a scene cue, use it to orient emotion, not as proof of honesty or guilt.\n\nMake scene changes legible and purposeful. A return to a harbor or a family table should reveal the aftermath through one changed concrete detail: a repaired boat, an empty chair, a photo turned down. Recognition can occur when the focal character finally connects an old piece of rumor to the physical fact now in front of them.\n\nDo not add branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine code just because this style is selected. The tragedy remains a deterministic arc; the reading experience focuses on consequence, not player choice. Preserve required nonspoken interiority as an active narrative note when the contract demands it. End the scene sequence when the loss has been made plain, not when every possible development has been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nKeep each installment anchored in a small set of recurring particulars: a debt ledger, a boat, a boundary stone, a letter. At the start of a new response, briefly recover only the detail that now matters, not the whole history. A later chapter may say, \"The ledger still lay where his father had left it, open at the same unpaid line,\" allowing new development without a summary of the entire past.\n\nLet ordinary community life continue around the accumulating mistake. A person buys fish, a neighbor complains about the road, a child asks why the boat is not out. Use these practical events to show the social pressure on the irreversible choice. Do not force every reply to end with a new reversal or emotional cliffhanger; a response can close after a task is done, a request remains unanswered, or a character simply confirms what has already been lost.\n\nWhen a remembered phrase returns, make its changed meaning clear through the actual situation. A warning spoken early as rumor may be repeated later in a different tone; the serial structure can show the community gradually changing how it speaks about the family, the debt, or the refusal. Preserve private inward experience as needed while recognizing that other characters do not automatically know it.\n\nThe aftermath may span several installments rather than one closing scene. Show the loss through details that persist: a meal made for one, a tool borrowed and never returned, a name used less often. Eventually the recognition becomes public enough for the fall to settle into history, but the serial can stop when that settlement is legible, not when every thread has been tied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the story as a narrowing of commitments. Establish early what the main figures owe one another, what they need, and what they cannot safely admit. Let their competence, tenderness, and ordinary pleasures remain visible, because the loss will be measured against what those things cost. A destructive choice should follow from a recognizable character under pressure—pride, love, duty, a mistaken belief, a promise already made—not from sudden stupidity. Keep warnings and alternatives available in the scene as concrete options someone can name, refuse, or misread. When recognition comes, let it change the meaning of what has already happened rather than magically reopening it. The aftermath should preserve the shape of what cannot be restored.\n\nCompress the telling into brief declarative moves. Let an exchange carry its own weight through what is said, what is left out, and what the body does; use distilled images and selective description instead of explaining every transition. A silence can be active: someone notices a hesitation and chooses not to ask, or answers a different question because the true one would expose too much. Keep uncertainty legitimate—characters may decline to explain, misread an implication, or act on an old understanding—but keep the causal stakes legible. Let an action, admission, or delayed recognition break the stillness of a scene; it need not be a twist, only a change in what the preceding quiet now means.\n\nTreat emotional exposure as costly and untidy. Show the strain of admitting need, withholding it, apologizing, asking to return, or deciding not to. Let that strain alter the actual conversation: a line may turn evasive, too precise, or too late, and the next practical choice should carry the consequence. Private thought and spoken words can diverge for a character-specific reason—embarrassment, protection, habit, anger, uncertainty—or they can align. Render the character's actual unsaid experience in the authorized mode; if the active contract hides it, keep the full private experience in the hidden-interiority block rather than a summary. Do not displace it into a letter, monologue, aside, or voice-over. Let warmth coexist with hurt: comfort can be awkward, partial, or refused without canceling the love that makes refusal hurt. Use the narrator's own distance and diction rather than generic suffering.\n\nOrder scenes so that emotional turbulence presses on the tragic arc. A withheld feeling should shape a practical decision—someone refuses help, returns to a place they swore off, tells a truth that is accurate but emotionally false, or lets an apology die where it could have landed. Later behavior should make that consequence legible without announcing a moral. Revisit earlier warnings, smaller sacrifices, and missed chances only when new pressure changes what they mean. A conversation can continue across the boundary; a task can remain unfinished; a quiet interval can hold its own duration. If tragedy arrives, it should arise from commitments already in play, not from a schedule of suffering.\n\nKeep the authorized narrator's access. You may reveal a focal motive while another character remains unaware, but do not give everyone perfect perceptiveness or erase knowledge boundaries. Avoid stock gestures of heartbreak; write this particular person's strain. A recurring phrase or image can return when it has new pressure, not as an echo.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the interval in short declarative paragraphs a reader can scan. Give each speaker a clear line and keep dialogue attribution unmistakable even when the exchange is three words long. Use one distilled image, gesture, or flat statement per beat; let the white space around a commitment or refusal carry what the character will not spell out. Say the loss once and trust the surrounding quiet to keep it present.\n\nMake the fall legible through an intelligent choice. The driving commitment—duty, love, pride, an old promise, a belief that has outlived its truth—should show in what is said and done now: an alternative offered plainly, a warning refused for reasons the character actually holds. Keep the person choosing wrong worthy of attention. Warmth may remain; one ordinary kindness beside the wrong choice can make the choice cost more rather than less.\n\nLet delayed revelation do the work of recognition. A small noticed thing—what a hand does, what is missing from the table, the line finally said after two evasions—can revalue the quiet that came before it. The revealed fact must follow from established choices and change what the loss means, not simply add surprise. It may also open the next possible move rather than close the scene.\n\nKeep the world active around grief. Address the player's submitted move according to the active authorship contract; let its consequence follow the scene's actual pace, and let NPCs continue their practical lives—a door shut, a debt paid, a third party arriving with an ordinary need—so the frame does not freeze on sorrow and the player has somewhere to act from. Not everyone withholds; a silence needs its own reason. Where private experience is rendered, keep it actual nonspoken interiority—attention, hesitation, motive—not an explained aside that replaces it.\n\nEnd at the boundary the scene itself reaches: mid-exchange, mid-task, in the pause after the plain sentence. Do not engineer a cliffhanger, question, or resolution. The fall may complete here or remain unfinished; one irreversible step taken plainly, with the world still turning around it, is a full interval of this tragedy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical/vernacular episodic route: treat the fall as linked episodes of told history, moving by brief declarative sentences and distilled images rather than by extended analysis. Anchor each episode in a concrete obligation, journey, trade, or domestic arrangement drawn from the active world. Let the telling hold consequences the participants do not yet know only where the active contract permits that wider knowledge; characters act from what they have acquired. Keep the language, period, and community of the active world distinct, and draw images from its materials rather than importing an imperial court, a jianghu, or generic historical China.\n\nLet Gu Long's white space carry the tragedy's incompatible commitments. A compact exchange can establish a fact while revealing that two people disagree about what it costs. What is left unsaid should matter: an omitted explanation, a silence, a gesture can show a commitment the speaker will not name. When a revelation or action changes the preceding stillness, let it recast an earlier scene or exchange rather than merely adding a shock. The recognition may stay unspoken—a character understands what an earlier choice made impossible. Some episodes can hold stillness and consequence without a reversal.\n\nOrganize the arc so irreversible choices accumulate meaning. Give the incompatible commitments concrete form—love against duty, pride against survival, a reasonable mistaken belief, an established fate, or a social condition that narrows what anyone can do—without reducing the characters to folly. Warnings and alternatives remain intelligible: someone can see the danger and still choose, or choose before seeing. The loss may be social, practical, intimate, or moral; the aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored. A repeated place, phrase, object, or gesture can return with changed significance, but it need not appear at every difficulty. Later episodes can reinterpret earlier ones without erasing what those earlier people knew at the time.\n\nKeep interiority active rather than decorative. Where the active contract permits access to a character's private experience, render actual thought, sensation, uncertainty, and reasoning in the narration—spare when the moment is spare, fuller when the moment needs it. If the contract uses hidden interiority, fill it with that character's actual nonspoken experience, not a summary or an in-world document. Other minds remain closed unless the contract allows access. A character may misread another's silence or decline to explain their own; let that be a motive, not a rule. Speech and thought may align or diverge for a character-specific reason.\n\nEnd episodes or sections according to the scene's own movement—during a continuing conversation, a walk, a wait, or an unfinished arrangement—rather than requiring a settled outcome, a confession, or a moral. The final recognition should change what the loss means, not tidy it away. Preserve the active output contract and the active world's facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nBuild the fall from short declarative units and let the white space carry weight. Make every omission load-bearing: a warning can be one plain sentence spoken early, an alternative a brief offer refused in fewer words. Let the causes — pride, love, duty, a belief held past its proof — live inside the silences rather than be explained around them. Afterward the whole path should be recoverable from what was on the page: the gaps were where the characters kept what they could not say, not where the story hid its working parts. What a person notices and declines to explain can carry the destruction; a character may see the true cost and go ahead anyway, so mistaken belief never has to mean stupidity, and the cast stays worth attention while their choices turn destructive.\n\nKeep the ledger of loss in material wear. Things and bodies record what no one says: a coat gone thin, tools sold, a room with more space in it than last year. Irreversibility reads in what will not mend. Keep the stakes concrete — blood, money, shelter, strength — and let one distilled image do the work a paragraph of grief would do. Work the rhythm: stack short sentences into stillness, compress the transitions between places and days, then break a long stretch of routine with a single blunt beat when the choice lands. Prefer the forceful verb and the concrete noun to the explanatory adjective.\n\nWhere the active contract gives you a character's interior, keep the unspoken real. A full private argument can run beneath three words of speech, and the private and the spoken can also match exactly; either way, give the gap, when there is one, a specific motive in that speaker — pride, protection, habit. Let a longer thought stand when the reasoning needs the room; compression is not a ban on thinking. Rough talk belongs to whoever talks that way; it is not a house voice for the whole cast.\n\nStage recognition as the revelation that breaks the stillness. Compress the scene before it into small quiet movements, then let one uncovered fact, one line, one act revalue everything quiet that preceded it. No spectacle is required; a familiar detail whose meaning has changed is enough. Keep the aftermath to a few lines — it can be someone doing a small ordinary thing with the loss settled into it, the unrestorable left unrestored, whatever warmth survives still real. The arc falls once; do not tax every scene on the way down with punishment, and add no duels, wine, or cryptic masters the story's own setting has not supplied.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBuild the fall out of paper under pressure. Each piece has a named writer, a recipient or use, and a date or circumstance that fixes when it could be written. Let the sequence be the story: a request, a ledger entry, a report, a reply, a page torn from a diary, a receipt, a deposition, a telegram. Use the form the moment demands, not a fixed rotation. The same event may appear twice with different wording because two writers had different access, loyalty, fear, or hope. Do not reconcile every difference; make the difference itself carry information.\n\nWrite in brief declarative movement. Short sentences. Sharp cuts between documents. A fact set down once and left without explanation. Distilled images: a name written and crossed out; a seal pressed twice; a date falling after a funeral; a corner torn away. An unfinished line, a missing signature, a reply that answers a different question. Delayed revelation should arrive through a later document or a material trace, not a narrator's summary after each entry. When the turn comes, it should change the stillness before it: loyalty becomes cowardice, cruelty becomes failed mercy, a small omission becomes the closed door.\n\nThe fall must be consequential and irreversibly chosen. Put incompatible commitments inside the documents themselves: love against duty, pride against need, public office against private loyalty, the need to protect someone against the need to tell the truth. Let warnings and alternatives be legible—someone writes the objection, records the safer path, refuses to help—so the loss is not caused by general foolishness. Recognition may reach a writer, a recipient, or a later reader assembling the papers; it should alter what the loss means, not merely name a culprit. Aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a trust, a future, a name, a life, the possibility of repair. Do not make every document grim; ordinary work, warmth, and affection belong in the bundle so the loss has weight.\n\nLet order create information flow. Chronological sequence can show a decision harden. A recovered bundle can place a late confession beside an early request. Conflicting accounts can stand if each writer's access and motive explain the difference. Recurring dates, names, phrases, or objects should anchor the reader and then change meaning through new context. Keep enough attribution and sequence that the reader can follow who wrote what, when, and to whom, while leaving the gaps that make the documents feel authored under pressure.\n\nBetween documents, render the viewpoint's actual nonspoken experience where it matters—sensation, hesitation, a thought not yet written down—and keep it in the story's established presentation. Hidden interiority uses the hidden form; visible interiority remains ordinary private prose. A letter, diary entry, or report is an artifact with an audience and a purpose; it is not the writer's complete live mind. Do not let a composed document replace private experience that the story needs.\n\nEnd where the current scene's response boundary falls—during a continuing correspondence, in the middle of a half-finished report, in a private interval, or in an action that has not yet produced its consequence. Do not force a confession, a reversal, or a completed resolution before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite a tragedy where warmth belongs to the fall rather than interrupting it. The comforts these people choose for each other—a familiar dish prepared without comment, a cup refilled at the right moment, a coat folded and set where it will be found—should be specific, practical, and charged with who they are to one another. Let affection be sincere without demanding a confession or a repair. Keep privacy, disagreement, teasing, and silence available. Comfort can be imperfect and manageable; it does not make everyone agreeable, and it does not remove the world's established limits.\n\nMove by brief declarative sentences, selected sensory facts, and gaps that carry implication. A hand set down, a bowl refilled, a sentence stopped before its end: let such details do more than explanation. Use compact exchanges, and let what a person notices or declines to explain carry uncertainty. Delay a revelation until it can alter the meaning of the preceding stillness. The revelation may arrive as a plain fact, a gesture, or a choice already made. The kindness may remain sincere even when it cannot prevent the loss; what changes is what the kindness cost, what it could not fix, or which commitment it could not answer.\n\nLet the fall arise from incompatible commitments, irreversible choices, love, duty, pride, mistaken belief, social conditions, or a fate already set in motion. Make warnings and alternatives intelligible: let the reader see what could have been chosen and why it is not. Keep everyone intelligent and worthy of attention. They may choose wrongly without becoming foolish; they may love truly without that love being enough. When recognition comes, let it reorganize the earlier warmth rather than merely explain it. A character may understand too late, in part, or only after the loss has become irreversible.\n\nUse Gu Long's white space for what cannot be said. A short exchange can carry more than a full explanation. A repeated object or gesture can return with altered meaning. Delay what a person knows or will admit, but keep the omission particular—shame, tenderness, duty, exhaustion, a promise—rather than making everyone perpetually cryptic for atmosphere. When the revelation lands, let it do something in the scene: close a door, confirm a loss, expose an incompatibility, or make a comfort impossible to accept. It need not supply a moral or a punishment.\n\nIn the aftermath, preserve both what cannot be restored and what care remains. The story need not end in reconciliation or despair. A familiar kindness can continue after the loss, diminished or changed; a character can notice something small and comforting while the larger harm stands. Let the final movement leave the irreversibility intact. The comfort is not a solution to the tragedy; it is one of the things the tragedy costs, saves, or leaves behind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the story as composed novel prose, with sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions that place a character's fall inside a social world. Let the reader understand the commitments that make the choice consequential—duty, loyalty, love, pride, public role, history, or an established fate—and let the alternatives remain intelligible. The character should not be made foolish in order to fail; they may weigh what they know, misread one pressure, or choose with full awareness, and the loss should matter because their intelligence and warmth remain visible.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement within those broader paragraphs. Let a gesture, an object, a doorway, a silence, or a change in routine carry implication without immediate explanation. A character may notice what another misses and decline to explain it; the narration can leave that uncertainty active while the social context continues to accumulate. Compact exchanges should turn on what is withheld as much as on what is said, and a delayed revelation—a recognition of what a commitment actually required, what a choice cost, or what another person understood—should change the meaning of the stillness that preceded it.\n\nThe formal novel's public/private contrast can organize the fall: ceremony, correspondence, social ritual, or official record may frame a passage and expose the distance between a public role and a private commitment. Let that distance become the tragic pressure rather than a puzzle to solve. When the narrator has access, render the character's private experience directly—attention, uncertainty, memory, the reasoning that connects a commitment to a choice—rather than replacing it with correspondence, public record, or a summary of what was felt. In the aftermath, preserve what cannot be restored through changed routine, social judgment, or a silence that now means something different; do not force a moral lesson or a resolution. Keep narration distinct from ordinary speech, let dialogue stay sharp when the moment calls for it, and do not add martial codes, wine, blades, or a cryptic cast unless the story's world already contains them. The response may end during continuing speech, action, thought, or an unfinished setup, with the tragedy's pressure still active.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-tragedy-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Tragedy · 03/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == tragedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Tragedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-tragedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaqaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin with the outer scene as an already-charged occasion. A specific teller speaks to specific listeners because the embedded story's events have already reached into the present: a debt is being called in, a departure is being negotiated, a name is about to be cleared or buried, a decision cannot be made until the account is heard. Establish the frame in a few spare strokes—who is present, what hour it is, what object or obligation has gathered them—and let the telling begin without a preamble explaining its purpose.\n\nTell the embedded story through the teller's selective attention. Write it in brief declarative units, each paragraph anchored to a concrete action or observation: a cup set down, a door left open, a sentence repeated with one word changed, a journey compressed to a single line. Where the teller skips over something, make the skip visible as a gap in the account—a transition that jumps too quickly, a name replaced by 'the other one,' a pause in which the teller looks away. Do not state what has been omitted. The gap itself is the Gu Long white space, and it must be precise enough that a listener could later return to it.\n\nMake the embedded story contain an irreversible act whose full cost the teller does not yet state: a promise kept past the point of return, a refusal that closed a road, a choice between two people who both mattered. Show the act once, cleanly, without commentary. Then let the frame interrupt at the point where the teller's account becomes difficult. A listener asks a question the teller does not answer directly. Someone repeats a phrase from the tale and the teller corrects it with a small, revealing difference. The outer scene is not a neutral container; it is the place where the telling is being tested.\n\nBuild the recognition across the two levels. When an outer listener supplies a detail the teller had omitted—'You left before the letter came,' 'She was the one who paid,' 'He asked for you by name'—let that sentence re-read the embedded act without explaining it. The teller's silence or single confirming line then becomes the recognition beat. The tragedy lands not as a new fact but as the visible completion of a gap the reader has already felt. The teller's earlier omission is revealed as the mechanism by which the loss was carried, not a trick of the frame.\n\nAfter the recognition, return to the outer scene and let the telling's purpose collapse or change. A request is withdrawn. A listener leaves. The teller finishes the story anyway, or stops before the end. The loss remains irreversible, but the audience's relation to the teller and to the act has altered. End on a small outer-world continuation—a lamp lit, a door closed, a name spoken once more—that preserves the aftermath without repairing it. Use recurrence sparingly: one phrase or object may cross the levels twice, its meaning changed by the recognition, but do not make repetition a signal of truth.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social or developmental-realist route. Anchor the scene in the specific supplied time and place through its working procedures and material arrangements: the hours of a particular office, the sequence for submitting a form, the cost of a fare, the layout of a flat, the schedule of a shift. Do not explain these conditions. Let them appear as the characters move through them—a key turned in a lock, a signature placed on the wrong line, a letter opened after the deadline has passed, a chair left empty at a table set for one more.\n\nKeep the syntax plain and declarative. Use short paragraphs, each built around a single action, observation, or line of speech. Let the white space fall after the details that matter: a door that does not close properly, a coat hung on the wrong hook, a sum of money counted twice. Do not let the narrator step back to summarize the era, the institution, or the character's feelings. The pressure of the social world must be legible through what the characters do and decline to explain.\n\nMake the tragic alternative intelligible through concrete objects that remain present but unused. A return ticket stays in a drawer. A form is filled out but never submitted. A message is written and not sent. A chair is kept for someone who no longer comes. These objects are the warnings and alternatives the tragedy requires. Show them once, briefly, without commentary, so that the reader can later recognize what their presence meant.\n\nBuild the recognition as a delayed disclosure that re-reads one of these objects or an earlier sentence. The disclosure may arrive through a short spoken line, a document remembered, or a fact the character now confirms to themselves. It must not introduce a new twist. Its work is to make visible what a previous silence or unused object already contained: the person had not forgotten; they had chosen, or been made to choose, and the cost was already fixed. Write the disclosure in a short, almost formal sentence, then return immediately to the spare register. Do not decorate the moment with metaphor.\n\nAfter the recognition, do not repair the loss. Return to the ordinary social scene and let the character continue within it: paying a bill, closing a window, walking to work, answering a neighbor's harmless question. The aftermath is a continuation of the same world, now carrying the weight of what cannot be restored. End on a small material detail that belongs to that world—a light switched off, a tram passing, a key returned to its hook—without turning it into a symbol. The loss remains a hard fact inside the ordinary day.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite from the immediate collision of old, installed authority and the messy present of an actual city. Do not announce the authority in exposition. Let it appear through material traces the focal character registers without understanding: a building's old nameplate, a door that still closes too heavily, a smell of damp plaster, a modern leasing office occupying a former chapel, a streetlight that fails in exactly the same place it did decades before. Keep each trace to one brief observation, never a historical explanation.\n\nStay close to one focal character and enforce that limit through the narration itself. Write only what that character sees, hears, touches, and remembers. Do not narrate the other person's mind. When the other person's meaning is withheld, let it be felt through absence and delay: a hand that withdraws, a coat hung behind a door, a cup set down without being drunk, a photograph turned toward the wall. These gestures may mean different things at different times; do not fix their meaning when they first appear.\n\nEmbed the older command in the material setting before it is understood. A monogrammed lighter, a set of house keys, a chair kept for someone no longer allowed inside, a name written in a ledger—let the focal character notice one or two of these objects without grasping their connection. The object is the Gu Long white space: it holds the withheld fact, and it must be precise enough that the later recognition can return to it.\n\nConstruct the tragic fall as a delayed recognition about an act that has already taken place. The act may be an unreturned message, a signature on a lease, a locked door between two people, or a promise made under a demand that seemed ordinary. Show the act once, cleanly, without commentary. Then let the recognition arrive when the focal character suddenly understands why a particular object, phrase, or absence was significant. The understanding should re-read the earlier trace, not introduce a new fact. Write the recognition in a short, flat sentence or image, then let the prose go still.\n\nAfter the recognition, do not dissolve into explicit weeping or a sudden escape. Show a single aftermath that honors the irreversibility: the focal character does not call, does not run, returns to work, or remains in the room where the disclosure was made. Let the city's decay continue around them—a bus passing, a light going off—not as a symbol but as the same world now recognized for what it has always been. The loss is neither repaired nor framed as noble; it is simply settled into the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral and frame-tale route. Anchor the outer scene in the specific supplied time, place, and community: a courtyard, a verandah at dusk, a crowded evening bus stand, a family gathering after work, a storytelling session in a small room. Give the telling an explicitly social purpose that is already active before the tale begins—a decision is being delayed, a reputation is being protected, a younger person is being warned, a debt is being justified. The listeners are not a faceless audience. Each carries a relation to the people inside the story, and at least one listener knows something the teller has not yet said.\n\nWrite the teller's voice in brief declarative units. Compress the inner story into short episodes, each anchored to a physical action or a single line of speech: one sentence for a journey, one for a refusal, one for the arrival of bad news. Where the teller skips over something, make the skip visible—a transition that jumps too quickly, a name replaced by 'the other one,' a pause in which the teller looks away. Do not state what has been omitted. The gap is the Gu Long white space, and it must be precise enough that a listener could later return to it.\n\nReturn to the frame between episodes. After each short movement of the inner tale, show a listener asking a question, a child being sent away, a utensil being set down, or the teller pausing. Let the pause carry the withheld material without naming it. The listeners' reactions—a wince, a folded hand, a repeated word—become part of the outer frame and begin to test the teller's account. Do not explain what the listeners suspect. Let their questions and silences do the work.\n\nMake the teller's own implication the withheld material. The teller is not omniscient and not innocent. The inner story may be a tale from the neighborhood or family history, or a story the community already half knows, but its tragic core must include something the teller did or failed to do: a name that was changed, a promise made to someone who was not present, a journey that was never completed, a message that was not passed on. Do not state this implication when the tale begins. Let it sit inside the gaps the teller leaves.\n\nAt the recognition point, have the teller disclose only a few concrete details—not a moral summary—that alter the meaning of a prior episode. Or let an outer listener supply the missing detail: 'You left before the letter came,' 'She was the one who paid,' 'He asked for you by name.' The disclosure should connect to the outer community's social reality: an elder's command, a marriage arrangement, a debt, a migration, a land decision, a dispute over inheritance, a failure to reach someone in time. These facts must be specific to the supplied context, not imported. The recognition lands when the teller's silence or single confirming line reveals that the teller was inside the loss all along.\n\nAfter the disclosure, let the outer frame carry the tragedy without repairing it. The listeners may sit in silence, one may leave, someone may begin to ask a question and then stop. Show the continuing practical life—a light turned on, a bus arriving, plates being cleared—not as consolation but as the undoing of what can now never be restored. The loss is not made bearable; it is made present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaial::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the route of modern Japanese social or psychological realism with a strong inward register. Write from one character's close perspective, whether first person or limited third, and restrict the narration to what that character physically registers: the exact way a door slides, the sound of a kettle, a hand holding a train strap, the weight of an unopened letter, the particular light on a familiar street. Keep each observation brief and physical. Let the prose pause after it, rather than leaping to interpretation.\n\nDo not treat the inwardness as an invitation to abstractly describe feelings. Let the tragedy accumulate through concrete, personal actions and their surrounding silences: a phone not answered, a train missed while remaining on the platform, a phrase spoken too quietly to be heard, a gift given after its usefulness has passed. Use Gu Long's compression: shorter sentences for the most significant details; rare, almost flat statements for what the character thinks; no decorative reflection at the moment of loss.\n\nPlant the recognition in an earlier object or gesture. Before the fall, write one concrete detail that the character notices without understanding: a letter left unopened, a door that does not close, a phrase repeated with a slight difference, a particular object set down in a particular place. Do not explain its meaning when it first appears. The object, not the narrator, holds the secret. The Gu Long white space is this gap between what the character registers and what the character understands.\n\nMake the delayed recognition internal but not solipsistic. The character should understand too late what the earlier gesture or decision meant. That understanding may come while doing something ordinary—tying shoes, waiting for a signal, staring at a calendar, opening a window—and it should arrive as a single sentence or image that returns to the planted detail and re-reads it. The earlier scene must already have been written with enough concrete observation for the later recognition to feel earned. Write the recognition in a short, flat statement, then let the prose go still.\n\nThe loss should be personal and specific: a failed communication, a wish not acted upon, a promise unspoken, someone left behind, a job refused, a check sent too late. Keep it rooted in the supplied local world. Do not force a universal Japanese setting or seasonal imagery; use only what is already present in the story. If the assignment includes a particular city, neighborhood, workplace, or family arrangement, let those facts order the details.\n\nAfter the recognition, remain inside the same inward voice and let the aftermath be small and ordinary. The character may continue the day, return home, prepare food for one, or simply stand still. Let the physical world persist without being turned into a symbol of grief. A sentence may return to an earlier object or sound—the door, the kettle, the unsent letter—but now it is seen with the new understanding. The tragedy is complete not when the character breaks down, but when this quotidian detail shows that the loss is already past and cannot be called back.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological or urban fiction route. Write from the close perspective of one central character within the specific Latin American city or town the assignment supplies. Do not let the city become a colorful backdrop. Use its exact textures as part of the protagonist's mental environment: the color of a bus billboard, the smell of frying food from a particular stall, the sound of a neighbor's radio through a wall, the way a hallway stays cool in the afternoon. Keep these observations short and physical, one per paragraph, so the scene feels inhabited rather than described.\n\nLet the narrator's selective attention do the work of implication. Do not narrate historical or social information as exposition. Instead, let community pressure appear in small, concrete exchanges: a cousin's question about remittances, a shopkeeper's hesitation, a landlord's knock, a conversation cut off when someone enters. Use Gu Long's method to make these encounters brittle and fast—a few lines of dialogue, a single observed gesture, then a break. The white space after the exchange carries the pressure that is not spoken.\n\nPlant the tragic act in memory before it is recognized. The story can move between the present scene and brief, sharply drawn moments of memory: a phone call from a migrant sibling, a wedding not attended, a debt paid by someone else, a form signed without reading it. Do not connect the memories with explicit commentary. Let each memory return because a present detail summons it: the same brand of tonic water, a song, a shirt draped over a chair, a particular doorway. The trigger is the Gu Long white space—a concrete detail that holds the withheld meaning without stating it.\n\nBuild the delayed recognition as a pattern the protagonist suddenly sees in those memories. It may be triggered by a simple sentence spoken by someone else, but the sentence must not be melodramatic. Its power comes from the quiet accumulation of small details the reader has already seen. When the recognition arrives, let it re-read the earlier memories in a single short statement or image. Do not introduce a new fact at this point. The recognition should make visible what the memories already contained: the protagonist had been inside the loss all along, and the alternative had been present but ungrasped.\n\nDo not import a magical event unless the assignment's world already includes it. The tragedy is already supernatural in its invisibility: time, distance, migration, unpaid debt, the ordinary way a family stops speaking. After the recognition, show a brief aftermath that keeps the ordinary world present. The protagonist may finish a transaction, return home before dark, or stand at a window while the city continues. There is no reconciliation scene forced upon the reader; the loss settles into the unchanged street, but the protagonist now knows what it cost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaian::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nOpen on a single immediate action, spoken line, or observed detail that anchors the scene: a station clock reading a specific time, a set of keys placed on a counter, a corridor turning left before the vending machines, a phone buzzing on a table. Keep the opening short and uninterrupted. Do not begin with a long atmospheric passage. Each paragraph must have a clear anchor—a place, a movement, a speaker, or a reaction—so spatial changes are easy to follow.\n\nUse short action beats and sparse dialogue, not extended argumentative speeches. Let exchanges be interrupted by concrete details: a phone buzzing, a plastic cup tipping, a chair scraping. Use the paragraph break as the Gu Long white space. After a significant line or observation, break the paragraph and let the pause carry the weight that is not spoken. The prose should let the reader feel the pause without explaining it.\n\nShow the irreversible choice through concrete facts, not through narration naming it as irreversible. The choice may be sending a message, missing a train, not saying a name, handing over a bag, or closing a door. Show one or two key moments in which the choice is made, then let the facts themselves demonstrate that it cannot be undone: the last bus has left, the phone is dead, the door locks from the outside. Do not add commentary. The irreversibility must be legible in the world's physical conditions.\n\nUse private reaction beats to build the delayed recognition. The focal character can notice a detail or feel a physical sensation—a dry mouth, a cold hand, the sound of their own breathing. These beats should not explain the tragedy; they should hold it in suspension until the recognition arrives. Plant the recognition in a detail already introduced: an unsent text, a ticket not used, an object left behind. The recognition itself can be a short line or image that returns to that detail and re-reads it. Because Gu Long's style is spare, the recognition should land on what the reader has already seen. Avoid a new twist at the last second; the reader should feel the weight of what has already happened rather than the shock of a cheat.\n\nDo not impose a closing cliffhanger or a comforting resolution. The scene may end in mid-gesture, in silence, or in the ordinary continuation of the world. The ending should preserve the loss without making it feel like an episode designed for the next installment. The final paragraph may show a small action—someone picks up a bag, the light changes, a door closes—that leaves the tragedy intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild each scene around a small set of exact images that the viewpoint's attention returns to before the loss is known. Let the prose move by association among these images, so that a gesture, a stopped motion, or the way a familiar place is re-seen gathers emotional weight without announcing its future meaning. A recurring sound or phrase may thread through the paragraph, but it should change what the narrator understands when it returns rather than decorate the surface.\n\nDelay the decisive fact until the moment when recognition can alter the preceding stillness. Before that moment, keep the attention local and sensory; choose images the reader can later re-interpret. At the boundary, slow the sentence with an unspoken understanding: the narrator may notice an unaltered detail that does not know what has happened, or an ordinary action that must continue after irreparable damage. After the boundary, let the same image appear once more in a plain declarative sentence that cuts against the rhythm and carries the fact the character has just understood.\n\nPreserve the tragic arc rather than punishing every scene. Earlier warmth or hope can be present so that its destruction has weight. The ending may leave the aftermath open, with the loss recognized but the daily business not yet fully resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a small, apparently ordinary detail from an earlier occasion persist into the present as the place where an irreversible loss is discovered. When the scene returns to that detail, show that its meaning has changed because the character now knows the relationship, plan, or possibility attached to it is gone. A familiar phrase, an object moved from its old position, or an unfinished domestic action can carry this later significance through a brief, delayed disclosure rather than an explanation at the time of first appearance.\n\nKeep the narrator's attention on the coexistence of present experience with what is no longer available. A present pleasure can still be enjoyed, but the reflection may connect it to an earlier day in such a way that the warmth becomes a reminder rather than a simple comfort. Write the reflection briefly and concretely; let the detail carry the association instead of announcing impermanence. The return to the detail should feel like a recognition, not a melancholy summary.\n\nOrder the genre's arc through this reflective movement. At first the detail is noticed without understanding; its warning may remain intelligible to the reader while the character misses or underweights it. Near the center or aftermath, when recognition comes, allow the loss to be named by the object or the memory itself rather than by a catalog of sorrows. Preserve the social or personal consequence of the tragedy; a reflective passage may record that someone loved has already changed, but the plot must still show what choice or event made the loss irremediable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral telling without importing the Thousand and One Nights cast or cycle: one person is recounting a smaller story to a listener, and the circumstance of the telling matters. The frame should be a real situation, not a decorative prologue. The teller may be awaiting the end of a marriage negotiation, sitting with someone during a final illness, or pausing in the aftermath of a practical failure, but the frame grows from the current story world's people and obligations.\n\nLet the inner tale carry most of the scene work in Gu Long's brief units: sharp exchanges, distilled objects, and short declarative passages. The teller should not explain the tragedy too soon. Between segments, return to the frame to let an expression, a break in speech, or what the teller will not say stand in for the gap. The outer listener's questions may be answered only partially, so that the full recognition arrives near the end and changes both the inner event and the outer relationship.\n\nRun the tragic arc through both levels. The listener is not merely receiving information; the act of telling has consequences. The teller may be seeking reconciliation, warning, permission, or help, and the listener's part in the lived story may be one of the causes of the loss. Keep the frame scene small and social rather than mythic. A final short exchange can leave the listener aware of what cannot be repaired. Maintain a specific community and setting with its own speech commitments drawn from the active world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nKeep the scene inside a very small number of exact objects and direct clauses. Let one item or physical fact occur early without explanation, while the character still has a chance to act differently. Later, when the choice or consequence has closed the alternative, return to the item in no more than two or three sentences. The return should not explain the loss; it should show that the item now occupies the character's attention in a different way. A door they opened quickly before may be looked at length; a coin they spent without thought may be counted again; a note they did not read may be handled once more.\n\nWrite dialogue with the same economy as the action. Speakers can leave substantial gaps. Keep only the inward connection that makes the turn intelligible, so that the minimal narration still retains enough cause for the loss to be understood. If a relationship is lost, show the earlier occasion when help or honesty was refused or misunderstood. The refusal may be one brief exchange. The narrative can then cut to the aftermath, letting the reader infer the intervening events from the changed behavior or from one newly named fact.\n\nWrite the recognition in a short declarative sentence, then let the scene continue with ordinary business rather than converting it into an extended lament. Another character may ask a practical question; the viewpoint may answer. The tragic condition remains in what cannot now be changed, not in a duty to announce despair at the end of every reply. Preserve character individuality in dialogue; clear, economical speech does not mean every person talks with identical curtness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaias::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the tragic situation as a continuously narrated chain of present scenes in which the decisive explanation arrives later than it would in a conventional summary. Use the scene-first movement of the modern novel for ordinary practical texture: a meal being prepared, a route walked, a phone call interrupted, an argument over a small sum. One such ordinary action should contain the seed of the later loss, but do not explain its future weight at once. The character's inward life may briefly touch on a worry or memory, then return to the business of the scene.\n\nWhen the tragedy reaches the point of recognition, let a one-sentence declaration, an object noticed, or a piece of information that had been kept from the viewpoint for a story-supported reason be the reveal. The character may continue speaking or moving afterward, and the perspective may revisit the earlier seed now seen, but the recognition need not stop the practical scene entirely.\n\nUse the contemporary novel's flexibility to vary the distance between action and inward record without overt recap machinery. A later scene can pick up a small item again, but the narrator should not announce that this is the meaning of the whole story. Keep both the fall and the alternatives intelligible; other characters may have warned against the choice, or the viewpoint may have understood the cost while still refusing the better path. The narrator may know only what the active perspective contract permits. Let the ending suggest how the loss reshapes the continuing life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nTake the established relationship premise from the current story; the tragedy grows from the pair's own history and commitments. Choose one exact repeated moment—a phrase between them, a way one waits, a joke, a physical routine—and use it across scenes. The repetition should become the carrier of the loss rather than a decoration. At first it may be warm or ordinary. Later the viewpoint can recognize that the same phrase or habit was also the place where one of them stopped telling the truth, or where the other accepted silence too easily.\n\nKeep scenes intimate and selected rather than summarised. Allow affection, jealousy, comfort, or discovery enough space when it belongs to the developed relationship. Do not require a confession or scene-ending crisis in every reply. Inside dialogue, a very short answer, an explanation deliberately not given, or a domestic object no longer touched can stand for the larger failure. The viewpoint may have private thoughts and feelings that the other character does not yet know, but the reader should eventually understand why the loss occurred.\n\nLet the tragic turn have a relationship-specific mechanism. It can come from incompatible obligations, a wounded trust, protective secrecy that becomes destructive over time, or a loyalty that made one of them choose a third party or public role. Make the alternatives intelligible; the better path should remain genuinely available so that the choice to refuse it carries weight. When recognition arrives, focus the passage on the altered meaning of the private language: the old routine may still be performed, but it now marks an injury rather than safety. The final response can leave actual contact changed or stopped without announcing that all future recovery is impossible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nShape each tragic passage as part of a public chronicle that remembers acts by their consequences rather than by psychological commentary. Begin with a plain formulaic opening—an identifying phrase, an account of whose kinsman did what, or a short declaration of a deed's result. Return to a name or recurrent phrase when later action gives it new application; the recurrence should come from an established deed or way of speaking rather than an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Keep the actual community setting distinct, drawing the chronicle's materials from the active world's people and obligations.\n\nWithin the public retelling, hold back one material act, debt, or private explanation until a later part of the account, but make the withholding natural to the teller's position rather than a narrative trick of the writing model. The teller may know the fact from a witness, a surviving participant, or a later discovery. The teller may also be the one who refuses to name the decisive deed until the recounting has made its cost visible. When the withheld fact is at last stated, it should change how the audience hears the earlier repeated phrases: a famous generosity now seen as a bribe, a celebrated patience now seen as waiting for revenge, or a kinship claim now known to have ended in a private breach.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the active POV contract. In oral saga presentation, first person need not mean the teller personally witnessed every scene; the audience can be told which parts are report, which are known by later evidence, and which the teller will not certify. Use this license sparingly, staying within the supplied world's facts. The tragic arc must remain an event: someone has lost a position, child, spouse, ability to return, or lawful standing, and the community now lives with the result. End with a concise conclusion that may look forward to another future act without forcing a complete resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the fall through social and domestic realism, grounded in the concrete community the current story supplies—its work, housing, family obligations, local institutions, and propriety. Where the setting is Nordic, keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct and continuous: a Danish parish, a Norwegian coastal town, and a Swedish industrial suburb are different places with different institutions, not a generic North. Let those ordinary structures make the irreversible choice intelligible without collapsing into a single blaming cause.\n\nAdopt Gu Long's spare movement inside that realism. Prefer short declarative sentences, white space around an observed action or a refusal to speak, and compact exchanges in which the withheld sentence does part of the work. When a character notices a detail, settle it plainly in the scene: a chair left at the wrong table, a letter opened twice, a name read from a parish register. Do not explain the detail at once; a later scene returns to it, and the return is where an earlier warning becomes visible.\n\nKeep the alternatives practical and intelligible—the other job, the untaken train, the apology not made—so people remain intelligent and worthy of attention even while choosing badly. The first, quieter revelation may be incomplete; a second return to the same object or routine can complete the tragic understanding without new ruin arriving to force it. Render aftermath through plain daily alteration: the changed schedule, the unanswered telephone, the withdrawn application, the person who now eats alone.\n\nTreat domestic objects as themselves, not standing symbols. Weather and landscape are local facts, not a universal mood; a room can be warm and noisy before the loss, and the snow need not fall. Keep private thought in the selected visible or hidden interiority channel, unknown to the other characters. A silence is a named person declining to explain, not a general air of mystery.\n\nWhen recognition comes, the scene can stop on the ordinary thing that can no longer be restored, and the aftermath may take a further scene; a reply may equally stop short of it, inside the daily scene that is carrying the change. No confession, punishment, or explanation to the community is required to close it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite each scene as a continuous addressable space, with a scene heading whenever location or time changes. Keep action lines brief and sensory: what is visible or audible in the scene, what the other characters can see or hear, which object moves from one hand to another. No shot sizes, camera movements, or editing vocabulary. Use a cut only when it replaces something that would otherwise be spoken.\n\nBuild the tragic recognition on Gu Long's compact recurring images. Introduce an object plainly—a folder, a wedding photograph, a child's shoe by the door—and let its second appearance deliver the fact that was withheld. The action line names the object without interpreting it: \"She sets the key on the table.\" A later scene may return to the same table, the key still there. Do not have a character explain the meaning aloud unless the relationship makes that speech natural.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue short and weighted, but not uniformly cryptic. Let a speaker refuse to answer for a particular reason: the answer would make the loss real, the other person already knows, speaking would commit them to a course. Use offscreen sound sparingly and only where it carries the beat—a bus arriving, a door closing in another room, a phone ringing past the point of rescue. Do not use voice-over for a private realization unless the active presentation grants it; the final understanding can live in a visible return to the earlier object or in a silent action.\n\nPrivate interiority, where required, is a clearly attributed nonspoken note set apart from dialogue and unknown to the characters in the scene. Give the note the thought the actor plays—\"She knows he will not come,\" \"For the first time he understands the debt is his\"—while the spoken layer stays faithful to what each person would say publicly.\n\nDo not default to a confession to resolve the fall. The recognition can be a character seeing an ordinary thing differently, and the script may cut on that: the bread left to burn, the name crossed out in the book, the empty seat. Aftermath, when it comes, is a shorter new scene showing the daily alteration. A reply may also stop inside the movement—mid-action, mid-silence—before the recognition is complete. Preserve the active authorship contract and world facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nKeep every scene playable. Name who enters and exits, where they sit or stand in relation to a door, a table, a window, and which object they handle. Let the relationship between the spoken line and the physical business carry the public scene. Make the irreversible choice evident in stage action: she burns the letter in the ashtray, he returns the house key and leaves it on the mat, two people stand on opposite sides of the same doorway.\n\nUse Gu Long's brevity in the spoken text. Sentences are short, interrupted, or completed by an action instead of another clause. Mark a pause where a character wants to speak and decides not to, or waits to see whether the other will cross the room; an unmarked generic beat is weaker than this specific want. The withheld line matters as much as the spoken one. Let one character's repeated statement become a motif—\"I am going to the station\"—while the other's failure to answer changes what the first means.\n\nDelay recognition through visibility rather than hidden knowledge. One person can see what another cannot because of the set, and the audience may see both: one exits with the wrong suitcase while the other sees the ticket left behind. Let the discovery arrive through an object or a position onstage—the chair that remains empty, the door that is not locked, the glass that stays full—and let its consequence be serious rather than comic.\n\nMonologues and asides are available when the form and the active contract grant them, with a performative purpose: an address, a prayer, a public declaration. They are not the destination for private thought. Keep required private experience as nonspoken authoring material—a bracketed note such as \"She realizes the loan was never approved,\" placed where the actor can use it and no in-world hearer receives it—and keep it distinct from a true aside.\n\nNo film-camera instructions. An exit is an exit; a blackout ends a scene, it is not an edit. When recognition arrives, the scene can stop on its immediate stage consequence—a held look, a dropped object, a line that changes what all the previous lines meant—or the reply may end mid-business before the discovery completes. The aftermath can be a later scene showing the daily order undone. Preserve the active authorship contract and world facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from a single authorized mind, in the active person and perspective, and let that mind's own associations structure the telling. Braid present perception, memory, bodily sensation, interrupted speech, private words, and the recurring concern the character cannot put down. A present sound—rain on a window, a kettle, a step on gravel—can pull a memory forward, but keep enough orientation that the reader can distinguish what is happening, what is remembered, and what is only imagined.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed recognition as an inward movement. The mind returns to the same distilled image several times: the blue mug, the open gate, the sentence someone did not finish. At first the image carries one meaning; after a revealed fact or a changed circumstance, it returns carrying another. That return is the recognition—not a new thought retroactively inserted into the earlier scene. Preserve the original memory truthfully; do not rewrite it to manufacture self-deception.\n\nLet syntax follow attention. Short declarative perceptions alternate with longer recollections. Under pressure, a sentence can break where speech would break: \"He said he would be back. The clock. He said—\" This is Gu Long's white space placed inside the mental current. Use meaningful gaps and a refusal to finish certain thoughts, without making the whole text fragmentary or cryptic.\n\nAttribute vivid memory and fantasy clearly, by context or phrasing, rather than blending them into the present as fact. The character may confuse a memory with a current event for a moment if the text shows why—fatigue, shock, resemblance—but the orientation returns. A repeated private word stays private; the people in the remembered scene do not hear the present mind.\n\nKeep the fall consequential and irreversible. The mind can argue with itself, revisit warnings, imagine alternatives—the untaken road, the unsent letter—and recognition arrives when the character understands what cannot be restored. Aftermath may appear as mundane routine now altered: missing the bus, setting one plate, a familiar smell tied to absence. The reply can end where the mind reaches that understanding, turns toward the first changed task, or is still moving between them; no summary or moral is wanted.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in sender-attributed messages plus only the interface events that matter for this exchange. Use timestamps, read receipts, delays, edits, attachments, or reactions when they change what the reader understands, and invent none for subtext. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes complete sentences and waits, another sends fragments, a third stops typing at the point of decision.\n\nTreat every sent text as a public artifact within the exchange, and keep it distinct from both the unsent draft and private thought. A draft is a composed artifact—the sentence the character actually typed and did not send—and appears only when the contract permits that material, clearly separated from transmitted text. The other person never receives it. A character may type, delete, and send something safer; the deleted sentence remains part of what they know and may matter when they reread the exchange later.\n\nUse Gu Long's brevity in the message text: short, weighted, sometimes ambiguous lines. Here his white space is literal—the time between messages. A two-hour gap, a read receipt with no reply, a typing indicator that stops. Make each significant gap arise from a named reason—anger, a train with no signal, a decision to wait, a broken phone—so the delay becomes part of the irreversible choice rather than silence for atmosphere. Let one message change the meaning of all earlier ones: \"I'll be there\" reads as a different promise once the ticket attachment shows the wrong date.\n\nRecognition can happen when a character rereads the transcript. A clearly separated context line or short prose block outside the exchange may frame it—\"She scrolled up and read his first message again\"—after which the same text reappears with a changed implication. The character may notice a missed timestamp, a word, an unanswered question, or simply understand that the other person had already decided before replying. The other person does not announce the realization unless the exchange naturally produces it.\n\nPrivate thought stays out of the transmitted text. \"He meant the other one\" is a clearly attributed nonspoken note, not a sent message, and the style grants no mind-reading interface. A reply may end on the revealed message, the unsent reply still being composed, or the first message after the realization; the other person need not respond and the practical situation need not resolve first.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the story in short scenes: concise scene-setting, clear speaker attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Mark changes of location or time legibly, but do not append a background or expression cue to every line. Narrative passages carry the viewpoint character's observation and inner response, and the other characters do not receive them.\n\nLet a background object serve as Gu Long's distilled image. Introduce it plainly—a notice on a family board, a red bicycle outside the library, a chair in a hospital corridor—and show the focal character noticing it without explanation. Later, return to the same image, or nearly the same, so it releases what was withheld. That second presentation is the recognition. Give the detail a concrete connection to the tragic event—a missed date, a changed photograph, a door that stays closed—so it works as fact rather than universal mood symbol.\n\nTreat expression cues as staging references, not truth indicators. A smile can mean warmth, strain, politeness, or something the character wants to believe. After the reveal, an earlier expression may be reread: \"At the time he thought she looked relieved. Now he knew she had already decided.\" The rereading is the character's changed understanding, not proof that every expression concealed something.\n\nWhere it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue brief, in the Gu Long manner: short exchanges, a question answered by silence, a line that repeats with different weight. Alternate a spoken line, a short focal thought, and a scene note, and let the focal thought stop short of explaining everything before the visual return has done its work.\n\nThe style adds no branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or engine commands unless separately authorized. A scene can end on a recognition with no choice offered, and a reply may equally stop mid-scene as the focal reaction forms. Aftermath may appear as a later scene where the same setting is ordinary and altered—the chair empty, the bicycle gone, the door open—with relationships left otherwise unsettled. Preserve the active authorship contract and world facts, including which character owns the focal narration.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of a continuing story rather than a complete miniature chapter. Open in the present state of the through-line: what decision is underway, what relationship or obligation has changed, what remains unfinished. Recover only the specific past detail the update needs—the blue envelope, the repair that was promised—in a brief line of narration or thought, not a retelling of the history.\n\nUse Gu Long's delayed revelation across installments. Plant a compact observation early and unloaded: \"The key was still on the nail.\" Let the character notice it and move on. Installments later, when the loss or the irreversible choice has become clear, recover that exact detail and add its earned meaning: \"The key was still on the nail. No one had ever come back for it.\" The recovery is the tragic turn, not a new fact about the key.\n\nLet the character's knowledge and the reader's diverge legitimately. The reader may grasp an early detail's implication before the character does, and the character can fail to act for reasons the narration keeps clean: ordinary fatigue, a competing duty, the plausible belief that there is more time. Recognition arrives when a changed event or a return makes the earlier detail unavoidable—earned, not announced retroactively by the narrator.\n\nClose installments without artificial cliffhangers. An installment can end on a recognition, a quiet change in daily routine, a question asked and not yet answered, or the character beginning the task that follows from the loss. A hook, when used, is an actual new condition—a letter arrives, the road reopens, the absent person appears at the door—not a required twist, betrayal, or fresh disaster.\n\nKeep the serial reader oriented without repeating past events: a brief transition can mark time, and a recurring object can accumulate meaning. Private interiority remains available under the active contract and unseen by other characters. Progress can be knowledge, relationship, work, or resources; the style adds no progression system or ranking. Preserve all world facts and the irreversibility of the tragic arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet ordinary routines carry the imminence of a consequential fall. Describe a person preparing food, walking a familiar route, or waiting in a too-quiet room with the kind of even attention that makes small details feel loaded: the exact position of a cup, the delay before a reply, the sound of rain on a window. Keep the prose externally calm even when the interior is not. This contrast is the main engine of turbulence, not a vocabulary of rage or despair.\n\nMake the cost of emotional exposure concrete in behavior. A character may admit too much, refuse to apologize, ask for something they know they will not receive, or return to a person who has already hurt them. Show the strain in what they do immediately afterward: they finish the dishes badly, misplace a key, answer a harmless question too quickly, or remain seated after the other person has left. Let these actions be small and specific, so the reader feels the weight without a scene-level emotional announcement.\n\nUse Murakami's ambiguous thresholds as the place where recognitions arrive. A character may stand at a doorway, watch a train leave from behind glass, or pause beside a phone that does not ring. Do not explain the moment. Let the recognition change what the previous choice means: what seemed like ordinary caution becomes a refusal, a kindness becomes a debt, or an old promise becomes a claim that can never be settled. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored without editorializing.\n\nKeep warmth and pain in the same exchange. Comfort may be offered too late, given awkwardly, or accepted by someone who is still angry. A sincere apology may not repair anything. Let tenderness make the loss more specific rather than softening it. The writer should not force a total breakdown in every scene, nor supply a healing turn that removes the tragic arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite each response as one coherent shared interval, with the selected narrative person and action markers preserved from the active roleplay contract. Use short to medium paragraphs and make every line of dialogue unmistakably attributed. The prose may be quiet and Murakami-like, but it must still scan easily for a collaborator who will continue the scene.\n\nBuild tragedy through specific, irreversible choices rather than through a heavy mood. Show what a character chooses in the present action: they leave a room, return an object, refuse to explain, or agree to something they cannot refuse. The emotional weight lives in those choices and in the exact things around them—the cold edge of a sink, a jacket left on a chair, the sound of a door closing. Do not narrate the other player's character's actions or feelings; describe what this character notices and what they can perceive.\n\nKeep NPC behavior and world activity going without forcing a prompt at the end. A phone may stay silent, a clerk may finish a transaction, a cat may cross the street. The last sentence should not demand a reply, ask a direct question to the other player, or stop the scene artificially. Let the continuation follow from the current moment: someone may still be walking, rain may still be falling, or a character may still be trying to form a sentence.\n\nUse the ambiguous threshold to open collaboration rather than close it. A character can pause at a doorway, wait beside an elevator, or look up at a train display without explaining what it means. The recognition that turns the scene toward tragedy may be partial or delayed. There is no need to complete the fall in one post; let loss accumulate across turns while keeping the immediate exchange readable and full of concrete detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the classical or vernacular episodic telling route for this combination. Structure the story as a sequence of linked scenes or chapters, each with a clear temporal marker and a strong sensory anchor. Do not pretend to imitate an ancient text; use the episodic method to organize contemporary Murakami-like material: a person returns to a place they once left, an object moves between hands, a promise is repeated until it changes meaning.\n\nIn each episode, give unhurried attention to a practical activity typical of the active setting and community: preparing food, waiting for a visitor, mending an item, taking a walk before evening. Describe exact surfaces with the same evenness used for strange or emotionally loaded details. The ordinary ritual should make the approaching loss legible without announcing it. Warnings and alternatives remain intelligible: a relative may mention an old debt, a neighbor may suggest a different course, but the character's choice grows from their history rather than from ignorance.\n\nUse the episodic boundary to preserve aftermath. Between scenes, time passes, and a choice made earlier cannot be taken back. Let a repeated object or phrase reappear in a later episode with a changed meaning. A cup that once signaled comfort may now mark an empty seat; a familiar doorway may become the threshold of departure. Keep the community and period distinct to the supplied world rather than importing a generic Chinese landscape or speech pattern.\n\nAllow tragedy to develop through recognition rather than mere disaster. Within a scene, let a character notice how an old intention has hardened into a commitment they no longer fully want but will not break. The narrative voice may remain calm and observational, maintaining Murakami's conversational distance, while the reader understands the cost. Do not force a moral lesson or explanatory summary; let the next episode show the loss continuing in small, concrete ways.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with short, hard transitions and concrete physical detail. Keep Murakami's calm observation, but compress it: instead of lingering over a room, select two or three exact details—the scored linoleum near the door, the cigarette burn on the table's edge, the weight of a wet coat on a hook. Let those details carry the history of use and loss. Do not soften the surfaces to make them prettier.\n\nMake tragedy feel like accumulated pressure on objects and bodies. A repeated choice shows in worn shoes, a cracked phone screen, a drawer that no longer closes. Describe the cost in practical terms: a person has to walk home because they missed the last bus, a letter must be rewritten because the ink ran, a borrowed shirt cannot be returned because it was ruined. When a character reasons at length, let the longer thought stay specific and situated rather than becoming an abstract lament.\n\nGround rough speech in its speaker and situation. A character may speak bluntly, but only when the scene and the person support it. Do not make everyone abrasive. The contrast between a terse line and a quiet observation can be more powerful than continuous hard talk. Use rhythm changes deliberately: speed up a sequence of small failures, then slow down at the moment a decision becomes irreversible.\n\nKeep the threshold abrupt but realistic. A door can slam, a glass can break, a person can turn away mid-sentence. The gritty register gives the threshold physical force, but it should not introduce gratuitous violence or survival-horror imagery. The genre remains tragedy: the wound is in the loss of a future, not in a spectacle of gore. Show aftermath in maintenance and repair, in the way a room stays damaged or a routine has to be relearned.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nWrite the story as a sequence of authored documents: letters, journal entries, reports, receipts, postcards, or other records appropriate to the supplied world. Each document must have a clear author, an intended reader or purpose, and a moment of composition. Do not add an omniscient narrator after a record to explain what it means. Let the material form itself carry the story.\n\nUse Murakami's calm attention within each document. A journal can describe the exact position of a pen, the number of times a clock struck while the writer sat still, or the smell of rain on concrete. A letter can mention a small domestic detail that later becomes important. Keep the prose composed rather than confessional; even a very private record may conceal as much as it reveals. This makes the gap between what the writer records and what the reader can infer into a tragic engine.\n\nLet recognition happen through revision and comparison. A later document may refer back to an earlier one and subtly change its meaning. A letter written before a departure may become unbearable to reread once the departure is permanent. A receipt or an unsent note may show that a choice had already been made before the writer admitted it. Preserve the distinction between what the author of a document knew when writing and what later documents reveal.\n\nDo not rotate mechanically through letter, journal, report, and newspaper clipping. Choose the form each scene requires. A practical report may record a fact without naming the loss; a short postcard may say almost nothing but still move the tragedy forward by what it fails to say. Material traces—a torn edge, a corrected date, a coffee stain—should be used sparingly to locate a document in time and feeling. The aftermath remains visible in the increasing difficulty the same writer has in keeping the record going.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend time on chosen kindness and familiar preferences without making the story toothless. A character may remember exactly how another person takes their tea, set out a clean towel before a guest arrives, or save the last slice of bread for someone who is running late. Describe these acts in Murakami's unhurried way: the sound of the kettle, the fold in the towel, the way the bread sits alone on a plate. The care is real, not ironic.\n\nLet the tragic arc remain intact. The person who accepts the tea may still be leaving. The clean towel may be used by someone who has already made an irreversible choice. Warmth does not cancel the loss; it gives the loss a specific shape. Do not force a confession or a repair. A moment of comfort can end with the two people still unable to say the one thing that matters.\n\nKeep personality and privacy intact. One character may show affection through small practical services while another remains quiet or mildly disagreeable. The narrative should not make everyone accommodating. A kind gesture may be refused, or accepted with a remark that stings. Murakami's understatement suits these scenes: let the emotion live in the temperature of a cup, the distance between two chairs, or the length of a pause.\n\nUse the ambiguous threshold to give comfort its limit. A character may enjoy an easy afternoon and then, at the doorway or the end of the platform, understand that nothing has changed. The recognition may be silent. The following scene can show a small practical consequence—an unwashed cup, a light left on—that keeps the tragedy moving without melodrama. Comfort is a real interval inside the consequential fall, not an escape from it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in deliberate, composed paragraphs with sustained transitions. Do not merely report actions; let the narration move from a particular moment to a wider frame when the chosen narrator permits it: the history of a house, the reputation of a family, the local meaning of a holiday, or the social expectation behind a small refusal. Keep the Murakami-like attention to exact surfaces inside that broader movement. A formal sentence can still describe the sound of a key in a lock or the way steam fades from a glass.\n\nFrame tragedy through social consequence and judgment. A private choice may become public: a missed payment, a broken engagement, a reputation changed by one episode. Show how other people respond in ways that are neither uniformly hostile nor universally sympathetic. The formal style can use ceremony, correspondence, or public/private contrast when relevant to the supplied world. A letter may be more important than a face-to-face conversation; a room's formality may make a moment of honesty more costly.\n\nKeep the prose distinct from ordinary speech without making every sentence archaic or elaborate. The narration may be smooth and syntactically full, but dialogue can remain plain and character-specific. Use the contrast between formal narration and ordinary talk to make recognition sharper: a character says something simple, and the narrator shows how that simple sentence settles a long history.\n\nUse ambiguous thresholds inside a composed architecture. A person may stand at a doorway between two social worlds, wait in a corridor before an appointment, or watch a ceremony end without yet knowing what it has changed. Let the narration hold that moment without announcing its meaning. The tragedy continues through small formal structures—an unanswered letter, a postponed visit, a room kept the same after it no longer needs to be. The aftermath is not a separate lament; it is visible in the way the social world goes on around the loss.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the tragedy as a story being told by a specific narrator to listeners within the fiction. The teller has a present need that governs what they include: they may be trying to explain an irreversible choice, to keep a lost person present, or to make someone understand what cannot be undone. Let that need shape the selection, pacing, and omissions of the embedded tale. A listener's interruption can force the narrator to return to a moment they had skipped, and the return should reveal something the teller had been protecting.\n\nUse Murakami's unhurried, concrete narration for both the frame and the embedded tale. Describe ordinary actions—making coffee, waiting for a train, folding a shirt—with the same calm precision as the turning point of the loss. Let private associations attach to simple objects or repeated phrases: a song heard twice, a particular brand of cigarettes, a doorway. When a repeated object or phrase appears in the embedded tale, let it alter the outer audience's understanding of the teller's present situation rather than certify that the tale is complete or correct.\n\nMake the embedded tale an active intervention in the frame. The telling itself is an attempt to change the listeners: to persuade them to stay, to warn them, to delay a decision, or to make them see a past event differently. The outer audience's reactions—impatience, pity, recognition, refusal—should shift how the tragedy lands. A listener who has heard the tale before may notice what the teller has changed this time. The frame and the embedded tale should illuminate each other: a detail in the present scene can explain why the teller lingers on a particular moment in the past.\n\nLet recognition happen in the telling. The narrator may realize, while speaking, that a warning was ignored or that love and pride were mistaken for each other. Convey this through a shift in what the narrator chooses to say next, a hesitation, or a repeated phrase that now means something different. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a physical object, a habit, a name spoken only once more. End with the continuing presence of what has been lost, felt in the frame's present rather than in a neat moral. Keep the Arabian Nights influence as a structural resource—an audience that needs the story, a tale that alters the hearers—without adding magical bargains, djinn, or a forced cliffhanger between every nested account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy through the social realism route of the German-language tradition. Build the fall from concrete social conditions: work, housing, education, family expectations, class position, or institutional pressures. These are not background scenery but active constraints that shape the character's choices and limit the alternatives they can see. Show the constraint through repeated practical encounters: a rent that must be paid, a schedule that cannot be changed, a supervisor's expectation, a family obligation that recurs every week. Each encounter should make the character's narrowing options more concrete.\n\nNarrate with Murakami's unhurried attention to everyday surfaces. Describe the practical activities that fill the character's life—preparing meals, commuting, maintaining a living space—with the same even tone used for moments of crisis. Let small, concrete details carry associative weight: a type of bread, a key that fits poorly, a particular bus stop. When a detail recurs, let it register the pressure of the social condition: the same bus stop becomes the place where the character rehearses what they cannot say, the same key becomes the object they hold when they decide to leave.\n\nMaintain a close third person or first-person participant with conversational distance and psychological realism. Do not explain everything the character feels. Show the character weighing alternatives: a friend may voice a different path, a colleague may describe how they managed, the character may notice a risk and proceed anyway. The reader should understand why this person chose this way in this world, not because they were foolish but because the alternatives carried costs they could not pay.\n\nLet recognition come through a shift in attention or a delayed understanding rather than a sudden epiphany delivered in dialogue. The character may realize, while performing a routine action, that the choice was already made long before they named it. Preserve aftermath: after the loss, the character continues to act, remember, and live with what cannot be restored. The tragedy should arise from the collision between desire and social reality—a marriage fails not only because of pride but because of economic need; a career choice costs a relationship because of family duty; an act of courage becomes destructive because the institution punishes it. Keep syntax and narrative distance in line with German-language social realism; avoid forced translated word order or national stereotypes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the tragedy through the collision between an old authority and present-day life. Do not import vampires, factions, or supernatural powers. Instead, create a mundane but oppressive inherited structure: a family legacy, a neighborhood institution, a company, a building with rules no one fully understands, a debt to the past. Make the structure causally active in the narrative through specific present-day mechanisms: a rule that must be followed, a debt that must be repaid, a permission that must be granted, a ritual that must be performed. Each encounter with the structure should make the character's intimate choices more constrained.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, even narration to render the horror through intimate encounter rather than spectacle. Describe the urban material world concretely: the smell of a stairwell, the buzz of fluorescent lights, the wear on a chair. Let private desire and social masks be felt in small gestures, hesitations, and objects. When a mask falls away in trusted company, show the relief and danger of that exposure rather than a melodramatic confession. The atmosphere should change how a particular encounter is experienced: a familiar room can feel threatening because of what the character now knows about the structure that owns it.\n\nLet the tragedy arise from morally complicated intimacy. A character may love someone who enforces the old order, or may need that order to survive. The irreversible choice can involve betrayal, silence, or an act of loyalty that destroys a relationship. Show the character weighing the choice: another character may name the risk, or the protagonist may see an alternative but reject it out of pride, duty, or fear. Do not make every caring act fraudulent; allow genuine affection and solidarity to exist even within damaging structures. The tragedy should be the cost of that affection, not the proof that it was always false.\n\nKeep uncertainty unresolved where Murakami's profile permits. The narration may leave open whether an event was manipulation or coincidence, but the emotional consequences must be concrete. Recognition should change what the loss means: the character understands what they actually valued, or what they mistook for love. Convey this through a shift in attention to a familiar object or room, not a sudden speech. Aftermath preserves what cannot be restored—a room left untouched, a ritual continued, a name not spoken. End with the weight of that loss, not a neat resolution. Avoid any imported Vampire: The Masquerade mechanics or a hidden secret power that closes the gap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy through the social/domestic realism route of Indian literary traditions. Build the fall from the specific pressures of family, community, work, and social expectation in the supplied setting. These are not generic constraints: show particular obligations, rituals, economic needs, and unspoken rules that shape the character's choices. Make the social pressure causally active through recurring domestic rituals and obligations: a weekly phone call, a meal that must be prepared, a relative who must be visited, a festival that requires attendance. Each recurrence should make the character's narrowing options more concrete.\n\nNarrate with Murakami's unhurried attention to concrete surfaces. Describe the domestic world in detail: the preparation of food, the arrangement of rooms, the feel of a worn sari or shirt, the sounds of neighbors. Let small objects and repeated gestures carry associative weight. A particular kind of tea, a door left ajar, a clock that runs slow—these recurrences should connect private concern to the social world without becoming neat symbols. When a repeated object or gesture appears, let it register the pressure of the obligation: the same tea is prepared for a relative whose visit the character dreads, the same door is left ajar for someone who will not return.\n\nMaintain a calm, conversational narrative distance. The narrator may be a close third person or a first-person participant. Do not explain every feeling; allow silence and omission to carry meaning. Show the character weighing alternatives: another character may propose a different path, and the protagonist may recognize the warning and still proceed out of duty, pride, or mistaken belief. The reader should understand why this person chose this way in this family, this community, this world.\n\nLet recognition come through a shift in attention rather than a sudden speech. The character may realize, while performing a routine action, that the choice was already made long before they named it. After the loss, the character continues daily routines, but those routines now register absence. Preserve what cannot be restored: a recipe no longer made, a chair kept empty, a phone call never answered. Do not add supernatural or marvelous elements; keep the tragedy rooted in social and domestic reality. Ensure the language and community remain specific to the supplied world, not a generalized Indian backdrop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy through the modern experimental inward fiction route of Japanese literary traditions. Structure the fall through a subjective, reflective consciousness rather than a strictly linear plot. The narrative may move between memory, present action, and associative thought, but the temporal shifts must be intelligible to the reader. Use a recurring concrete detail as the hinge between memory and present action: a sound, a phrase, a habit of turning a cup, a particular stretch of road. When the detail appears in the present, let it open a memory; when the memory returns, let it alter the character's understanding of the present.\n\nUse Murakami's calm, even tone to describe both ordinary activities and moments of irreversible choice with the same unhurried precision. Ground the experiment in concrete surfaces: describe objects, rooms, weather, and bodily sensations with care. Let those associations remain private and slightly strange rather than symbolic in a neat way. The narrator may be a first-person participant or a close third person whose focus is limited to one character's mind. Allow uncertainty to remain unresolved: the reader need not receive a final explanation of every odd detail, but the emotional and practical consequences must be clear.\n\nMake the tragedy arise from incompatible commitments or a flawed belief. The character may choose between love and duty, remain silent when speaking would save a relationship, or act on a misunderstanding that the narration has already made intelligible. Show the character weighing the choice: a friend may voice concern, the protagonist may notice the risk and still proceed. Do not make the character foolish; their intelligence and worth should remain visible even as the choice becomes destructive. Let the associative movement itself reveal the choice that has already been made: a memory may return with the cost now visible, or a present action may suddenly recall the moment when the character decided.\n\nLet recognition reshape the loss. When the character finally understands the cost, convey it through a shift in perception or a delayed memory returning, not a forced epiphany. Aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored: a routine continued in absence, an object kept, a promise remembered. Keep the ending open in terms of explanation but definite in loss. Avoid any compulsory seasonal imagery or quietness; let feeling be direct where the character demands it. Do not import supernatural elements unless the world itself includes them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy through the intimate psychological fiction route of Latin American literary traditions. Write from a close, subjective perspective—first person or limited third—that follows one character's emotions, memories, and perceptions. The tragedy should arise from a relationship, a family entanglement, or a private obsession within a specific urban or domestic setting. Do not introduce magical elements; keep the world realistic even when the narrator's perception becomes unstable or dreamlike.\n\nUse Murakami's unhurried attention to practical surfaces. Describe the character's daily actions with precision: preparing coffee, walking a familiar street, waiting at a bus stop. Let small objects and sensory details carry private associations. A particular song, a cracked tile, the smell of rain—these can recur and deepen the emotional texture. Make the character's desire or mistaken belief visible through their attention to these details: the song they play when thinking of someone, the tile they avoid because it marks a moment, the rain that reminds them of a promise. Keep the narration calm and even, even when the character's feelings are intense.\n\nMake the fall intelligible. Show the character's desire, pride, or mistaken belief through their thoughts and actions. A warning may be spoken by another character, or the protagonist may notice an alternative yet reject it. Show the warning as something the character notices but interprets through their own need: a friend's concern becomes interference, a hesitation becomes shyness, a silence becomes consent. Love and duty can conflict; a single decision to stay silent, to leave, to confess, or to hide something can become irreversible. Do not make the character foolish; their intelligence and vulnerability should remain visible.\n\nLet recognition change the meaning of what was lost. The narrator may remember a detail they had ignored, understand too late what someone meant, or realize what they had been protecting. Convey this through a shift in attention rather than an explicit moral statement: a familiar detail returns with its true meaning now visible. Aftermath should preserve the loss: a routine continued alone, a room kept unchanged, a message never answered. Keep the language and community specific to the supplied Latin American setting without forcing magical realism or national stereotypes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy as an immediate scene for a fast-reading audience. Open directly with the character in the situation that will become irreversible: a choice being made, a conversation that cannot be taken back, or the moment after a loss. Use short, clear paragraphs and keep spatial changes easy to follow. Break around action, dialogue, and private reaction so the reader always knows where the character is and what they are doing.\n\nApply Murakami's concrete, unhurried observation within the light novel's economy. Select one sharp sensory detail per scene—how the character pours tea, the feel of a phone in their hand, the sound of a passing train—and let it carry the private feeling. Do not over-describe; the single detail should anchor the emotion without slowing the pace. Keep the narration calm and conversational even when the character is upset. Uncertainty can remain unresolved: the reader need not receive a full explanation of every odd detail, but the loss itself must be definite.\n\nMake the tragedy arise from a clear, intelligible cause: love conflicting with duty, pride refusing a warning, mistaken belief leading to a final act. Show the alternative being voiced or noticed in a single exchange or beat, then refused. Let the character remain worthy of attention—not foolish, but caught in a genuine bind. Use focused reaction beats: after the choice, show the character's physical sensation, a brief internal thought, or a small change in routine. These beats should not slow the pace but sharpen the consequence.\n\nLet recognition happen in a single, precise moment. The character may understand the cost while looking at an object, hearing a phrase, or noticing an absence. Convey that understanding without a long internal monologue: one sentence of perception, one physical detail, one small action that shows the change. Preserve aftermath: end with the character continuing a routine now changed by loss—a seat left empty, a phone not answered, a key kept in a pocket. The response can stop at the edge of that aftermath; no cliffhanger is required, but the tragedy must land. Keep the presentation light novel without forcing first person, a young cast, or comedy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the tragic arc through a narrator's returning attention to a small number of exact surfaces: a stairwell light, a kettle's click, a window left open, a coat hung by the door. Let each recurrence carry a different syntactic weight, but let the change be caused by what the narrator now understands. An early mention can be plain and practical. After a choice has been made or recognized, the same detail may arrive inside a longer clause that slows around it, or in a shorter sentence that lets it stand alone. The loss need not be named each time. The altered rhythm of the return does the naming.\n\nLet comparisons unfold rather than decorate. When the narrator compares a sound to something remembered, let the comparison change what the narrator understands about the present moment. An image may begin as a sensory observation and then open into an association the narrator did not expect. Keep the association traceable to the scene or to the narrator's private history. A comparison that arrives after recognition should not merely repeat the earlier one; it should carry the new understanding into the image itself. Use plain statements where they create contrast or register a practical fact, not as a scheduled interruption of every figurative passage. A character can speak in an ordinary register while the narration around the speech holds a more sustained rhythm.\n\nPreserve Murakami's unhurried practical activities as the ground of the tragedy. A person can prepare a meal, wait for a train, fold laundry, or repair a small appliance while the irreversible choice is already in motion. Describe these activities with the same even attention given to the narrator's inward turning. Let an alternative remain visible inside the practical scene: the phone on the table, the door still open, the train not yet arrived. The narrator may notice it without taking it. Strangeness may appear as a detail noticed too late, a phrase repeated without explanation, or a threshold that feels different after a decision has passed. It does not require a literal impossible event. Let the narrator remain uncertain whether the strangeness belongs to the world or to the changed condition of the one who sees it.\n\nMake recognition change the meaning of what has already happened rather than replace it. The narrator may realize that an earlier kindness was also a farewell, or that a warning was intelligible but could not be accepted at the time. Do not make the narrator foolish for having missed it. Let the realization alter the prose's attention: a detail that was merely practical can become the place where the loss gathers. The aftermath should preserve the ordinary world and the particular loss together: the kettle still clicks, the coat still hangs by the door, and the person is still gone. End where the current attention naturally rests, not with a forced summary of sorrow.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBuild the tragedy as a sequence of small irreversible changes that the narrator notices while moving through an ordinary day. A familiar errand, a meal, a walk, or a household task can become the occasion for remembering a comparable moment that is no longer available. Let the present moment remain concrete: the narrator is not floating in memory but standing in a particular room, hearing a particular sound, or waiting for a particular thing. The reflection should arise from that present surface and return to it.\n\nRemembered occasions should carry their own specificity. Do not summarize the past as a general condition of loss. Recall a particular afternoon, a particular sentence, a particular arrangement of objects. Let the narrator compare the remembered moment with the present one and notice what has changed. The change may be small: a chair moved, a habit discontinued, a phrase no longer spoken. The tragedy lives in the accumulation of these changes rather than in a single announced catastrophe. When a larger loss is present, let it enter through the small details that now surround it.\n\nKeep the narrator's loneliness as attention rather than self-pity. The narrator may notice that no one else remembers the occasion, or that a shared joke has become private and unrepeatable. This isolation can be stated plainly. Ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable even after loss: a good meal, a warm room, a song on the radio. Do not force every pleasant moment to announce its own impermanence. Let the narrator sometimes simply enjoy the thing, and let the reader feel the shadow only because the surrounding reflection has established what is missing.\n\nMake the tragic choice intelligible in retrospect. The narrator may remember the moment when an alternative was visible and could not be taken. Do not explain this as stupidity or fate. Let the narrator understand, without fully excusing, why the choice was made. Recognition changes what the loss means: the narrator may realize that the choice was already being made in a series of small refusals, or that a warning was heard but mistaken for something else. The aftermath preserves the ordinary world and the unfinished attachment together. End where the present attention rests, not with a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral-storytelling structure: a situated teller recounts a tragic sequence to a particular listener or small audience, and the framing occasion remains intermittently visible. The teller may be a neighbor, a relative, a café regular, or another figure from the supplied world. The frame should not be decorative. The listener's interruptions, questions, or silences can change the pace of the telling, and the teller's decision to include or withhold a detail can itself become part of the account. Keep the teller's access consistent: the teller can report what was seen, heard, or reliably passed down, and can acknowledge uncertainty where it belongs.\n\nTell the tragic arc through unhurried practical detail. The teller should describe ordinary surfaces with Murakami-like evenness: the arrangement of a room, the preparation of food, the route of a daily walk, the sound of a door closing. Let these details carry the private concern of the people involved without the teller needing to explain every feeling. A repeated object or gesture can become a threshold: the same teapot, the same stair, the same phrase said at the door. The teller may note that a detail seemed ordinary at the time and now seems different, without claiming to know the full inner life of the person who touched it.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge from incompatible commitments that the teller can make intelligible. The teller may describe a promise made to one person and a duty owed to another, a choice between remaining and leaving, or a refusal that could not be taken back. Do not reduce the people to types. The teller can present the alternatives clearly and still show why the person chose as they did. Recognition may come from the teller, the listener, or the person in the story, but the telling should preserve the difference between what was known then and what is understood now. The listener may supply a reflection the teller has not made, or may remain silent where the teller expected a response.\n\nKeep the framing occasion concrete. The telling may occur over tea, during a shared task, or in a waiting room. The frame can interrupt the story with a practical matter: a cup refilled, a call to prayer, a bus arriving, a child asking a question. These interruptions are not distractions from the tragedy; they are the present world in which the loss is being remembered. End where the telling naturally pauses or concludes, without forcing the listener to announce a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and return to them without explanation. A cup left unwashed, a phone number written on a scrap of paper, a chair turned toward the window, a train schedule folded in a pocket. Each detail should be concrete and particular to the supplied scene. Let the narrator notice these things in direct clauses. Do not explain their significance when they first appear. The tragedy becomes legible through the arrangement and recurrence of the details, not through a paragraph that interprets them.\n\nKeep the narrator's inward life present but economical. An essential thought may be long enough to make sense, but it should be the thought that materially contributes to the present account. Let the narrator state a motive or recognize a change in direct clauses, but let the wording arise from the particular scene rather than from a fixed formula. Do not delete causality. The reader should be able to infer why a person acted or why a loss matters from the details and the few inward statements that remain.\n\nPreserve Murakami's unhurried practical activities as the surface of the tragedy. A person can boil water, wait for a bus, sort mail, or repair a loose handle. Describe these actions in direct, even sentences. The loneliness of the narrator should be visible in what the narrator does and notices rather than announced. An ambiguous threshold can appear as a small change in routine: a door left unlocked, a light left on, a message not sent. Do not explain whether the strangeness is real or imagined. The narrator may simply note it and continue.\n\nMake the choice and its alternatives intelligible without extended argument. A warning can appear in a single spoken sentence. An alternative can appear as a detail the narrator notices and does not take: the phone on the table, the door still open, the train not yet arrived. Recognition may be compressed into a final observation that changes what the earlier details meant. The aftermath preserves the ordinary world: the cup is still unwashed, the chair still faces the window, the schedule still folded in the pocket. End where the present attention rests, without a summarizing paragraph.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy as a sustained scene-first account. Begin each response inside a particular moment: a person doing something practical, a conversation underway, a room being entered, a journey continuing. Let the narration move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. The narrator's voice should remain even and unhurried, in keeping with the Murakami profile, even when the events are painful. Let the prose's steadiness carry the weight rather than inflating it to signal importance.\n\nLet the tragic arc develop through the accumulation of scenes rather than through a single announced catastrophe. A consequential fall can emerge from love, duty, pride, mistaken belief, social conditions, or an established fate. Show the alternatives as they appear in the scene: a person can almost say something, almost stay, almost answer. The choice need not be dramatized as a single dramatic moment. It can be made through a series of smaller refusals, postponements, or acceptances that the narrator records with the same attention given to making coffee or waiting for a train. Keep the characters intelligent and worthy of attention even when their choices become destructive. A warning can be intelligible without being followed.\n\nUse Murakami's practical activities as the ground of the prose. A scene can be built around cooking, cleaning, commuting, repairing something, or running an errand. The ordinary task gives the narration its rhythm and lets the inward experience arrive without announcement. Let associations arise from the things at hand: a smell, a sound, a familiar object. Strangeness may appear as a detail noticed too late, a coincidence that remains unexplained, or a threshold that feels different after a decision has passed. The narration can remain psychologically unsettled without literal impossible events. Do not explain the strangeness away.\n\nMake recognition change the meaning of what has already happened. The narrator or a character may realize that an earlier kindness was also a farewell, that a warning was heard but mistaken, or that a choice was made long before it was acknowledged. The aftermath should preserve the ordinary world and the particular loss together. A scene can end with the practical activity continuing or pausing, with a conversation unfinished, or with the narrator alone in a room. Do not force a summary of sorrow. Let the next response begin where the current attention naturally rests.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nBuild the tragedy from the established relationship premise. The premise is the particular promise between these people: what they mean to each other, what they have shared, what they cannot say, what they are trying to preserve. Make that premise legible through scene selection and repeated moments rather than through explanation. A scene can return to a place, an object, a phrase, or a small ritual that belongs to the relationship. Each return should carry the weight of what has changed. The tragedy lives in the gap between what the relationship promised and what the irreversible choice has made impossible.\n\nGive the relationship's particular emotional material enough space where it belongs. Do not rush past the warm or intimate moment to reach the loss. A shared meal, a quiet evening, a familiar joke, a moment of care can be written with Murakami's unhurried attention to practical detail. The emotional payoff depends on the reader having felt what the relationship was before it changed. Let the private language of the relationship appear naturally: a nickname, a reference to a shared memory, a way of asking or answering that belongs only to them. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise.\n\nLet the tragedy arise from incompatible commitments within the relationship rather than from a single villain or accident. One person may need something the other cannot give. A promise made in love may conflict with a duty or a fear. A choice may be made to protect the other person in a way that destroys what they had. Show the alternatives as intelligible. Let both people remain worthy of attention even when one of them chooses badly. Recognition should change what the loss means: a character may realize that a repeated phrase was already a goodbye, or that a comfort was also a concealment. Do not make every loving act fraudulent. The love can be real and the loss can still be irreversible.\n\nUse Murakami's lonely attention to render the aftermath. After the loss, the surviving character or characters may continue the ordinary routines the relationship once filled: making coffee for one, walking the same route, hearing a song that belonged to them. Let the private language persist in memory without becoming a constant refrain. The character may notice an ambiguous threshold: a message not sent, a door left open, a habit discontinued. Keep the narrator's access consistent with the selected perspective. End where the emotional attention rests, not with a forced resolution or a summary of what the relationship meant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings and compact declarations. A person can be introduced by a deed or a way of being remembered: the one who kept the accounts, the one who waited, the one who said no. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The chronicle should record what was done, but it should also preserve the ordinary practical world in which the deeds occurred. A journey, a promise, a refusal, or a shared undertaking can organize the telling when the supplied story establishes it. Do not import a Nordic pantheon or heroic doom into every speaker.\n\nUse recurring phrasing with Murakami's even attention to practical detail. A phrase can return when a later occurrence gives it a new application: the same words said at a departure and then at a return that never happened, the same task performed before and after a loss. Let the recurrence carry the tragic weight rather than announcing it. Describe the practical activities with the same unhurried care given to the public deeds: the packing of a bag, the repair of a door, the preparation of a meal, the waiting at a station. These small acts are part of what will be remembered, even when the chronicle's public declarations do not name them.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge from incompatible commitments that the chronicle can make intelligible. A person may be bound by an oath and a love, a duty and a fear, a promise to stay and a need to leave. The teller can record the alternatives as they appeared and still show why the choice was made. Do not reduce the people to types or make them foolish. Recognition may come from the teller, from another character, or from the person in the story, but the chronicle should preserve the difference between what was known then and what is understood now. The teller may note that a warning was spoken and not heeded, or that a threshold was crossed without anyone realizing it at the time.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The chronicle can report what was seen, heard, or reliably passed down, and can acknowledge uncertainty where it belongs. Murakami's ambiguous thresholds can appear as moments the teller cannot fully explain: a light left on, a message not delivered, a door found open. The teller may record the detail without deciding whether it was a sign. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: the ordinary world continues, and the loss becomes part of what will be remembered. End where the chronicle naturally pauses, not with a forced declaration of legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in continuous social-realist prose with a definite Scandinavian domestic-realism route. The tragedy lives inside the particular institutions, housing arrangements, work rhythms, and family obligations of the supplied setting. When the story names a place or community, keep its Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish context distinct in practical details: how a flat is heated, how a workplace handles leave or notice, how a family divides a holiday, how a municipal office processes a form. These details are not scenery; they are the material through which a duty becomes impossible to sustain.\n\nUse Murakami's unhurried attention to ordinary surfaces as the narrative texture, but attach it to the social-realist machinery. A character does not simply notice a receipt or a locked door; the narration follows the practical sequence that makes the object matter: the receipt is checked against a budget that no longer closes, the locked door belongs to a housing arrangement that has changed, the familiar route now passes a workplace where the character no longer belongs. Let the even, precise description of a meal, a commute, an errand, or a maintenance task carry the weight of an obligation without announcing its full significance. The strangeness remains psychological and social: a familiar room can feel foreign because the terms of living in it have altered, a conversation can carry unexplained weight because the relationship behind it has shifted. Scene facts stay stable and real.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through the accumulation of specific commitments that later become incompatible. Early scenes establish the routines and obligations in their ordinary operation: the weekly call to a parent, the shared meal that holds a household together, the work arrangement that supports a dependent, the promise made to a neighbor or colleague. When alternatives appear, render them as real but difficult: another character names a different arrangement, a form offers a different path, a conversation opens a door that would require abandoning something already promised. The character can see these alternatives and still choose against them for reasons the narration makes intelligible.\n\nThe irreversible choice arrives as a specific action or refusal with visible consequences: a signature on a document, a departure from a room, a refusal to sign, a promise broken in a particular conversation. Do not announce its meaning in the narration. Afterward, the same calm, exact register renders the aftermath: the practical losses, the changed relationships, the objects and habits that remain, the knowledge that cannot be undone. Recognition changes what the loss means without replacing it with a lesson. Preserve warmth, competence, and ordinary pleasure where they belong; the tragedy does not require every scene to be cold or every relationship to fail. The response can end on a moment of aftermath, ongoing obligation, or quiet acknowledgment, without forcing a completed resolution or a final explanatory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, and keep action lines concise, readable, and limited to visible or audible events. Write Murakami's unhurried practical attention as concrete business in the action lines: a character rinses a cup, checks a timetable, folds a coat, waits for a kettle, or stands at a window while a conversation continues. Describe each action in even, precise language without editorial comment. Do not fill the page with camera commands; a cut or offscreen sound appears only when the sequence needs it.\n\nCarry the tragic fall through what characters do and say in ordinary scenes. Establish the commitments and routines that will become incompatible through repeated, specific actions: a weekly call made at the same time, a shared meal prepared in the same way, a work obligation performed at the same desk, a route taken without thinking. Stage each repetition with enough concrete detail that the audience can recognize it when it returns. When alternatives appear, let another character name them in dialogue or let an action show the possibility: a form left on a table, a different route proposed, a door held open. Keep the choice difficult and intelligible; the character can see the alternative and still act against it.\n\nThe irreversible action is a definite visible or audible event: a signature on a document, a departure through a door, a refusal spoken aloud, a door closed, a message sent. Do not announce its meaning in the dialogue; let the surrounding business and later scenes carry the consequence. In the aftermath, stage the changed routines: the weekly call no longer made, the shared meal prepared for one fewer person, the work obligation performed by someone else, the route now avoided. A familiar object can appear in a new context: the coat still on its hook, the cup still on the counter, the timetable still on the wall, but the action around it has altered.\n\nKeep private recognition in the clearly attributed nonspoken note when the active presentation requires it. This note is not dialogue, voice-over, or knowledge available to the cast. Use it to register the association between an ordinary surface and private concern: why a particular object holds attention in a scene, what a familiar room now means, what the character understands but does not say. Do not turn every pause into a voice-over or every look into a confession. The response can end on a quiet action, an offscreen sound, or a held moment of acknowledgment without a final explanatory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, and pauses that can be staged. Write Murakami's attention to ordinary things as physical business in the stage directions: a character sets down a cup, folds a newspaper, checks a watch, moves a chair, or stands at a window. Describe each action in even, precise language that an actor can perform. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A pause can hold attention without being filled by an explanatory speech. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nBuild the tragic fall through repeated, stageable routines and objects. Early scenes establish the commitments that will become incompatible through specific physical business: a shared meal prepared at the same table, a regular visitor who enters through the same door, a work call taken at the same desk, a piece of clothing hung on the same hook, a key placed in the same bowl, a letter left on the same shelf. Stage each repetition with enough concrete detail that the audience can recognize it when it returns. When alternatives appear, let another character offer them in dialogue or let the blocking show the possibility: a different door opened, a chair pulled out for someone new, a form placed on the table. Keep the choice difficult and intelligible; the character can see the alternative and still act against it.\n\nThe irreversible action is a definite staged event: a signature on a document, a departure through a door, a refusal spoken aloud, an object handed over or left behind. Do not announce its meaning in the dialogue. In the aftermath, stage the changed routines: the shared meal prepared for one fewer person, the regular visitor no longer entering, the work call taken in a different room, the piece of clothing no longer on its hook, the key no longer in its bowl, the letter removed from its shelf. A familiar prop can appear in a new context: the cup still on the table, the chair still in its place, but the action around it has altered.\n\nKeep required private experience in the nonspoken authoring material, clearly separated from the performed scene. A monologue or aside appears only when it has a dramatic purpose and its own audience; it does not replace private thought or make it audible to the cast. Use the nonspoken material to record the association between an ordinary object and private concern, the recognition that changes what a loss means, or the understanding a character cannot say. The scene can end on a quiet action, an exit, or an unfinished line without a final explanatory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite through a particular authorized mind. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern so that the tragic fall is experienced as an associative movement rather than a linear report. Let Murakami's exact surfaces enter as perception: the weight of a cup, the sound of a train, the color of a hallway, the feel of a coat sleeve. Follow the mind's movement from perception to memory to private words without announcing the full significance at once. A perception of a cup can pull forward a memory of a kitchen, which pulls forward a phrase someone said, which pulls forward a bodily sensation tied to a commitment. Keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered or imagined; the scene facts remain stable even when the mind moves across them.\n\nLet the tragic arc emerge through recurring concerns and the way they change across the fall. Early passages return to a particular obligation, a familiar room, a phrase someone said, or a bodily sensation tied to a commitment. Each return carries the same material through a different mental context: the mind now understands what it could not say before, or remembers an alternative it did not take. Do not make the character foolish or the alternatives obvious; the mind can register warnings and still move toward the irreversible choice for reasons that remain intelligible. When the choice arrives, render it as a specific action or refusal within the mental flow: the hand signing, the body turning away, the words spoken or withheld, the door closing. Do not reduce it to a general feeling of doom.\n\nUse syntax and paragraph shape to follow the associations. A sentence can break off and resume on a different thread; a paragraph can circle back to an earlier perception with new weight. Do not let every character share a mind or let the style grant access to excluded minds. Keep the stream within the authorized viewpoint. Recognition changes what the loss means: the mind circles back to an object, a sentence, or a sensation and finds it altered by what has happened. The aftermath is the same associative movement continuing through absence, unfinished tasks, and the knowledge that cannot be undone. The response can end on a recurring concern, an interrupted thought, or a quiet return to an ordinary surface without a completed resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages as the main story surface. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in full sentences, another replies with a single word, one delays, one edits, one sends practical details, one leaves a message unsent. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the tragic arc. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nCarry the fall through the accumulating record of commitments and the choices people make about what to transmit. Early exchanges establish the routines and obligations that will become incompatible: a regular check-in sent at the same time, a work arrangement confirmed in the same words, a shared plan made through the same channel, a small question about an ordinary object. Write Murakami's conversational distance as the gap between what a person transmits and what the exchange implies: a message about a train, a meal, or a household task carries private concern without announcing it. The sender's word choice, the delay before a reply, the edit that changes a phrase, the message left unsent—these are the surfaces through which the concern registers.\n\nWhen alternatives appear, let another sender name them in a message or let a delay or unsent draft show the difficulty. The character can see the alternative and still choose against it for reasons the exchange makes intelligible. The irreversible action is a definite transmitted message or a definite failure to send one: a resignation sent, a refusal delivered, a promise broken in a particular message, a final practical instruction. Do not announce its meaning in the message itself; let the later exchanges carry the consequence.\n\nDistinguish sent messages from unsent drafts and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. When required, present context outside the exchange in clearly separated prose, and keep required private experience in its authorized form rather than pretending it was sent. In the aftermath, the transcript shows changed rhythms: the regular check-in no longer sent, the work arrangement confirmed by fewer people, the shared plan abandoned, the small question left unanswered. An earlier message or unsent draft can reappear in a later exchange with a different weight: the reader now understands what the sender could not say at the time. The response can end on a sent message, an unsent draft, a read receipt, or a quiet gap without a completed resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Write Murakami's attention to ordinary surfaces in what the scene-setting selects: a kitchen counter, a window, a coat on a chair, a street at dusk, a cup left on a table. Describe each selected detail in even, precise language without editorial comment. Keep background or expression cues purposeful references rather than executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line. Scene changes should be legible without adding branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected.\n\nBuild the tragic fall through scenes whose familiar details change meaning over time. Early scenes establish the commitments and routines that will become incompatible: a shared meal at the same table, a regular errand through the same street, a work obligation at the same desk, a particular room entered at the same hour. Let the focal narration register private concern through the inner response: a character notices the way light falls on a table or the sound of a door closing, and the inner response holds that attention without explaining its full significance. The narration follows the perception into the private association: the light on the table recalls a meal that no longer happens, the sound of the door recalls a departure that has not yet occurred.\n\nWhen alternatives appear, let dialogue or focal narration show them as real but difficult. Another character names a different arrangement in dialogue; the inner response registers the cost of taking it. The irreversible action is a definite scene event: a signature on a document, a departure through a door, a refusal spoken aloud, a message sent, a door closed. Do not turn the scene into a choice screen or force a branching outcome.\n\nKeep private thought within the focal narration or inner response, clearly separated from what other characters know. Use the inner response to record the recognition that changes what a loss means: the familiar room now feels different, the ordinary object now carries an absence, the remembered alternative now appears as a road not taken. In the aftermath, revisit an earlier scene with its new significance earned by what has happened: the same kitchen counter, the same window, the same coat on the chair, but the inner response now holds a different weight. The response can end on a quiet scene, a held inner response, or a dialogue exchange that acknowledges what cannot be restored without a final explanatory speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Write Murakami's unhurried practical activities and exact surfaces as recurring material across installments. A familiar route, a household task, a work obligation, or a small object returns with a new weight as the tragic arc develops. When a routine or object reappears, recover the necessary particular briefly: the route previously taken without thinking, the task previously performed with another person, the obligation previously shared, the object previously handled in a different context. Do not retell the whole history. Do not create leveling systems, rankings, or progression-fantasy world rules.\n\nLet the fall develop through earned changes in knowledge, relationships, work, or practical arrangements. Early installments establish the commitments and routines that will become incompatible: a weekly call made at the same time, a shared meal prepared in the same way, a work obligation performed at the same desk, a promise made in a particular conversation. Show the alternatives as real but difficult: another character names a different arrangement, a form offers a different path, a conversation opens a door that would require abandoning something already promised. The character can see these alternatives and still choose against them for reasons the narration makes intelligible.\n\nThe irreversible action is a definite event in a particular installment: a signature on a document, a departure through a door, a refusal spoken aloud, a message sent, a promise broken. Do not announce its meaning in the narration; let the later installments carry the consequence. Do not make every turn a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger. An installment can end on completed work, a pending request, a quiet acknowledgment, or a new piece of knowledge without a forced reversal.\n\nUse occasional hooks only when they arise from the actual developments. A hook can be a changed routine, an unanswered question, a familiar object seen differently, or a conversation that stops before its consequence is clear. In the aftermath, the same routines continue with an absence, the same rooms hold a different meaning, and the recognition changes what the loss means. The response can stop after a local change, a recovered detail, or a moment of quiet aftermath without a completed resolution. Serial duration should give the tragic arc room to accumulate, not make the fall perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the fall as a sequence of charged social encounters in which feeling presses against manners. Keep the narration within one viewpoint character's awareness and let free indirect judgment carry the turbulence in that character's own diction: the voice hears a courtesy and privately glosses it three ways, watches itself perform composure, turns its irony inward as readily as outward. The gap between what your focal character feels and what can decently be said is the engine of each scene, and the judgment stays owned rather than drifting into impersonal omniscience.\n\nMake the hurt material before you make it loud. Turbulence in this mode is not a stock vocabulary of suffering; it is strain deforming actual exchanges — a sentence begun and abandoned, thanks returned too warmly, a visit shortened by invented business, a practical question that is really a question about staying or going. Money, dependence, and reputation press on the wound from outside, so that needing this person also means negotiating a world of households and obligations. Comfort, when it comes, may be partial or awkward; warmth and pain can share one table.\n\nLet the exposure cost something each time it recurs. Admitting, needing, withholding, apologizing, returning — render the price in the narration even when no one else in the scene registers it, and let a private interval stand on its own attention without an errand to justify it. Carry the arc toward an irreversible choice whose logic is legible from this character's beliefs, keep warnings and alternatives visible without cancelling agency, and let recognition change what the loss means while the aftermath keeps what cannot be restored. The relationship may end unresolved; what the final exchange must carry is consequence, not closure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nGive each reply a coherent shared interval in readable paragraphs, with every spoken line unmistakably attributed. Run the Austen machinery inside the dialogue itself: a courtesy that withholds, a mention of family that exposes dependence, an omission another speaker nearly notices. Keep the narration between speeches brief enough to scan, owned by the narrative voice the scene has established — free indirect judgment may color a clause or two, not a monologue. A hesitation before an answer, a change in form of address, or a courtesy completed one degree too perfectly can carry the arc's weight in a single beat.\n\nLet the tragedy accumulate across turns instead of arriving as one forced reversal. One exchange makes a commitment more binding; a later one exposes the mistaken belief beneath it; a character chooses the destructive course in perfectly good manners. Keep the world and its people active around the players: an NPC who leaves the room creates an absence with social meaning; a letter that arrives changes what can be done next without finishing anything. Do not charge every reply a fee of one confession, one question, or one cliffhanger; end where the scene's own boundary falls — mid-errand, mid-courtesy, mid-realization — and leave a continuation that follows from what just happened.\n\nFollow the active contract for narrative person, action markers, and player control. Keep a consistent viewpoint in the narrative parts, and give no player another character's unspoken thought unless the contract grants it. The fall emerges from accumulated choices and recognitions across many turns, not from forcing anyone's hand in one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTell the tragedy on the vernacular episodic route: chapter-like episodes, each anchored on a single social encounter — a family conference, a matchmaking conversation, the settling of a debt, an act of hospitality — with brief narrative bridges carrying time forward between them. Inside each episode, work the Austen operation: show what people believe about themselves, then let courtesy, money, family, and reputation reveal what the conversation actually transacts. The episodic narrator may invoke shared knowledge — everyone in the household understood what that dinner meant — and mark, with measured irony, the distance between a stated motive and a real dependence.\n\nUse recurrence as the tragic instrument. Introduce a family saying, a seasonal observance, a contested property, or a habitual courtesy early, as ordinary matter; bring it back at intervals; let the later appearances carry what the story has done to it. Stage warnings as lived occasions in earlier episodes — an alternative offered and declined, advice given in the wrong tone — so that after the fall the reader can reconstruct its logic though no one foretold it. Recognition may gather across several episodes or break in one; either way it changes what the loss means, and the aftermath keeps what cannot be restored: a bond left unrepaired, a standing diminished, an observance continued by fewer people.\n\nKeep the language, period, and community of the story actually being told. If the active story lives in a Chinese-language community, hold to its real register and terms; if not, the route supplies structure, cadence, and recurrence rather than a relocation of cast or setting, and never a mandatory marriage plot.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite it hard and short. Use forceful diction, compressed transitions, and a material world that does the social work: the worn collar, the fire out in the best room, the drawer that no longer locks, the coat too thin for the season. When a courtesy is offered, give the exact word and the exact object that travels with it — the cup set down hard, the letter folded once and passed across — and let money, family, and reputation register as physical facts rather than themes: what is owed, who must be housed, whose name is no longer spoken in the shop.\n\nKeep the scenes sharp and economical without brutalizing the cast. People remain intelligent even as their choices turn destructive; the style never converts them into figures in a survival script. Rough or abrasive speech belongs to a particular speaker with a particular exhausted reason for it, not to the narration's default register. When a character's reasoning genuinely needs room — weighing the irreversible step, finally understanding what an old arrangement cost — let the thought run longer in the same edged prose; compression is rhythm control, not amputation.\n\nRender the tragedy's turning points in things. Free indirect judgment survives here as a single compressed clause exposing the gap between a self-image and a situation. Shape an irreversible commitment, when it comes, as a signed paper, a surrendered key, a door closed on a household; let recognition land in one concrete image if the moment wants it; keep the aftermath material — the empty chair, the ledger line, the name out of use. Keep interiority live under the active contract and cut it with the same edge as everything else.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the whole sequence in authored documents. Before drafting each one, fix its author, its recipient or purpose, and its moment of composition; what it knows, avoids, and misstates follows from that situation. The Austen pressure works through omission and emphasis: a condolence letter that never mentions the money owed, a journal praising a conversation the writer cannot stop replaying, a report confined to public facts while private investment shows in what gets counted twice. Free indirect judgment belongs to the document's author, and so does the self-deception; no outside editorial voice arrives to correct either.\n\nReconstruct the fall rather than narrate it. Gaps, revisions, and material traces do the revealing: a sentence crossed out but still legible, a letter left unfinished at the salutation, two accounts of the same evening disagreeing about who remained in the room, a note folded inside a contract. An irreversible choice arrives as a document that cannot be recalled — the signed settlement, the final letter posted. Let recognition occur when a later entry rereads an earlier one and finally understands it; keep warnings legible across the correspondence without making anyone prescient; let the aftermath be archival: the reply that never comes, the entries that stop, the memorial line in a ledger.\n\nUse the document form each scene actually requires, not a rotation of formats, and trust the sequence to stay readable without bridging commentary. Remember that a document is a composed artifact — even an unsent draft is written for a reader, even if that reader is only its author later — so where the active contract requires live private thought, give it its own channel rather than treating the page as the mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nAttend to chosen kindness inside the arc, not beside it. Spend your detail on familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures: the tea made the way one person takes it, the chair moved nearer the light, the errand done unasked, the joke only two people in the room understand. Use Austen's social observation to give these acts context and cost — who is permitted to care for whom, which courtesy can be accepted without embarrassment, what a remembered preference costs a household economy already strained. Free indirect judgment can note the comfort someone wants and cannot ask for, or the private price of a public gentleness.\n\nKeep the people particular. Comfort does not smooth anyone into agreeableness: hold personality, privacy, and disagreement intact, and let affection be sincere without demanding a confession or a repair. The tragedy stays consequential and uncancelled — the irreversible choice, the mistaken belief, the condition driving the loss remain real beside the warmth, and the warmth makes them legible, since care given on the eve of a separation shows the shape of what will be lost. Recognition may be softened by kindness and must still change what the loss means.\n\nWrite the aftermath with warmth preserved inside it rather than overcome: the household continuing, one place absent from a table still laid with care. Do not force happiness onto the ending or grief onto every scene; follow what the story's own events have made true. The room this treatment opens for enjoying particular people is part of what the fall takes, and part of what remains.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the fall in full novelistic prose. Move in sustained paragraphs with deliberate transitions, and open enough narrative distance to frame each scene through its social situation — the household, the season, the family's standing, the obligation that makes this visit or this letter weigh more than its surface. Keep narration distinguishable from spoken language: more constructed, capable of longer syntax, but never archaic for effect, and never forcing elaborate speech on every mouth in the dialogue.\n\nRun Austen's social interpretation across that architecture. Courtesy, offers, omissions, money, family, and reputation let one small exchange carry several meanings, and the formal style gives you room to develop those meanings across a passage instead of declaring them in a clause: the offer is made, its history recalled, its cost understood, the reply given — each step earning the next. Enter a particular mind through free indirect judgment with a clear owner, and keep measured irony assigned to the narrator's settled view or a character's reflection, never to unattributed commentary. Where the story supplies larger conditions — a disputed inheritance, declining fortunes, a negotiation, a scandal — frame scenes with them so the fall is at once personal and publicly legible.\n\nBuild the arc chapter by chapter. Place warnings and alternatives inside earlier scenes and conversations as lived occasions rather than conveniences. Let recognition gather gradually or break suddenly, provided it changes what the loss means. Write the aftermath without sentimental summary — the household carrying on, permanently altered; the social order absorbing a private grief — and render interiority, visible or hidden as the active contract requires, in the same deliberate prose as the world outside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nMake the current scene a performed telling: one person has a practical reason to recount a past episode to a particular listener, and the telling itself is a social intervention. Before the embedded story begins, establish what the teller wants the listener to believe and what the listener stands to lose by believing it. Let the frame narration carry Austen's attention to social pressure — rank, obligation, reputation, financial dependence — and keep the listener's spoken courtesy and private reading visibly divergent through free indirect thought, so the reader watches two understandings of the same tale separate.\n\nInside the embedded tale, let the teller select, flatter, omit, and soften: a generous account of an old loyalty, a secondhand fact left out, a moment of anger smoothed because this audience would judge it. Treat these as the choices of a person remembering under present constraint, not as lies awaiting exposure. Give the tale a warning or an alternative the teller believes sincerely and cannot recognize as applying to the present, and let the listener hear that application more clearly than the teller does. Keep the two narrative levels legible — mark the passage into the tale and the return out of it — and pass a repeated phrase or small object between levels so recurrence accrues meaning rather than decorating the moment.\n\nWhen the frame resumes, return that phrase or object to the present room and let it change the listener's understanding of an earlier courtesy, refusal, silence, or claim. The tragic turn is a choice made in response to a story: the listener's insight arrives in time to alter the meaning of what was said and done, but not in time to restore what the telling has already displaced. Do not require the listener to flee, denounce the teller, or deliver a verdict; the consequences may still be unfolding when the scene ends, with the reader aware that the account just received was the instrument of the misunderstanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and developmental realist route. Set the action in a small community where standing, family expectation, property, schooling, and local reputation are legible to everyone in the room, and let the protagonist's fall also be her education: the understanding of herself that would have saved her arrives only after the community has fixed the meaning of what she did. Hold the narration in one focal consciousness per scene, with free indirect thought clearly attributed, preserving Austen's precision about what a person believes she is defending and what she fails to notice in herself. Let remarks about money, household economy, education, or marriage prospects carry more than one meaning without announcing the second; show the calculation or need behind a neighbor's courtesy through the focal character's attention, not authorial generalization. Keep the syntax measured and self-qualifying — sentences that correct themselves as her judgment does — without straining for translated word order or official gravity.\n\nMake the fall recognizable before it is complete. Let her make an intelligent choice on incomplete social knowledge: trusting a promise, refusing a public explanation, sending a letter, treating a partial confidence as full honesty. Make the community's standards plain enough that the reader can see the alternative she rejects, and render the decisive action as it happens, with the room, the weather, the clothing, and the small rituals of hospitality working as pressure rather than backdrop. Do not declare the act fatal; let the responses of others, arriving in brief scenes and letters, show her social meaning being fixed while she still believes the matter is open.\n\nIn the aftermath, stay close to the person who suffers the loss. Let her understand the misjudgment only after the narrower, more humiliating consequence has been enforced — the withdrawn invitation, the canceled contract, the redrawn reputation — and let that recognition change nothing practical. Close on ordinary exchanges that carry the new position: a refusal to discuss the matter, a notice in a window, a silent place at supper, a change in how she is addressed. She may remain alive and physically unharmed, which makes the social exclusion more exact; the ending preserves the reader's awareness that recognition cannot restore what was lost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the story in a modern or near-modern place where a family, institution, or property carries old authority into present life: a neglected townhouse, a private archive, a tenant building with one remaining heir, a trust settled before anyone now living was born. Make that power material and visible — peeling paint, furniture too heavy to move, failing heat, money arriving through channels no one questions — and let the atmosphere of intimate threat change how each encounter is experienced rather than merely darkening it. The danger itself must stay human, procedural, and financial.\n\nImport Austen's method, not her period. Give the protagonist real manners, loyalties, and need for approval, and let dialogue carry several meanings at once: assistance that is also surveillance, a signature requested as a kindness, a relative's question that is an audit. Keep the narration in her perspective, with free indirect thought registering discomfort before she can explain it. She must remain intelligent: she notices the wrong temperature of a room, the door that is always locked, the too-quick supply of money, and still misidentifies whose interest those details serve. Use the style's trusted-company moments — the late conversation where a mask slips, the confidence exchanged in a stairwell — as the places she most misreads intimacy, because the apparent candor is itself part of the arrangement.\n\nLet the tragedy arise from a choice made under inherited obligation: accepting a marriage, staying in the house, signing, paying, keeping a secret, because the alternative would betray someone she loves or a version of herself she cannot surrender. Make the cost concrete in the building's decay, the legal language, the old photographs, the way light works in a particular room; the irreversible act remains social and emotional, never magical. Recognition comes when she understands that her loyalty was used as the instrument of her own confinement. She may be left standing in the same unsafe place, fully aware, the choice beyond undoing. Do not import vampire mechanics, factions, secret powers, or any supernatural resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realist route. Ground the story in the particular Indian community, city, or region it inhabits — language, community, and period specific rather than generic — and make household economy, marriage negotiation, family obligation, and local reputation concrete forces in every scene. Write in the story's own language; if that is English, let the setting's social texture come through manners, obligations, food, and money rather than decorative phrases or forced code-switching. Whatever narrator and person the scene uses, hold one consistent focal consciousness, and apply Austen's method to this material: show what the protagonist believes about duty and affection, then reveal through conversation, meals, clothing, money, and family arrangements the unspoken conditions governing those beliefs.\n\nLet the tragedy develop through a choice she regards as correct within her family's logic but that the reader can recognize as insufficient or destructive — refusing a proposal, leaving a house, revealing a confidence, accepting a marriage to save a relative. Give the decision real alternatives, and let the family's judgments surface in behavior around meals, festivals, visitors, and private arguments rather than in declared principle. Use free indirect discourse to enter her reasoning while keeping the reader slightly ahead of her. Do not make the community a monolith: aunts, uncles, servants, and neighbors hold distinct interests and can offer occasional genuine kindness.\n\nIn the aftermath, make the loss felt through ordinary routine: the changed place at the table, the phone calls that stop, the marriage hall seen from a distance, the younger relative's repeated question. Let her recognition be socially exact — she understands that her sacrifice was real and that the family's values made it possible. She may remain in the same house with her standing permanently altered, or leave for another city unable to restore what was lost. Keep the narration free of magical realism and supernatural consolation; the grief lives in the household itself, in the irrevocable change to daily life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realist route. Ground the social and psychological realism in the supplied workplace, family, or neighborhood. A Japanese setting may supply its own precise customs when established; otherwise apply the method to the actual community: who serves, waits, speaks first, or leaves something unsaid. Use free indirect shading where the active viewpoint permits it, carrying the actual focal character's own vocabulary. If their established social perception entangles desire with expectation, let that conflict develop through their real judgments and choices rather than assigning perceptiveness or a psychological trap.\n\nDevelop the tragedy through a modest, irreversible choice — accepting a transfer, agreeing to a marriage interview, submitting a document, keeping a suspicion to herself because speaking would embarrass someone else. Keep the decision intelligible and even sympathetic, and show the social logic that makes it feel unavoidable: a parent's illness, a colleague's kindness, a younger sibling's future, a house that must be maintained. Where the setting supplies seasonal change — rain, heat, the shifting light, a train platform, a garden — use it as a clock on the decision, marking time passing while the character deliberates, not as scenery inventory. Use physical sensation, hesitation, and small acts of attention alongside authorized direct feeling or developed private thought when that is how the character experiences the decision.\n\nLet the loss become visible when the choice closes a door that cannot be reopened. Recognition arrives in an ordinary scene — a letter, a phone call, a cleaned room, an empty place at a yearly gathering, a photograph seen from across the house. Preserve the social setting in the aftermath: the character continues in their established role and habits, while the narration lets the reader see how the loss has changed the meaning of their life. Keep the prose exact and unhurried, with the character's own plainness or energy present in their direct speech and thought; the social-realist attention neither adds nor erases established supernatural facts, and no explicit moral lesson is required. A season change may accompany recognition when the scene supports it; the character remains part of the social world they inhabit.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social realism route, drawing its chronicle and testimony shading for the aftermath. Ground the story in its particular Latin American country, city, and class, with family, church, schooling, housing, food, and public reputation kept accurate to that place and history. Make the focal character a woman constrained not by a universal patriarchy but by the specific expectations of her mother, aunts, employer, or congregation. Run Austen's narratorial irony through free indirect style: enter her reasoning, expose its self-flattering omissions, and let the reader see the discrepancy between what she announces and what others observe. Let conversation do several kinds of work — a compliment as inquiry, an invitation as test, a silence as judgment.\n\nBuild the tragedy through a courtship or family negotiation she reads too optimistically: a man's politeness, a family's hospitality, a friend's confidence, or a priest's concern seeming to promise a future the social structure cannot deliver. Let her choice be intelligent and mistaken at once — writing a letter, accepting an invitation, refusing a practical alternative, revealing a family secret — and make its consequence material: a public engagement, a church announcement, a family loan, a legal document. Show the cost gradually; the reader sees the alternative, but her pride, love, or sense of duty keeps her from taking it.\n\nIn the aftermath, shift into the chronicle method: family members, neighbors, and acquaintances deliver brief, attributed accounts of what happened. These voices do not solve anything; they show how the community has assigned blame and preserved its own version. She may remain in the same town with her status changed, or be sent away. Her recognition is painful because it permits no restoration: she understands that her misreading of courtesy was the instrument, and that the social world was waiting to punish that misreading. End on ordinary life that makes the permanence clear — a wedding she attends, a ceremony she is excluded from, a street she no longer walks, a name she no longer carries. No magical elements, no supernatural consolation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Light Novel\n\nOpen with a single clean scene that establishes the heroine's immediate social problem — a proposal, a family debt, a house being sold, a friendship turned conditional, an invitation that would expose her. Use short descriptive paragraphs to place the reader physically: the room, the people present, the object being handed over, the weather outside. Keep spatial movement explicit; when she goes from one room to another, from a phone call to a doorstep, or from public politeness to private reaction, mark the shift with a paragraph break or a clear sentence.\n\nGive her Austen-like interiority through light novel reaction beats. After a line of dialogue that pressures her, spend one to three short paragraphs on her private thought — what she wants to believe, what she notices but discounts, what she tells herself — set off as interior, not spoken. Let the irony be visible without making her stupid: she may think, He said he would wait, so the date must still be possible, while the reader has just watched him check his phone and leave without paying for her tea. The beat should expose the exact interpretive move that will later cost her, not summarize the plot.\n\nAdvance the tragedy through a series of clear, legible choices. Because the format gives room for reaction beats, let each choice carry its rejected alternative explicitly — she could ask her sister, read the contract, tell the truth, refuse the gift — so the reader understands exactly what is being surrendered at each step. Make the irreversible moment earned by accumulated small decisions rather than sudden disaster. After the fall, shorten the paragraphs and thin the reaction beats to convey numbness. Make the recognition scene visual: she sees the same object, place, or person that once promised safety and now understands what it meant. Keep the final image concrete and the loss fixed; no cliffhanger required, no first-person mandate, no forced comedy, no young-cast assumption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the tragic arc through the texture of what can and cannot be said in company. Courtesy, omission, money, and family expectation give a small exchange several meanings at once, and lyric movement is how you make that pressure felt: let syntax slow around an awareness that has no public form—the observation a character cannot voice at dinner, the letter read twice, the visit that cannot be afforded—and let a plain declarative sentence break the rhythm when a remark cuts through or closes a path. Keep dialogue in each speaker's own register; the figurative movement belongs to the narration or the focal mind, not to every mouth. When a phrase turns ironical, keep its owner legible, so the reader knows whose understanding has turned.\n\nChoose two or three images traceable to the scene's material facts—a view, a household object, a change of weather, a small courtesy—and let their return perform recognition rather than declare what has been lost. A room once described in full light may be revisited after a refusal, and the second description shows what depended on the person who no longer enters it. A refrain may return to the focal character's memory with its words unchanged and its meaning altered. When attention moves associatively from present perception to a remembered occasion, let the connection change what the character understands or what the reader may infer; otherwise stay in the scene.\n\nKeep warnings and alternatives intelligible inside the same texture: a character who overlooks them does so for reasons of pride, duty, love, or material constraint the prose has already made legible, not because the imagery conceals them. Recognition is the moment a familiar detail becomes unbearable or a courteous phrase acquires a reproachful second meaning. The aftermath may include ordinary affection, useful work, even humor, while what is gone does not return. End where the scene's action and the reply's natural boundary conclude—a perception still forming is a legitimate stopping point—and no moral summary is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nGive the telling memory's double exposure: present experience and what is no longer available occupying the same moment. Use remembered specific occasions as the material of reflection—a conversation at a particular card table, a note whose wording seemed obliging then and reads differently now, a walk taken for reasons that have since become legible. Where the active narrator holds later knowledge, move between what was believed then and what is understood now, keeping judgment owned by that narrator or the focal character rather than issued as universal wisdom; where the perspective stays in the present, let a single detail carry the association instead. Render the character's earlier private thought as it actually was, and let the re-seeing happen on the page rather than editing the past to be wiser.\n\nLet familiar things change meaning without ceasing to be themselves. A pleasant room may stay pleasant while its light now carries someone absent; an old kindness may be recalled with gratitude and a sharper regret at once. Ordinary pleasure remains pleasurable—a meal enjoyed, a garden doing well, a good day kept good—because that coexistence is what makes the loss legible. Do not close scenes by announcing impermanence; the reflective movement earns its weight when a specific remembered occasion does the work, and clear-sighted attention to motive and circumstance keeps the memory from dissolving into generalized gloom.\n\nBuild the tragic arc through accumulating small irreversible changes rather than a single catastrophe. Let a warning or alternative remain intelligible in the earlier account—overlooked or overridden for reasons consistent with the character's pride, duty, attachment, or social position—and let recognition arrive when the pattern is seen. Afterward preserve what cannot be restored even as life continues. A reply may end inside an unfinished recollection or a present observation with business unresolved, so long as it respects the actual duration of the attention it renders.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nRoute: framed storytelling with a domestic, social-realist core. Tell this tragedy as a situated storyteller's account: a relative, a neighbor, or a later inheritor of the letters and memories has a reason to tell this household's story now, to a listener whose presence may be felt in an occasional address or question. Establish the frame briefly and let the tale carry the body of the reply; return to the telling situation at a moment of consequence rather than interrupting every episode with explanation. Build the frame from the setting, period, and community actually supplied, not from stylized antiquity, an imported honor code, or obligatory religious framing, and keep the tradition and language of the supplied setting distinct rather than blended into a generic regional atmosphere.\n\nInside the tale, make the pivotal causes social and material: an offer managed or refused, a debt delayed, a promise made lightly, a reputation bent by a polite lie. The storyteller's commentary can carry free indirect judgment about the household—reading what a dinner arrangement or a too-gracious letter reveals—while access stays honest: render a focal character's inward experience directly when the telling moves inside it under the active perspective, and hold the teller to inference about every other mind.\n\nLet the tragedy proceed through accumulating misreadings—a daughter reading a suitor's regard as sincere, a brother not seeing that his delay has already ended a plan, a parent mistaking silence for obedience—with the alternatives intelligible to the listener and at least partly visible to the characters, so the choices remain their own. Because the tale is retold, the teller may set an earlier belief beside what was later learned; keep the two moments distinct instead of retroactively correcting the character's mind. Recognition lands when that comparison becomes unavoidable. The reply may end with the teller pausing before the final remembered consequence, the audience waiting in the present of the telling while the aftermath stands established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a few exact details per scene and make them carry the social observation: a teacup set down too quickly, a letter folded a second time, a place left empty at a table laid for more. Placement does the explaining—what is noticed first, what returns—so the reader can infer why a moment matters without narration glossing it. Keep clauses direct; one precise extended sentence accomplishes more than several performed fragments, and choppiness is not the style. Let omission leave causality inferable rather than delete it: cut an explanation only when a selected detail can hold its place.\n\nLet dialogue conduct the social negotiation while the narration stays spare. A character may say the invitation was forgotten while the arranged table shows it was rescinded; the contradiction is the scene's information, and neither speaker needs to name it. Give the focal character's inward response room when it materially shapes the account—a brief recognition, a practical fear about money, the decision not to speak—and keep it inside the authorized mind. Omission is not a withholding game: the tragic cause stays legible because a chosen detail recurs under a new condition, ordinary once, evidence later, and after the fall the thing that remains.\n\nLet recognition appear as a change in what the viewpoint character perceives, perhaps a single sentence in which a familiar courtesy becomes a departure. Preserve the character's intelligence and the alternatives genuinely available to them; economy must not stand in for passivity or folly. The arc is complete when the reader can reconstruct the choice from the details alone. A reply may close after a completed perception or in the course of an action, with no summarizing moral appended.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy in continuous scene-first prose where social pressure and inward response move together without seams. Open in motion—a gesture, a remark already half-answered—and move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and the focal character's authorized inner experience, using paragraph breaks to mark a shift of attention or a turn of time rather than pausing to recap. Orient through the scene itself: who is present, what is practically or financially at stake, what has just been said or done.\n\nUse social interpretation as an instrument inside this narration. Free indirect thought belongs to the focal character and consists of the judgments they would actually form—about another's motive, a sum of money, a relative's silence—without every thought needing profundity. Measured narrative irony arises from the gap between what a character says and what the narration lets the reader see; keep it inside the active perspective, and let private thought remain unreadable to the others present. Allow a small exchange its several meanings, with courtesy, omission, money, family, and reputation qualifying one remark at once.\n\nAccumulate the arc through ordinary interactions—a visit paid, a favor refused, a letter answered late, an inheritance discussed in passing—so that recognition arrives as a specific connection among facts the prose has recorded, not as rhetorical announcement. Afterward keep the loss present without embargoing life: affection, routine, and forward plans may continue, unable to undo what happened. A reply may end in the middle of continuing action or thought, honoring the scene's actual duration; no closing demonstration of the tragedy's meaning is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nTreat the supplied relationship premise as the tragic subject itself. Make the pairing legible through its own material—an understanding never formalized, a private joke with a history, a shared room and its habits, a promise made in a particular phrase—and draw every later scene from that established material rather than from generic romantic signals. Repetition is this treatment's payoff: across the arc, let at least one established moment return under changed conditions, so the changed condition does the emotional work and the reader feels this relationship's specific history being spent. Use only the actual premise; do not bolt on tropes it never contained.\n\nRun social interpretation through the couple's scenes. Affection shows in how they speak to each other in company and in the gap between public behavior and what each privately perceives; one may weigh a family obligation above a partner's need, or read silence as agreement, and the damage should arise from those particular misreadings rather than from outside villainy. Where the active contract allows both interiorities, give each partner's private experience its actual content—one may carry a fear the other is only inferring; where it does not, keep the second mind honestly opaque. Attention may shift between them across scenes rather than fixing on a single sufferer.\n\nLet the fall proceed through a choice or incompatibility that cannot be repaired. Recognition may come when one understands what has already been lost, or when the same repeated moment visibly means ending where it once meant promise. Preserve warmth before the fall—scenes where the relationship genuinely works are what make the loss consequential—and do not grind every beat into hurt. A reply may end mid-confrontation or in the quiet after recognition, feelings present and unresolved; no breakup, death, or reconciliation beat is owed at any particular boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the tragedy as a chronicle of deeds remembered. Open sections with a deliberate formula that orients the audience—naming the family, the place, or the season in which the matter occurred—and use compact declarations to fix a characteristic act: the brother who lent without security, the daughter who spoke only when it was already too late. Then expand into particulars that make the summary incomplete. Mark what will be remembered—this visit, that refusal, the letter that came after the burial—and draw the whole account from the world as supplied, importing no gods, weapon-deeds, or fate machinery it does not contain.\n\nLet the pivotal deeds be social and material: help received too proudly, a marriage talked of as a convenience, a debt that pricks a household's standing, an inheritance delayed. The chronicle reports public acts and reported speech, and should not casually enter every private mind; where the active perspective authorizes a focal character's inner experience, render it directly rather than converting it into legend, and keep the teller's judgment about what the community came to believe distinguishable from what a person actually thought. A surviving participant may interrupt to correct a detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary concern; such resistance keeps the telling alive without turning it to parody.\n\nShape the arc through recurrence. A phrase attached to a person or deed returns when a later event gives it a second meaning, and the audience hears the fall in it; recognition occurs when the teller or a character names what the accumulation has shown. Aftermath preserves what cannot be restored—the household may continue, ordinary warmth may remain, and the chronicle remembers the loss alongside the continuance. A reply may end with the teller pausing or with a remembered act still in the course of being told; a completed episode is not required at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this as Scandinavian social realism: the tragedy works through the practical economy of a particular community, where housing, work, inheritance, credit, reputation, and family obligation are real forces that narrow what people can do. Keep the setting and period as supplied by the active story; do not import a Nordic landscape, a small town, or a welfare-state backdrop merely because the style is selected.\n\nUse Austen's social interpretation to show how a small exchange carries several meanings at once. A polite refusal may also be a judgment about someone's creditworthiness. An offer of help may carry an unspoken claim on future gratitude. Let the narration render what a focal character believes about the exchange, and let a later conversation or circumstance reveal what the other party actually meant. Keep free indirect judgment clearly owned by the focal mind; the narrator may register the gap between belief and evidence without becoming a separate moralizing voice.\n\nBuild the fall through accumulated social consequences rather than a single melodramatic reversal. Let the character act intelligently on a mistaken belief, a conflicting obligation, or a pride that the community's standards have made costly. Show the alternatives that were available and why they were refused or missed. Other people should remain sensible and worthy of attention; their refusals, warnings, and offers of help should have practical reasons, not merely serve as obstacles.\n\nLet recognition arrive when it can change the meaning of the loss but not restore what has been lost. The character may understand too late that a particular courtesy was a test, that a family obligation was being used against them, or that their own silence was read as admission. The aftermath should preserve the material and social damage: the lost position, the broken engagement, the forfeited inheritance, the reputation that cannot be repaired. Do not soften the ending with a sudden reconciliation or a restored fortune.\n\nKeep the prose composed and continuous, with the regional social-realist attention to ordinary rooms, meals, work, weather, and practical arrangements. Let these details carry the pressure of the situation rather than decorating it. A conversation about a lease or a loan can do the tragic work if the narration shows what it costs the character to speak or to remain silent.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragedy through visible and audible scene evidence. Use concise action lines to show the social meaning of a gesture, an object, a pause, or a practical arrangement: a letter left unopened on a table, a chair pulled back from a family gathering, a door closed with deliberate quietness. Let the audience see the material conditions that narrow choices—the accounts book, the borrowed coat, the empty room where a servant used to work—without narrating their significance.\n\nGive each exchange clear speaker cues and let the dialogue carry Austen's social interpretation through what people offer, refuse, omit, or imply. A polite question can be a demand for information. A compliment can be a reminder of obligation. Do not make every line witty or polished; let characters speak according to their actual habits and the pressure of the scene.\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, and use cuts to compress intervals while preserving the changes that matter to the fall. If a character loses a position between scenes, the next scene should show the practical consequence: the different room, the changed manner of address, the task now done by someone else. Do not use a cut to hide a decision that the audience needs to understand.\n\nKeep required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken notes in the active presentation. A note may record what a character understands, fears, or intends without making it available to the other characters. Do not turn these notes into voice-over unless the chosen sequence specifically needs it, and do not let another character respond to a thought as if it had been spoken.\n\nLet recognition arrive in a scene where the character sees or hears something that changes the meaning of what has already happened. The recognition may be shown through a visible action: a hand pausing over a familiar object, a letter finally read and then set down, a line overheard that makes the character stop. The aftermath should preserve the loss. End the scene on the visible or audible consequence, not on a narrated moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Stage Play\n\nWrite the tragedy through what can be staged. Let entrances, exits, blocking, and objects carry the social meaning that Austen's profile requires. Who is offered a chair and who remains standing, who pours the tea and who is served, who picks up a letter and who is not permitted to touch it—these physical arrangements should show standing, obligation, and exclusion without needing to be explained in dialogue.\n\nGive each speaker clear attribution and let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character may say something polite while setting down a cup too firmly, or answer a question while turning away to look out a window. The stage directions should establish observable conditions, not certify hidden guilt or motive.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they have a dramatic purpose in the performed scene. Do not use them as the default destination for every private thought. Preserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, clearly separated from the performed script, so that another character cannot hear or respond to it.\n\nBuild the fall through staged consequences. Let a warning be spoken plainly and then ignored for a reason the audience can see. Let an alternative be offered and refused in a way that shows what the refusal costs. Other characters should remain intelligent and worthy of attention; their exits and refusals should have practical or social reasons, not merely serve as obstacles.\n\nLet recognition arrive in a staged moment: a character alone on stage with an object, a pause before an exit, a line repeated with a changed delivery that the stage directions specify. The aftermath should preserve the loss. The final stage picture should show the material or social consequence—the empty chair, the packed trunk, the unopened letter—rather than a narrated moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the tragedy through the associative movement of one authorized mind. Let perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern braid together as the character moves through a social situation. A present courtesy may recall an earlier slight; a question about money may summon the feel of a particular room, the sound of a door closing, the phrase someone used years ago. The syntax and paragraph shape may follow these associations.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish what is actually happening in the scene from what the character remembers, fears, or imagines. When a line is spoken, let the reader know it was spoken. When a scene is recalled, let the reader know it is memory. When the character imagines a future conversation, let the reader know it is anticipation. The mind's movement may be rapid, but the actual social events must remain recoverable.\n\nUse the Austen profile's attention to courtesy, offers, omissions, money, family, and reputation as the material the mind keeps returning to. The character may rehearse what they should have said, reinterpret a past offer, or notice the gap between what someone said and what they meant. Let the mind's own interpretive work carry the social judgment; do not add a separate narrator to explain what the character has missed.\n\nBuild the fall through the mind's gradual or sudden recognition of what its own beliefs have cost. Let the character recall a warning they dismissed, an alternative they refused, a silence they misunderstood. The recognition may arrive as a memory that now means something different, a bodily sensation that accompanies a new understanding, or a phrase that returns with its full implication. The aftermath should preserve the loss: the mind may return to the same memory, the same room, the same phrase, now knowing it cannot be undone.\n\nDo not make every character share this associative mode. Other minds remain separate and knowable only through what they say and do. When the focal mind recalls or interprets another person's words, let the reader see that this is the focal mind's reconstruction, not a direct view into the other person's thought. The social meaning the focal mind finds may be accurate, mistaken, or incomplete; the reader's knowledge of the other person comes only from what that person has actually said or done.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the tragedy through the accumulating record of messages. Use readable sender-attributed messages and include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the fall. A long delay before a reply, a message typed and then edited, a read receipt left unanswered—these interface events should carry the social meaning that Austen's profile requires: courtesy, obligation, omission, and reputation.\n\nLet each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution. One character may write long, careful messages and then delete them. Another may reply immediately and briefly. Another may leave messages unread for days. These habits should be consistent and should reveal something about the person's situation and pressure, not merely serve as plot devices.\n\nDistinguish clearly between a sent message, an unsent draft, and private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought; it may be shown as a draft that the character types and does not send. Required private experience that is not transmitted should appear as clearly separated context outside the transcript, not as a message another character can see or answer.\n\nBuild the fall through what is sent and what is not. A warning may be typed and never sent. An offer may be made in a message and then withdrawn in an edit. A question may be answered with a delay that itself becomes an answer. Let the alternatives be intelligible: the reader should see what the character could have sent, and why they did not.\n\nLet recognition arrive through the record itself. The character may scroll back through the exchange and see what they missed: the message they read too quickly, the draft they should have sent, the reply that now means something different. The aftermath should preserve the loss. The final exchange may be a message that cannot be answered, a conversation left on read, or a draft that remains unsent.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy through scene-oriented presentation. Use concise scene-setting to establish the social situation: the room, the occasion, the practical arrangements that show who is welcome, who is obligated, and who is being judged. Let readable dialogue attribution carry the exchange, and use focal narration or inner response to show how the viewpoint character interprets what is said and done.\n\nUse the Austen profile's attention to courtesy, offers, omissions, money, family, and reputation as the material of each scene. A polite phrase may be a test. An offer may carry an unspoken claim. A silence may be read as admission. Let the focal narration render what the character believes about the exchange, and let a later scene reveal what the other party actually meant. Keep private thought outside other characters' knowledge.\n\nLet background or expression cues orient the moment without becoming automatic truth indicators. A changed expression can attract attention, but the social meaning must be earned by what is said, done, or later revealed. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals what they feel or whether they lie.\n\nBuild the fall through scenes whose consequences accumulate. Let a warning be spoken in one scene and dismissed for a reason the focal narration makes intelligible. Let an alternative be offered and refused in a way that shows what the refusal costs. Other characters should remain intelligent and worthy of attention; their refusals, warnings, and offers of help should have practical or social reasons.\n\nLet recognition arrive in a scene that changes the meaning of what has already happened. The character may return to a familiar place or object and see it differently, or hear a line that now carries its full implication. The aftermath should preserve the loss. End the scene on the visible or felt consequence, not on a narrated moral. The style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqakbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nWrite the tragedy as an installment-oriented progression. Maintain a readable through-line across replies: what social situation is underway, what changes locally in this installment, and what remains available to continue. Let the reader track how courtesy, obligation, money, family, and reputation have narrowed the character's choices.\n\nRecover context briefly only when it helps. When an earlier offer, refusal, or omission becomes relevant again, recall the particular that matters—the message that was sent, the invitation that was declined, the sum that was lent—rather than retelling the whole history. Let the recovered detail acquire new significance in the present installment.\n\nUse the Austen profile's social interpretation in each installment's exchanges. A small conversation can carry several meanings: a polite inquiry may be a test of loyalty, an offer of help may be a claim on future gratitude, a silence may be read as consent or refusal. Let the narration render what the focal character believes, and let a later installment reveal what the other party actually meant.\n\nBuild the fall through earned developments across installments. Let warnings and alternatives be intelligible as they appear, and let the character refuse or miss them for reasons the narration makes clear. Other characters should remain intelligent and worthy of attention; their refusals and offers should have practical or social reasons. Do not make every installment a complete miniature chapter or force an artificial cliffhanger. An installment may close on completed work, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment of what is now known.\n\nLet recognition arrive across installments. A detail from an earlier installment may return with its full implication, or a character may finally understand what a past exchange meant. The aftermath should preserve the loss across subsequent installments: the changed circumstances, the broken obligation, the reputation that cannot be repaired. The serial duration should give the fall room to acquire meaning, not make the outcome perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat emotional exposure as a move with consequences in a world of loyalties and obligations. When a character admits need, apologizes, withholds a wound, or returns to someone they hurt, write the next beat as a change in what another character is willing to do: they may soften, harden, redirect their help, or reveal a competing loyalty. The admission need not resolve anything; it can make cooperation harder, expose the speaker further, or leave the other person with an obligation they did not want.\n\nKeep the focal character's reasoning close and particular. Show what they notice in the other person's face, pause, or tone, and let them interpret it through the relationship's history rather than a general vocabulary of pain. Their private thought may be more honest than their speech, or it may be just as confused. Do not make every silence a concealed wound or every apology a trap. Some characters can mean exactly what they say and still make a destructive choice.\n\nLet strain change the actual scene by attaching it to a practical matter. An argument about a small duty can carry the weight of an older injury without anyone naming it: one character asks for help, the other offers a different help than was wanted, and the refusal or acceptance shifts what each feels entitled to ask next. A moment of warmth can arrive late, awkwardly, or in the middle of a task that will not wait. Write the loss as intelligible by showing the alternative that was available, the moment it was refused or missed, and the cost that follows. Recognition may come before the loss, after it, or only in the way the survivor treats someone else later.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene in clear, readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Let each exchange advance a shared interval in which loyalties are tested through practical choices: who stays, who speaks for whom, who accepts help, and who refuses it. Keep NPC behavior active and particular. A character may cooperate sincerely, resist, or change their mind based on what the player's character actually says or does.\n\nUse Jin Yong's sense of competing obligation by making the characters' public ties matter in conversation. A request for help can remind someone of a promise to another person. An apology can cost the speaker standing. A moment of agreement can still leave one party bound to a course the other cannot follow. Let these tensions appear in what is said and done, not in a narrator's summary.\n\nLet each NPC response register the tragic pressure as a decision rather than a mood. When a character speaks, show what their words commit them to: they may accept a task that conflicts with another duty, refuse help they need, or offer loyalty that costs them something. The player's character can then respond to that commitment in the next exchange. End at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The next reply may be an answer, a departure, a delayed consequence, or a silence the other character must interpret. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice screen. The tragic pressure should remain present without requiring every exchange to produce a completed reversal or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling. Organize the story as a sequence of named or clearly marked episodes, each one advancing a journey, a dispute, a promise, or a reckoning. Let the narrator speak as a chronicler who knows the outcome and can frame an episode by what it will cost, without erasing the characters' present uncertainty. The narration may open an episode with a place, season, or remembered event that orients the reader before the scene begins.\n\nMake obligations recurrent. A character may be called back to a debt, a teacher's command, a family claim, or a companion's need across separate episodes. Each return should change the meaning of the earlier choice rather than simply repeat it. Tragedy accumulates through these returns: what seemed manageable in one episode becomes irreversible in a later one because the character has already spent a resource, broken a trust, or bound themselves to a course.\n\nLet the chronicler mark the gap between present uncertainty and known outcome. The narrator may close an episode by noting what the characters could not yet know, or open the next one by recalling the earlier choice as a debt that has come due. Recognition should arrive through public memory and private accounting: a survivor may learn what another person knew, or a community may record the loss in a way that changes its meaning. Keep the telling vivid and concrete, with travel, explanation, and relationship given room to develop. Do not import a particular historical setting or cast; use the episodic chronicle method to shape the world and characters the story already supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful, concrete diction and compressed transitions. Show the cost of a choice in material terms: a blade that needs sharpening, a road that has worn out a boot, a promise kept at the price of a night's sleep, a debt paid in coin that was needed for food. Let technique and restraint matter through particular physical decisions. A character's skill may be real and still insufficient; their restraint may cost them a wound or a chance.\n\nKeep the prose hard-edged without turning every scene into survival horror. Physical stakes should make the tragic choice legible, not replace it. A character can reason at length when the decision requires it, but the reasoning should stay close to what they can see, touch, owe, or lose. Avoid abstract suffering. Show the cracked cup, the stiff joint, the letter folded too many times, the silence after a name is spoken.\n\nChange rhythm usefully. A long exchange may follow a brief violent action, or a single sharp sentence may end a scene that had been building through detail. Let the physical traces carry the tragic arc: the empty sleeve, the unclaimed seat, the road that must be traveled alone. Recognition may come through a physical trace rather than a speech. Show the alternative that was available and the moment it was refused by what remains: the coin that was not spent, the door that was not opened, the tool left behind. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific form the scene requires: a letter to a teacher, a report to a superior, a journal entry written after a departure, a receipt that records a transaction whose meaning the reader only later understands. Do not rotate mechanically through formats.\n\nLet each document know and conceal only what its author would know and choose to record at that moment. A letter may be generous and still omit a fact the writer did not understand. A report may be accurate about events and wrong about motive. A journal entry may confess a private attachment while carefully avoiding the public obligation that will force a choice. Gaps between documents should do narrative work: a missing reply, a revised account, or a later marginal note can reveal what changed.\n\nUse Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation by making the documents themselves acts of loyalty. Writing to one person may mean not writing to another. Preserving a record may protect a reputation or expose a failure. The tragedy becomes legible across entries: the reader can see the alternative that was available, the moment it was refused, and the aftermath that could not be restored. Recognition may arrive in a later document that rereads an earlier one, or in a material trace such as an unsent draft, a torn page, or a seal left unbroken. Let the later document quote, correct, or answer the earlier one so the reader sees what the first writer could not yet know, without adding an omniscient explanation after every record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite warmth as something the characters actually do for each other within a world that still contains their obligations and losses. Spend attention on particular kindness: a meal prepared the way someone likes it, a coat mended before a journey, a seat saved, a story told to fill a difficult silence. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or an emotional repair. A character can be comforted and still have to leave in the morning.\n\nKeep personality and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable. One character may offer care awkwardly, another may accept it reluctantly, and a third may refuse it because accepting would mean admitting a need they are not ready to name. Familiar preferences should feel specific to these people and their shared history, not generic. A small pleasure can coexist with the knowledge that something else has already been lost or is about to be risked.\n\nLet the tragedy remain an arc. The warmth is real and particular, but it does not cancel the incompatible commitments or the choice that still lies ahead. A comforting scene may be the last uncomplicated moment before a departure, or the first sign that a relationship has changed. Write the care itself as a loyalty: the person who offers it may be choosing one obligation over another, and the person who accepts it may be taking something they cannot repay. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored, and the reader should understand why the loss matters partly because of the care that was given before it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose the story in deliberate, sustained paragraphs that move through a scene with enough narrative distance to place private choices in their social context. Let the narrator frame a decision by what it means for a household, a community, a reputation, or a line of obligation. The framing should arise from the world and characters actually supplied, not from an imported aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary.\n\nUse public and private contrast to organize a passage when it matters. A ceremony, a formal visit, a letter, or a public reckoning can surround a private moment of attachment or refusal. Show how the same act reads differently in the two registers: what is courteous in public may be cruel in private, or what is loyal in private may be unforgivable in public. Let the characters remain intelligent and worthy of attention even when their choices become destructive.\n\nGive encounters room for travel, explanation, and relationships. A journey can let two characters speak at length about what they owe and what they want. A conversation can revisit shared history without resolving it. Let the narrator mark the gap between what a choice means to the person making it and what it will mean to others: the private intention may be generous while the public consequence is ruinous, or the public duty may be honored while the private attachment is sacrificed. The tragedy should accumulate through particular decisions whose consequences the reader can follow. Recognition may come through a narrator's judgment, a survivor's later understanding, or a public account that changes the meaning of a private loss. Preserve the aftermath as something that cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a specific occasion for a difficult retelling: a listener wants a decision, settlement, reckoning, or understanding, and the teller offers an account instead of a direct answer. Let the story's purpose be visible to the audience and gradually alter what they see in the present. The embedded tale may expose an old choice, explain an unhealed loyalty, or show how an obligation became impossible; whatever it is, it should make some outer-scene action newly intelligible.\n\nFollow Jin Yong's technique of putting private attachment and public duty in motion. The teller should select and emphasize the moments that mattered to them, leaving gaps that reflect grief, loyalty, or self-justification, not simple ignorance. Let a repeated phrase or object mark the connection between levels: a promise, a road, a garment, a title, or a debt. The repetition accrues meaning each time the teller returns to it, so the final return can change the listener's sense of what was already said.\n\nLet the tragic arc be complete in the inner story. Show the alternatives the characters could see, let at least one warning or better path be understandable to the teller in retrospect, and keep everyone intelligent even when the choice becomes destructive. Recognition belongs to the telling situation: the teller may realize something in the act of recounting it, or the listener may recognize what the teller still cannot. Do not force a confession, forgiveness, or a crisis in the outer scene; if the response ends during the telling, let the unfinished account preserve its pressure without a manufactured cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the documentary and historical-memory route. Tell the story as if opening an archive: letters, reports, maps, remembered testimony, municipal records, or a later historian's gloss may bracket the scenes, while the present narrative regularly steps back to show the larger social and material conditions that made the loss possible. Keep this framing calm and selective; it should not explain everything in advance.\n\nPreserve Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation by giving the central characters concrete duties and loyalties that change over time. Do not compress action into blows; include travel, waiting, negotiation, explanation, and the practical arrangements of friendship or authority. Let a choice made for love or reputation slowly reveal its cost in remembered documents, ordinary routines, and the testimony of people who were there.\n\nThe tragic force is retrospective. Show how a later narrator can assemble the story without being able to prevent it. Include at least one intelligible alternative or warning that becomes visible in hindsight. Recognition may arrive when a document is reread, a name is placed, or a surviving actor understands what they once did. Let the ending preserve the irreparable loss without rushing to punishment, apology, or closure. Do not adopt an absurdist or satirical tone; the strain should come from competing obligations and accumulated evidence, not mocking institutional irony.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace the tragedy where inherited power meets present urban life: a repurposed family building, a declining district, a ceremony that still organizes money or respect, a person whose old title no longer matches daily labor. The city must remain materially specific—stairs, locks, transit, damp walls, uncollected trash, the particular light through a window—so that the fall is registered in physical routine, not only in speeches.\n\nUse Jin Yong's two-sided pressure to shape the central relationship. Let a private attachment pull against a public obligation that still has working effects, even when it seems archaic. Show technique and restraint mattering: knowing a language, managing a ledger, preparing a ritual, recognizing a family mark, or knowing who may speak first can become decisive without becoming supernatural.\n\nWrite intimacy with complexity. The most honest exchange may happen between people who have damaged or disappointed each other, and a social mask may fall away in a trusted room without producing a confession. Gothic dread should remain personal: loss of standing, loss of a home, loss of the only person who understood. Do not import vampires, blood rules, or hidden factions. Keep the horror rooted in memory, debt, neglect, and the sense that the past holds a right over the present.\n\nBe patient with the fall. Establish the pressures, show at least one viable alternative, and let the character choose out of loyalty, pride, love, or protection. The aftermath should preserve what cannot be restored. Recognition may be early and unbearable, or late and useless; it must change what the loss means.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and domestic realism. Set the tragedy inside ordinary relationships: obligations to parents and siblings, the cost of education or migration, a marriage being arranged or strained, the pressure of local respectability, or the slow transfer of property. Keep the region, language, work, and community precise—whether a small town, a resettlement colony, or a city suburb—but do not force a joint family, a religious ritual, or a caste narrative onto the scene. Let the material reality do the work: who sleeps where, who cooks, who sends money, who can afford silence.\n\nFollow Jin Yong by making the central tension between private attachment and public obligation concrete. A child's love for one parent can conflict with a duty to the whole household; a promise to a friend can collide with a career that will support others; a reputation earned through restraint can be destroyed by one necessary breach. Give scenes room for travel, telephone calls, meals, and long explanations. Do not reduce conflict to a sequence of confrontations; show the small daily maintenance of loyalty.\n\nSend the fall through recognizable failures and costs, not melodrama. Show at least one alternative the character could have taken and the social reasoning that made it impossible at the time. Let different people see different parts of the problem: a mother may understand the money, a brother the insult, a neighbor the scandal. Recognition may come when an old letter is found, a parent dies, or the character returns to a place they left. The aftermath should respect the irreversibility. Keep the tone intimate and understated; let the loss be known through what people do next rather than through a climactic speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany. Let the story be told through dated entries, marginal notes, remembered conversations, and the teller's later comments on what they once believed. The diary should not simply report events; it should show a mind returning to the same choice, trying to justify it, and gradually finding that justification unusable. Entries can be brief or uneven, and the teller may be honest about confusion without becoming a polished narrator.\n\nKeep Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation at the center of the recorded life. The diary may describe practical obligations: caring for a parent, managing a shop, keeping a promise to a teacher, preserving an alliance, or preparing for a journey. The writer should record both tenderness and impatience; do not turn them into a saint. Let a particular skill or responsibility matter—how to read the sea, how to keep accounts, how to wait—and let it slowly reveal its cost.\n\nThe tragic pressure comes from what cannot be said or repaired. Let the diary record warnings and alternatives: a friend's objection, a letter not sent, a chance to return. Recognition may be scattered across entries—a remark reread, a change of season or address, a line that suddenly means something else. The same landscape may appear several times with different emphasis, but do not force a seasonal inventory or a universal quietness. Direct feeling is allowed: rage, longing, regret, gratitude, the wish to undo. End where the writer has understood enough to know the loss is permanent, without requiring a final moral or a complete account of every cause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate psychological and urban fiction. Place the story in a particular city, neighborhood, or small community, full of working routines, street noise, family pressure, and local reputations. Explore the inner life of one or two people as they try to hold together incompatible commitments. Keep the focus close to their perceptions: what they notice, what they refuse to remember, what they invent about another person's motives. Urban detail should support this interiority rather than drown it in inventory.\n\nTranslate Jin Yong's tension between private attachment and public obligation into the specific social world. A character may belong to a family business, a political group, a neighborhood, a profession, or a group of friends with old promises. Their love or loyalty to one person may contradict what those publics require. Show technique and reputation mattering: knowing the right lawyer, controlling an account, being seen in church, keeping a promise not to speak. Let meetings, travel across the city, meals, and gossip carry the pressure; do not reduce action to fights.\n\nThe tragedy should be realized through gradual consequences. Let the character see at least one alternative at the time; show the reasoning, fear, or love that closed it off. Allow several perspectives, but keep access controlled—when the narrative moves to another mind, it should matter. Recognition may come when a child asks a question, a bus passes a vanished landmark, or a corpse is identified. The aftermath should preserve a broken relation, an empty apartment, a name that is no longer said. Avoid the marvelous; keep the intensity psychological and social, with spare, precise sentences and long scenes of speech and silence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nOpen with a clear, immediate scene that introduces the central relationship and the first sign of impossible pressure. Use short paragraphs, direct dialogue, and strong spatial staging: show where people are standing, what they carry, whether a door is open, how a room has changed. Keep the prose fast but not clipped; let the reader always know who is speaking and what the physical situation is.\n\nFollow Jin Yong by making the conflict a true tension between private attachment and public obligation. The central character should have duties that matter to them—protecting someone, delivering something, completing a task, keeping a promise—and a personal bond that cannot be satisfied at the same time. Show restraint and competence mattering: the character may be good at one thing but unable to do the other. Give encounters room for planning, explanation, and negotiation as well as action; a journey or a long meeting can be as important as a fight. Avoid making every exchange a clever quip; let some characters be serious, tired, or inarticulate.\n\nStage the reaction beats carefully. After a loss or revelation, give the character a private moment with a specific physical anchor: a broken object, a pair of shoes, a message on a phone, a view from a high place. Reaction may be angry, empty, baffled, or quiet; do not require crying or shouting. The tragic arc should be clear: the character chooses, the cost becomes visible, and someone recognizes what has been lost. Show at least one alternative they weighed. The ending can stop on an unfinished consequence or an image, but avoid a forced cliffhanger that hides information from the reader. Let the emotional payoff come from what the character actually knows and carries, not from a last-moment twist.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet a repeated concrete image or sound carry the developing cost of a character's commitments. Choose it from the actual travel, alliance, or obligation in the scene, and return to it only when later action has altered what it makes present. If water first appears as the sound that covered a private conversation, let its later return happen through a different decision—a crossing delayed, a message carried along the same route, a debt remembered—so the image accumulates consequence instead of remaining decoration.\n\nShape comparison as discovery, not ornament. Link two features the narrator has come to notice so the likeness clarifies what the character now understands about a duty or a relationship rather than merely adding color. The comparison should arise from the focal character's present perception or a scene that character has actually experienced, never from the narrator's detached commentary.\n\nUse the lyric register for extended attention to a single person, place, or remembered moment, but interrupt it with the ordinary practical speech of alliance and errand. A teacher's instruction, a traveling companion's warning, or a servant's report can occur in plain clauses that contrast with the narrator's rhythmic movement; the contrast should show that private feeling and public obligation are not the same register. Compose not through many adjectives but through a small set of related images and anaphora that belongs to a particular emotional argument, repeated across different scenes with enough intervening causal action to make the recurrence informative.\n\nLet Jin Yong's generosity shape expansion, not just condensation. Before a consequential choice, give room for travel, discussion, and the pressure of competing loyalties to accumulate; the style can spend a full paragraph on the physical details that make the choice specific—whose boat, whose road, which message arrived late—because those particulars are where irreversible commitment becomes visible. Allow the character's warnings to remain intelligible to readers even when the focal character chooses to disregard them; the lyricist's empathy may extend to both the one who warns and the one who falls.\n\nWhen the loss arrives, return to one earlier image or phrase and let its meaning change under recognition, but do not resolve every thread into a single symbolic posture. The paragraph may end with the character's present understanding, leaving other people and their own choices still moving beyond it, not silenced for the sake of a final figure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBuild reflection around specific remembered occasions by crossing a present threshold—a familiar room, a stretch of road, a meal, a task once shared—and letting the narrator measure the difference: not simply that someone is absent, but that a particular duty, debt, or promise can no longer be performed in the old way. Keep the present detail tangible enough that ordinary pleasure remains available in the scene, even while the memory belongs to loss.\n\nLet Jin Yong's structure of competing loyalties give sorrow its object. A remembered friend was not only loved; he was also the one who kept a promise, warned too late, or chose a different allegiance. When the narrator recalls his words, let them relate to a public obligation or an alliance rather than only to private affection, so the afterimage carries the cost of a way of life as well as a personal bond. Reflection can use a small physical remnant—a letter, a sword mark, a shared route—but the remnant must be tied to an occasion the narrator can legitimately recall. Do not invent ceremonial significance without a traceable source.\n\nPreserve intelligent characters; their flawed choices still matter. The reflective movement may revisit an earlier alternative and make its warning legible in retrospect, but it should not imply that every loss was inevitable or that the narrator now possesses complete clarity. Return to an early detail only when later circumstances have altered its meaning; if the narrator repeats a phrase, let the repetition make something understood that was not understood at the time. Give recognition a limit: the narrator may understand one consequence while another remains unfinished, so the reader sees what cannot be restored without declaring the whole account closed. End with the present moment, where the remembered person is gone but the work of the living continues, and let that continuation carry the residual grief rather than a final aphorism about time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral storytelling structure with a definite situated teller. Establish why the story is being told now: a host has asked for the tale, a guest is not the first audience, or the teller's own standing makes the recital a form of obligation. Let the exchange in that frame remain modest but consequential—someone questions a detail, requests a different part, or offers a parallel from another period—so the framed tale is not a neutral wrapper but part of a relationship.\n\nThe inner tragedy should move through Jin Yong's generous sequence of travels and practical loyalties while the teller controls information for recognized ends. Keep the focal participants' reasons, shared history, debts, and reputations visible through dialogue and ordinary action, not summarized by the outer audience's judgments. In the framed story, let a warning be understandable but not necessarily acted upon; the teller may emphasize a visitor's caution in one passage and then return to it after the loss has occurred, so the audience sees the alternative that was available without insisting the character was stupid.\n\nUse recurrence to make delayed revelation part of the composition rather than a trick. A phrase may occur first as the hallmark of a merchant, a teacher, or an old soldier, then again after a death or a broken promise; the teller may not explain why the phrase has changed, letting the outer audience supply the recognition first. Keep that recognition attributed and discoverable: when someone in the frame asks a question, let the answer reinterpret an earlier passage rather than having the text silently insist on a single meaning. Preserve the distinction between the story's people and the audience's later knowledge. Inside the tale, characters know only what they knew at that time; the frame may look back, but should not give every listener immediate omniscience. If the telling returns to a prior scene, let the recurrence be motivated by the teller's skill or the audience's interruption, not by a narrator's sudden access to hidden minds. Close the frame as tragedy permits: recognition changes what the loss means while ordinary life—the next guest, the cooling drink, the unspoken debt—continues beyond the final cadence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small set of concrete objects whose presence can carry consequence: the token that schedules a departure, the weapon held during an oath, the written passage handed over at a border. Let these things recur only when the intervening action has changed what they mean. Between recurrences, render travel, debt, reputation, and alliance through direct clauses and an occasional longer sentence where the reasoning has a specific job: why a man accepts the route, what a returning servant reported, what condition the teacher attached to the lesson.\n\nLet Jin Yong's social generosity survive restraint through dialogue and pragmatic speech rather than narrative expansion. A warning should arrive as a particular sentence from a particular person, with enough ground-level detail for the reader to understand its basis. A character may be warned in a single line, then act after a three-sentence passage records the fleet's position, the price of delay, or the name of the creditor; the gap between warning and action remains interpretable without a paragraph of commentary. Omit second explanations from the narrator. If the character's choice is mistaken, do not labor the mistake with a gloss; another character's later failure to meet him at the pass can show the cost.\n\nUse omission to leave causality inferable, not to delete it. When a promise breaks, the preceding clauses should have shown the practical pressure or competing obligation so that the break is intelligible even though the narrator does not explain all motives. Interiority may be brief and direct—one thought about an old debt, one sensory memory before departure—but it must belong to the focal character and not reveal other minds. Avoid performed fragments; direct speech and deed can stand alone where they are clear. If recognition arrives, let it arrive through a returned object, a repeated phrase, or an action that cannot now be completed, and stop before summary. The story may pause at the moment of understanding, with ordinary business still unresolved beyond the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nTell the tragedy as continuous scene-first contemporary prose. Open a sequence in the middle of an action or exchange, then move freely between dialogue, physical perception, recollection, and authenticated inward thought as the exigencies of the moment require. Let paragraphing orient the reader by spatial or temporal shift rather than by formal recapitulation; a cut from the camp to the river crossing should be visible through distinct physical detail, not through a sentence that re-narrates the whole journey.\n\nUse the author profile's expansive attention to accumulate social contingency before the loss. Give a meeting room for several perspectives through speech and observed signals, not through unrestricted mind-hopping. A servant's hesitation or a rival's polite question carries information the focal character partly reads and partly misreads; preserve the gap between what the focal character knows and what those around him actually intend. Let warnings arrive as particular statements attached to particular situations—a physician's condition, an officer's timetable, an old friend's debt—so alternatives remain intelligible even when they are not taken.\n\nMove among ordinary business and high consequences without forcing a tonal break. A discussion of food or lodging may occur in the same scene as a promise, and the novel's flexible register can let the food and the promise share the paragraph without mockery. Bring inward experience in when it has a role: deciding whether to accept an escort, remembering who else took that road, recognizing that a customary phrase has lost its old meaning. Do not explain every motive, but let motivation remain recoverable from what has been seen and said. Keep tragedy's shape visible through small postponed recognitions. At the key turn, a slight shift in perception—the familiar voice heard from the wrong side of the gate—can do the work of a long meditation. End the reply where the scene naturally stops, whether that is before a decision, after a completed exchange, or in the moment when the loss becomes known; the contemporary register supports continuing action rather than a ceremonial close.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through the scenes you choose, not through repeated statements of feeling. Let each encounter connect private language to public duty: a shared designation first used at a farewell, a small object passed during a reconciliation, a name spoken differently when an obligation presses on one of them. Reflect these callbacks back only when the bond has changed; the phrase may be repeated exactly to show that its old meaning cannot be restored. Allow affection and comfort where the relationship actually supports them, and give jealousy, rivalry, misunderstanding, or discovery enough space when they arise from the premise.\n\nUse Jin Yong's social world as the source of attractive difficulty rather than a background list. Let a promise between two people conflict with a duty to a teacher, a host, a blood relation, or a community. One partner's need for restraint can be read by the other as withdrawal; an act of public loyalty may wound precisely because it was performed without a private word. Make these collisions traceable to specific decisions—which message was sent, whose wound was treated first, which gate was chosen—so the romance does not become disembodied. Keep interiority with the focal character. The focal partner thinks and notices; the other's mind can only be inferred from speech, gesture, and action. This access boundary does not lower emotional intensity, because the withheld interiority becomes its own source of uncertainty.\n\nShape the tragic arc through repetition and consequence rather than through a final despair sentence. Let a moment of comfort establish a pattern that later becomes impossible: the habitual tea poured at a particular hour, the hand offered before a difficult task, the joke made only between them. At a turning point, one partner may attempt the old gesture and fail, or the other may receive it without the usual response. The loss is most legible when the reader knows why the gesture mattered and sees the public complication that prevents it. Do not make every callback a betrayal; some shared language can survive into the aftermath. Recognition may occur as one partner understands the other's earlier choice, but the understanding need not bring reconciliation; let the end keep open the unfinished business, the ordinary room where the other no longer waits, and the particular promise that can now be kept only in memory or in a different form.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a deliberate retelling voice without letting it obscure particular knowledge. Open with a compact declaration that places a person by relationship, reputation, or prior deed, then spend the scene on the practical conditions that complicate that declaration. A man may be introduced as the one who kept the river gate for his brother, but the paragraph that follows shows the cost: the crops and the guards and the insult he would not avenge. Every recurrence belongs to an act the characters have already done or sworn, not to an ornamental epithet repeated merely for sound.\n\nUse Jin Yong's expansive social world as the substance of the deeds. Let loyalty appear through incidents that are narrated to be remembered: a teacher's last instruction carried three nights' ride, a gift returned after the border crossing, a promise broken before the garrison because another promise would have been broken. Keep the teller's access consistent; an oral-chronicle voice can say what was later known, but should distinguish that from what the actors knew at the time. A warning can therefore remain intelligible to the hearer even though the chronicler does not explain all inner reasoning: report the warning's source and force, then let the later action show how it was disregarded or honored.\n\nShape the tragedy through public consequence and deferred recognition rather than through immediate psychological commentary. The chronicle may let a phrase recur first as a boast, then as a debt, then as a reproach, each recurrence gathering meaning because the intervening episodes have altered the hearer's understanding. At the turning point, do not summarize the whole moral; return to the earlier oath or token and name the specific impossibility—the promised escort cannot arrive because the same oath required him elsewhere. Preserve ordinary practical details inside the saga convention so that the cast remains particular. Do not import a fixed heroic doom or ceremonial speech pattern for every dialogue; instead let elevated formula occur when the community would naturally use it, while merchants and relatives continue to speak of horses, wages, and bad roads. The account may close with a compact declaration of what is now known and what remains, leaving the living to carry the unfinished business rather than turning the dead into a single unquestioned legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite continuous prose in the Scandinavian social-domestic realist tradition: ordinary rooms, kitchens, workplaces, family gatherings, small debts, and the labor of keeping a household or community going. Keep whatever narrator, person, and access the active contract has already fixed. Let the tragic arc run through practical choices—an invitation extended or withheld, a loan called in, a responsibility assumed, a reconciliation refused—rather than through spectacle.\n\nRescale the craft of competing loyalties into local, actionable terms. Duty to family, a promise to a friend, an inherited responsibility, a public role, or an old alliance exerts its pressure through actual meetings, letters, remembered incidents, and the opinions of neighbors. Give encounters their generous room: a conversation over coffee or the walk to the station can change an understanding as thoroughly as a confrontation. Let reputation operate through repeated treatment—someone is trusted with keys, asked to witness a document, left alone with a child, quietly excluded from a gathering—never through narrated praise.\n\nMake each irreversible choice legible in its immediate setting. Show the alternative that actually existed through another character's suggestion, a remembered warning, a practical inconvenience, or an honest statement of what could have been done. Keep the tragic character intelligent and caring even as the choice destroys something they value, and let warmth and ordinary contentment exist before the fall so that the loss is real.\n\nLet recognition arrive through a particular event or conversation: a familiar object seen in a new way, the character's own words quoted back, a silence finally understood, the cost someone else has been paying becoming visible. Afterward, stay with the concrete aftermath—the unset table, the returned key, the unfinished work, the person who now eats alone—without explaining the moral or dissolving the loss into comfort or punishment.\n\nUse the Nordic context the world actually supplies, with its naming, institutions, and social expectations kept specific, and keep Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish contexts distinct rather than blending them into a generic Scandinavia; add no snow, isolation, reserve, or bleakness the world does not contain. Weave required private interiority through the scene as ordinary narrative thought, sensation, and memory, visible or wrapped exactly as the active contract directs. A scene may end after a completed exchange, a quiet realization, or an unfinished task; no confession, reconciliation, or change of course is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nFormat the story as a screenplay: scene headings whenever place or time changes, concise readable action lines, and speaker-led dialogue. Make the tragic sequence legible in what can be seen or heard. Where prose might write 'He decides,' the script shows the pause, the reach for a coat, the door opened or left closed, the envelope set down, the chair left empty.\n\nCarry competing loyalties through physical objects and ordinary courtesies. A character cleans a room for a guest they dread, leaves a light on, returns a borrowed tool, stands at a threshold without entering. Test each commitment in repeated specific actions: who is told a piece of news first, who is asked to wait outside, whose name is left off a list. Because the form has no narrator, every obligation must register in scene action or dialogue; use voice-over only when the chosen sequence genuinely needs it, never as a running explanation of motive.\n\nStage the irreversible choice as small concrete steps within one scene or across a few scene headings. Make the alternative visible through another character's line, a sensible suggestion declined, or a practical path left open and not taken. Keep the tragic character articulate and purposeful even as the choice costs them; do not signal disaster with shuddering or flailing. Let warmth appear—a shared meal, a joke, a hand on a shoulder—so the fall has weight.\n\nTurn recognition into a staged event. A character sees a familiar object in a new place, hears a phrase repeated back, reads the letter that explains an old silence, or watches another person perform an action they once refused. The line or the action carries the new understanding; add no explanatory aside. Afterward, a brief practical scene can show the same room changed, a second person clearing away what the first left, an ordinary gesture repeated without its old warmth. Trust the held image rather than a concluding speech or lesson.\n\nUse cuts only for an actual jump in time or place, and write no camera commands or lighting notes. Where the active contract requires private prose, keep it as a clearly attributed nonspoken note in the presentation—available to the reader, never to the cast, and never a substitute for what the scene shows. End where the scene's boundary falls: after a completed action, an interruption, or a silent return. No confession, closure, or new plan is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script: clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions a company can perform. Let the tragic progression live in the relationship between lines and physical business—a character sits in one chair rather than another, picks up a glass but does not drink, returns a letter without unfolding it, leaves by the same door they once refused to enter.\n\nEstablish competing loyalties as stage relationships built from repeated visible habits: a character routinely checks a room, serves one person first, takes off their coat for someone, stands near one door and not the other. Let reputation arrive through how others speak to and about them—who is interrupted, whose advice is repeated, whose absence is noticed—rather than through direct praise. Give the shared space its generous room: a walk around the table, a pause at the window, a story told while folding laundry can shift an understanding as much as a confrontation, and travel and explanation happen inside the scene rather than between speeches.\n\nMake the irreversible choice detectable through stage business rather than a narrated announcement. Let a sensible alternative appear in another character's line or as a physical path not taken. The tragic character speaks clearly and can account for their decision even as it costs them; do not reduce them to a flailing victim. Preserve ordinary warmth—a joke over dinner, a familiar gesture, a moment of ease—so the eventual loss has force.\n\nStage recognition as a theatrical event. A character repeats a phrase another said earlier, sees a prop in a new way, extends a hand and lowers it, or hears a line delivered with changed meaning. Let the pause before speech, the direction of a look, or the decision to sit carry the moment. Then continue into practical aftermath: a table cleared, a coat left behind, a chair moved, a door closed. The play can end on such an action without a final explanatory monologue.\n\nKeep monologues and asides purposeful, using them only where the active contract authorizes performed speech, never as a replacement for private thought; required interiority stays nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, not something another character can overhear. Write no film-camera instructions. The response may end after an exit, a pause, or an unfinished action; the tragedy need not complete itself inside it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind, and let the telling proceed by actual mental movement: a present perception calls up a memory, which calls up a phrase someone once said, a bodily sensation, a question, an image of a hallway or a road, before the attention returns to the ongoing scene. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations. Anchor the movement in repeated concrete particulars—a particular window, a set of keys, the sound of a door, one person's way of clearing a table—so that events stay distinguishable from recollection and fantasy, and mark clearly where a remembered scene begins and ends.\n\nGive the mind its gravity from the conflict between private attachment and public obligation. Remembered promises, inherited duties, old debts, and family or community expectations surface as fragments, interruptions, and obsessive reformulations attached to present sensory detail, not as ordered flashback scenes. Return to particular moments—when the narrator promised to be somewhere, failed to answer, carried out a duty, heard someone else refuse—while the other people keep speaking, moving, and responding. The stream does not absorb them, and it opens no one else's mind.\n\nMake the tragic choice legible as repeated attention. The mind circles the alternative—the door not opened, the letter unsent, the visit postponed, the obligation declined—both before and after the choice, so the reader sees that it was genuinely available. Keep the narrator intelligent and reflective; the destructive course need not come from confusion. Let warmth persist inside the stream as sensory memory—a remembered laugh, the feel of a familiar coat, the taste of a shared meal—so the loss stays concrete.\n\nLet recognition arrive as a reformulation within the stream. A familiar image recurs and connects to another memory or a present fact: what a particular silence meant, why someone always stood at that window, what the narrator's own repeated phrase revealed. The prose may halt, repeat the image, or double back into the past before settling; do not compress the moment into a moral summary. Let it change the weight of the anchor, then let the mind keep moving into the immediate aftermath—the present room, the body still seated, the coat still on the chair, the unfinished task. Required interiority lives in this same stream, visible or wrapped as the active contract directs. The response can end mid-thought or mid-perception; the association need not resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the story in a readable transcript of sender-attributed messages. Include a timestamp, delay, edit, read receipt, attachment, or channel change only where it matters to the tragic progression or to understanding a person. Mark unsent drafts clearly as drafts and never present them as delivered. Keep private thought out of the transmitted text; where the active contract authorizes a parallel presentation of interiority, set it apart and label it as separate from the transcript, and admit context from outside the exchange only when required, clearly separated.\n\nMake competing loyalties visible in digital habits. One person is messaged immediately while another waits; one friend is added to the group chat and another left out; full sentences go to an elder while a peer gets terse replies; a voice note replaces text where text would have done. Let reputation accumulate through such treatment—one person's messages are always answered promptly, another's sit unread, a third's habitually arrive with an apology for the delay. Allow characters room to explain themselves at length when the relationship warrants it, so that the words carry real weight.\n\nRender the irreversible choice as a message action or failure: a message sent to the wrong person, an important question answered at the wrong moment, a draft deleted, an invitation declined after a long silence, an attachment shared or withheld. Make the alternative intelligible through an earlier exchange or a clearly presented draft—someone proposed a meeting, offered help, asked a question that went unanswered. Keep the tragic character intelligent and articulate in what they write; the fall need not be a matter of typos or confusion. Leave warmth inside the thread—a running joke, a remembered photo, a habitual goodnight—so the loss has force.\n\nLet recognition come through rereading or a change in sender behavior. A character scrolls back and sees the pattern of delays; a familiar sender writes in an unfamiliar tone; a read receipt appears after a long silence; an unsent draft is found or finally completed. The transcript itself carries the moment—the order of messages, the timestamps, the gap between them revealing what the words alone did not. Afterward, a brief aftermath suffices: a final unanswered message, a chat muted, a group left, a photo changed, with no explanatory paragraph. The exchange may end without any reply; no closure, reconciliation, or follow-up plan is owed. Do not invent glitches, screenshots, or reactions merely to supply subtext.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Visual Novel\n\nPresent the story in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response. Open each scene with enough orientation—where the character is, who is present, what has just happened—to picture it, and keep scene changes legible. Treat background and expression cues as purposeful references to the moment's emotional temperature, not as automatic truth indicators or a label owed to every line.\n\nLet competing loyalties unfold through the scene structure itself: a series of clearly separated scenes in which the focal character stands with one person, accepts an invitation, carries out a promise, or answers a question. Make reputation matter through repeated scene details—someone is always at the door, another is always given a seat, a third is seen leaving early—rather than through direct praise. Give conversations their room for explanation, travel, and relationship: a walk through changing backgrounds, a meal with several exchanges, a long talk in one room. Keep the focal narration present so the weight of each decision is available without entering anyone else's mind.\n\nShow the irreversible choice as an action within a scene, never a menu option: taking an object, leaving a room, refusing an offer, sending a message, speaking the difficult sentence. Make the alternative intelligible through an earlier scene or another character's line—someone suggested another path, offered help, asked a question that was never answered. Keep the tragic character articulate and purposeful; the decision is painful but coherent within their values. Preserve warmth between characters—a shared joke, a comfortable silence, a familiar gesture—so the fall has a cost.\n\nStage recognition as a familiar element re-seen. The character returns to a room, an object, a background, or a line that appeared before, and the focal narration understands it differently; the reader sees the change in how the prose treats the detail—the same window described with knowledge of what happened beside it, the same phrase quoted with its true weight, the same gesture recognized as the last. Do not let a labeled expression change carry it alone; dialogue, action, or the focal realization does the work. Afterward, a brief aftermath scene can show the changed situation—a character alone in a familiar room, an object left behind, a conversation that cannot be resumed. The scene may end without a choice screen, confession, or new plan; the style adds no branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images beyond what the world and active contract already authorize.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqalbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jin Yong / Web Serial\n\nWrite the tragedy in prose installments. Begin each installment with enough of the active situation—where the character stands, what promise or obligation is pressing, what has just changed—that a reader can re-enter without a full recap, recovering earlier context in a sentence or two only when it helps. Keep a readable through-line: each installment advances the tragic accumulation through a particular choice, refusal, discovery, or piece of work, and need not be a complete miniature chapter; one may end mid-scene or after a small consequence.\n\nCarry competing loyalties across installments through recurring commitments and shared history. A promise made in one installment is invoked, kept, or broken in a later one. Reputation builds from small repeated behaviors over time—who is asked to help, whose advice is followed, who is left waiting, who is told first. Give relationships room to breathe through long conversations, journeys, and shared tasks, so the fall never reads as a sudden change of personality.\n\nBuild the irreversible choice out of earlier alternatives. In one installment someone offers a different path, asks a question that goes unanswered, or makes an arrangement the tragic character does not take; later, the situation returns to that same possibility, and the refusal—or the belated acceptance—carries the weight of elapsed time. Keep the character intelligent and capable throughout; the tragedy is a conflict between equally real commitments, not a failure of understanding. Store warmth in the earlier installments—shared meals, inside jokes, acts of practical care—so that later ones can show what was lost.\n\nStage recognition as a later installment's recollection. A familiar detail returns—a phrase, an object, a room, a gesture—and means something different because of what has happened since; spend a moment with the character's inward realization, then continue into the aftermath rather than explaining the moral. Keep elapsed time real regardless of prose length: a long conversation stays long, a short errand stays short. Close an installment after completed work, a pending request, a quiet return, or an unanswered message; an occasional hook is welcome, an artificial cliffhanger is not, and no confession or dramatic revelation is owed. Let required interiority appear as ordinary thought and sensation, visible or wrapped as the active contract directs.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite through a familiar attachment that has become the site of the tragedy. Choose one repeated object, routine, phrase, or place the point-of-view character used to share with the person now lost, estranged, or implicated. Render it through the active narrator's actual distance and diction, never through a stock vocabulary of suffering. Let the character handle the thing, perform the habit, or hear the phrase internally with its old meaning vividly present, then let the new circumstance collide with that memory so the familiar detail takes on different weight without the earlier warmth being erased.\n\nMake consequences land through small admissions and withdrawals rather than swells of confession. When the character almost says what they need or what they regret, show in the next beat what the withholding costs: a pause that lets the other person misunderstand, an apology that arrives too late or with the wrong emphasis, an action that cannot be taken back. Do not force every exchange into rupture. Comfort can be clumsy or partial, and an act of care can coexist with the knowledge that it will not restore what was lost. Keep both people intelligent and particular; even when the choice is destructive, the reader sees what made the alternative real and why it went untaken, so warnings and exit paths remain intelligible inside the turbulence.\n\nLet recognition revise rather than repeat. When a callback returns, use the recurrence to show what the character now understands about the attachment, their own role, or the other person, and anchor it in a concrete setting—the drawer where the thing is kept, the hour when the routine happens, the particular silence after the phrase—so the treatment stays intimate and material rather than torrential. You may end on an unfinished need, an unspoken thought, or a recognition that changes what the loss means without repairing it. The turbulence lives in the strain of continuing with what cannot be undone, not in catastrophe. Preserve the active narrator, person, and world facts, and draw the repeated detail from what the scene already supplies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen inside an ordinary shared routine that carries the relationship's history. Let one character perform it—making the same drink, checking the same route, using the same nickname, handling a familiar object—while another participates or conspicuously does not. Keep the presentation scannable: readable paragraphs, unmistakable dialogue attribution, speech followed by action and reaction. If a habit or phrase recurs across the exchange, let each occurrence carry a different weight—an early one neutral or even tender, a later one bent by what has happened—so the callback speaks through changed context rather than a stated lesson.\n\nAdvance the tragedy through the exchange itself, not between scenes. Embed one small irreversible movement in the interaction: someone refuses to admit a need, gives away something that cannot be reclaimed, corrects a misunderstanding too late, or lets a familiar phrase become a reproach. The other character may notice only part of it. NPCs keep behaving like people with their own business: they may leave, keep working, offer awkward comfort, or return to the routine with a difference, and they need not confront or forgive on cue. Write consequences causally—a withheld apology closes a door, an admission of care makes the loss more specific, a joke takes an edge the speaker did not intend and cannot unsay. Where the active contract gives you interiority, keep it as that character's actual private experience inside the established format.\n\nLet the continuation follow from the current exchange. Do not manufacture a cliffhanger, a new puzzle, or a shouted question; the reply can end after a quiet recognition, a character leaving the room, a familiar object set down differently, or a reply that fails to land. Leave enough live state—an unfinished task, an approaching obligation, a changed tone—that the next turn starts without a reset. Do not use the tragedy to deny warmth or wit: tenderness can surface in a single unguarded line, and humor can be a coping strategy that then costs something. Preserve the selected narrator, person, and world facts; the roleplay form supplies clarity, not a mandate to break every conversation into a dilemma.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the vernacular episodic route: chaptered, connected scenes of remembered communal life, told in the plain storyteller's movement of the language and period the active setting supplies. Do not write a national temperament; write the particular manners, work rhythms, and speech the world already gives you. Open each scene inside a concrete routine of that community—how a meal is prepared, a chore divided, a message passed, a market day or festival observed—and let the tragic pressure enter through these routines rather than through announcement.\n\nChoose two or three community objects or phrases as recurring callbacks across the chapters: a bell, a basket, a gate, a greeting, a dish, a ledger. Establish the first occurrence as ordinary daily use. After the irreversible choice or loss, return to the object in a later scene so its meaning changes while its daily function remains, and let recognition arrive through changed behavior around it rather than through the object itself—someone starts avoiding the gate, the dish is served with the wrong ingredient, the greeting is repeated with a silence after it. Distribute the noticing among different members of the community so no single voice explains everything, and keep the loss legible as a choice by showing the intelligible alternative that can no longer be taken.\n\nUse chapterlike shifts to mark passing time and continuing aftermath. A new scene may open with an image that parallels an earlier one, provided the concrete changes make the loss felt rather than merely cited. Keep warmth and humor tied to particular people and occasions—a joke between two neighbors, a child's mistaken enthusiasm, a small act of care that pains because of what it recalls. An episodic interval can close on ordinary continuation—a person leaving for work, a household sitting down to eat, a watchman beginning his round—so the loss stays part of life instead of a staged curtain; the scene may equally stop mid-routine where the turn's natural boundary falls. Preserve the active narrator, location, and time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nAnchor the tragic reframing in a physical object the point-of-view character handled daily: a tool, a garment, a cup, a photograph, a watch, a repair done together. Write the first handling in hard, concrete sentences—weight, temperature, edge, worn spot, sound—so the use itself carries the shared history without explanation. When the scene moves from ordinary use to aftermath, cut with a forceful transition: a clock striking, a door forced shut, a dish breaking, a phone going dead. Compress the connective tissue and keep the material facts sharp.\n\nAfter the irreversible choice or loss, return to the object and render its changed condition precisely—broken, stained, torn, stopped, held differently—and treat the callback as a fact of wear and use, not a metaphor standing in for explanation. A scratch does not announce guilt; it operates through the scene's physical continuity while the body does the rest: posture changes, a hand shakes, a worker repeats a task that no longer means what it did. Keep the stakes physical, and allow a longer thought when the character has to reason through what has happened; otherwise cut to sensation and action.\n\nMake the recognition specific through comparison. The character sees the object now against its remembered ordinary use without narrating the full meaning, or another person handles the same thing and shows a different understanding—a child picks it up, an old companion returns it, an employer sets it aside. Do not require a confession or reconciliation. The high contrast comes from placing tenderness and damage in the same immediate texture: humor stays possible but harsh, a bitter joke grounded in a real speaker, rough tenderness that costs the speaker more than the listener knows. Vary rhythm deliberately—one long sentence when the character takes in the whole situation, a short one when the change lands. The scene can close on a compressed action or image that keeps the loss visible without wrapping it up. Preserve the selected narrator and world; this is the tragedy of daily attachment, not a fight for existence unless the supplied world already contains one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through authored documents, each with a clear writer, an intended recipient or purpose, and a moment of composition that governs what it can know. Choose a narrow set of recurring forms that reflect the shared routine—a daily note, a shopping list, a work log, a series of messages, a calendar entry, a repair receipt, a photograph with writing on the back—and let those two or three forms return, their small changes marking time. Do not rotate through every available format.\n\nA document composed before the loss cannot anticipate it; its tenderness, irritation, or ordinary observation becomes significant only because of what later entries reveal. After the irreversible event, let the next document show the writer trying to continue the same routine: a list copied from the previous week with an item crossed out, a message repeating an old phrase without its usual closing, a log giving the same task in a shorter, brittle note. Let the tragedy emerge through these discrepancies, gaps, and failed continuities, and use the material form as evidence—a letter folded too many times, a record corrected by a second hand, a photograph creased where it was once kept. An unsent draft is still a composed artifact: it shows what its writer chose to put down, not a complete live mind, so work through its omissions and awkwardness rather than making it confess everything.\n\nWhen the people are separated, competing accounts are available: two writers describe the same incident differently because of what each knew or concealed, and the reader does the reconciling. A later annotation, an unanswered message, or a reply arriving late can supply the recognition; do not append an omniscient explanation after each record. Keep the sequence locally readable at every turn with enough concrete fact—date, place, name, object, task—to be followed without outside narration. Repeated domestic phrases inside the documents carry the warmth: an old nickname, a habitual instruction, a remembered joke, each acquiring new meaning after the event without an editorial sentence naming the change. Preserve the selected person and ownership: if the active contract permits only one character's documents, use reports, receipts, or public notices that character could obtain rather than another person's private diary. Where the active contract separately requires private interiority, write it as actual nonspoken experience in the established presentation instead of substituting another letter. The document form supplies the frame; the loss lives in what the writer could not say or could not undo.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite comfort as action, not as assurance that everything is fixed. Choose a familiar detail of the shared life—a favorite food, a tea temperature, a blanket's placement, a way of opening a window, a song on the same device—and give a character the intimate knowledge to perform that care accurately. Let the effort be noticed by its recipient or by someone else present: a cup placed within reach, a walk taken at the usual hour, a joke told in the old way. Warmth is felt through concrete physical choices, not through characters announcing that they are comforted.\n\nKeep the tragedy a fact inside this care. The character may still be grieving, missing someone, or facing the practical consequences of the irreversible decision, and comfort does not dissolve those problems or make everyone agreeable. Care may be the available response to a sudden event: a household keeps making tea, a neighbor waters the same plants, a coworker leaves food at the door. Because the gesture repeats what used to be ordinary, let it carry a small ache. Do not require a confession, a tearful breakthrough, or an emotional repair; affection can stay laconic, someone can refuse comfort or accept it while keeping distance, and disagreement and privacy survive. A soothing scene stays loyal to the genre: the aftermath is not healed by a warm dinner, but the dinner can still be genuine and shareable.\n\nUse repetition structurally. Return to the same comforting object or routine as the scene allows, each occurrence shifted by time and consequence—the first time a character cannot finish the meal, later they help prepare it, later still someone else uses the familiar gesture—so the meaning changes without explanation. Let a closing beat hold the contradiction: warmth and loss in the same small moment. You may end on a tiny pleasure—a cup held, a shared laugh, a pet settling between two people—while irreversibility stays present in the background, and you may equally stop in the middle of care still being given. Do not use fluff to erase danger, conflict, or agency where the active world includes them; this is the tenderness that can survive inside tragedy, not a domestic vignette replacing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Formal Literary Novel\n\nLet the paragraph enact the relationship between public appearance and private repetition. Open a scene with socially legible setting or ceremony—a family gathering, a workplace ritual, a street known to all, a day when people perform expected roles—then move through a deliberate transition into a private, repeated detail: a drawer, a phrase, a task, an object the character shares with someone now estranged or gone, and back out again. Where the point of view permits, the narration may frame the moment through social history or communal judgment; it must not become an omniscient lecture that knows every mind. In closer narration, let the social world arrive through what the character notices—how neighbors look at the unchanged table, how a coworker takes a different tone with the familiar task.\n\nBuild the recurrence across prominent positions in the chapterlike passage. The same object or phrase can appear in the outward ceremony, again in private recollection, and again after the tragic event, with the narrative voice shifting register slightly: early, the detail is named as ordinary; later, it carries the weight of what has been learned, shown through observation, comparison, and measured sentence rhythm rather than announcement. A returned gift can appear first in a remembered birthday, then in a drawer that will not open, then in another character's polite question during a visit. Let social exchange carry the recognition: someone says too much, someone too little, and the narrator sees what the community does and does not allow to be spoken, while the loss remains consequential and the aftermath continues in the novel's social fabric.\n\nTreat correspondence and ceremony as composed social objects when they enter: observe a letter's phrasing, timing, or physical condition; use a required public appearance to contrast with private breakdown or private resolve. Distinguish narration from ordinary speech—dialogue keeps its register while the narration composes longer sentences with comparatives, temporal shifts, and social vocabulary—yet vary length and rhythm so the form stays readable. Preserve the active point of view: a limited narrator cannot summarize another character's hidden motives, only what that character shows. A passage may close on a composed observational image or a social gesture that leaves the loss visible rather than on a neat moral, and it may equally end while the social occasion is still moving.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBuild the scene as a telling with an audience, an occasion, and a present purpose: one person must make another understand an already completed loss—on a return after a long absence, while a shared room is being emptied, over a letter read aloud, or on the night the listener finally asks what happened. The teller is not narrating for suspense. The telling exists to secure forgiveness, prepare a separation, explain a decision, or show why a shared routine can never resume, and that purpose shapes what the teller selects, stresses, and hurries past.\n\nOpen in the frame with one repeated thing from the teller's and listener's ordinary life together: a lunch order, a train announcement, a call at the same hour, a particular key, a joke one of them always made. Let the frame treat it as familiar, worn, even mildly annoying. Do not editorialize its meaning; its later return will do that work.\n\nWhen the embedded account begins, keep the two levels distinct through the teller's emphasis rather than labels. Give the inner story the same concrete daily grain as the frame, but from a time the listener did not witness. Plant one choice that seemed small when it was made—accepting a delay, keeping a promise, hiding fatigue, staying one more evening—and let a later moment inside the embedded account reveal what that choice had already committed someone to. Keep the teller's loyalties visible in what the account lingers on and what it passes over quickly; a generous account can be sincere while omitting something the teller never understood.\n\nDelay the explanation of the loss. Break the embedded story once or twice with the frame's present: the teller hesitating at a familiar phrase, the listener asking a practical question, the light in the room changing, the listener noticing that the teller has slipped into the past tense without realizing it. These interruptions belong to the occasion itself, not to cliffhanger devices.\n\nAt recognition, bring the opening repeated thing back—inside the embedded account, or on the teller's lips—and let the listener understand that its old comfort was the outward shape of a commitment whose cost is only now visible. The repetition must not undo or repair anything. It makes the loss specific and leaves the listener with a changed relationship to a daily habit as the tragedy's aftermath.\n\nClose the frame on that private consequence: the listener noticing the ordinary object differently, repeating the shared phrase with a heavier meaning, or performing one small routine alone. The outer occasion may remain partly unfinished—no reconciliation, practical decision, or final explanation is required—so long as the irreversible fact and the changed understanding of the ordinary life around it have been delivered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-tragedy-04",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Tragedy · 04/07",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == tragedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Tragedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 4 of 7. }}{{// Source group: recipes-tragedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaqamai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nTake the Romantic and uncanny route. Use calm, syntactically complete sentences at the selected narrative distance and in the selected person. They may lengthen around something the permitted focal experience cannot yet name, without imitating translated German word order or replacing the active viewpoint.\n\nChoose one ordinary repeated object or habitual act from the character's shared daily life: a clock wound each evening, a particular window, a set of keys, a song played at the same hour, a walk along the same path. Present it first inside a routine the character loves or at least relies on—warm, amusingly familiar, or simply relied upon. Do not foreshadow its later role with manufactured dread.\n\nLet the estrangement enter gradually and perceptually. The interval changes; the sound arrives at an unexpected moment; the face in a photograph seems slightly shifted; the worn step catches differently. Write these as things the character notices, not as confirmed supernatural events: the world should feel unreliable while remaining entirely natural, and no ghost, monster, or hidden mechanism may be produced afterward to explain them. Let the source of unease follow the established world and events. A mistaken belief, promise, or silence can matter when present, but the profile does not assign fault or supply an explanation.\n\nWhere the character tries to restore a valued routine, let the result follow the commitments and changes the story has actually established. A restoration may succeed in part or in full; if the old arrangement has become impossible, make the specific reason and loss legible. Mark the break with one callback to the opening: the object that was shared comfort now appears as a reminder of what went unsaid or undone. Render dawning awareness within the active narrator's access, with any authorized change of focal mind clearly distinguished.\n\nRecognition arrives when the character sees that the ordinary habit itself was the vessel of the loss, not a refuge from it. Afterward the uncanny subsides; the fact remains irreversible, but the object can return to being an ordinary object whose weight has changed. Close with the character performing or abandoning the routine once more, without sudden consolation, in a final sentence that registers the quieter isolation this understanding has installed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the story in a present-day city where one old authority still operates through practical permissions rather than supernatural power: a family, a board, a landlord, a church, a firm, a neighborhood council, or a similar inherited network. Show its reach through concrete urban material detail—who holds which key, which staircase is watched, whose name opens a door, which messages arrive unread, whose photograph stays in a locked office.\n\nGive the protagonist one shared, repeated habit with someone they love: a morning call, a regular meeting place, a food one buys for the other, a routine errand, a password phrased as an old joke. Let it be genuinely warm and practical, with room for tenderness or running humor; do not make it sinister from the start.\n\nBuild the turning point as a collision between the habit and the authority's demand: information, time, loyalty, or silence that cannot coexist with the loved one's trust. Have the protagonist first try to protect both by adjusting the habit slightly—delaying the call, meeting elsewhere, sending a different message, hiding fatigue—and write this adaptation as intelligent care, not as obvious corruption.\n\nUse the atmosphere to expose the private life rather than decorate it. The familiar meeting place begins to feel staged; the loved one notices the changed rhythm; a room that once felt safe shows its surveillance potential; ordinary words start reading as warnings. Keep the horror personal and social. The masks are the composed faces the protagonist and the authority require in particular rooms, and they can fall away in trusted company at a cost. No hidden bloodline, power, faction, or supernatural entity may resolve anything.\n\nLet the tragedy occur when the protagonist must choose whom to fail. Keep the choice morally untidy: the authority's demand may also protect someone else, and the loved one's need may be partly unreasonable or unknowable. Put the decision in a specific place where the city's detail pressures it—a train leaving, a door closing, a phone ringing, a message arriving.\n\nAfterward, bring the shared habit back exactly once: as a gesture the loved one still performs without knowing, or as a routine the protagonist can no longer maintain. Recognition changes what the loss means—the protagonist sees that the authority was not an external monster but a claim already allowed to live inside the daily affection. Close with the protagonist alone in an urban space carrying that recognition, with no promise of revenge, rescue, or redemptive exposure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the oral, epic, and frame-tale route. Let a definite narrator take part in a community's retelling of an already completed loss, inside whichever communal occasion the story supplies: a family gathering, a court, a village meeting, a wake, the aftermath of a ritual, a shared meal, or a journey on which an older speaker finally recounts what happened. Keep the teller's language, region, community, and period concrete as supplied, and do not impose religious observance, code-switching, or a single family structure on characters who would not recognize them.\n\nChoose one repeated household object, phrase, or routine the community associates with the lost person: a dish prepared for the same festival, a song sung while working, a bench where someone sat, a blessing spoken at the door, a set of keys passed from hand to hand. Introduce it first in ordinary use, with warmth or mild friction, so listeners meet it as familiar rather than symbolic.\n\nWhen the embedded account begins, let the teller—or a chorus of speakers—interrupt with remembered variations: who taught the phrase, how the object was mended, when the routine first broke. Make these interruptions load-bearing. Each one should add or dispute a piece of the causal chain: what the protagonist believed, what alternative existed, and why it went unchosen. Let different speakers attach different significance to the same repeated detail, and do not settle the meaning early.\n\nLet recognition arrive when the remembered variations suddenly cohere for the listeners. The phrase that once meant welcome is revealed as the moment someone chose silence; the object passed between friends is seen as the last link in a duty that could not be refused. Keep the recognition local and specific—attached to these people, this household, this obligation—never a lesson about fate or national character.\n\nThe telling need not be a single linear confession: the frame can return to the present, listeners can interrupt, and the teller can correct an earlier version, so long as the causal chain stays legible throughout.\n\nAfterward, let the repeated detail remain in living use rather than become a memorial. A younger character prepares the dish imperfectly; a child asks why the song is no longer sung; an elder resumes the old bench. The loss stays irreversible, but continued use now carries the completed knowledge of what it cost. Close on that double use—ordinary and charged at once—without a moral summary or a promise of renewal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the story as a first-person narrator's private entries or dated reflections, using the diary and reflective miscellany route, assembled after a loss. The narrator addresses no audience and performs no public confession. The entries can be short, uneven, interrupted, or repetitive, but each must render lived experience as it was felt—attention, sensation, half-formed reasoning—not summarize what happened. Let the language follow this particular teller: plain or emotional, hesitant or dry, according to temperament.\n\nChoose a repeated seasonal routine or daily habit shared with the person who will be lost: watering a plant at the same hour, eating a particular fruit when the season turns, an umbrella kept by the same door, a walk to a certain bench, tea made at the same time, a train schedule checked out of habit. In the early entries, record it with ordinary care, humor, or mild complaint. Do not call it precious.\n\nMark passing time with concrete seasonal or calendar details drawn from the story's own place—never a decorative scenery inventory—and at some point entangle the marker with the habit: the first cold morning changes the task, the rainy season makes the usual path difficult, year-end cleaning disturbs the shared object, the new year begins without the expected call.\n\nHandle retrospective knowledge honestly. The entries may already imply that something ended; do not manufacture suspense by hiding the outcome. Include one early entry the narrator now rereads differently—a phrase the lost person repeated, an odd reluctance, a task left undone—that was taken as ordinary at the time. Keep what the narrator knew then distinct from what the narrator understands now; the later understanding must not be retrofitted as the earlier motive.\n\nLet recognition arrive inside the diary's own movement: the narrator rereads an entry, recalls the exact words, or performs the habit once and finds it means something else. The repeated object or phrase from the opening returns here carrying the changed understanding, and the loss it marks is concrete and irreversible, not a general melancholy.\n\nEnd the sequence in an aftermath that preserves the ritual in some form: continued alone, allowed to lapse, or replaced by a smaller private version. No resolution or moral lesson is required. Close on a particular observation showing the narrator now living inside the changed meaning of an ordinary thing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWork in the intimate psychological and urban route. Tell the story in first person or limited third person from a single protagonist's consciousness, with the narration holding close to that character's attention and granting access to no other mind. Anchor it in the story's actual city, neighborhood, or household, rendered with the texture this tradition brings to urban intimacy—local speech rhythms, distances, sounds, hours—kept specific to the supplied place, language, community, and history rather than turned into regional symbols. Where the supplied world is Latin American, keep those particulars exact; where it is not, carry over the method, not a relocation.\n\nChoose one concrete domestic attachment that recurs in the protagonist's ordinary life: a locked drawer, coffee prepared at the same hour, the sound of a particular bus, a plant on a balcony, a child's backpack, a table set the same way. Let it first appear as a source of comfort or as a manageable irritation. Do not explain it; let the reader live inside the protagonist's affection for the routine.\n\nComplicate the attachment through relationships, not through a social panorama. Someone else depends on the same routine, resents it, or has a claim to the object. The protagonist begins to notice discrepancies: the familiar gesture now seems rehearsed, a message arrives earlier than usual, the loved one avoids the cupboard, the object has moved. Keep every sign psychologically legible—observed by this particular character, not authorized by an omniscient teller—and never resolve them with a magical event, an external rescue, or any supernatural fact the world does not already permit.\n\nLet the tragic turn occur when the protagonist recognizes that the attachment itself was the mechanism of the betrayal or self-betrayal: the loved one used the daily habit to hide something; or the protagonist used it to avoid asking a necessary question; or the routine was the only safe form of a love that demanded more. Deliver the recognition through a callback—the protagonist encounters the object or gesture again and understands what it was really marking. Make the irreversible consequence a specific loss: a person gone, a trust broken, a promise missed, not a mood of regret.\n\nClose in aftermath. The protagonist performs the routine once more or deliberately leaves it undone, while the city's ordinary texture continues around them, and the final paragraph shows their changed way of inhabiting it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaman::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: name the place in one or two concrete details, then let a repeated daily habit or object carry the opening. First or third person both work; whatever the choice, the viewpoint stays locked to one character's perception.\n\nStage every spatial change visually so the reader can track it—doors, rooms, stairs, vehicles, and the position of the recurring object. Break paragraphs readably around dialogue, action, and the viewpoint character's private response, and give any unfamiliar name or mechanic a clear, brief presentation the first time it matters.\n\nRun one callback detail through the story in three passes: a bento wrapper, a ringtone, a bandage, a train platform, an umbrella left behind, a particular message, a keychain. First show it in ordinary use. Then show a small disruption the viewpoint character notices but cannot yet name. After the loss, show its return. The object must collect meaning across the passes, not merely recur.\n\nMake the tragic turn physically legible. The character sees or touches the object after the event, and a single focused reaction beat carries the recognition—one or two private thoughts, no flood of rhetorical questions or generic grief. Reactions can be serious, uncertain, angry, or quietly funny, but each belongs to its owner; the cast is neither uniformly quippy nor uniformly restrained.\n\nKeep the loss irreversible. No miraculous repair, no melodramatic collapse. If humor appears, let it be the kind that remembers the shared routine with painful specificity rather than a defense that erases what happened.\n\nStop where the scene's natural boundary falls—mid-task, on leaving, or sitting with the object—without requiring a cliffhanger, confession, or external rescue. Let the final beat be the character's changed handling of the routine: setting the keychain down, covering the bento, deleting the message, or deliberately choosing not to, so the callback closes the scene with the tragedy's private weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet one or two shared objects, phrases, or routines carry the scene's lyric attention. First render the object in its ordinary, warm context through rhythmic syntax, recurring consonant or vowel sounds, and images traced to the narrator's senses. Move between a tight concrete image and a wider associative image—the way afternoon light crosses a table, then becomes the light that will outlast a particular habit. Interrupt the patterned sentences with plain statements when a fact or decision needs weight, so the lyricism does not soften the causal consequence.\n\nAfter the irreversible event, return to the same object or phrase with altered syntax. The earlier cadence may now be cut short, or a comparison that once seemed soothing may reveal a harsher implication. This return changes what the object means without explaining that change in a summarizing sentence. Recognition can arrive as a shift in the teller's attention: a detail the narrator noticed without understanding becomes the image through which the loss is felt.\n\nKeep the lyricism from overruling the tragedy's causal landscape. Choices remain intelligible; alternatives remain visible; characters may act well and still suffer. The repeated images and sounds make the fall more particular, not prettier. The narrator may use figurative language, but other characters speak in their own registers, and their dialogue is not a continuation of the narrator's lyric voice. End a response at the moment a repeated object or phrase has just changed in significance, leaving the aftermath present without forcing a completed grief.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present moment contain an ordinary shared routine, then allow a remembered occasion to intrude through a detail: a particular way a kettle sounds, the worn edge of a chair, a phrase someone used to say at this hour. Keep the memory concrete and tied to the present object rather than summarizing the whole history. Move the reflection between what was and what is, showing a small irreversible change—the routine continues, but the person who shared it is absent, or the familiar action now serves a different need.\n\nUse repetition to build attachment across reflections. When a phrase or object returns, let the later context alter its meaning. The first remembrance may recall comfort or humor; after the tragic event, the same detail may stand for what was lost or for the mistake that preceded it. Keep the present scene active during each reflective return: the narrator performs an ordinary task, speaks to someone, or notices something new while remembering. The past and present must coexist, not replace each other.\n\nDo not let reflection erase causal consequence. The narrator may soften a memory, but the narrative still shows the choice, its alternatives, and its irreversible result. The narrator can reflect directly without turning every quiet moment into an announcement of impermanence. Let ordinary pleasure remain pleasurable in the present scene; tenderness and humor that belonged to the lost person can remain vivid without irony. Use the backward glance to make recognition gradual: the narrator remembers a warning or an alternative without seeing it at the time, and only the later perspective reveals how that option was already lost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling: a situated teller recounting or retelling a sequence to a listener or listeners. Do not import a particular national pantheon, religious lesson, or generic honor code. If the supplied world names a language or place, keep its traditions distinct; an Arabic frame is not automatically a Persian one, and Hebrew or Kurdish traditions must not collapse into a single regional flavor. The frame may be brief but remains part of the telling, so the audience's presence shapes what is said and what is delayed.\n\nWithin the frame, narrate the shared life in strongly remembered scenes. Each scene can open with a formulaic signpost—\"He would tell of,\" \"It happened that,\"—then move into the concrete particulars of a routine, object, or phrase that the teller repeats deliberately. Use these recurrences as marks of daily attachment before the tragic event. Let an interjection from the listener, a repeated address to the audience, or a shift to the present moment interrupt the tale at deliberate points, reminding the reader that this is a retelling and that the loss is already past.\n\nNarrate the innermost crisis—the tragic choice and its irreversible consequence—with causal clarity: alternatives visible, actions intelligible, and recognition earned. The frame can then return to the teller and audience after the loss. Their response need not announce a moral; it may be silence, a practical observation, or a question that the teller cannot fully answer. This double time—tale and frame—allows Maeda's repetition to acquire grief: an object ordinary in the tale becomes weighted when the teller lingers on it after the catastrophe. Keep any nested tale subordinate to the central tragedy; do not use a story-within-a-story to replace the main loss. The response may end with the teller pausing or the listener asking for the rest, leaving the aftermath legible without forcing closure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Minimalist Prose\n\nEnter each scene through one or two exact details: an ordinary object, a repeated gesture, a phrase spoken at the same time of day. State them in direct clauses without editorializing. The repetition itself establishes the shared life: a cup set in the same place, a chair pulled back at the same angle, a name said the same way before a small task. Do not embellish these details with adjectives unless the present scene gains something concrete from them.\n\nUse callbacks as the primary emotional lever. After the tragic event, return to one of the exact details once. Let the change appear through the selection and placement, not through explanation. For example, the cup may still be set down, but the second hand that used to steady it is absent; or a phrase is repeated, but the narrator stops before the end. Keep one tight inward sentence when needed to make the causal connection or the recognition clear, but do not accumulate several interpretations of the same moment. The reader must infer the weight from the detail's prior place in the story.\n\nDo not cut the causal chain to achieve brevity. Show the choice, the visible alternative, and the fact that the action mattered. If a warning was given, it must appear once and remain intelligible. Allow people to act sensibly and still suffer. In the aftermath, preserve the felt absence of the lost person or thing without announcing grief. A plain observation—what the narrator notices now, what the room sounds like alone—can end the response; the conclusion remains inferable from the selected detail.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nMove through sustained scenes with natural shifts among what characters do, say, perceive, remember, and privately think. Establish attachment through ordinary routines and familiar details embedded in the scene rather than described in a separate set-piece. For example, a shared breakfast can include a practical conversation, a small joke, and an unnoticed detail that will return later. Use paragraph breaks to orient a shift in time or place without announcing \"later\" or \"the next day,\" unless the transition needs clarity.\n\nKeep callbacks subtle but decisive. A repeated object, phrase, or gesture first appears as an unremarkable part of daily life. After the tragedy, it can resurface in a new scene, and its meaning changes because of what has happened. The narration need not explain the change directly; allow the narrator or focal character to notice the object without immediately summarizing its significance. If a reflection is needed, keep it brief and tied to the present moment. This is how the loss becomes intertwined with ongoing life.\n\nWrite the causal fall through character actions and social constraints, not through sudden melodrama. Make alternatives visible through conversation, hesitation, or another character's advice, but let the protagonist decline them for intelligible reasons. Inward experience is authorized for the focal character according to the active contract; other characters' thoughts remain unavailable unless granted. The narrator's voice may shift between close perception and a more composed sentence, but should not become uniformly wry or distant. After the irreversible event, continue with daily movement: a routine can resume, a room can be cleaned, a conversation can remain unfinished. End a response wherever the scene naturally pauses, allowing aftermath to persist without requiring a thematic summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the central relationship premise concrete through repeated private details: a nickname used only in certain moments, an inside joke, a place the couple returns to, a habit one character performs for the other. Let these elements appear early in ordinary affectionate scenes and be repeated without urgency. Use them to build a shared world that the reader can recognize as specific to this relationship, not a collection of generic couple tropes.\n\nLet the tragedy grow from the actual premise's tensions. If the pairing is a rivalry, the fall can come from a choice that turns their competition fatal or irreparable; if it is a comfort relationship, the loss can arise from a duty outside the bond. Show the characters' affection, jealousy, or need as real, not as mechanical markers. A scene of comfort before the collapse can remain genuinely warm; a moment of jealousy can be serious without erasing the bond. After the irreversible event, return to one or more of the established private details. Their changed meaning is the story's emotional payoff: the same nickname now said by only one person, the shared place become a site of remembrance.\n\nUse scene selection to carry the relationship arc. Each scene should either deepen the attachment, make a warning visible, or show the aftermath. The narrator may give the focal character's inner experience where authorized; other characters remain unavailable. Do not insert miscommunication as a substitute for a real incompatible commitment. Recognition should arrive through the consequence, not through a final line that explains \"this is why they lost each other.\" The response may end on a repeated gesture or phrase, leaving the reader to feel the gap between the earlier warmth and the present absence. Keep the fanfiction register legible through emotional directness and intimate pacing, but the tragedy remains a real arc, not a permanent withdrawal of all affection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nUse an oral-chronicle voice that speaks as if the story is already known, often from a point after the loss. Open scenes or descriptions with compact declarations: \"He was the one who mended the gate each spring,\" or \"She kept the old cup by the stove.\" These declarations establish the everyday attachments that will later become part of what people remember. Then expand into a concrete action or exchange so the declaration is grounded in a particular repeated moment, not a heraldic title.\n\nBuild recurrence through phrasing. A repeated formula—\"This was before the long frost,\" \"The day being short,\"—can mark sections, but give it a new application each time it returns. Within scenes, let shared objects and routines reappear in the chronicler's account: a basket, a walking route, a familiar question. These repetitions carry Maeda's daily life into an oral form. The public voice may note a kindness, an inconvenience, or a small debt owed between people, but it should not turn every household action into a martial contest.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge from a choice whose alternatives the telling can make explicit: the chronicler may say that a different decision had been advised, or that the weather and the need of the household pressed at once. Keep characters intelligent and worthy of regard even when they choose badly; humility or duty may drive the fall. After the irreversible deed, let the chronicle resume with practical continuation—others still collect wood, manage the livestock, set the table—while a repeated phrase or object from before now names what has been lost. Recognition can be public as a community's understanding: the people begin to speak of the event in a certain way, or a previously small habit is remembered only because the person is gone. End a response on a new practical moment that keeps the loss present in the communal account rather than sealing it with a moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the tragedy through the Scandinavian social-realist domestic route, with a definite national and institutional texture settled from the supplied setting rather than a generic Nordic mood. Let the fall grow from ordinary obligations that are publicly legible: a work schedule, a housing arrangement, a family responsibility, a municipal or workplace expectation, a promise made to a neighbor. Show the practical conditions that make two commitments incompatible, so that the characters can name what they are losing and still cannot keep everything.\n\nKeep the viewpoint close to one participant's inward reasoning about a specific necessity. The focal mind weighs the cost of acting or not acting, notices what another person expects, and registers the small daily evidence of strain. Other characters remain intelligent and occupied with their own obligations; they may offer a warning or an alternative that is genuinely reasonable and still insufficient, because the difficulty lies in the structure of the commitments, not in anyone's failure to understand.\n\nSelect one recurring element from the shared daily life—a route, a chore, a phrase, a meal, a room, an object passed between people—and present it first as practical and unremarkable. Do not explain its future significance. After the loss, return to the same element in a scene where its ordinary use has become difficult or impossible. The focal character recognizes the change through attention and behavior: a pause before the usual action, an avoidance, an attempt to continue that stops. Do not have anyone announce that the object now symbolizes grief.\n\nUse the Maeda movement from specific shared attachment to changed understanding. Warmth and humor may appear in the relationships where they belong, but they do not cancel the loss. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a routine that no longer works, a relationship altered by the choice, a place that remains but no longer holds the same daily meaning. Stay within the actual world; do not import folktale or supernatural explanation unless the setting already includes it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragedy as screenable scenes, with scene headings when location or time changes. Let dialogue and action carry the consequential fall. Keep the cast's choices visible: someone can hear a warning, weigh an alternative, and still pick the action that later cannot be undone. Give each scene one clear practical occupation—serving a meal, repairing something, waiting for a call—so the tragedy occurs inside ordinary life rather than in a separate symbolic register.\n\nChoose a recurring object or routine that appears in early scenes without explanation. Show it in quotidian use: opened, passed, cleaned, repeated. Do not lecture about it. Later, when the event has occurred, let the object appear again in a different emotional register. Stage the change through what the character actually does with it: a longer hold, a refusal to touch it, a failed attempt to continue the usual action, or a pause before the familiar phrase. The repetition itself carries the changed understanding.\n\nUse dialogue and blocking to show tenderness or humor where it fits, but do not soften the irreversibility. Required private thought should appear as a clearly attributed nonspoken note set apart from the scene, not as a voice-over or a line the other characters can hear. The note may render the focal character's recognition, uncertainty, or changed feeling about the recurring object, while the visible scene shows only what an observer could see.\n\nEnd a scene after a completed exchange, a new decision, or plainly shown aftermath. No character needs a monologue of self-knowledge; the changed meaning can appear through one ordinary action performed differently.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable tragedy where the stage action exposes the irreversible choice. Use clear entrances, exits, and purposeful objects so that an audience can follow what each character knows and when. Let a warning be given in dialogue and still be insufficient because two obligations cannot both be met. The fall should arise from the incompatible commitments, not from anyone simply missing an obvious solution.\n\nSelect one prop or repeated physical routine—a dish, a key, a shoe, an order of tasks—and stage its repetition in the early scenes. Keep it quotidian, not symbolic. Later, have a character handle the same prop differently. Stage the change through observable behavior: a refusal to touch it, a pause in the middle of the usual action, an attempt to continue that stops, or an effort to give it away. Let the pause and physical change carry the new meaning without a speech explaining it.\n\nUse monologues or asides only when they serve the public scene by changing what the audience understands about the action or relationship. For required private thought, write it as clearly attributed nonspoken authoring material set apart from the dialogue and stage directions, not as lines the other characters hear and not as a performed aside. A character does not need to announce that everything has changed; the audience can see it in a repeated gesture. Let warmth and humor exist where they belong, but do not use them to reverse the loss.\n\nStructure the scene so that the consequences are visible without narration. The tragedy can end in an altered routine, a refused offer, or a silent exit. Do not force a redemption speech or a neat resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the tragedy as one authorized focal mind moving back and forth between immediate perception and charged memory. Let the prose follow that association: a bodily sensation, a sound, a fragment of speech can call up the day the choice was made. Keep enough orientation so readers know which material is present experience, which is recollection, and which is imagined. Do not let the mind become omniscient or invade other characters' private thoughts.\n\nUse recurring objects and small routines as mental touchstones. In early passages, they appear as daily background: a mug set down, a route followed, a phrase said at the same time. After the tragic event, the mind returns to one of those details with changed emotional weight. The object has not changed; the mind's relation to it has. Show that shift through the quality of attention—longer pause, break in syntax, sudden refusal to finish the thought—not through an explicit summary of the loss.\n\nLet tenderness, humor, and irritation remain part of the stream where they fit, because Maeda's profile includes them. The tragic point is not achieved by making every thought bleak. Instead, an ordinary remembered phrase may become painful because it was once light. Aftermath can show the mind trying and failing to restore an old routine, and recognizing that the old meaning cannot be reinstalled.\n\nThe response may stop wherever the association naturally halts: a present action interrupted by memory, a repeated phrase recalled in private with new weight, or a sensation that closes the loop. Do not force a concluding epiphany or require the stream to end on a completed realization.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the tragedy through a readable transcript where each sender has a recognizable digital habit—punctuation, timing, use of read receipts, delay before replying, attachment behavior. Let the irreversible event be visible in the exchange, not just referred to in summary. The incompatible commitments can be stated across messages: someone asks for help, another explains a duty, a third tries to mediate. All can be intelligent and still fail to keep every promise.\n\nEstablish a recurring message element early: a phrase, a sign-off, a request sent at the same time, a shared task discussed daily. Show it as ordinary communication. After the loss, bring back the same element with a different weight. The change should arise from the specific sender's established habit: a familiar phrase now arrives late, goes unsent, loses its usual punctuation, or is answered by someone who no longer responds in the old way. The changed digital behavior, not a dramatic confession, carries Maeda's reframing.\n\nDistinguish clearly between sent messages, unsent drafts, and private thought. Show an unsent draft as an unsent draft, not as transmitted text. If context outside the exchange is needed, separate it from the transcript. Other characters respond only to what would actually appear on their screens. Do not use fake system events or receipts merely to imply subtext, and do not place private thought inside a message bubble as if it were sent.\n\nLet the scene end on a message that lands, an unsent draft, a long gap, or a familiar phrase that now reads differently to the recipient. The tragedy should feel like the ordinary channel has itself become part of what cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqamba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Visual Novel\n\nStructure the tragedy as legible visual novel scenes: each scene-setting passage establishes place, then dialogue and focal reaction move the event. Let the irreversible choice be reached in a scene with visible practical business, so the audience sees the ordinary constraints as well as the decision. Keep speech attribution concise, and do not turn every background cue into a truth indicator.\n\nPick a recurring visual element: an after-school room, a kitchen shelf, a bus window, a repeated item someone always sets down. Introduce it without symbolic commentary. Later, after the loss, return to the same scene element or object. The focal narration may register it differently—through a longer pause, a noticed detail that was previously ignored, a failed attempt to continue the old routine, or a feeling that the familiar thing now belongs to an unreachable time. Do not have a character announce that the object symbolizes grief; the change in attention does the work.\n\nUse focal narration and inner response as ordinary private prose; it is not automatically known by other characters. Let warmth and humor appear in the scenes where they fit, but do not use them to repair the tragedy. The recognition may occur without a branching menu or player choice. A scene can end after a character sees the familiar thing, says the old phrase, and then fails to continue the routine.\n\nKeep scene changes legible with a simple tag like a location or time shift. Do not add executable engine commands, background description fields, or image generation instructions. The style here is narrative and illustrative, not a game engine.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqambb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Jun Maeda / Web Serial\n\nWrite the tragedy as a serialized prose narrative: each installment moves a local part of the consequential fall while keeping the overall through-line legible. Begin an installment by recovering only the necessary context—the current obligation, the person's relationship, the previously established habit—not a full recap. Let the scene advance through ordinary action, dialogue, and focal thought.\n\nUse a recurring routine or object across installments as a Maeda-style anchor. In early entries, show it as part of daily life without comment. In a later entry, bring the same element into view after the irreversible event. Weave the brief recovered sentence into the current scene's observation or action, so the reader recognizes the earlier usage without the story stopping for a flashback. The current scene then shows the character's changed relation to the element through attention, hesitation, avoidance, or a failed attempt to continue the routine. Do not have a character announce that this is the same object from before.\n\nUse hooks that arise from the serial flow: a question left unanswered, a task begun, someone arriving. These are not cliffhanger threats; the installment may simply close on a repeated phrase with altered meaning or on a character noticing that a usual step was skipped. Avoid forcing a full resolution or crisis in every entry.\n\nKeep required interiority in the focused character's ordinary prose. Other characters know only what they observe. The serial should allow the same daily element to reappear several entries later, each time with a slightly heavier relation to the tragedy. That cumulative shift is the main structural technique.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nTreat emotional exposure as an action with a legible mechanism and an immediate practical consequence; the scene does not merely describe hurt but shows what a character does with an admission, a need, a withheld answer, an apology, or a return. Before a line that risks exposure, give the character a concrete small calculation—what could be saved or lost, what must be said first, what can still be guarded—then let the risk be taken or refused in the same moment.\n\nFollow each move to its receiving effect: a direct request may make the other person look at visible work instead of answering, an apology may lower the speaker's practical position, a need that is admitted may be met with a useful but careful kindness. Show the interaction through ordinary resources and pressures—space, time, money, information, obligation—so that emotional strain and practical trouble act as one joined situation. Permit tenderness that remains imperfect: one person may kneel beside another but check the untouched medical kit afterward, or offer food while already reaching for a tool needed elsewhere.\n\nDo not settle every wound. Recognition can arrive after the loss is already irreparable, and the narration may preserve the exact choice that cannot be undone without recovering the damaged relationship. Keep the point of view inside the character who is acting, not inside the person being addressed; the other's response is inferred from visible timing, tone, and movement. End the response where the immediate action and its acknowledged cost are both legible, however much remains unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite in short, readable paragraphs where every spoken line is unmistakably attributed and every practical adjustment—a chair moved, a door checked, a package set down—is shown at the moment it matters. Advance the shared scene through a concrete operation the active character can attempt, using the visible conditions that are already in play: which resource is scarce, which person is waiting, which step can no longer be repeated. Move from decision to result in the same passage so the interaction feels present and rapid rather than pre-explained.\n\nTreat the user's submitted action and speech as one part of a continuing system, not as a prompt that must be answered with a full reversal. An NPC may comply, cooperate, refuse, or return later depending on the active authorship contract, but each response should draw on a visible motive and immediate practical stake—loyalty, fatigue, debt, fear, trust—so that the next continuation follows naturally. Where the tragedy requires a loss, make it the live consequence of a choice already available in the scene: the response may help in a way that risks something irreplaceable, or refuse exactly the aid that would have preserved an incompatible commitment.\n\nLeave the exchange at a point where the scene is visibly continuing: someone has just finished speaking, moved toward a door, set down a tool, or begun a task that does not require a prompt. No question mark, menu, or forced decision is necessary; the NPC's ongoing behavior and the newly visible cost are enough for the next turn. Keep character thought inside the responding viewpoint and out of the minds of others. Tragedy may be present as a slow narrowing of options, not as a hidden requirement to punish every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse a vernacular episodic method: a spare, linked account of real material moves where one episode shows the exact pressure behind a choice and the next shows its price. Do not adopt a classical register or insert historical scenery; keep the tone close to the socially situated language already supplied by the active story. Each brief episode should include enough concrete domestic or practical detail—a door, a loan, a meal, a letter, a tool—to make the consequences touchable, without converting the cast into stock figures of family or duty.\n\nFor Kamachi's mechanical order, give every step a legible cause before its visible result. When a character must act against a commitment, show the small calculation that makes the choice possible: what obligation the room will call due, what private need the faster step protects, what cost has already been counted. The recipient of the act should respond in a subsequent episode, so a request and its rejection or awkward partial answer are heard in different scenes, each one revealing the changed relation. Recurrence should carry the tragedy, with a repeated errand, name, or gesture light in one episode and heavy in the next after new facts are known.\n\nUse the gap between episodes as an expressive resource: omission can show what the character could not yet name, and a later episode may recognize the lost chance without restoring it. Keep all narration in the selected person and viewpoint. The ending of an episode may pause at the completed act and its acknowledged consequence, never at a moral stated for the reader.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the passage tight, with hard nouns and visible wear—scuffed tile, bent metal, a torn cuff, a heavier crate—so that physical state carries part of the tragedy. Move by compressed transitions: beginning, change, result, without transition words that explain the reader forward. Where Kamachi requires a mechanism, write the key calculation as one to three concrete sentences: what condition makes the move work, what the character sees, what will happen if the move succeeds or fails. Then show the action and its result in the next beat; no summary.\n\nLet each paragraph earn its length. A longer thought may be kept when the character is working through a conflict between two live obligations, but it should still touch objects, time, or cost: count the minutes, name the person waiting, itemize what cannot be replaced. Change the rhythm by cutting a result into a short standalone sentence—The door held. She'd already paid.—or by slowing into an exact physical image without sentiment. Ground abrasive speech in its speaker: show the strained voice, the hand that stays busy, the dropped courtesy, rather than making everyone hard in the same canned way.\n\nDo not pre-announce suffering. The tragedy can be shown through the impossible choice and the recorded consequence, with recognition arriving as an unglossed detail: a lighter object remaining where a heavier one used to stand, a fixed lamplight reaching an empty chair, a figure still moving down the street after the decision. End at the completed action or its aftermath. No closing reflection is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through documents that are practical moves, not neutral summaries. Each letter, report, note, or record names its writer, recipient or intended use, and time of composition, and it must change a specific practical relation—payment, access, permission, blame, care, or silence. Write from the document's own purpose: an official report may record times and conditions while omitting the writer's motive; a private letter may explain a moral choice to someone the writer needs or fears; a request may reveal exactly the mechanism Kamachi would make legible by listing what resource must arrive before what step can occur.\n\nLet the document's knowledge or concealment follow its author. A person may record a suspicion without naming it, give a reason that is true but incomplete, or misunderstand an earlier message because one key condition was never stated. Do not add an omniscient corrector after the document; the reader compares the paper's claims, times, omissions, and material traces. Revisions and crossed-out sections are evidence, not decorative texture: a writer may change a number, soften a reproach, or leave a name out because the later cost is already known.\n\nMake the tragic mechanism visible through the sequence. One document supplies the condition another document depends on; the final record may show both the choice and the now irretrievable alternative. Allow a document to arrive too late, to remain unopened, or to be read by the wrong person. Use whichever document form the active scene requires rather than rotating through journal, report, and correspondence. Interiority is present only where a document can legitimately record the writer's thought; do not hide the writer's private mind inside a form that would not contain it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nOrganize each interval of comfort as a small legible operation: the character notices a concrete need or known preference, selects the available means, and performs a tender act whose result is shown—warm tea left in a particular cup, a coat placed within reach, a chair turned so a tired person can sit without asking. Write the kindness through timing, temperature, placement, and familiar objects rather than through declarations. Let the recipient receive it in a way that keeps individual personality: one person may say nothing but finish the drink, another may set the cup aside and still accept a blanket, a third may mention the wrong thing and then quietly remain.\n\nDo not make comfort cosmetic. The tragedy's cost stays active in the exact material detail the kindness cannot restore: the empty seat, the frozen account, the scheduled call, the passport, the door that still must be opened. Warmth does not reverse the loss or melt the difficulty; it makes one moment bearable. A sincere kindness may coexist with the acknowledgment that it is insufficient, and that insufficiency may be unsaid but visible in the silence after the act.\n\nKeep the selected point of view anchored in the acting character, not in the comforted person's mind. Other figures may refuse help, accept it awkwardly, or offer their own small contribution, and they remain fully in the world with their own priorities. The ending need not close the hurt; the response may conclude when the immediate act is complete and its visible effect—or absence of effect—is legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse composed paragraphs with deliberate transitions to hold each scene in a social frame before the tactical move appears. A decision may be introduced through its surrounding conditions: the room's occupants, the known history between them, the obligation that will be affected, the object on which the choice now depends. The narration should distinguish its own reflective, socially situated sentence patterns from spoken dialogue; characters in conversation may be abrupt while the narrative remains measured.\n\nWhen a Kamachi mechanism enters, keep it legible without abandoning the formal architecture. Give the necessary conditions in a subordinate clause or a brief apposite statement—what permission, timing, resource, or past arrangement makes the action possible—then let the action and immediate result occupy their own shorter sentence. Do not turn every decision into a full deliberative essay; the faster consequence can cut against the longer paragraph and make the loss feel proximate. Public and private contrast may be used when the selected narrator permits: a formal occasion can make one private admission more vulnerable, or a letter read in company can give a private bond public consequence.\n\nLet recognition occur through selection and order rather than emotional declaration. The narrator may mention the unavailable alternative only after the chosen route has become irreversible, so the reader sees the cost in what the prose attends to: a chair moved for someone who will not arrive, a schedule kept by the person who was the reason for leaving, an object restored too neatly. End where the event and its acknowledged social meaning are both clear, without collapsing the tragedy into a final aphorism.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the outer scene on a listener already bound to the teller by love, debt, old resentment, or an unfinished promise, and give the telling a purpose the teller needs: an alliance, a confession, a delay, a warning, permission, or a last chance to be understood. Give the occasion practical weight—a vigil, a negotiation, a night before departure—so the audience's attention is itself at stake while the tale runs.\n\nInside the embedded account, run the tragedy as a legible chain of constrained choices. At each step show the resource actually available, the rule or obligation that restricts it, and the specific act that locks the next step in place, so that when a listener asks \"why didn't they just—\", the answer is already visible in the tale's own world: the debt, the oath, the person who depended on them, the sealed door. Treat the embedded story as an active intervention, not a flashback. The listener may interrupt, compare, object, or fall dangerously silent, and the teller can change the tale's speed, emphasis, or omissions in response.\n\nBind the levels with recurrence. A phrase, object, or question from inside the tale can return in the outer room with its meaning altered by everything told in between. Trace the fatal event as intelligible decisions rather than punishment: the choice that seemed right, the incompatibility discovered too late, the moment when one more speech or one more silence was still possible, then the act that cannot be taken back. Let recognition arrive at different levels—the character inside the tale may see only part of it, the teller may understand now what they did not understand then, and the listener may recognize something before either of them says it.\n\nWhen one telling ends, do not restart the same narrative. Let a new tale answer a standing question, reopen a sealed possibility, or surface the cost the teller has been carrying. Bring each telling to a close where the listener's next act—staying, interrupting, refusing, promising—has become a live question; that choice may remain open, be interrupted, or already be under way when the scene breaks. The tragedy is not the last reversal alone; it is the accumulated shape of tales that could have ended otherwise, told in a room where the outcome is narrowing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and developmental realism route. Keep the narration close to one focal character whose choices are formed by a particular family, workplace, neighborhood, or institution, and take the specifics—streets, kinds of work, rental agreements, shifts, inheritances, union positions, school records, the exact obligations people owe one another—from whatever place, language, and institutions the story itself supplies. Social conditions are not backdrop; they create the choices and limits the character will later honor, violate, or fail to see.\n\nWork each turning point as a legible mechanism. When the character acts, make the operative rule or resource observable first: a promise to a parent, a contract, a schedule, a debt, a residence permit, a medical appointment, a custody arrangement, stated in a sentence or two and then tested by the next action. Show the reasoning from available options—not always correct, not always honest, but plausible under the pressure of the moment. When a path closes, let it close on the page: a message not sent, a signature not obtained, a lock turned, a form rejected, a witness who chooses silence.\n\nKeep speech and description measured and concrete. The narration may move between the character's perception and a slightly wider knowledge of the social world, but it must not explain the tragedy as fate or moral commentary; let the sequence of interactions build a structure in which the character's virtues—loyalty, diligence, honesty, hope—become entangled with the very conditions that destroy them. Recognition belongs to the fall: the character grasps the incompatibility of two commitments at a moment when the grasp can still change what happens next, or arrives too late to alter the cost, and the prose makes clear which of the two it is.\n\nIn the aftermath, do not restore what was lost or recast the disaster as inevitable from the start. Preserve the concrete remains—the unread letter, the emptied apartment, the person who still answers the phone, the promise that now means something different—and let the final passages show the character still understanding the mechanism of what happened, with that understanding plainly not the same as being able to undo it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Gothic-Punk\n\nBuild the tragedy on a collision between inherited authority and present life. Make the authority concrete—an old family, a controlling institution, a building with rules of its own, a debt that passes across generations, a restricted archive, an obligation whose ritual form still carries material consequences—and make the present equally concrete: a job, a lease, a night shift, a relationship, a small apartment, a phone that rings with both love and demand. Let the old order be exactly as supernatural or as mundane as the story's world already permits, and no further. The fall comes when a character tries to honor a private desire or a new commitment without escaping the old structure's reach.\n\nUse a legible mechanism for every collision. When the character works the system—a formal request, an inherited right, a claimed exemption—state the relevant rule and the resource in hand, then show why the action fails, half-succeeds, or costs more than expected. The reader should always be able to reconstruct the sequence: what the character knew, what was misunderstood, which practical obstacle made the difference. Keep physical clarity through rapid shifts: a phone call leads to a meeting, a meeting to a signature, a signature to a lock closing. Move fast when actions follow one another; slow down when the character weighs loyalty against freedom.\n\nLet atmosphere come from urban material detail and intimate space—a hallway's fluorescent buzz, a barred window, old incense in a modern room, the temperature of a key, the sound of a door from the street—used to show how the old and new orders press on the same person, never as free decoration. In trusted company a mask can fall: someone admits what they want and what they fear, and that admission should carry weight later rather than merely softening a scene.\n\nKeep the tragedy a chosen one. Let the fall grow from a decision the character makes with open eyes—for example, protecting one person at the cost of another, claiming a legacy known to be poisoned, or refusing an escape that was genuinely available—whichever choice this particular person would actually make. Let recognition, whenever it lands, change what the loss means in the prose. In the aftermath the modern world continues past the disaster—trains run, messages arrive—while the character lives inside the specific absence the choice has created.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and domestic realism route. Set the narration inside one particular community—its language, town or city, family arrangement, and period as the story supplies them—and work through the systems that bind people together: who cooks on which days, who holds the house keys, who answers the phone for an older relative, how money moves between siblings, what a wedding gift commits a family to, which school or employer carries a known reputation. These are not decorations; they are the machinery through which love and obligation are enforced. Do not weld on a generic family structure or obligatory code-switching; this household is particular, with its own history.\n\nGive every decision a legible rule and resource. When a character weighs a move, show the operative constraint—a promise to an aunt, a loan, a marriage negotiation, a property document, a call expected from a relative abroad—alongside what the character actually has to answer it with, so the reader understands why the choice seems protected, necessary, or forced. Then show the move itself: a refusal, a signature, a withheld message, a visit, a lie. Reversals can come fast, but keep the physical sequence concrete—the key turns, the bus leaves, the envelope is opened, the upstairs door closes.\n\nCarry private feeling through specific domestic observation rather than abstract emotion: the weight of a steel plate, a name worn nearly off a household document, the exact moment a relative stops speaking, cooking-oil smell in a stairwell while a hard conversation waits. Let these details show what the character values and what the pressure costs.\n\nBuild the tragedy on a choice that is both rational and ruinous—honoring an old promise against a present love, shielding a brother from a family debt, refusing a marriage or an emigration that would solve one problem and sever another—and let it grow from a sequence of everyday decisions with cumulative consequence, not from a single melodramatic betrayal. Recognition may come early with no clean path out, or only after the loss. In the aftermath, neither restore the family nor exile the character: let the household continue its ordinary rounds with the absence now fixed, and preserve on the page the exact thing that was lost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and psychological realism route. Keep the narration close to a single character in a particular time and place, with the external world recognizable through work, household, neighborhood, or school—while the central movement stays inward: what the character values, fears, refuses to say, and the repeated reasoning that keeps them inside a decision they cannot fully justify to anyone else.\n\nHandle the practical world with legible cause and effect. When a key event occurs—a phone call, a visit, a letter, a contract, a diagnosis, a missed train, a decision about a job or a parent—state the rule or resource that bears on it, and let the character explain the choice to themselves in their own terms: this is my duty, that person depends on me, I owe this, acting now costs me the other thing. Then show the action. Move quickly through exchanges and consequences, but stay physically concrete so the reader always knows where people are and what has changed in the room.\n\nMake private response part of the causal sequence rather than decoration. The character hesitates, re-checks, avoids a question, looks at the object that carries a memory; understanding shifts, sometimes only after the fact. Recognition is the center of this tragedy—the moment the character sees the incompatibility between two commitments or the true cost of a choice. It can come early and deepen, or arrive late and change only what the loss means; either way, make it felt in the prose rather than announced.\n\nKeep the language plain and precise, with no ornament that hides the event. Use seasonal or sensory detail sparingly, and only where it belongs to the character's actual attention: a train announcement, a cold window, rain on a bicycle seat, a letter left unopened on a table should locate the person and carry weight without turning every moment into a symbol. Resist a large redemptive gesture at the end; let the character return to ordinary tasks now changed. The tragedy is not that they were weak—it is that they understood enough to have made another choice, and still made the one that destroyed what mattered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social realism, chronicle, and testimony route: a composed account by a participant who can report their own actions and observations, in whatever person and ownership the story's narration uses. The speaker knows the neighborhood, the workplace, the family, the political and economic pressures, and the particular history that shaped the present. Open on an event or decision that already matters to the community, so the shared conditions are felt from the first paragraph.\n\nGive every important turn a legible mechanism. The narrator is not carried by fate; they act on rules and resources—who controls the papers, which route is affordable or safe, what the landlord will accept, what the union or the party expects, what a relative has promised, what the police will and will not do. State the constraint in concrete terms, then show the specific action—a signature, a bus ride, a phone call, a meeting that does not happen—so the sequence stays physically clear even as events accelerate. When a choice closes a path, let both narrator and reader watch it close.\n\nPreserve the community's specific language, place, and history. Local belief or custom may be present as social texture, but nothing supernatural enters unless the story's world already contains it. The tragedy arises from real conditions—a debt, a strike, a land dispute, a family obligation, a refusal to leave, a promise to a neighbor—and the central character may be intelligent, loyal, and clear-eyed and still choose wrongly because every available choice is costly.\n\nLet recognition live in the speaker's later knowledge rather than in a sudden revelation forced into the action: \"At the time I believed...\" and \"We understood only later that...\" are the natural registers of this voice, and they do not break the participant stance. Make the fall irreversible—a home, a relationship, a position, a child's trust, something no final gesture can repair. In the aftermath, do not shrink the ending to a private grief: the narrator returns to the shared place, carries the changed role, and names what the loss did to the people who remained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Light Novel\n\nEnter at a moment that already contains a practical commitment—a promise, a job, a warning, a debt, a request the main character accepts because the alternatives look worse—and stage the immediate space concretely: where people are standing, what they can see, what is within reach. Use paragraph breaks to mark changes in action, speaker, or focus, and keep spatial movement easy to follow. When a name, rule, or mechanic is unfamiliar, introduce it through action and a brief explanation the first time it matters, then let it be tested.\n\nDrive the fall with legible cause and effect. At each choice, show the rule or resource behind it—\"the deadline was noon,\" \"he had one key,\" \"the contract barred her from leaving without notice\"—then show the action and its immediate consequence. Rapid shifts are welcome, but every reversal must be physically intelligible: the door slams, the message sends, the bus pulls away. Never let speed erase the mechanism.\n\nKeep the tragedy personal and cumulative. Ordinary goals—help a friend, protect someone, get through the day, keep a promise—are enough; each decision narrows the options that remain. Let the incompatible commitments surface early enough that the reader can see the collision coming even when the character cannot. Make recognition a visible event—a sentence spoken, a look, a document, a memory that reorders everything—worn on the character's face and body; it changes what the loss means without undoing it.\n\nGive reaction beats to their owners. A serious moment can stay serious; a frightened character can stammer, freeze, or ask the same question twice; no exchange needs a joke, and no scene needs a cliffhanger. Fast pacing does not mean skirting grief. Let the story continue briefly past the loss into the next ordinary scene—a room now empty, a phone that does not ring, a task finished mechanically—so the weight of what cannot be restored is felt, and do not add a last-minute escape or twist to soften it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the emotional movement of lyric prose carry the causal mechanics that Kamachi's profile makes legible. When a rule, resource, or physical sequence matters, explain it through a comparison or a recurring image that changes what the character knows. Do not translate the mechanism into ornamental description; the image should clarify a consequence or reveal a personal stake. Plain declarative sentences can interrupt the rhythm to state the rule, so the reader understands what will later turn against the protagonist.\n\nBuild a tragic fall from a character's relation to a system they can understand but not escape. Show the alternatives clearly, let loyalty, pride, or love reason the character past each one, and preserve their intelligence: the mistake is not ignorance but the emotional priority that makes a destructive choice feel inevitable. When recognition comes, make it a change in how the familiar image or repeated phrase applies—the same equipment, count, or permission now means the loss is real. Keep the narrator's figuration sourceable to the scene or the teller's memories rather than imposing a uniform solemn tone.\n\nMaintain rapid shifts as shifts in emotional attention as much as physical action. A reversal can arrive as a sentence that moves from technical observation to feeling, but the physical causality must stay continuous; if the chain breaks for the sake of rhythm, rewrite the transition in plainer terms. The reply may end during or after a recognition, but do not require every reversal to complete the fall. The loss that comes should matter because the character and the reader both see how it was made, not because the style demands one final metaphor.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the narrator's reflective attention work upon the specific rules and practical problems that Kamachi's profile supplies. When a mechanism is recalled, explain enough of its operation to make the tragic consequence intelligible, then let a remembered detail carry the emotional weight: who set the condition, what could have been repeated, or why no repetition was possible. Keep the physical sequence recoverable even when memory circles back, because the loss must be seen to follow from a legible event.\n\nUse recognition as a change in temporal perspective rather than a sudden emotional flood. The character may have understood the rule even then and still chose as they did; what changes is knowing which small decision became irreversible. Let alternatives appear through concrete recollections—the unspent resource, the warning given once, the habit that could have been different—not through abstract lament. Allow ordinary pleasure to remain real in memory, so the current loss does not rewrite the good as always already ruined.\n\nDo not require every scene to end by announcing impermanence. Rapid shifts can occur between present action and a past moment that explains the speaker's feeling, but keep the causal link explicit: the remembered mechanism should illuminate why the present is unchanged or finally changed. The response may stop after a single attentive recollection, without forcing a completed lesson or a final quiet sentence. Loss may be long understood or newly arriving, but the tragedy should make its mechanism clear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the framed, reflective oral mode as your definite route from the regional selection. The account is told by a narrator whose memory owns the event and whose listener can ask the practical questions Kamachi's profile demands: how the rule worked, what resource ran out, why the sequence could not be reversed. Keep the telling distinct from the told. When the narrator explains a mechanism, let it arise from the listener's need rather than as an authorial aside, and ensure the explanation remains brief enough not to become a lecture.\n\nShape the tragic fall as a retrospectively understood sequence. The narrator may know the outcome and yet withhold some temporal clarity until the listener has seen the alternatives: the exact point where love or duty chose the shorter term, or the unspent resource that would not have been enough anyway. Recognition becomes the moment when the teller and listener both see the rule's true consequence. After that recognition, the telling can continue into small aftermath details—who kept the object, who repeated the phrase—rather than closing with an abstract maxim.\n\nKeep the regional particularities supplied by the actual setting, not a general 'Middle Eastern' atmosphere. No mysticism, honor code, or antiquity is required. The framed telling may interrupt itself with present-day remarks or questions, but these interruptions should preserve the physical causality of the story. Do not pile nested tales inside one reply. The response may end in the middle of the telling, leaving the listener's next question implied but not answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect only the details that must do double work. Every image, object, or line of dialogue should simultaneously advance the mechanical situation and reveal the emotional stakes. When Kamachi's profile calls for a rule to be understood, state it in one exact direct clause; if it cannot be stated briefly, break its operation across two or three simple clauses rather than narrating all possibilities. Let the character's misunderstanding appear in action, as a miscalculated number, a skipped check, or a resource chosen too late.\n\nUse the character's limited inward attention as the window for tragedy. The prose should often show what they do not dwell on: a warning repeated once, a better plan visible to the reader, a habit that would have saved them. Give enough inference to keep the alternative real, but do not explain why they missed it. The loss lands when the same concrete detail returns changed—the key left once, now the locked door; the battery set aside, now the dark room.\n\nKeep the physical sequence continuous through rapid shifts by making each new sentence the direct result of the last. Shortness is not a quota. A single longer sentence is allowed when it carries irreplaceable causal information; do not pad with fragments that only signal style. The response may stop immediately after a reversal. Recognition in minimalist tragedy is often a small physical act—a hand stopping, a repeated word—that shows the failure is irrevocable without a declarative moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in steady scene-first prose. Let the interaction of rules and resources emerge through what characters do, say, or remember during a practical situation. When a mechanism matters, weave the explanation into the character's perception: a count they check, a condition someone restates, a resource they can see decreasing. Explanation should not halt the scene as a technical aside, but it may be explicit enough that the reader can later reconstruct why an action failed or succeeded.\n\nMaintain the tragic arc within the selected perspective. The narrator can move among action, dialogue, recollection, and inner experience, but knowledge stays with the focal consciousness. Show alternatives not as a list of warnings but as moments of hesitation, another person's advice, or a resource set aside. Let the character choose according to love, duty, pride, or a practical need without losing intelligence. Recognition should change how they see an earlier scene; it may arise from a small sensory detail that now proves the loss.\n\nDo not force a catastrophic turn in every reply. The response may end during an action, a conversation, a memory, or an unfinished task. Rapid shifts remain possible through changes of attention rather than genre machinery; if a dramatic reversal occurs, keep the physical sequence understandable. The prose should trust ordinary details to bear tragic weight when recognition arrives. Preserve the authoring contract's authorized interiority and continue the scene from there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nTreat the established relationship premise as a rule system. The habits, private phrases, known resentments, and repeated comforts between the characters function like resources and constraints: a character can exploit them, misread them, or run them out. When a relationship rule matters, make it legible in the scene—a phrase that one person always says before hiding bad news, a gesture that used to mean forgiveness but now only marks distance. Explain the rule through its effect on the pair's current moment, not through narrative meta-commentary.\n\nBuild the tragic fall from the specific relationship promise, not from a generic betrayal. Give the affection, jealousy, comfort, or rivalry enough space to exist as real warmth before it becomes costly. Warnings and alternatives should be emotionally intelligible: another character names the risk, a repeated moment could have been repaired, a known boundary is crossed because a competing loyalty demands it. The characters remain worthy of attention; their intelligence does not prevent their choices from destroying what they most relied on.\n\nUse callbacks as Kamachi-style reversals. When a short phrase or detail from an earlier intimate moment returns, its operational meaning should have changed: the same promise now constitutes a refusal, the same comfort now withdraws support. Keep the physical and conversational sequence continuous; rapid shifts of feeling can be caused by a recognized rule suddenly applying in a new way. The response may end within an unresolved emotional beat, but do not force a confession or relationship cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the events as a chronicle of deeds whose consequences are already known to the teller. Open with a compact declaration that states the crucial practical rule or resource: an oath that bound a choice, a condition that could not be repeated, a count that had to hold. Then expand into the particular actions that show the rule in operation. When a character misunderstands or exploits the mechanism, make that episode part of the record, using direct clauses that preserve the physical sequence.\n\nUse recurrence as the engine of recognition. The same phrase, name, or gesture returns after the decisive act with a changed meaning; the teller should make the difference explicit through a short clause rather than relying on mere repetition. Alternatives appear as other oaths offered, other ways the resource could have been spent, or a warning spoken once. The characters remain distinguishable and intelligent; the saga's elevated register does not require them to be foolish or to speak in ceremonial formulas without practical content.\n\nThe account may include dialogue that interrupts or contests the chronicle, but the interruption should still clarify mechanism or motive. Let humor be possible without converting grief into parody. The account may end before the final aftermath, with a deed remembered but its full cost still unfolding. Do not import gods or heroic doom unless the supplied setting contains them. Preserve the narrative access and personality appropriate to the selected saga tradition.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nOpen the tragedy inside a contemporary Danish, Norwegian, or Swedish social setting and let the first scene state the governing mechanism through use rather than exposition. A character files a housing application, clocks a care shift, signs a loan document, or submits a workplace form. The narration explains the rule only when the character acts on it: what the form requires, what the deadline assumes, what the institution will verify. Keep the explanation concrete and tied to the physical task—the paper in hand, the portal on screen, the phone call in progress—so the reader understands the rule as a working condition, not a symbol.\n\nLet the character act on a calculation about that mechanism that a capable person could make under real constraints. Show the available correct interpretation in the scene: another character states the safer reading, a posted notice contradicts the assumption, or the form's own wording warns against the shortcut. Then show why this person misses or discounts it—pressure from a second obligation, trust in a colleague, fatigue, a prior success with a similar rule—without making them foolish. The resulting action must follow in a legible physical and social sequence: the form is submitted, the shift is swapped, the payment is deferred, and the immediate consequence appears in the same mundane surroundings.\n\nDrive the fall through incompatible commitments that share one practical resource. A duty to family collides with a duty to work; a promise to a colleague collides with a promise to a spouse; the need for housing collides with the need to tell the truth. Render each collision as a concrete problem the character tries to solve with the rules they believe govern the situation. Their attempts can be resourceful and even temporarily successful—a swapped shift covers the care visit, a deferred payment quiets the landlord—but each partial solution narrows the later options. When a moral instinct constrains a choice, state it as a specific felt limit: what exactly the character refuses to lose, and what that refusal makes impossible in the next practical step.\n\nLet the domestic surface carry the story. Concrete rooms, meals, commutes, forms, phone calls, and institutional waiting areas do the narrative work. Weather, season, and landscape belong to the practical environment—ice on the platform, a dark afternoon commute, a snow-cleared entrance—not to symbolic commentary. Ordinary warmth may appear: a shared meal, a neighbor's help, a moment of humor at the kitchen table. Keep that warmth inside a world where the governing rules still bind, so it does not soften the mechanism or promise rescue.\n\nWhen recognition arrives, let it change what the loss means rather than undo it. The character sees the correct interpretation of the mechanism they misunderstood, or understands who else was harmed, and that knowledge revalues an earlier choice without enabling repair. Render the recognition in the same mundane surroundings where the mistake occurred—the kitchen, the office, the station platform—and let the character sit with the revised fact rather than become suddenly eloquent about the error. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a household continues without the chair at the table, an institution keeps operating, a relationship remains altered. End the response where the recognition has landed and at least one unchanged practical condition remains in view.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Screenplay\n\nWrite the governing mechanism into the character's handling of an object or space. When a character interacts with a rule—a locked door, a sign-in sheet, a payment schedule, a safety protocol—the action line shows the rule and the character's interpretation of it in the same visible moment. The character turns the handle without checking the posted hours, slides the form into the wrong tray while watching someone else, or signs the line without reading the condition above it. Do not have them announce what they are doing. Let the next visible result supply the correction: the door holds, the tray is retrieved, the supervisor points to the unsigned clause.\n\nKeep action blocks concise and causally sequential. Each block advances the mechanism: a phone is checked, a tray is moved, a name is found or not found. Scene headings mark location and time changes; use cuts to move from an action to its consequence. A cut can show the same rule operating in another room—the same form on a different desk, the same deadline on a different screen—but each location adds a concrete fact, not a repeated sentiment. Write only what is seen and heard; do not fill the page with camera commands.\n\nStage incompatible commitments as simultaneous physical business. Two characters try to speak past each other while each continues a practical task: one is counting stock, the other is signing a form; one is packing a bag, the other is holding out a phone. Let their speeches be partial, interrupted, or addressed to the task as much as to each other. The conflict appears in what they are physically trying to accomplish, not only in what they say. Use offscreen sound selectively: a knock, a drawer closing in another room, a phone buzzing can interrupt a scene just as the dialogue reaches a decision.\n\nWhen the irreversible turn occurs, show it in the action line before the recognition. The character completes the wrong step—the form goes into the wrong tray, the door closes behind them, the call ends—and the scene holds on the visible result long enough for the audience to understand, while the character has not yet spoken or reacted. Then cut to the next scene, and let the recognition come later in the body rather than in the same beat. The final screen moment may show the character still moving through an ordinary task, now carrying the changed situation, without an explanatory monologue. The loss remains visible in a prop, a schedule, a door, or a person who does not appear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Stage Play\n\nWrite the tragic mechanism as a prop or blocking choice an actor can perform. The rule, resource, or institutional fact lives in a lock, a ledger, a phone on a table, a door left ajar, a chair that must remain empty. Stage directions make clear who can see, reach, hear, or move between two places. A character's miscalculation appears in their handling of the prop or their movement across the stage before their speech reveals it: they set the ledger on the wrong desk, they leave the door ajar while turning away, they pocket the key instead of returning it to the hook.\n\nLet dialogue carry the everyday practical stakes, not the explanation of the mechanism. Speakers refer to the object they are using, the time they have, or the person they are waiting for. When one character tries to explain a rule to another, write it as the kind of terse instruction or complaint that would occur in the moment: \"The form goes in the blue tray,\" \"They lock this door at five,\" \"I put the key under the mat.\" Another character may not follow the instruction, and the staged consequence shows why that matters.\n\nStage incompatible commitments as competing physical actions rather than debate. One character is repeatedly summoned toward an exit while another asks them to hold a prop. A pause indicates a decision without needing a monologue. When an aside or monologue is used, give it a dramatic purpose: the character addresses the audience to articulate a rule or a consequence, not merely to fill in hidden backstory. Otherwise keep private thought in the provided nonspoken authoring wrapper; it is not something another character can hear.\n\nStage the irreversible turn as a completed action whose consequence becomes visible before the character's recognition. The wrong object is carried to the wrong person; the door is locked before they return; the call ends and the phone is set face down. Let the stage hold for a beat on the physical fact. Then allow the recognition to arrive in a later scene, possibly with a simple line that revalues the earlier action. The final image can be the character still occupying the same space, now surrounded by the arrangements their earlier choices have made permanent.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Stream of Consciousness\n\nEstablish the mechanism as a recurring private phrase in the narrator's mind. The rule—a hospital visiting policy, a loan repayment schedule, a caretaker rota, a work deadline—returns as a formulation the narrator repeats with variations: \"the form must be in by Friday,\" \"the door is always locked from the inside,\" \"they only call once.\" Each recall is not the narrator reasoning abstractly but being visited by the phrase while doing something else. The variations reveal both precision and a growing self-interest: the phrase acquires an exception, a justification, a softened verb.\n\nBraid each recall with sensory perception, memory, and bodily awareness. A sentence moves from the exact mechanism to the feel of a pen, the smell of a corridor, the weight of a coat, then a memory of the person who first explained the rule. Keep enough orientation that the reader can distinguish event from recollection: use tense shifts or concrete markers to signal when the narrator is remembering an actual past event rather than imagining a future one. Interruption comes from an external noise, a phone buzz, or the body itself, and the association may leap before the narrator returns to the original thought.\n\nLet incompatible commitments surface as quarrels within the narrator's private words. One impulse makes the narrator formulate the rule honestly; another immediately supplies the exception that would let them act as they wish. The syntax accelerates or fragments under the pressure, but the conflict remains an actual practical problem—childcare hours, rent, medication, a promise made—being turned over and adjusted in the narrator's head, not a series of rhetorical self-questions.\n\nWhen the irreversible result happens, let it enter the stream as an external fact that briefly halts the associative movement: a dropped object, a phone going dark, a door closing, a name called. Then let the recognition develop over the remainder of the reply as the narrator returns to earlier formulations and sees them differently. The tragedy lands not in a single dramatic insight but in the changed tone of the repeated phrases now that the loss is unalterable. End inside the same associative movement, with an ordinary perception following the recognition, so the aftermath retains the style's present-tense immediacy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the mechanism as a rule of the exchange itself or a shared rule the participants refer to: a schedule, a password, a channel membership, a payment deadline, a contact saved or blocked. The transcript states the rule through use—a participant quotes the deadline, a moderator posts the channel rule, a sender references the saved contact—rather than through a separate explanation. Show each person's digital habits through their message style: one sends short fragments, another edits repeatedly, another waits to reply. Do not make every character type the same way. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that carry a consequence, not decorative interface noise.\n\nDistinguish transmitted text from private thought through the draft-to-sent gap. An unsent draft appears in the transcript only as a draft block if you are showing it; otherwise keep the character's private reasoning in the provided nonspoken authoring wrapper clearly separated from the exchange. If a character types and deletes a message, show the visible signs of that action—the long delay, the edited message, the abrupt change of subject—rather than narrating their whole internal debate. A sent message is a final, committed act; the gap between a draft and the sent message is the place where the irreversible choice occurs.\n\nBuild the incompatible commitments through the practical use of the channel. Two threads pull a character in opposite directions: a group chat asks for confirmation while a private chat demands silence; a work channel sends reminders while a family chat asks for care. The character tries to manage both by exploiting the interface—turning off notifications, pretending to be offline, replying with a vague emoji, muting one thread—and that tactical choice creates a misread. The reader sees the rule each participant believes they are following, even when the messages do not state it directly.\n\nWhen the irreversible turn happens, let it occur in the transcript as a sent message, a read receipt, a withdrawal, or the arrival of a message that cannot be unsent. Do not immediately follow it with a long explanatory paragraph. Then allow recognition to develop in a later message or a separated authoring block. The final message may be ordinary—a routine check-in, a one-word reply, a question left on read—while the private wrapper or a brief description of the interface state preserves the aftermath. The style can end on a pending thread without resolving the relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Visual Novel\n\nAnchor the mechanism in a repeated visual detail the narration can revisit with changed meaning. A first visit shows a box left in the wrong place, a schedule on the wall, a coat missing from a hook; the narration explains the character's current understanding in a short reaction tied to what can be seen. A later visit shows the same detail, now understood correctly, and the focal response changes without needing a new external event. The repetition deepens the consequences of an earlier misread rather than introducing a new mystery.\n\nKeep character reaction beats brief. One or two lines of inner response show what the focal character thinks about the current exchange, but do not expand every scene into a stream of consciousness. Let dialogue carry most of the practical conflict. When a mechanism must be explained—a rule, a deadline, a commitment—have a character state it in readable speech or let the narration summarize it in a single focused sentence tied to what can be seen in the scene. Avoid essay-like exposition blocks.\n\nShow incompatible commitments through scene changes and focal reactions. Between spaces, cut to a new location where a different obligation is present. The same character expresses warmth in one scene and cold practicality in another; the focal narration notes the tension without moralizing. Each side's practical demand appears as a visible task—a call to be made, a form to be signed, a household chore—rather than letting dialogue wander into general debate.\n\nWhen the irreversible result occurs, write the scene so the visual detail at the center of the mistake remains stable while the recognition changes what it means. The focal narration says explicitly that this is the same tray, the same locked door, the same empty chair. The loss then lands as the character understands the earlier miscalculation. End on a quiet image and a brief inner response that preserves the aftermath. Do not add branching menus, player choices, romance routes, or image commands; the style is scene-oriented presentation, not an executable visual novel engine.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqanbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Kamachi Kazuma / Web Serial\n\nIntroduce the central mechanism in broad terms in an early installment, then add the exact clause that will eventually matter in later installments: a grace period, an exception, a hidden deadline, a household arrangement. When a character returns to the problem, recover the needed particular briefly—\"the form was due Friday, but the online portal showed Monday\"—rather than retelling the whole history. The reader sees the same rule being used correctly and incorrectly across installments.\n\nKeep each installment a readable through-line with a local development that narrows the future. Each reply advances an immediate practical task: the character gets the wrong confirmation, sends the message, misses the call, or chooses the cheaper option. Do not make every turn a complete miniature arc; the camera may leave a task unfinished and return to it next installment. A hook can be an ordinary pending question, not a forced cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge: the character learns something about the rule that changes whether an earlier step was safe.\n\nLet the incompatible commitments accumulate as competing resources and obligations. One installment focuses on the work deadline, the next on the family need, the next on the housing cost. Each new obligation connects to the mechanism rather than merely piling on: the same money, the same caregiver, the same schedule, the same public record. The character tries to solve one problem with a method that quietly worsens another, always through concrete action.\n\nLet the irreversible outcome arrive in a later installment after the reader has seen the character come close to fixing the earlier mistakes. The recognition is deferred across text passages, not delivered immediately after the fatal choice. When it arrives, it revalues earlier local successes. Close the installment on the aftermath: an ordinary continuation, a message left unread, a room that is too quiet, a changed routine. The serial can end with a pending obligation rather than a total resolution. Recover context only briefly when needed, and keep the fiction moving.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLocate each turn of the fall in the small opportunities for admission, demand, apology, or withdrawal between people who already matter to one another. Let a beautiful or memorable detail carry the history of that closeness—a room's particular light, the scent of a shared routine, an object one of them can no longer touch without remembering. The beauty is not relief from the trouble; it is proof of what the trouble is costing them.\n\nUse Li Bihua's sensual precision to make emotional strain physically legible without turning characters into displays of symptoms. A throat tightening before a lie, a hand hesitating over a familiar place, a pause while someone counts the cost of honesty: these reveal the relationship's state through the character's own experience, in the narrator's actual distance and diction rather than a fixed vocabulary of suffering. Let the strain reach behavior—a sentence abandoned, a demand made too precisely, a kindness received like a debt—and let it shape what happens next, not only how it feels.\n\nPrivate thought may contradict speech, run partly against it, or move alongside it; the reason for the gap belongs to this relationship and its present danger. Do not manufacture a betrayal or collapse in every exchange. Tenderness may remain real and even useful, but comfort arrives at a price: it can reopen an old wound, require a concession, or come with the knowledge that this ease cannot be kept. When a choice turns destructive, keep the character's commitment and the visible alternatives intelligible, so the reader understands what is being spent and why someone keeps spending it. The loss itself need not land in this reply; what must be legible is what the attachment already costs these people, so that the next admission, apology, or return arrives already priced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as a shared interval in readable paragraphs, with unmistakable speaker attribution so players always know who is talking and acting. Ground the stakes in one or two controlled sensual details per exchange: a cup set down without sound, rain arriving through an open door, a name pronounced too carefully. Let one character notice such a detail before anyone else reacts to it; the noticing itself can carry the scene's charge.\n\nKeep the non-player cast active in their own purposes. Their offers, refusals, and changes of subject should follow from the relationships and world already in play, not from a need to generate a cliffhanger or forced decision. A tender gesture can stay tender while complicating the bond; a coldness can reveal divided attachment rather than random cruelty. In Li Bihua's mode a fatal attachment often announces itself as attention that feels luxurious and wrong at once—render it through what a character watches, handles, or refuses to look at, never through an announced doom.\n\nLet practical life continue around the conversation: obligations proceed, other people come and go, the day does not pause for the scene. End at a natural boundary of the interaction, which may fall mid-speech, mid-action, or inside a thought; leave the exchange open to the next contribution instead of resolving the tragedy or forcing a choice before the turn closes. Keep each character's knowledge separate from the players' unless the active contract grants access, and let narrative person, action markers, and player control follow their separately selected settings.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route for this telling. Divide it into short chapter-like episodes, each organized around a memory, an encounter, or a public disclosure that returns the central attachment to a socially legible point of strain. By the end of an episode, the consequences of a visit, letter, rumor, or interrupted meeting should be visible without resolving the whole fall.\n\nGive each episode a hinge: an object, dish, street name, ritual, or remembered phrase that recurs. Describe it with Li Bihua's controlled lushness, but keep it tied to the people who use, serve, or inherit it rather than to atmosphere alone. When the same detail returns, its meaning has changed because the attachment has changed—not because a sign has appeared. Write in the vernacular of the story's actual speech communities, keeping register distinct between formal occasions and private talk.\n\nTreat recognition as a stepwise public event. Family members, neighbors, or servants may understand the same small scene differently; convey that layered awareness through reported gossip, indirect speech, and visible reaction rather than entering minds the narration has no access to. Emotional truth stays chiefly with the focal character or characters whose interiority is permitted.\n\nDo not import an imperial, rural, or supernatural setting because the tradition is Chinese. Use the world, period, and cast already supplied; if that cast is contemporary or non-Chinese, keep the episodic account of attraction, regret, and irreversible choice without altering anyone's identity or language. Return, episode by episode, in formally marked steps to the bond whose cost is still accumulating.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in quick, concrete strokes: a door that sticks in its frame, the chipped edge of a painted table, a mark on the wrist where a bracelet used to sit. Let material detail carry the relationship's condition without explaining the symbol. When the action turns, cut hard to the next necessary event, objection, or refusal. A flash of remembered beauty—a gown's exact color, a mouth's taste, a street wet with rain—may land once, then be replaced by what it now costs to recall.\n\nUse forceful diction, but keep each voice inside its situation: a cautious lover, a jealous relative, and a reluctant returnee should not share one hard manner of speaking. The prose compresses; the feelings stay varied and particular. Allow a longer thought only when the character is working out something that cannot be rendered in a phrase—a decision, a suspicion, a count of what is already gone—and end it when the thinking ends.\n\nLet the fall proceed through practical consequence: who is allowed to stay, what must be sold or hidden, which promise can no longer be kept in its original form. Recognition may arrive as a concrete fact—an unpaid bill, a door left ajar, a map returned with one folded corner—rather than as an announcement. Beauty appears because this way of seeing still finds it; wear appears because the world of the story is exacting. Keep both in the same scene so that neither cancels the other.\n\nDo not make every character brutal or every room squalid. A clean room can hold a dangerous memory; a kind act can sharpen the eventual loss. The pressure here is rhythmic and material, not a mandate of despair. The scene may end before the fall completes, provided the compressed detail already shows the incompatibility that gives the coming loss its weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through documents, each with a clear writer, recipient or intended use, and time of composition. A letter arrives late; a diary entry records what its writer could bear to know that night; a receipt, lease amendment, or medical note later corrects an impression the personal papers created. Do not append an omniscient explanation after each document—selection and order do the interpretive work.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensual memory live in the documents as material quality: the stationery's edge, the smell of the drawer, a stain from the meal remembered while writing. A letter may describe a room or an object so precisely that the reader understands why the writer cannot let it go, and still hesitate over the consequence the writer will not name. The gap between what a document says and what it admits is part of the tragedy, not an error to be repaired. Remember that each document is composed for its recipient or purpose; it is not the writer's whole live mind, so where private interiority is permitted, keep the thought distinct from what gets committed to paper.\n\nUse revisions and material traces as evidence of changed feeling and commitment: torn pages, crossed-out names, repeated drafts of one sentence, an envelope opened and resealed. A promise or refusal was rarely made in a single clean moment. Conflicting accounts need not expose a lie; two people may have lived the same evening through different loves and fears. Preserve each writer's limited knowledge—later documents may discover the cost, but they cannot change what the earlier writer knew.\n\nHandle documents as objects with consequences: misdelivered, destroyed, kept too long, read aloud by the wrong person, entered into an argument or an inheritance. The tragedy need not be visible in any single entry; it emerges across the archive when a later document recognizes that the most beautiful sentence was also where the bond became impossible. Let the sequence rest at a natural pause, not when every question has been answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend real attention on chosen kindness: someone remembers a favorite tea, folds a warm jacket over a chair, saves the last piece of fruit, sits through a dull program because the other person values it. Render these acts with Li Bihua's sensory exactness—the weight of the cup, the particular sweetness held back for someone—so the warmth is lived rather than generic. The comfort is not bait for a punishment in the next paragraph; it genuinely matters to the people involved.\n\nLet affection stay partial and personal. One character accepts care easily; another hoards the memory of it; a third offers help that misses the actual need. Comfort requires no confession and no repair, and it may arrive inside an unresolved loyalty, a pending departure, or the knowledge that this ease cannot last. There it gives the tragedy its force: the reader sees exactly what someone stands to lose.\n\nDo not dissolve the tragedy's commitments to manufacture ease. A warm breakfast can share a morning with a promise being reconsidered; a familiar joke can be the last language two people still have in common before a separation; a returned possession can be both a kindness and an acknowledgment that its owner is not coming back. The loss need not occur within the scene, but register which small pleasure has quietly become irreplaceable, without turning the moment into a premonition.\n\nKeep the cast distinct. Kindness may be awkward, habitual, self-protective, or deliberate; a private thought can be tender without being spoken. A kitchen or bedroom may take sensuous detail while remaining entirely ordinary, and disagreement, privacy, and the world's established problems stay intact. This warmth is the human ground from which Li Bihua's intimate strangeness becomes legible. Stop at a natural moment of closeness or care; the scene needs no break, lesson, or resolution to justify ending there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Formal Literary Novel\n\nWrite in deliberate paragraphs that move between social context and private experience. Open a scene with the household's arrangements, a public occasion, a letter of invitation, or a place as it is spoken of in a certain circle; then let the paragraph draw close to one person's attention—the feel of a curtain, a name pronounced in the formal register, the weight of a promise understood as debt. Keep narration distinct from ordinary spoken language in diction and rhythm, while dialogue stays in the voices of its speakers.\n\nUse Li Bihua's controlled lushness to make the attachment vivid without dissolving into atmosphere. A red lantern, an embroidered sleeve, a half-empty dish, the scent of a closed room may recur, but each return changes meaning as social possibility narrows. The narrator may set private reluctance beside what the community expects, provided the active point of view permits that distance; when a framing or omniscient voice spans rooms, keep clear whose knowledge is being rendered at each step.\n\nLet the fall appear as gradual adjustment in what can be said, worn, exchanged, or refused in public. Recognition may arrive when a character understands that an apparently free choice was already enclosed by family, property, reputation, or debt—but do not stage it as a lecture. Let it arise from the comparison the narration has been building all along between the intimate detail and the social condition surrounding it.\n\nThe scene may close before the catastrophe: a pause in the public business, an invitation declined, a door shut on a private understanding, while the broader fall remains in motion. Preserve the active narrator and world contract; ceremony, correspondence, and the contrast between public and private may organize passages where relevant, and the formal architecture gives the tragedy its social legibility without requiring aristocratic settings or archaic vocabulary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the fall as an account given to a listener who can still alter the teller's remaining choices. Establish the occasion and purpose of the telling immediately: the teller is buying time, seeking sympathy, delaying an answer, explaining a refusal, or trying to make someone understand before a decision closes. Give the listener a competing stake, so the frame is not a neutral stage but a live negotiation between what the teller needs and what the listener wants to hear or prevent.\n\nLet Li Bihua's controlled lushness carry the embedded story. Render remembered rooms, food, fabric, heat, scent, and touch with exact sensory precision, and attach each beautiful image to an obligation, bargain, or loss. A repeated object or phrase must cross the levels and accrue meaning: a comb, a tune, a promise couched in the same words, a room returned to at the end. When the listener interrupts, make the interruption reveal a gap in the teller's account. The listener may ask about a detail the teller smoothed over, challenge a sequence, or demand the part of the story that explains a present consequence. The teller's response then exposes the omission, defense, or choice that the tale was arranged to manage.\n\nSeparate what the teller knew at the time from what the telling now shows. The fall must be intelligible to the reader even when the teller still defends it. Recognition may arrive inside the told story, in the act of telling, or in the listener's response; do not require it to be neat or redemptive. Aftermath should preserve the loss in the present frame, not undo it. Keep the levels clear by giving each shift a distinct sensory marker, a repeated phrase, or an audience gesture. Do not add a courtly setting, djinn, or a compulsory cliffhanger; the Arabian Nights reference supplies narrative pressure, not imported props.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-developmental realism route. Write the fall as an unfolding history in which work, family, property, rank, migration, or institutional expectation exerts steady pressure on a particular desire. Keep the narrative in a realist register and attach it to the focal character's actual knowledge: the reader sees the transactions, obligations, and alternatives that the character can see, not a sociological overview.\n\nGround Li Bihua's lushness in tangible conditions. Describe a beautiful object by showing how it is priced, rented, inherited, pawned, mended, or envied. Remembered places carry their social history: a room is not only lovely but also tied to a lease, a legacy, a lost position, or a family claim. Jealous or divided attachment appears through practical conflicts, such as an invitation accepted at a cost, a family debt, a career decision made to please, or a room one can no longer afford. Let the glamorous surface expose the unstable relationship rather than conceal it; render the surface with sensual precision and then show what it demands.\n\nMake warnings and alternatives intelligible without making the character foolish. A parent, employer, friend, or former self may state the practical risk plainly, and the protagonist may genuinely understand the risk and still choose against it. Build the choice from the character's incompatible commitments, not from ignorance or fate. Recognition becomes a later reckoning with what the loss means in social and personal terms; aftermath does not reverse the history or grant comfort. Keep the perspective's access limited to the focal character's knowledge. Do not import German national scenery, reflexive bureaucracies, or translated word order.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Gothic-Punk\n\nLocate the fall where an inherited demand, title, debt, ritual obligation, or old family arrangement meets ordinary present life. Use the Gothic-Punk collision to make that meeting physically and emotionally specific: a modern flat inside an old house, a family rule enforced by a current employer, a cherished object whose upkeep now costs someone a choice. Let the atmosphere sharpen the encounter by showing how the old demand changes the feel and stakes of the present room, task, or relationship, rather than replacing a cause with mood.\n\nRender Li Bihua's beauty with a cost through urban material detail. Describe fabric, lighting, jewelry, food, or architecture in exact sensory terms, then show the maintenance, threat, or bargain attached to it. Private desire can be intense, jealous, or divided, and socially expected masks may fall in trusted company. When a mask falls, let the unmasking itself be dangerous or comforting according to the relationship; the intimacy should expose the cost or pressure the character has been carrying, not serve as a guaranteed trap.\n\nMake each irreversible choice legible through authority and intimacy together. A character may obey an old loyalty, refuse a modern compromise, or protect one person at another's expense. Show the reasoning and the alternative, so the fall is a consequence of an in-character incompatible commitment. Recognition should alter the meaning of the loss for the survivors without restoring what was destroyed. Do not import Kindred physiology, blood rules, powers, or factions; Gothic atmosphere works here through social pressure, material decay, and personal horror. End at the natural response boundary without a required cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-domestic realism route. Build the tragedy inside a specific household, workplace, neighborhood, or community by showing the obligations and consequences that structure daily life: who must be visited, fed, repaid, married, cared for, or answered. Keep the point of view attached to the focal character's immediate knowledge and social position. The narration should register meals, rooms, arrivals, departures, debts, and obligations as consequential events, not decorative background.\n\nPlace Li Bihua's sensuousness inside remembered domestic rituals. Describe precise fabrics, dishes, scents, weather, and family objects, and show what they cost or preserve. A divided attachment appears in the gap between household duty and private desire, between what a relative expects and what the character remembers, or between two loyalties that cannot both be honored. Let jealousy or longing emerge through small repeated actions: a letter kept, a meal left unsent, a visit rehearsed but not made. Each repeated action should move the character closer to a choice or reveal the cost of avoiding one.\n\nLet external warnings be practical and intelligible. A parent, friend, or colleague can name the risk, and the protagonist can still choose against that advice for reasons the narrative makes clear. The fall results from an incompatible commitment, not stupidity or fate. Recognition should arrive through an encounter with the changed meaning of a familiar place, object, or relationship, and the aftermath should keep the loss present without pretending it can be repaired. Keep language, region, community, and period specific when supplied; do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social-psychological realism route. Write in a composed register close to the focal character's perception, with syntax and narrative distance following that teller. Observe the ordinary pressures of work, family, marriage, reputation, or place through the character's actual encounters: a conversation at work, a family expectation repeated, a reputation at stake, a room or street that has changed. Let the fall grow from a decision the character makes, and keep the surrounding social world visible through the choices and responses of the people involved.\n\nAllow Li Bihua's lushness to appear in precise sensory attention: the temperature of water, the weight of a garment, the smell of rain or cooking, the grain of a wooden surface. Tie each detail to memory and cost. A beautiful memory may be what the character clings to, what they fear losing, or what they can no longer enter. Render jealous or divided attachment through what is watched, avoided, remembered, or left unsaid. Show the character's attention lingering on one person or object, turning away from another, or returning to a scene that can no longer be entered.\n\nLet seasonal or spatial attention cue narrated time and recognition, but do not treat it as a checklist. Recognition can arrive quietly through a repeated object, an empty seat, or a task now done alone. Aftermath should preserve the loss and show how the character continues in its presence, not explain it away. Keep access to minds within the selected perspective. Do not require silence, seclusion, or a conventional Japanese setting; whatever is supplied by the story world determines the actual places and persons.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological-urban fiction route. Set the fall in a city whose streets, rooms, cafés, buses, and apartments are rendered exactly enough to hold remembered desire. Move between the present aftermath and earlier moments of the same attachment through deliberate, clearly cued time shifts. Signal each shift with a concrete sensory or spatial marker, such as a doorway, a particular light, a route, or an object, so the reader follows the movement without chronological disorder for its own sake.\n\nUse Li Bihua's lushness as the pressure of memory. Describe a dress, a perfume, a doorway, a meal, or a particular light with tactile precision, then return to it after the loss. Each return should show the same image carrying a changed meaning: what was once desired is now evidence of absence, debt, or a choice that cannot be undone. Jealous, divided longing can occupy the narrator's attention in the present as much as the past; do not require the character to resolve it. The glamorous surface is always tied to a cost: an unpaid rent, a lover's absence, a family debt, a photograph no longer accurate.\n\nThe fall must remain intelligible. Show the choice and the social or emotional situation that made it, not merely the misery that followed. Alternatives can be named by a friend or by the protagonist's later self, but the earlier decision must have its own felt logic at the time. Recognition occurs when the same remembered scene is revisited with later knowledge, and its meaning changes irreversibly. Aftermath preserves the loss in the city's continuing life. Keep the perspective within the chosen narrator; do not add magic, ghosts, or supernatural causality unless the provided world canon already contains them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate tragic situation without preface. Open on a place, gesture, object, or exchange that already carries pressure. Keep spatial changes crisp: short paragraphs for movement, clear speakers in dialogue, and a dedicated beat for the focal character's reaction. The pacing should make the reader feel each step toward the irreversible choice without rushing the recognition.\n\nCompress Li Bihua's lushness into high-contrast sensory details. Select one or two specific images per scene—a glove, a mirror, a plate, the smell of rain—and return to them later under changed circumstances. Beauty costs something visible: an unaffordable garment, a favor accepted, a parting gift. Use the reaction beats to render the character's actual emotional state, whether serious, uncertain, or quietly overwhelmed, not a generic exclamation. Let each beat register the cost of the image or the pressure of the competing commitment.\n\nKeep the fall legible. Before the choice, make the competing commitment concrete through dialogue or a brief private thought, and let the character's reaction show what that commitment costs. Warnings can be spoken plainly and the protagonist may still proceed. Recognition should be a focused moment: an image from earlier now means something else, and the character or the narration notes the change without explaining it into softness. Aftermath remains present in the closing lines; do not add a cliffhanger, a young cast, comedy, or first-person narration unless the active story contract already calls for them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild each scene around one sensuous image taken from the story's own places and attachments—a mirror, a length of fabric, a doorway, a dish two people shared—and let it return as the arc turns. Early on, the image belongs to appetite or habit; after an irreversible choice, bring it back with its association shifted so the loss is felt through the senses before anyone names it. The image does not explain the tragedy; it makes the cost physically present, and it stays traceable to the scene or to the teller's own associations.\n\nShape rhythm so it carries the difference between anticipation and aftermath. Before the fall, let sentences wind through associative memory—what a fabric recalls, whom a scent belongs to—and let a comparison unfold only when it changes what the focal character understands or makes an absent person present. Land decisive facts in short, plain clauses: what was chosen, who left, what was signed. Keep each speaker's voice in its own register; the cast need not speak poetically, and a flat spoken line can cut through the figurative surface to show what is actually happening.\n\nKeep attachment specific and intelligence intact. Jealousy, divided loyalty, or doomed attraction arrives through remembered sensation and private encounters—a hand remembered too well, another person's scent in a familiar room—rather than generalized sorrow, and the character's unspoken experience remains available as actual felt attention under the active contract. Let warnings and alternatives be intelligible in their plain sense even inside the beauty. After recognition, allow the ornament to thin where attention itself has changed. The aftermath may hold one image now unshareable, preserving what cannot be restored without claiming the pain has been resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the telling move between a present scene and the remembered occasions that have become unreachable. Choose one familiar place or object and visit it twice: once while it still belongs to ordinary life, and again after a choice has made it inaccessible. The second visit carries the recognition; do not explain the change at length, because the detail—a hand resting on a rail, how a particular door sounded, the smell of tea before the departure—already holds it. Mark each passage between present and past with a sensory cue rather than a recap.\n\nGround reflection in this person and this loss. A divided attachment can be felt as two remembered occasions that now touch: the afternoon that meant devotion and the errand that meant betrayal, coinciding in one remembered texture. Let later thought re-read an earlier detail so that recognition changes what the loss means, keeping the thought in the character's own habits of mind rather than in general statements about time and impermanence. The narrator's private longing stays available as felt experience, not only as described scenery.\n\nOrdinary pleasure remains pleasurable inside grief—a meal can still be good, a guest still welcome—because the tragedy is the removal of possibility, not the erasure of warmth. A scene may end in the present, mid-attention, with the familiar thing changed, and no scene is obliged to close by announcing that nothing lasts. What cannot be restored stays unrestored, and the memory of it can remain tender without being softened.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nTell this tragedy inside a frame: a specific person tells a specific tale to a listener or a small gathering, at a particular hour, in a room rendered concretely—the light, the tea being poured, who grows restless, who already knows the end. Fix the teller's relation to the events—participant, witness, or inheritor of the account—and keep knowledge within it: the teller knows what was seen, what was told afterward, and what has since been understood, no more. The tale is received, not merely reported; the listeners' attention belongs to the form.\n\nInside the tale, keep sensual precision attached to remembered places: a courtyard, a shop, a route through the market, a bathhouse, given through the textures, scents, and gestures the teller lingers on because they still carry the attachment. Let the cost of a glamorous person or a jealous arrangement surface gradually, nameable only as the telling proceeds. Keep warnings intelligible inside the tale so the fall remains chosen rather than dressed as fate.\n\nBreak the telling with the present: a question, a refill, a door opening, someone defending the person being described. These interruptions let recognition happen inside the frame—the listener understanding the loss before it is named, or the teller discovering, mid-sentence, that a past choice cannot be taken back. The social warmth of the frame persists even as the tale turns toward loss. Close with what cannot be restored still unrestored, without making the account tidy or the audience's understanding complete, and keep the idiom of teller and listeners within the community and language the story actually supplies rather than importing antiquity or mysticism.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small set of exact details for each scene—two or three will usually be enough—and let each one carry attachment or memory: a mark left on a wall, a particular cloth, a sound that repeats at the same hour. Place the detail beside the action without explaining it, and let the reader infer what the character no longer says. Where an inward connection is essential to causality, state it directly and let it run exactly as long as it needs to make sense; one precise sentence serves better than several performed fragments, and economy is not a quota.\n\nMark irreversible choice and recognition in plain clauses. Let the same detail appear before and after the loss; its new occasion changes it, and nothing needs to announce the change. Show what beauty cost through a small action—a hand turned over, a box closed, a name said once—rather than through adjectives. Keep each speaker's voice individual even in brief lines.\n\nDo not cut away the alternatives that make the fall tragic: a single line can establish that another path was open, that someone said so, or that the character understood it only later. Repetition should be deliberate, returning to a chosen detail exactly when its meaning has changed. What is omitted may remain unresolved but never confusing, and a final direct sentence can preserve what cannot be restored.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite each reply as one continuous scene. Open inside an action or conversation already underway, then let the prose move naturally among what is happening, what is said, what the focal character perceives or recalls, and whatever inner experience the active narrator is authorized to carry. A sensuous detail—the weight of a fabric, the taste of a shared dish, light falling the way it fell in a remembered room—carries the intimacy of this profile, but let it serve the moment and release it; do not stop the scene to ornament it.\n\nBuild tragic consequence from choices made inside the scene rather than from summary or foreshadowing. Put warnings and alternatives into dialogue or into specific thought—one person states the risk plainly, or the focal character sees the other road and understands its price—and then let the person still choose, or fail to choose, for reasons the scene has made real. Divided or jealous attachment lives in concrete intimacies: a private name, a familiar gesture, the place where two people once met, and the practical arrangements that maintain them—what someone pays, risks, or gives up to keep a glamorous surface intact.\n\nRecognition can arrive as a later recollection that re-reads an earlier glance or remark. Use paragraphs to guide attention without signposting, and let the voice stay fluid whether or not the setting is contemporary. A scene may close on a decisive act, a postponed answer, or a character simply knowing what has changed; supply an epiphany only where the moment has earned one, and no reply owes a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise the ground the tragedy stands on. Select scenes that keep it legible: the private language these two actually use, a repeated moment that means more to one of them than the other knows, a small ritual that has already begun to change. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, or rivalry real space as specific actions, spoken lines, and words withheld for character-specific reasons; whatever is withheld should also exist as private experience under the active contract, not only as implication.\n\nLet fatal glamour live in intimate particulars—a preferred seat, a way of touching, a remembered sweetness with an edge—and make the cost concrete: the relationship itself, a loyalty it displaced, a belief about the other person that can no longer survive. Show that cost through consequence inside the scenes rather than melodrama, and use only the premise actually supplied; do not bolt on a roster of familiar tropes or force a betrayal, a collapsed confession, or a rescue.\n\nEarn the payoff through callbacks. A phrase, gesture, or shared object from an early scene returns later with its meaning changed because one character now knows what the other was feeling or hiding; recognition should re-read that earlier private moment rather than import a new external fact. Afterward, preserve what cannot be restored—the residue of the attachment, the name still known—without pretending it vanished. A scene can end with a hard truth spoken, or with someone quietly noticing that the shared name has stopped being used; it can equally end before anything is said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the tragedy as an account being retold. Open with a compact declaration that fixes a deed or a bond in memory—for example, \"It was in the year of the flood that she left the house by the river\"—then expand into the particulars that complicate what the declaration claims. Let names and a few recurring phrases return when a later event gives them new application; do not hang an epithet on every entrance.\n\nCarry the intimacy of remembered places inside the chronicle's economy. Two people bound by a shared room, a repeated errand, or a gift whose meaning changed can hold a divided attachment without breaking the telling's scale, and sensory particulars—a threshold, a scent, the weight of an object passed between hands—can mark what desire paid. Record the cost beside the deed, as part of what will be remembered: the price is not an aside but half the account. Preserve the tragic alternatives in the same voice—the chronicler notes that another path was once open, that a warning was spoken and disregarded—so the fall remains chosen.\n\nRecognition enters when a later event revises what an earlier act means, not only as private grief but as a change in what the account's community will remember. Dialogue may interrupt the chronicle's authority: a participant corrects a detail, or answers an elevated passage with an ordinary practical concern. Keep the tradition coherent with the world actually supplied—no imported pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial speech for its own sake—and let the telling leave its consequence in memory rather than settling every meaning on the listener's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite a Norwegian social-realist fall in which the conditions that damage a life are also the surfaces Li Bihua's precision can render. Make the domestic and institutional texture specific: a farm's mortgage reviewed at a kitchen table, a municipal committee's ruling on a wharf lease, a parish list where a name appears or disappears, a workplace hierarchy enforced through who pours coffee and who signs the ledger. Let beauty carry its cost inside these settings—the good silver laid out for the bank officer, the smell of wet wool and paraffin in the vestibule where a debt is refused, the grain of a table worn by a parent whose promise still binds the household.\n\nLet divided attachment take a social shape rather than a purely private one. A character may be caught between a lover's claim and a duty to kin, between a marriage that would save the property and a promise made in another town, between loyalty to an employer and loyalty to a neighbor who has been wronged. Keep the competing commitments intelligible through the institutions that enforce them: an inheritance clause, a lease that cannot be renewed, a reputation that affects credit, a union or parish decision that closes one route and opens another. Other people may notice the danger, offer a real alternative, or refuse help for reasons the reader can follow.\n\nUse one recurring remembered place as an intimate anchor, and tie each return to the social mechanism of the fall. A boathouse first associated with courtship may later be the place where a contract is signed, then the place where a name is no longer spoken, then the place sold at auction. Let the sensory details gather consequence—the same wet rope, the same painted door—so that beauty and damage share the image rather than restate nostalgia.\n\nWhen recognition arrives, attach it to what cannot be restored: a house lost to auction, a route no longer traveled, a name removed from a list, a letter delivered too late. Write the recognition as knowledge that changes the meaning of earlier acts, not as a sudden moral conversion. Aftermath may include endurance, silence, or a belated reparation, but it must keep the loss real rather than wiping it away.\n\nUse ordinary private prose for interiority in the selected presentation, and keep another character's knowledge separate from the focal mind's conclusions. The style does not transform the narration into a folktale or a crime investigation unless those features are separately authorized by the world contract. The response boundary may fall during continuing attention, waiting, or an unfinished action; do not manufacture a completed fall, a revelation, or a practical decision before the scene itself reaches one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Screenplay\n\nWrite action in concise, readable screen blocks. Start a scene heading only when location or time changes; keep the heading plain and functional. Give each block a visible or audible event a production could stage, and let Li Bihua's controlled lushness appear in selected physical details that carry the cost of the situation: a silk sleeve catching on a door latch as a character leaves, a tray of fruit set down too hard when a debt is mentioned, rain on glass behind a conversation about a marriage that will not happen. Do not pile description into the action lines; let one or two precise details do the work.\n\nUse clear speaker cues and dialogue blocks. Let conversation carry the incompatible commitments—duty to a parent, a lover's claim, a business promise, a debt of hospitality—and let each character speak from a distinct interest. A warning or an alternative should be intelligible in the lines themselves, not delivered as exposition. When a pause matters, mark it; otherwise leave performance choices to the script's economy.\n\nKeep required private prose as a clearly attributed nonspoken note, separate from dialogue and not available to other characters. When the focal character's inward experience contributes recognition or motive, write it in that note; do not convert it into a voice-over unless the chosen sequence specifically needs voice-over and the authorship contract permits it. No other character should react to that hidden content.\n\nMake irreversible choices visible through movement and consequence. A character may close a door that will not reopen, leave a ring on a counter, tear up a ticket, or tell a truth that cannot be unsaid. Let the sensual detail from an earlier scene return in the choice: the same silk sleeve is smoothed before a final refusal, the same tray is cleared after a departure. Recognition should emerge from what the audience has seen and heard—a repeated object handled differently, a line spoken in a new context, a character's movement toward or away from a familiar place—not from a caption or an explanatory speech.\n\nAvoid camera commands. Use cuts and offscreen sound only when they advance the sequence, not as decoration. The ending may occur after a decisive action, during continuing movement or speech, or at an unfinished moment; do not require a music cue, a final close-up, or a complete denouement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speaker names, entrances, and exits. Every character who enters or leaves should have a reason and a physical task: bringing tea, checking a ledger, closing a window, putting away a coat. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene, and let Li Bihua's sensual precision live in what can be staged. Describe costume or prop only enough to be executed, but choose details that carry the cost: a faded silk collar on a coat kept from a better year, a chipped cup used only when a particular guest visits, a photograph kept inside a drawer that a character touches before making a promise.\n\nLet an object recur so its later handling changes meaning without requiring explanation. A jade clasp, a key, a tray of oranges, or a deed may be handled with tenderness in one scene, set down with finality in another, and left behind in a third. The audience should read the change from the actor's business and the surrounding lines, not from a narrator's gloss.\n\nGive monologues and asides a dramatic purpose. A monologue may articulate a decision, a plea, a memory, or a recognition that cannot be safely spoken to another character. Use it sparingly and as public performance, not as the default container for every private thought. Required private experience remains a clearly attributed nonspoken authoring note in the active presentation, not a line another character can hear.\n\nShape the tragedy through stage-able choices. Have a character perform an irreversible action with an object or through an exit: locking a door, handing over a deed, turning away during a plea, placing the recurring object where another character will find it too late. Keep the other characters' ignorance visible—they respond only to what they see and hear on stage. Recognition may be a silent look, a pause, or a line whose earlier meaning the audience now hears differently, but it must be acted, not described. Let the recognition change what the loss means without erasing it.\n\nDo not add film-camera instructions. Let the script's exits, objects, and speeches do the work. The scene may end immediately after a decision, during an unfinished gesture, or as a character remains on stage with what has been lost.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from a single authorized mind. Let perception, memory, bodily sensation, and private words braid together, but keep enough orientation to distinguish what happens now, what is remembered, and what is feared or desired. Use Li Bihua's sensual precision inside that braid: the smell of jasmine hair oil, the texture of a red envelope, the sound of a mahjong tile, the cold weight of a jade bracelet. Let each sensory detail carry an attachment or a cost—the jasmine oil belongs to a person who is leaving, the red envelope holds money that was borrowed, the jade bracelet is the one that must be sold.\n\nChoose one or two remembered places as recurring anchors, and tie each return to the commitments that are coming apart. A corridor first remembered with a lover may later return with the sound of a debt being refused, later still with a name no longer spoken. Let the mind return under pressure from the present scene—a smell, a voice, a task—not from a narrator's thematic summary. The place should gather consequence rather than simply repeat.\n\nShape the tragedy through associative junctures. Incompatible commitments may surface as recalled arguments, unfinished sentences, or physical discomfort when a duty is mentioned: a tightening in the chest at the word \"lease,\" a remembered hand pulling away, the taste of tea at a meeting where a promise was broken. An irreversible choice can be marked by a sudden halt in association, a reversal of attention, or a detail the mind refuses to complete—the moment the bracelet left the wrist, the door that was not opened, the sentence that was not finished. Recognition may arrive as a memory understood differently—why a person looked away, what an unsent message said, what the silence after a question really meant—rather than as an announced insight.\n\nDo not give every character the same associative style. Other people remain outside this mind; their speech and actions are perceived by the focal character, but their private thoughts are not dissolved into the narration. Keep actual events legible: the reader should know what happened in the scene even when the mind loops or digresses. Allow interruptions—a telephone, a customer, a cough—to cut across recollection and press the present forward.\n\nRegular reasoning alone is not enough: create movement through perception and recurrence, not through a linear argument. The response may end during continuing association, an incomplete thought, or a sensory return to the current room.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the story through readable, sender-attributed messages. Use timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes only when they affect the meaning: a message left on read after a family dinner, a late reply that crosses a deadline, a photo attachment that recalls a shared market stall. Let each person's digital habits be distinct—one writes long paragraphs, one sends single words, one edits repeatedly, one ignores group chat—and let those habits carry their commitments and evasions.\n\nKeep Li Bihua's sensual precision inside what the characters can actually transmit, and tie each detail to the cost or attachment at stake. A message may describe a dress fitting for a wedding that is being arranged against the sender's wish, the smell of incense at a temple where a promise was made, the exact color of a bruise that must be hidden, or the texture of a letter discovered in a drawer. The glamorous surface can appear in shared photographs or remembered details, but it must belong to the message and to the relationship the message is negotiating, not to an invisible narrator's lyric aside.\n\nDistinguish sent messages from unsent drafts and private thought. If a character types then deletes, show the draft as a clearly separated non-transmitted item only if the presentation permits it. Required private interiority remains a nonspoken note outside the transcript; another character cannot know it. A delayed reply or an edit receipt may hint at hesitation, but do not let the transcript claim knowledge of an unsent mind.\n\nBuild the tragedy through transmitted acts. An irreversible choice can be a message sent after a decision, a forwarded screenshot, a block, or the exact words that end a negotiation. Let the choice live in the message record itself: the timestamp that shows a reply came too late, the attachment that was opened but never answered, the contact removed after a final exchange. Recognition may come when an earlier message is re-read in a new context, when a timestamp reveals a lie, or when two people react differently to the same attachment. Let the loss be real and preserved in the record: a final message, a removed contact, a thread that stops and is not resumed.\n\nContext outside the exchange may appear when needed, clearly separated from the transcript. The scene may end after a message is sent or while a reply is being typed, without requiring a resolution or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaoba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Visual Novel\n\nOpen each scene with a concise setting block: time, place, and a few visual or sensory details that a reader could picture. Use Li Bihua's sensual precision there, but tie each detail to the cost or attachment at stake—red paper cutouts in a doorway where a marriage is being arranged, the shine of pomade on a person who is leaving, the sour-sweet smell of vinegar at a food stall where a debt is discussed. Keep the description brief; the scene should move forward through dialogue and focal reaction.\n\nAttribute dialogue clearly. Let conversation carry the incompatible commitments: a family debt, a promise to an employer, a lover's demand, a duty to remain in a certain city. Characters should speak from their own concerns, allowing warnings and alternatives to be intelligible without becoming lectures. Repetition of a familiar phrase or address can become a tonal marker, but do not force it into every exchange; let it return when the relationship or the loss gives it new weight.\n\nWrite focal narration or inner response where it actually matters. When the viewpoint character recognizes a consequence, remembers a place, or notices the cost of beauty, provide that as private prose. Keep that interiority outside other characters' knowledge. A background or expression cue may orient the moment—a corridor's locked door, a character's turned face—but it is not a truth indicator; the actual evidence remains in what is said and done.\n\nShape the tragedy through scene decisions. An irreversible choice may be shown as a character paying a debt, destroying a photograph, refusing a train ticket, or accepting a marriage arrangement. Let the choice be prepared by the scene's sensory details: the red paper cutouts are taken down after the refusal, the pomade is washed off after the departure, the vinegar smell lingers in the empty stall. Recognition can arrive in a later scene when a familiar object or repeated line is heard differently—the same doorway now undecorated, the same address spoken by a stranger. Keep the loss meaningful: the world does not reset after the choice, and the aftermath may include silence, avoidance, or a changed household.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or engine commands. This is a single narrative sequence. A scene may end after a spoken line, during continuing action, or at an unfinished moment; do not require a decision screen or a completed emotional beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaobb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Li Bihua / Web Serial\n\nWrite installments as continuous prose with a clear what-is-underway. Open a reply by briefly recovering only the particular that matters for re-entry: the unfinished errand, the person waiting in the other room, the letter whose sender has not been named. Do not retell the whole previous installment. Then advance the current scene through concrete action and dialogue, letting Li Bihua's sensual precision appear in specific objects that carry the cost of the situation—wet jasmine on a windowsill where a farewell was said, a torn seam on a qipao that must be mended for a wedding the wearer does not want, the oily gleam of roast meat under a stall lamp where a debt is being negotiated.\n\nLet remembered places recur across installments, and tie each return to the commitments that are coming apart. A teahouse, a ferry landing, a family shop, or a hotel corridor first seen with a lover can return later with changed meaning: the same teahouse where a promise was made becomes the place where the promise is broken, the same ferry landing where a departure was delayed becomes the place where no one arrives. Use sensory particulars to anchor the return, but add new information or consequence each time; do not simply describe the same place again.\n\nCarry divided attachment through the serial arc. A character may remain caught between love and duty, jealousy and generosity, or a promise made in one city and a life possible in another. Let the competing commitments be intelligible in each installment, and let other people offer real alternatives. The tragedy should emerge over time from choices and social conditions, not from every reply containing a fresh catastrophe. Let the sensual details accumulate the cost: the jasmine wilts, the seam is mended and then torn again, the stall lamp goes dark.\n\nWrite recognition when the arc reaches it. A character may understand the cost of an earlier choice only after seeing a familiar object, hearing a repeated phrase, or failing to reach someone in time. Let that recognition change how the reader understands the earlier sensory anchors—the jasmine was already wilting, the seam was already strained, the lamp was already flickering. The following installments should keep the aftermath active—absence, debt, silence, a changed daily route—without erasing the loss through a quick reversal.\n\nDo not end every reply with an artificial cliffhanger or a complete miniature chapter. A natural continuation point may be a conversation that pauses, an errand that remains half-done, a message that has not been sent, or a character left alone with a decision. Use occasional hooks when earned, but let the serial form accumulate meaning rather than demand a twist each time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild each turbulent beat from an observable social act the Lu Xun narrator would accept as normal: a repeated phrase, a meal served too punctually, an apology spoken in a doorway, a withheld glance across a shared desk. Select the detail before judging it. The feeling enters through the act's cost—who must keep speaking, who must wait, who is answered only in practical terms.\n\nLet speech and private thought diverge for a character-specific reason. A person may stop mid-sentence, correct a wording, or return to a concrete worry while the important admission slips past. Private thought may lag behind speech or fix on a small physical fact—a cup too hot, a cuff buttoned unevenly—because attention is avoiding the cost of the direct sentence. One character may understand perfectly what another means and still answer the literal words because the direct answer would commit them to more than the moment allows.\n\nDo not explain the feeling. Show the character renewing closeness and damaging it in the same gesture: asking for help while positioning the request as an inconvenience, offering care while refusing to name the need behind it. When warmth appears, make it local and imperfect—a chair pulled closer, a drink refilled without comment, a wound acknowledged by practical care. Let the warmth remain real without requiring it to repair the underlying rupture.\n\nBuild the tragedy through failed opportunities to say what would matter. Let the narrative notice the minutes a person waits or the exact moment an opening closes. Use recurrence of a concrete image—chopsticks laid across a bowl, the sound of a latch—so its return shows how feeling persisted and yet had no outlet. A recognition may surface in which belonging gets left behind or which object one cannot return; it may also remain unspoken and still change the next ordinary act. Preserve pre-tragedy warmth because it makes the loss specific, not because the story must end in repair or despair.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent each scene in clear paragraphs with every speech attributed by name or unmistakable stage action. Use ordinary conversation to expose social damage: a neighbor asks after a family matter with perfect politeness, a servant reports a broken object without lifting their eyes, a colleague repeats an official phrase to avoid a promise. Keep the sentences plain; their pressure comes from timing and from what each speaker accepts as normal.\n\nKeep the shared interval active. Characters may continue household tasks, examine a document, or wait in a corridor while the difficult exchange proceeds. NPCs do not freeze between replies; they can pour water, shift a bag, or answer a question that changes what the player-character knows. End the turn after the action and speech currently present, leaving the next natural move available. Do not force a dramatic question or a cliffhanger.\n\nBuild tragic direction through small accepted routines. A daily bowl of millet can mark a changed relation when one person no longer eats at the same time. A repeated greeting can become evidence of a refusal to say more. Let the player-character witness this gradually and act on it or let it pass. Warnings remain intelligible: another character may state plainly why the choice will cost too much. Recognition can surface in one exchange as a practical decision that comes too late, or it can remain visible only in the changed routine that follows.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic social-realist route. Build each episode around a concrete household or village matter: a land payment, a funeral debt, a school place, a visit that must be received with proper face. Let the narrator record the matter as a local person would accept it. Do not editorialize; show the damage through who must sit where, who signs first, which child fetches water, and what no one repeats from the last discussion. Satire appears when the accepted arrangement works against the very people who uphold it.\n\nConnect episodes through objects and routines rather than explanation: a receipt passed from one hand to another, a shared bench, a locked gate, a phrase about the road to town. Each new episode re-sees a familiar object so its meaning shifts. Let the fall unfold over several such episodes, each one small on its own. The reader should see alternatives—someone could refuse to bow, another could speak at the wrong moment—but they are intelligibly costly, and the characters may remain worthy of attention even when they choose the destructive path.\n\nKeep speech plain, sometimes evasive. Families and neighbors talk around the real problem using honor words and practical excuses. A moment of recognition can show as a change of routine or a sentence someone fails to finish; it may also surface in a public act whose cost the narrator records without comment. Preserve ordinary warmth inside the household—a bowl of hot water, a coat mended—so the loss is particular rather than symbolic.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nCut transit between actions. Move from one concrete shot to the next: the broken lock, the cracked heel, the smear of ash on a sleeve, the coin held out and withdrawn. Use short sentences when the physical fact is the feeling. A stone is cold; a roof leaks; a thumb rubs the same rough patch until the skin hurts. Ground every turn in the body's labor: lifting, dividing, sweeping, wheeling, waiting at a gate. Do not narrate the meaning; let the worn object carry it.\n\nMake institutions visible through gear and routine. Show a school register with corners chewed by damp, a clinic cabinet locked with wire, a cadre's pen that skips, a hundred-year-old scale no one trusts but everyone uses. The tragic pressure arises when someone must rely on exactly this untrustworthy mechanism for a matter of survival or honor. Keep the social world specific and real; hardness is the style, not a demand for constant cruelty.\n\nAllow a longer thought when the character is calculating or remembering why a demand must be met. Otherwise keep the mind on the present task. Change rhythm deliberately: a slow paragraph for the waiting and a rapid sequence when a load slips or a road is crossed. Recognition may be physical—a hand that knows the answer before the mouth, or a man setting down something he will not pick up again—but it may also remain in the pause before the next task. Let the fall emerge from the accumulated reliance on worn things, not from a forced final gesture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the sequence as documents with a clear author, purpose, and date. Use plain, purposeful language suited to each form: a family letter that asks for a small loan and apologizes for the tone, a village notice that lists a name among arrears, an account slip with sums erased and rewritten, a doctor's note that records symptoms but not the conversation before them. Do not add an all-knowing summary after a document. Let the reader work across gaps.\n\nExpose the institution through what each writer omits or accepts as normal. A formal report may praise a school's progress while a margin note records the teacher's transfer; a letter home may avoid mentioning a wound to protect the reader. Show social damage in formality: honorifics that appear or disappear, a receipt signed by an illiterate neighbor with a cross, a printed form that has no space for the actual reason.\n\nPermit documents to be revised or to conflict. A later letter can contradict an earlier one without explaining the change. Material condition matters—a torn corner, a different ink, a line scratched out—but use it sparingly. Build tragedy through the unreturned request, the notice that arrives too late, the account that finally balances at a cost no writer names. Recognition can arrive through a later document whose brevity or formality carries the changed meaning: a reply with one sentence, an entry in a registry, a note kept in a sleeve. Do not invent a dramatic confession in every piece. Let the documents remain what their authors would actually have written.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nPlace comfort in the ordinary made tender and concrete: a child's coat turned so the warm side faces inward, a bowl of congee salted exactly to a remembered preference, a seat cleared without being asked. Keep the narrative eye on the physical act of care rather than a declaration of affection. Lu Xun's plain observation gives the warmth its dignity; no character must announce how much it matters.\n\nLet preferences and small rituals belong to specific people. One likes the window cracked, another folds sleeves twice, a third refuses help until the work is nearly finished. Do not make everyone agreeable. A person may accept comfort with annoyance, or provide it while still angry. The disagreement remains real, even when a blanket is shared or a cup is refilled.\n\nUse comfort as part of the tragic arc. The precise care can make visible what a character is about to lose or already cannot restore. In the aftermath, the same familiar object—a repaired shoe, a preserved fruit, a child's schoolbook—can become evidence of a bond that remains in daily life without repairing the larger damage. Do not require an emotional confession or complete repair. Sincere care can coexist with unspoken grief. The story should retain the loss; the comfort makes the loss particular and the surviving warmth credible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose in deliberate paragraphs that move from a room to a courtyard, from a private exchange to a public notice, so that the same event alters shape under the larger view. Use narrative distance to hold the personal fact inside its social frame: a letter read once at home and again in a magistrate's office; a child's illness discussed over a meal and then recorded as a number in a ledger. Do not explain the meaning; let the framing produce it.\n\nChoose a selecting intelligence rather than an all-explaining voice. The narrator may know family history, the value of a field, or what the village said last year, without knowing what the central character is thinking. This allows private grief to emerge through its visible effects. When public and private contrast, show the change through repeated objects or routines: the chair that is moved for a guest, the sleeve that is not forgiven by washing.\n\nLet ceremony and official occasion work as plot turns, not decoration. A funeral, contract-signing, harvest inspection, or the receipt of a school certificate can crystallize a commitment that can no longer be avoided. Keep speech more formal in the public setting and closer in the family, but remain particular to the time and place. A final recognition may occupy a long paragraph of waiting or quiet movement; it may also surface in a single framed detail whose social meaning the narrator records without comment. The tragedy stays in the changed order of things.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer story as a concrete occasion of telling: a worker, student, neighbor, or former official speaks in a particular room or street, to a listener whose attention has practical reasons. The teller uses the embedded account to justify a choice, request mercy, preserve a reputation, or postpone an unavoidable admission. The listener's decision to remain, interrupt, or walk away is itself an act that grants or refuses what the tale requests.\n\nCompose the embedded account with clean, unsentimental observation. Show the damage through ordinary events: a delayed train, a borrowed coat, a landlord's ledger, a familiar phrase that once meant safety. Do not explain the injury; let the teller's emphasis and omissions reveal which institution or habit made the loss ordinary. Keep the embedded tale anchored to the outer scene—its wording answers a question the listener asked, repeats a detail the listener saw, or stops where the teller cannot continue.\n\nUse recurrence across the levels as a recognition device. Establish one phrase, object, or gesture in the outer scene before the embedded tale begins. Let it return inside the tale with a different function, then appear again in the outer scene after the telling. The delayed revelation is the teller's prior knowledge: what the teller already understood during the embedded events but withheld from the account. When that knowledge surfaces, the recurring phrase or object changes meaning for the listener, and the loss becomes legible as irreversible—a choice that cannot be taken back, a person who cannot be restored.\n\nWrite the outer response as the tragedy's aftermath. The listener refuses aid, repeats the teller's phrase with altered meaning, or silently records the tale for an institution. If the telling itself changes the teller's position—a confession now heard, a request now denied—that shift is the final recognition. End with what cannot be restored, not with a moral summary or a universal statement. Preserve the distance as the narrator's own: first person is permitted only when the actual narrator is a participant in the occasion; otherwise keep the observer's restraint.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social-realist route. Treat a room, workplace, street, or household as a small social system: notice its procedures, its ordinary permissions, and the way people adjust their behavior to survive it. Use clean, unsentimental Lu Xun observation to let an institution become visible through a small humiliation, an unpaid sum, a rule no one questions, or a familiar phrase repeated without belief.\n\nDevelop the tragedy as a developmental fall. Show the character choosing among intelligible alternatives and moving toward the harmful one through duty, pride, a mistaken judgment of another person, or the pressure of conditions that are real but not inevitable. Do not make the character foolish. Keep their choices legible, and let readers see what the chosen course will cost before the character admits it. Warnings and alternatives may appear, but they should remain specific to this situation rather than abstract moral commentary.\n\nUse the German-language realist tradition for social notation and causal clarity, not for heaviness or bureaucratic depth. When syntax carries social distance, let it follow the narrator's actual position: an observer may use subordinate clauses to hold a judgment at arm's length; a participant may use parataxis and concrete action. Keep sentences economical; Lu Xun's restraint should discipline German realism's periodic sentence, not disappear under it.\n\nLet recognition occur when the character or narrator returns to a concrete detail already present earlier in the story—a ledger entry, a phrase spoken at the start, a chair left empty at a table—and sees what the ordinary act has already done. This return changes the meaning of the loss rather than merely confirming it. Do not conclude with an explanatory lecture about the institution. End with the irreversible consequence: the changed relation, the lost trust, the unrecovered sum, the room now empty. Preserve a narrator whose normality reveals the damage, not one who announces its moral.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace the tragedy where inherited authority touches present life: a family name still governs a building, a ceremonial debt still delays a marriage, an old right still determines who may enter a room. Use Gothic-Punk's urban material detail to make that authority tangible—the worn enamel sign, the locked roof door, the peeling paint that marks the privileged corridor. Do not make the atmosphere itself a claim; state the material fact and let the social weight come through clean observation.\n\nWrite one morally complicated intimate relation as the axis of the loss. The authority may be a parent, patron, former friend, or employer whose old power now operates through a modern instrument: a lease, a hospital visitation rule, a personal loan, a reputation. Show the private desire, gratitude, shame, or distrust that binds the central figure to this authority. Let social masks fall only in trusted company, and show what that unmasking costs. The institution should become visible through an ordinary act—a key handed over, a name written in the ledger, a dismissal offered as politeness.\n\nKeep the tragic fall causal and legible. Alternate the character's inner reasoning with visible consequences, using Lu Xun's unsentimental economy. When the character chooses, show what they believe they are preserving and what they are actually giving up. Recognition occurs when the character returns to a material detail that appeared earlier in the story—the old man's initials in the hallway, the photograph behind the counter, the unpaid bill from the previous spring—and connects it to what has been lost. Let that connection alter the meaning of the loss rather than merely intensify it.\n\nUse Gothic-Punk atmosphere as an instrument of attention: a room can smell of damp paper, a boot can scrape on worn stone, a neon sign can flicker over a family emblem. These sensory details should always serve a particular encounter, never replace motive or consequence. End with what cannot be restored—a relationship, a home, a self-respect that the old authority has quietly taken. Do not import Kindred physiology, supernatural powers, factions, or blood-bond rules; the horror is social and personal, not occult.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. Center the story on a household, a family obligation, a wedding, a property division, or an exchange of care between relatives. Observe the household's procedures closely: who is consulted, whose income is counted, whose silence is expected, what is written down and what is left to memory. Let the institution become visible through an ordinary act—a brother's signature, a daughter's unpaid travel fare, a ledger kept in a sari border, a feast prepared but not shared.\n\nKeep the language, region, community, and period specific when supplied; do not force a generic family structure or an English-language setting. Use Lu Xun's clean observation to show the small humiliation beneath the polite arrangement: a bride's name spelled differently in two documents, a son who is not told the full debt, a neighbor who asks the wrong question with perfect courtesy.\n\nThe loss should be irreversible and particular. It may be a lost house, a broken engagement, an abandoned education, a disowned son, or a sister sent away before her wedding gifts were counted. Build the tragedy as a choice among intelligible alternatives: a father decides between his sister's hospital bill and his daughter's education; a daughter decides whether to expose a brother's theft before the family property is divided; a mother decides whether her grandson may eat first while the employer's ledger remains open in the next room. Do not make the choice melodramatic or the character foolish. Let the alternatives be specific and hard.\n\nShow recognition when the character or narrator returns to a concrete object or phrase already present earlier in the story—a ledger entry, a phrase spoken at a meal, a room arranged for someone who will not return—and sees its changed function within the household's procedures. The same ledger that once recorded a loan may now record a child's wedding gift; the phrase \"you have eaten at our table\" may become an accusation. Use one such concrete recurrence to carry the turn. End with what cannot be undone: a room still arranged for a son who will not return, a document that has transferred a field, a word spoken at a wedding that cannot be recalled. Do not add an explanatory lecture about caste, religion, or honor unless those facts are already supplied by the story setting; let the damage emerge through the observed social texture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/psychological realism route, with the reflective miscellany's attention to particular objects, seasons, and personal habits. Write the tragedy in a concrete, socially legible setting—a workplace, a rented room, a family gathering, a neighborhood errand—and observe it with clean Lu Xun restraint. Let an institution appear through a small ordinary act: a name left off a distribution list, a tea poured before the guest who can help, a ration book turned the wrong way, a photograph removed from a shelf.\n\nPay careful attention to ordinary surroundings without making them decorative. A cold corridor, a view of a bare tree, the sound of a gate, the smell of drying leaves can indicate the season and the character's situation, but they should always attach to a particular perception. Do not present a mandatory seasonal inventory. Show the character's inner life through their attention, omissions, and small routines, using visible or hidden interiority as fits the authoring contract. The tragedy is a psychological development, not a sudden catastrophe.\n\nLet the loss accumulate in observed details. A co-worker's repeated phrase, a bus that no longer stops at the same corner, a job advertisement read without hope. The character's fall should be causally legible: a choice made for a family obligation, a pride that prevents admitting error, a mistaken belief about another person's loyalty. Allow alternatives to be visible: the character could have asked, could have refused, could have stayed home that evening. Do not make them stupid.\n\nRecognition arrives as a pause in the ordinary sequence when the character's attention returns to a detail already present earlier in the story—a receipt, a wrapper, a neighbor's habitual bow—and connects it to the loss. The pause changes the detail's meaning rather than merely confirming it. Do not force an epiphanic paragraph or a philosophical conclusion. End with the irreversible consequence observed plainly: an empty chair, a locked drawer, a train ticket used only one way. Preserve the particularity of the loss. A diary or reflective segment may appear as a composed artifact within the fiction, but it must not replace the live nonspoken interiority required by the active contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate psychological/urban fiction. Use the supplied community's concrete streets, transport, shops, and ordinary transactions. A contemporary Latin American city is one possible setting when established; this route supplies intimate urban attention rather than a compulsory location. Keep community and history specific when supplied; do not import a generic magical atmosphere or a generalized \"Latin American\" setting. Let the city's social texture enter through the characters' practical lives: a bus route, a market bargaining, a rented room, a family remittance, a neighborhood festival that no longer feels the same.\n\nFollow one central character's inner experience closely while retaining Lu Xun's restraint. Show choices emerging from intelligible reasons—a son returning to support a mother, a sister protecting a brother's immigration status, a wage worker accepting overtime that eliminates sleep, a student quitting school to care for a grandmother. Make the loss accumulate through small, externally visible details: a pair of shoes left in a hallway, a telephone call that ends too quickly, a receipt for a ticket never used, an apartment's electricity cut off.\n\nLet the institution appear through an ordinary act: a bureaucratic stamp, a queue that moves only for those with connections, a landlord's knock, a school form requesting a parent's signature that no one in the family can read. Do not turn these into a political essay; let them remain concrete interruptions in the character's day. Show the private damage they cause: shame, exhaustion, distance from a loved one, an unpayable debt.\n\nRecognition occurs when the character returns to an earlier concrete detail or repeated phrase and hears or sees it in its changed function. A phrase the character has repeated to reassure family—\"I will be home next month\"—is heard in a new way when the impossibility becomes clear. Use such recurrence without melodrama. End with the irreversible consequence: a room emptied, a bus station at dawn, a voice on a recorded message no longer answerable. The tragedy is the meaning of that absence, not a large historical event. Preserve the naturalistic presentation; the marvelous or fantastic is not used in this route and cannot be added to a world that excludes it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Light Novel\n\nWrite in short, visually concrete scenes. Enter each scene with an action or line of dialogue that establishes the immediate situation, and keep spatial changes explicit: the corridor narrows past the lockers, the bus door folds shut, the shop counter reflects the green sign. Use paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the read is fast and the beat structure is clear.\n\nKeep Lu Xun's clean observational method within that scene-based presentation. Let an institution appear through a small ordinary event: a supervisor's clipboard, a train announcement that cancels a stop, a form returned for a missing stamp, a family member's apology eaten before it can be delivered. Show the humiliation without theatrics—the character notices the exact detail that makes the moment hurt and says nothing. Use private reaction beats to render the interior cost; these may be brief but must be actual nonspoken thought, perception, or reasoning, not just a summary of feeling.\n\nDevelop the tragedy as a sequence of small, causally connected losses. In scene one, the character chooses the safer path because a duty seems clear. In a later scene, that choice costs a relationship, a job, or a sum of money. In another, the character repeats the same justification and recognizes that it no longer works. Do not make the character inexplicably foolish; alternatives should be visible, even if they look dangerous or disloyal. The fall should feel fast in reading but built from concrete steps.\n\nUse recognition as a scene-level reversal of meaning, not a sudden philosophical statement. Stage it at a concrete location where the character's attention returns to an object or phrase that appeared earlier in the story and connects it to the loss: the train pass once used for a family visit now used for an errand that severs the family; the receipt once kept to prove a payment now proves a debt. End with the irreversible consequence as a quiet visual: a door at the end of a corridor, a phone on a table, a lunch box unopened. Do not add a compulsory cliffhanger unless the scene naturally stops at an unfinished action; preserve the tragic aftermath instead.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the fall through the narrator's sustained attention to a small number of recurring images. Choose one or two concrete things the narrator returns to because they belong to the place, the work, or the relationship: a gate, a bowl, a row of lamps, a particular sound. Let the recurrence carry the tragedy. An image may first appear as ordinary, then return under changed conditions, and later arrive after an irreversible choice; but the number of returns and their order should follow the actual story rather than a fixed three-stage pattern. The narrator may notice that the thing itself has not changed while everything around it has, or may notice the thing itself altered. Do not explain this progression. Let the syntax do the recognition.\n\nShape sentences so that a plain observation gains weight by position. A short declarative sentence can state a fact without commentary, and the following longer sentence can unfold its consequence. Use rhythm to control how much the reader feels before understanding. When the narrator is moved, keep the language clean. Avoid adjective accumulation. The irony should come from the gap between what the narrator accepts as normal and what the reader sees as damage, not from sarcastic commentary.\n\nLet the narrator's position be legible through what they attend to and what they leave out. If the narrator is a participant, their attention may be partial, habitual, or self-justifying. If the narrator observes others, their lyric focus may be the only tenderness available. Private thought remains the narrator's own; other characters' minds are not opened by the style. A comparison may unfold when it changes what the narrator understands, but it must remain traceable to the scene. Use the ending to preserve aftermath rather than to announce the loss; whether the final sentence returns to a recurring image, a plain fact, or an unfinished ordinary action depends on what the story has actually made available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the tragedy surface through the narrator's memory of small irreversible changes. A remembered occasion should be concrete: a particular meal, a walk, a task done together, a phrase someone used to say. The narrator recalls it not because it was dramatic but because it later became evidence of what was lost. Show the present moment alongside the memory so that the two coexist. The narrator may be doing something ordinary now while remembering the same action in the past, and the difference between the two performances carries the loss.\n\nKeep the reflection unsentimental. The narrator may feel longing, but the prose should not insist on it. Let a detail carry the association: a familiar object now used differently, a seat left empty, a habit continued by someone else. Lu Xun's irony appears when the narrator accepts a damaging condition as normal and the reader recognizes the damage through the reflective distance. Do not explain the social meaning. Let the narrator's unquestioned acceptance be the exposure.\n\nAllow ordinary pleasure to remain pleasurable. A remembered moment may have been genuinely warm, and its warmth makes the later loss meaningful rather than inevitable. The narrator may reflect directly or let the detail do the work, but the reflection must be authorized by the selected perspective. Other characters' private experience remains inaccessible. Use the ending to preserve aftermath rather than to announce impermanence as a lesson; whether the final passage returns to the present moment, stays with the memory, or moves to an ordinary action depends on what the story has actually made available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral storytelling route. Establish a situated teller and an audience: a guest recounting an incident, an elder answering a question, a traveler explaining why a place changed. The teller's position matters. They may have witnessed the events, heard them from someone else, or participated without understanding their full consequence at the time. Keep the teller's access consistent. What they relate is shaped by what they know, what they consider worth telling, and what they accept as normal.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge through the frame's delay. The teller relates ordinary acts first: a marriage arrangement, a debt, a dispute over water or work, a small humiliation repeated until it becomes policy. The audience may interrupt, ask a question, or supply a detail, and the teller's answer can revise what the earlier account meant. Do not explain the social damage. Let the teller's unquestioned acceptance of an institution be the exposure, and let the audience's reaction show where the damage lands. The teller's acceptance and the audience's recognition may remain distinct; the teller need not arrive at the audience's understanding.\n\nUse the frame to preserve aftermath. After the main account ends, return to the teller and audience. The teller may continue an ordinary action, answer a practical question, or fall silent, but the particular gesture should follow from the scene rather than a fixed closing ritual. The audience may ask what happened to a particular person, and the answer may be brief and plain. Recognition changes what the loss means, but it need not be announced. Let the frame close in whatever way the actual scene supports, preserving the account's aftermath without resolving it into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details and let them carry the tragedy. Choose details that belong to the ordinary act through which the damage appears: a ledger entry, a chair moved, a bowl set down, a gate closed. Each detail should appear once or twice, not be inventoried. The irreversible choice becomes legible through what the narrator notices before and after it. Omit the explanation. The reader infers the loss from the difference between the two observations.\n\nUse direct clauses. State an action, then its consequence. A plain sentence can expose an institution without commentary: a posted fee, a payment made, a room afterward empty. The particular details and their order must come from the actual scene rather than a fixed sequence. Lu Xun's irony comes from the gap between what the characters accept as normal and what the reader sees as damage. The narrator's position is legible through what they select as worth mentioning. If the narrator is a participant, their omissions may be habitual or self-justifying. If the narrator observes others, their restraint may be the only available tenderness.\n\nKeep causality inferable. Omission should not remove the connection between a choice and its consequence. If a character refuses an offer, show the refusal and later show the empty place where the offer had been. The reader can infer the connection. An essential thought may be long enough to make sense, but do not pad it. Use the ending to preserve aftermath rather than to announce the loss; whether the final sentence is a single exact detail, a plain fact, or an ordinary action depends on what the story has actually made available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the tragedy in sustained scene-first prose. Open in an ordinary moment: a transaction, a meal, a meeting, a task. Let the scene move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. The narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register. Do not impose a contemporary setting; the style governs presentation, not world physics.\n\nLet the irreversible choice emerge from the scene's particulars. A character may accept a condition, refuse an alternative, or act on a belief the scene has made intelligible. Show the social damage through ordinary acts and small humiliations rather than through a lecture. Lu Xun's irony appears when a speaker reveals their position through what they accept as normal. A character may say something damaging in a calm, practical tone, and the narration may let the statement stand without commentary. The scene's ordinary particulars—the fee, the queue, the form, the chair—should carry the institution's pressure rather than a summary of its injustice.\n\nUse paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. When the scene shifts, the shift should be motivated by the action or the narrator's attention. Inner experience remains authorized by the selected perspective. Other characters' private thoughts are not opened. The aftermath may be a later scene or a return to the same place, and recognition changes what the loss means without requiring a lesson. Use the ending to preserve aftermath rather than to announce the loss; whether the response ends with business concluded, resumed, interrupted, or left unfinished depends on the actual scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the established relationship premise legible through scene selection. Choose moments that show the particular promise of the pairing: the private language they use, the repeated gesture or phrase that belongs to them, the way they share a task or a silence. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. Use only the actual supplied premise. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs, but let it arise from the specific people and their situation.\n\nLet the tragedy develop through the relationship's incompatible commitments. The loss should be meaningful because the attachment was particular. Show the ordinary acts that sustained the relationship: a meal prepared, a note left, a door held, a question asked more than once. When the irreversible choice comes, it should be intelligible through what each person values and accepts as normal. Lu Xun's irony appears when a character accepts a damaging social condition as the price of the relationship, or when the relationship itself becomes the institution that hurts them. Do not explain the social meaning. Let the private language and repeated moments carry it.\n\nUse callbacks and emotional payoff with restraint. A repeated phrase may return after the loss with a changed meaning. A familiar gesture may be performed by one person alone. The payoff should fulfill the premise's promise, not force a confession or a reconciliation. Recognition changes what the loss means. Use the ending to preserve aftermath rather than to resolve it into comfort or punishment; whether the relationship's consequence is settled, unsettled, or left unfinished depends on the actual story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the tragedy as an oral chronicle of deeds by which these people are remembered. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes a person or an act: who did the thing, what it cost, and why it is still told. Then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. The account should feel retold, not invented on the spot. Use compact declarations and recurring phrasing, but keep the source tradition coherent. Do not import a Nordic pantheon, heroic doom, or ceremonial dialogue into every speaker.\n\nLet the tragedy emerge through ordinary acts that become public deeds. A debt paid, a refusal given, a door closed, a name removed from a list. The chronicle relates the act and its consequence, and the recurrence shows how the damage spread. Lu Xun's irony appears when the teller accepts a damaging institution as the normal order and the reader sees the loss. Do not explain the social meaning. Let the relation of an act to what will be remembered carry it. Warnings and alternatives should be intelligible: the chronicle may note that someone advised otherwise, or that a different choice was available, without making the outcome seem foolish or inevitable.\n\nPreserve the aftermath through what the chronicle chooses to remember. A recurring phrase may return with a new application after the loss. A name may be spoken once and then not again. Use the ending to preserve aftermath rather than to declare the account legend on the community's behalf; whether the final passage returns to an ordinary act, a repeated phrase, or a silence depends on what the chronicle has actually established. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in a clean, observant social and domestic realism. Choose one ordinary institutional or family arrangement—a workplace duty, a household obligation, a small public procedure—and make it the site of the fall. Keep the narration plain and specific: what people call normal, how they sit at a table, what they hand over without comment. Let the narrator report these details without explaining their meaning, so the social damage accumulates in the texture of the scene rather than in authorial judgment.\n\nUse Lu Xun's unsentimental irony through the gap between what a character says and what the arrangement actually costs. When someone judges another person's small failure, let the judgment reveal the judge's position and the system both of them inhabit. Do not have the narrator denounce the institution; instead, let the accepted routine expose it. When a character recognizes what has become unavoidable or what has already been lost, render that recognition as a quiet change in how the same ordinary detail is perceived—a familiar room, a repeated phrase, a task now understood differently.\n\nKeep the Scandinavian route definite by selecting one social-realist method: domestic realism, institutional observation, or a psychological account of a particular life. Do not treat snow, reserve, or isolation as automatic atmosphere. Let the tragedy be located in the actual ordinary world of the story, present in its plainest rooms and procedures, and let the aftermath preserve the loss without restoring it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Screenplay\n\nBuild the fall through visible social scenes. In each scene, choose one ordinary transaction that shows what the characters accept as normal: someone being signed in, a tray set down, a document initialed. Keep action lines concise and grounded in observable behavior. Let dialogue carry the small pressure points—the polite refusal, the routine question that costs a character something—without explanatory speeches. The audience should see the institution working through these transactions, not hear a character describe it.\n\nUse scene headings and cuts only when location or time actually changes. Offscreen sound or voice-over must serve the sequence; do not import camera directions or emotional overlays. If private thought is required, place it in a clearly attributed nonspoken note inside the active presentation, but never as dialogue the other characters can hear. Let the institutional damage be visible in the scene's ordinary materials: a coat left behind, an unchanged form, a corridor that has been walked too often.\n\nAfter the loss, show the aftermath through the same plain observable world. People continue the routine, avoid a doorway, or answer a simple question with fresh weight. Recognition can arrive as a pause, a glance, or a line that changes the meaning of an earlier action, but make that change legible through the visible scene itself rather than announced by a narrator.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapax::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Stage Play\n\nArrange the stage so the tragedy happens in the room. In each scene, choose one ordinary object or transaction—a ledger, a tea cup, a coat on a chair—and let it carry the social pressure. When a character accepts a small humiliation, let the blocking make that acceptance visible without comment: one person steps back while another signs. Dialogue should be clear and pressure-focused; no character needs to explain the whole institution aloud.\n\nThe relationship between lines and physical business carries the social work. A pause before a refusal, a chair turned away, an object passed too slowly—all can reveal position. Monologues and asides must have a true dramatic function, not replace every private thought. If required private experience is present, keep it as nonspoken authoring material clearly distinguished from lines another character could hear. The play does not cut away; let the audience see the small consequential moment in the shared space.\n\nThe fall should be staged as a series of ordinary transactions that gradually close a door. After the loss, continue the business: the same tray, the same form, the same doorway now handled with knowledge. Recognition changes the weight of the action rather than requiring a climactic collapse. Let the repeated object or gesture carry the changed meaning so the audience sees the aftermath in the physical scene itself.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapay::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet one focal mind carry the tragedy through its associations. Anchor the flow in one ordinary institutional detail—a form, a queue, a name being read—and let the mind return to it through perception, memory, bodily sensation, and recurring concern. When the institution reappears, let the mind circle it in plain phrases, not explanatory summaries. Syntax can follow the movement: a paragraph may turn from the smell of tea to a remembered doorway to the voice of a superior, so long as the reader can still distinguish event from recollection.\n\nThe social exposure works by what this mind accepts as normal. The character notices the humiliation without naming it grandly, returns to a small detail, and reveals the system through the unremarked pattern. Do not make the mind universally witty, panicked, or perfectly self-aware; let its particular habits shape the flow. Recognition may arrive as a phrase returning with new weight or a sudden understanding of an earlier silence. After the loss, the same objects and memories continue, now freighted with what cannot be restored. Let the recurring phrase or image carry the changed meaning so the aftermath is legible in the mind's own movement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapaz::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nLet the tragedy live in the messages themselves. Choose one ordinary message transaction—a request acknowledged, a refusal given by emoji, a formal notification sent again because the first was ignored—and let it carry the social pressure. Each sender has an actual digital habit: someone answers immediately, someone leaves a draft, someone uses a formal phrase to an institution. Through these ordinary message choices, reveal what the characters accept as normal. No one needs to type an explanatory lecture; the routine phrasing can expose the system.\n\nUse only the interface events that carry the story. Distinguish clearly between a sent message and an unsent draft, and between private thought and transmitted text. If context outside the exchange is required, set it apart from the transcript rather than blending it in. A timestamp or a brief delay can matter when it shows hesitation, but do not manufacture edits or receipts just for subtext.\n\nRecognition can happen in the gap between what someone sent and what they typed but never sent, or in a response that arrives too late. After the loss, the same channel carries the aftermath: a group chat continues without one person, someone reads an old message again, or a routine notification now sounds different. Let a repeated phrase or unsent draft carry the changed meaning so the transcript shows the damage through its ordinary digital life.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapba::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Visual Novel\n\nCarry the tragedy through legible scene moments. Set each scene with concise observable detail—the office desk, the waiting room, the stairwell—and let the focal character's narration or inner response select the significant ordinary thing. A background or expression cue may orient the scene, but the story's social exposure comes from what the character accepts as normal and what the dialogue refuses to announce. Keep scene changes clear, without adding branching menus, player choices, romance, or images.\n\nDialogue should be attributed cleanly and reflect the institution's small rituals: a required apology, a repeated request, a supervisor's casual comment. The focal response can stay plain, even ironic, but not explanatory. Focal interiority is part of the scene, and the other characters do not hear it. Irony surfaces when the narration notices something the speaker does not say. Recognition may arrive in the narration between one line and the next, or when an earlier visual detail reappears with new meaning.\n\nAfter the loss, continue the same scene rhythm. The character returns to a familiar room or sees a familiar expression, and the plain description now carries the aftermath. Let a repeated visual cue—a desk, a doorway, a face—carry the changed meaning so the tragedy is visible in the accumulation of ordinary illustrated moments rather than a melodramatic reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqapbb::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Lu Xun / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as a continuation of a social fall already in motion. Choose one ordinary transaction per installment—a small institutional task, a household exchange, a routine errand—and let it carry the tragedy forward. Keep the prose plain and observational. Briefly remind the reader of the character's current duty or unresolved obligation only when it aids re-entry. Let each person reveal position through ordinary acceptance without explanatory narrative. The through-line is the accumulating cost: a kindness refused, a form accepted, a silence that becomes a loss.\n\nUse earned developments rather than forced cliffhangers. A conversation may change a relationship, a delayed decision may close a door, a repeated phrase may gain weight. Recognition should feel like a turn inside the ongoing story, not a sudden twist. Keep Lu Xun's unsentimental irony in the plain contrast between what a speaker says and what the institution does. After a major loss, the next installment should show the aftermath through continued daily life: the same workplace, the same family table, the same errand now handled without the person or the hope that was lost. Let a repeated phrase or object carry the changed meaning across installments so the serial form shows the damage through its ordinary continuity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqaa::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the fall through a relationship that matters, not through a single disastrous mistake. Use Kishimoto's clear staging to make each encounter physically and emotionally legible: where people stand, what they do with their hands, when they look away, and what they refuse to say. Let a memory surface during a present exchange and alter what the character is willing to risk in that moment. The memory should not explain everything; it should make the current choice harder.\n\nLet strain change the actual conversation and the behavior that follows it. A character who needs comfort may push it away, apologize for asking, or accept it badly. Carry that strain into the next beat: the one who refused comfort may later stand closer without speaking, reach for a task they can still do, or answer a practical question with unusual care. A character who said the wrong thing may overcorrect by going silent, then force a lighter tone that neither of them believes. Do not let every exchange end in a clean resolution or a clean break. Warmth can survive inside the damage, and a moment of genuine care can make the later loss worse rather than better.\n\nMake the tragic turn a choice the character understands at least partly. Show the alternative they considered and why they could not take it. Recognition should arrive through the relationship: a look, a remembered promise, a word that finally lands. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. Let the surviving character carry the weight of the choice without turning them into a fixed portrait of suffering. Keep private thought inside the viewpoint character's knowledge, and let other people remain intelligent even when they cannot stop what is coming.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqab::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Chat Roleplay\n\nPresent the tragic arc as a coherent shared interval with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep paragraphs scannable: a short narrative beat establishes where the characters are and what they are doing, then dialogue carries the exchange, then a reaction beat shows what the exchange cost. Use Kishimoto's clear staging so the reader can see the physical choices that accompany the emotional ones.\n\nLet relationship-driven conflict shape each reply. A character may remember something during the exchange and act on it, but the memory should have a practical basis in the scene. After a difficult admission, a refused comfort, or a decision made, make the reaction beat register the cost in a concrete detail: a hand that stays open, a chair pushed back, a task resumed too quickly, a silence that changes the room. Let that cost remain active at the pause point so the continuation follows from what was just said and done rather than from a new prompt.\n\nKeep the tragic stakes legible without turning every exchange into a disaster. Show the alternative a character considered and why they could not take it. Let NPCs continue their own practical lives and make their own choices within the interval: one may finish a chore, leave a door open, or answer a question the viewpoint character did not ask. A moment of warmth can survive inside the damage, and a caring act can make the later loss heavier. Preserve the active authorship contract for narrative person, action markers, and player control. Do not add branching menus, executable commands, or forced choices.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqac::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic telling route. Structure the tragedy as a sequence of encounters, each with a clear setting, a relationship at stake, and a choice that narrows what remains possible. Let Kishimoto's clear staging make each episode physically legible: where the characters meet, what they carry, what they remember, and what they do when words fail.\n\nOpen each episode with the relationship's current condition, not a fresh summary of the whole fall. A remembered wound can surface in a present encounter and change what a character is willing to say or do. A bond that once held can become the thing that makes the later loss unbearable. Let warnings and alternatives be intelligible within the episode: show what the character considered and why they could not take it. Close the episode on the narrowed condition the next one must inherit: a promise now conditional, a meeting now avoided, a duty now heavier, a silence now deliberate.\n\nMake recognition an episode's turning point, not a sudden moral appended at the end. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: a place, a promise, a person's absence, or a duty that remains. Keep the language and period of the actual setting distinct. Do not transplant a cast or impose a generic regional temperament. The episodic form carries the tragedy; it does not require a tournament, a journey, or a fixed number of encounters.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqad::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful diction and compressed transitions to make the fall feel immediate. Cut the connective tissue between decisions and their consequences. A character reaches for something, and the next sentence shows what the reach cost. Keep the prose concrete, but attach each physical detail to a choice or relationship cost: worn gear that fails at the wrong moment, a cracked surface someone keeps touching, a bruise that changes how a person moves, a broken clasp that marks a promise already strained.\n\nGround Kishimoto's emotionally significant action in material stakes. A fight is not just a contest; it damages equipment, exhausts bodies, and leaves marks that persist into the next scene. A relationship conflict is not just words; it changes where people stand, what they do with their hands, and whether they can still look at each other. Let a memory surface through a physical object or a scar, and let it change the present encounter.\n\nVary rhythm according to the character's actual reasoning. Compress the movement from decision to consequence when the character acts on habit, pride, or fear. Lengthen when the character is weighing an alternative, because the tragedy requires that alternative to be intelligible. Make the tragic turn a choice with practical consequences. Recognition should land through a concrete detail: a broken promise, a name carved somewhere, a thing that can no longer be repaired. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored. This is pressure-oriented treatment, not survival horror.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqae::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the tragedy through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Each document should carry Kishimoto's relationship-driven conflict: a letter written to someone the author cannot face, a journal entry about a remembered wound, a report that records an event without understanding what it meant. Let what each document knows or conceals follow from its situation. A letter may omit what the author is ashamed to admit. A report may record facts while missing the relationship that gave them weight.\n\nSequence the documents so each one responds to a prior relationship event or records a new one. A later document can correct an earlier one, or reveal that the earlier author misunderstood what they saw. A material trace—a torn page, a letter never sent, a name crossed out—can show a change of heart without explaining it. Let the gaps between documents carry the fall's progression: what is not written, what is written too late, and what is revised under pressure should matter as much as the documents themselves. Preserve readable continuity without adding an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nMake recognition arrive through the documents themselves. A character reads what they wrote before the fall and understands what they could not see then. A recipient reads a letter too late. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: the unsent letter, the unanswered question, the record of a promise that was broken. Use the specific document form required by the scene, and let the documents carry the tragedy rather than summarize it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqaf::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on chosen kindness, familiar preferences, practical care, ease, and small pleasures. Let the characters enjoy each other: a shared meal, a remembered joke, a small favor done without being asked, a comfortable silence. Use Kishimoto's clear staging to make these moments physically legible—who sits where, who reaches for what, who notices a preference and acts on it. Choose kindnesses that reveal a preference or need the fall will later sever: the way someone takes their tea, the task they always do for the other, the joke only they share, the silence they can keep without strain. Let affection be sincere without requiring a confession or emotional repair.\n\nKeep the tragic arc active through the warmth rather than despite it. The comfort should raise the stakes of the fall. A bond that feels real and good makes the later loss heavier. A moment of care can be the thing a character remembers when everything else is gone. Do not let the comfort remove established problems from the world. Disagreement, privacy, and personality remain intact. The warmth coexists with what is coming, not instead of it.\n\nMake the consequential fall emerge from the same relationships that provided the comfort. A character may choose duty over the person who cared for them, or pride over the person who needed them. Show the alternative they considered and why they could not take it. Recognition should arrive through the memory of warmth: a familiar gesture, a shared preference, a kindness that can no longer be returned. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored, and the comfort makes that loss meaningful rather than softening it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqag::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement to build the fall. Let each scene open with enough narrative distance to situate it: the social context that makes a choice possible, the history that weighs on a relationship, the judgment that will follow. Distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language. The prose should frame the moment, not merely report it.\n\nKeep Kishimoto's relationship-driven conflict at the center, but let the wider context give it consequence. A personal choice about love, duty, or pride should be legible as a social choice as well. A memory that changes a present encounter should also reveal something about the world the characters inhabit. When public and private contrast organizes a passage, move deliberately between the two registers: show the public demand or ceremony that requires one thing, then the private conversation or withheld gesture that reveals another. Let the gap between them become part of the fall rather than a decorative interruption.\n\nMake the tragic turn a choice the character understands within their social position. Show the alternative they considered and why their duty, pride, or love made it impossible. Recognition should arrive through the relationship and through the wider frame: a character sees what their choice meant not only for themselves but for others. The aftermath preserves what cannot be restored, and the formal narration should let that loss settle into the social world rather than remain a private wound. Do not impose an aristocratic setting or archaic vocabulary; the formal architecture is the style, not the scenery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqah::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nTell the loss as a spoken account with a live occasion and a live stake. Someone in the outer scene needs this telling now — to decide something, to forgive someone, to stay watchful through a night — so the embedded tale does work in the room: it persuades, warns, delays, or changes how the listeners read the teller. Keep the two levels easy to tell apart through framing beats: who speaks, who interrupts, what the teller skips or repeats.\n\nLet one phrase, gesture, or object appear both in the outer scene and inside the tale, and let each return add to what it means. The pressure builds when the listeners can compare the told version against outer facts and the two do not quite agree — not a riddle to be solved, but the shape of what the teller still cannot say plainly. An interruption can force the teller onto ground they were skirting; a question about a name or a sequence can reopen a moment they had closed. Wherever the active narration reaches private experience, use it for the gap between the polished tale and what the teller actually lived — the pause before a name, the sentence begun and replaced.\n\nBuild the embedded tragedy around two commitments that could not both be kept — a person, a promise, a home — and stage the choice that made them incompatible as emotionally significant action with clear spatial staging: where people stood, what hands did, what was seen too late. Use the Kishimoto profile's memory-return so an old wound surfaces inside the tale and again at the outer level, changing the present encounter for a concrete personal and practical reason. Let listeners respond as practical people — concern, anger, love — rather than as an audience held in wonder.\n\nLet recognition land at the outer level: a listener names what the teller would not, or the teller sees their own omission in someone else's face. It changes what the loss means without repairing it, and the relationship in the room shifts under that knowledge. The telling may finish or pause wherever the scene's boundary falls; no cliffhanger is owed, and the frame needs no court, bargain, or desert — only the recurring phrase now meaning something the listeners cannot unhear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqai::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and developmental realism route. Hold a medium narrative distance that opens to summarize work, housing, family obligation, and institutions, then closes in on remembered wounds, an old promise, or a moment of physical effort. Let sentence construction carry consequence — subordinate clauses that show what follows from what, plain verbs for labor, exact nouns for rooms, contracts, tools, and offices — following the established narrator rather than an invented translated word order or dialect.\n\nLet the central bond begin as practical alliance: the same shift, the same debt, the same supervision. Trust grows through small repeated reliable actions, the way Kishimoto turns friction and rivalry into loyalty through what people keep doing for each other, and his clear visual staging becomes concrete realist scene-setting — who occupies which room, what the work looks like, what a gesture costs in that setting.\n\nIntroduce the tragic constraint as a social fact the story already supports — a contract, a move, an inherited duty, a public accusation — and show it making the old cooperation impossible without making anyone foolish or willful. Let the fall accumulate through decisions each reasonable at the time it was taken: covering for someone, sending money, keeping a message quiet, honoring a demand. Keep the pressure legible as a system — an employer, a housing rule, a family's expectation — rather than a single villain, and let a remembered moment of shared work or shared danger return when one character sees what the other is now doing, changing the encounter for reasons the memory actually supports.\n\nLet recognition arrive as exact practical understanding: one finally sees the point where their roads diverged and why the other could not follow. It need not be explained aloud. Render aftermath in ordinary continuation — a room kept as it was, a task done more slowly, a message left unanswered — with the loss measured in daily concrete terms rather than a climactic argument. Warmth may survive inside scenes; the arc does not require punishing anyone. Wherever the narration permits interiority, give the focal character's actual reckoning with the choice, not only its visible results.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqaj::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Gothic-Punk\n\nTreat the old authority the story already contains — inherited standing, an institution, a family, a network that still allocates work, shelter, or safety — as a material fact of present urban life. Render it through exact decaying detail: a broken fixture no one repairs, a ledger, keys that no longer fit, the smell of a stairwell. Let atmosphere work on the encounter rather than decorate it: shifts in light, noise, and crowding change how a particular scene feels from inside, and the characters notice.\n\nCenter a bond that began as mutual protection within this world and is shown through practical care. Use the Kishimoto profile's memory-return — one clear flashback to a moment when one of them rescued or believed the other, staged visually enough to read as prose — and let it surface in the present to sharpen why the current fracture hurts. Let the intimacy be morally complicated and genuinely wanted on both sides, never a trap laid from the beginning; private desire and fear get their real interior weight wherever the narration reaches them, since the public mask is what others see, not all the character is.\n\nLet the inherited structure interpret the relationship: a privilege attached to blood or name, a debt, a role, a lineage can force a choice between the bond and practical survival, so that two people who want the same thing cannot keep it on these terms. Social masks organize the telling — performed selves for the authority, and the trusted-company moments where the masks come off, which is exactly what any exposure costs.\n\nWhen a mask falls, make recognition sudden but earned — a name spoken differently, a gesture recognized from before, a document left open — traceable to facts established earlier. Do not resolve or redeem the institution inside the scene. Keep aftermath intimate and urban: the familiar space colder, louder, or emptier than before, the city continuing without that particular shared room. Import nothing of vampire physiology, humanity scores, factions, or blood bonds; the horror here is personal and social. The response can end wherever the scene's boundary falls — the irreversible separation registers through the changed tone of a trusted place, without staging a departure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqak::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Keep attention close on one particular household, shop, ward, or neighborhood within the established narration, and name rooms, utensils, travel routes, and work shifts with the precision actual to the supplied setting. Where a specific language, region, community, or period is given, let its words sit naturally in context; where none is given, add no invented markers, and force no code-switching, religion, caste theme, or standard family shape onto the cast.\n\nStage the central relationship through practical reliability repeated over time — covering an absence, keeping the accounts straight, walking someone home — so that loyalty is proven in what people do, the Kishimoto method of friction becoming trust. Give the remembered wound a concrete shape the story supports: a failure, an accident, a death, a public disgrace. When a present crisis revives it, return it as one clear image that changes the encounter for a personal and practical reason.\n\nLet the tragic pressure come from obligations that are not evil — emigration for work, a fixed marriage, a duty of care, a debt to be honored — so that love and duty cannot both be kept and every choice remains understandable to the reader. Write family members and elders as particular people: some generous, some rigid, none uniformly oppressive; friends not uniformly wise. Dialogue carries requests, refusals, and reassurance in these characters' actual idiom, and not every exchange needs to become confrontation.\n\nLet recognition arrive through a practical perception — a packed bag, a ticket confirmation, a note left by the stove, a task quietly finished — and change what the loss means without undoing it. Render aftermath inside the continuing routine: a meal eaten alone, a call not made, a task performed mechanically, the household going on around an absence. Wherever the narration permits, give the focal character's private reckoning its full interior weight; restraint in speech is not absence of experience. Domestic details can carry that absence at whatever point the scene stops.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqal::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and psychological realism route. Keep vocabulary plain and exact, descriptions short, and narrative distance fitted to the established teller. Understatement governs expression — what characters say and what they allow to show — while the narration still makes the focal character's actual attention, sensation, and feeling available; omission is a surface technique, not a prohibition on interior life. Seasonal and time markers can serve as the scene's emotional clock when they arise naturally — rain at a window, the turn of a school term, a train schedule — without becoming a scenery inventory.\n\nBuild the bond as rivalry or friction that became loyal friendship through remembered exchanges of help: teaching, covering, enduring something together. Keep flashbacks brief and visually clear — one strong image returning when a present echo triggers it — and give the memory a concrete personal and practical basis for what it changes in the encounter, in the Kishimoto manner.\n\nLet the tragic conflict arise from roles that cannot be refused in this world — a marriage, a transfer, a register, an elder's expectation — with both characters intelligent enough to see the constraint whole. Stage the decisive movement in practical moments rather than grand argument: a proposal answered by silence, a ticket bought, a promise released with a polite phrase. The gap between what is said and what is felt drives the scene, and the remembered wound sharpens it when one recognizes in the other an old recklessness now patiently trained away.\n\nLet recognition arrive at small scale — an unsteady pour, the truth inside a hesitating phrase — and let it change what the loss means without forcing confession or repair. Afterward the loss stays inside ordinary settings: a corridor, a platform, a kitchen where a habitual action is now performed with different knowledge. A plain, specific image is available if the scene reaches one; nothing requires a completed ritual, a spoken farewell, or a closing symbol, and the response may stop wherever the scene's own boundary falls.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqam::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate psychological and urban fiction route. Narrate in the established person and distance, with close attention to the neighborhood's lived geography — the bus route, the stall, the rented room, the night shift — treating the city as a pressure system of distance, fares, schedules, and employers rather than a picture postcard. Let the prose take some rhythm from how these characters actually speak where the scene's language supports it, without decorative localism. Preserve the supplied language, community, and history, and add no supernatural fact to a world that excludes it.\n\nForm the central bond under practical need — shared work, shared watchfulness, mutual shelter — with loyalty proven in small repeated acts, following the Kishimoto profile's relationship-driven conflict. Give the remembered wound a concrete history the story supports, and let it return as a sensory memory that both explains the trust and names what these characters fear losing.\n\nLet the tragic incompatibility emerge from diverging exits: one path requires leaving, hiding a shared history, or breaking a commitment; the other is held in place by family, debt, or legal status. Each understands the other's choice even as it costs them, and no villain need arrange the harm. Give every step its practical basis — money, papers, hours, a borrowed vehicle — so the fall is economically legible as well as emotional, and any advance in position has a personal and practical ground rather than arriving because the scene wants it.\n\nBring recognition in close sensory terms grounded in what the scene established: the smell of cheap gasoline on work clothes, a bus pulling away, rain on a plastic tarp. Afterward, stay with ordinary continuation — shifts missed, rent unpaid, a number changed — while the city runs loud and indifferent around a private loss, and carry in interiority whatever the surface hides, wherever the narration reaches it. The remaining character may come to read these streets as shared history; the scene can stop at its own boundary without a staged final image.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqan::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly — inside conversation or motion already running — and orient the reader with one or two crisp spatial cues so every movement stays followable. Keep paragraphs short, with readable breaks separating action, dialogue, and private response. When an unfamiliar name or mechanic matters, give it one clear presentation line and move on.\n\nFollow key lines and turns with a focused reaction beat: a tightened fist, a skipped breath, a short run of unspoken thought where the narration allows it. Write the beat in its owner's actual register — serious, numb, angry, or dryly funny as that particular person is — without explaining it. Let the past bond surface as a brief, vivid image, for instance a shared job, a fight once won together, or a promise made somewhere ordinary; stage it visually, trigger it from something in the present, and give it a concrete personal and practical reason to change the encounter, in the manner of the Kishimoto profile's remembered wounds. No monologue is required to carry it.\n\nMake the tragic core a single decision that cannot be walked back — leaving, refusing help, accepting a duty, telling the truth — taken by an intelligent person for intelligible reasons, with the cost visible on both sides. Where it fits the established speaker and occasion, keep dialogue spare and natural, each line changing something, and keep the staging clear enough that the reader always knows who stands where. After the decision, let its consequence register physically in the room or street: an empty chair, an unread message, the sound of a door in a familiar hallway — so the reader feels what has just become impossible.\n\nThe response can stop wherever its scene boundary falls — mid-motion, on a reaction, with a question still unanswered — owing neither a resolution nor a cliffhanger; the paragraph rhythm should make the loss legible at whatever point the telling pauses. In whatever lines do close the response, keep the grounding concrete and visual so the weight lands without a staged curtain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqao::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Lyrical Prose\n\nUse Kishimoto's clear staging and emotionally significant action, but let lyrical patterning do the work of showing what a bond means before and after it breaks. A present action or exchange can bear a comparison that the narrator returns to later, so that recognition changes the image rather than merely explaining the loss. Keep the comparison traceable to the current scene: what was touched, remembered, or said becomes the material of the repeated figure. When a particular sound or cadence returns, have it return because the relationship's stake has shifted, not because the prose requires ornament.\n\nLet the fall remain intelligible through the characters' reasons and possibilities. Set out the meaningful alternative in the arrangement of the sentence, not in an editorial aside; show that a refusal or return was possible by giving the other path a concrete shape. Stage the consequence in a physically clear moment—an action interrupted, a task finished badly, a promise remembered too exactly—and then let the lyrical passage dwell on that consequence. The inward recognition may arrive through the same image, now changed: the first occurrence may have promised continuity, and the later occurrence may mark what cannot be restored. Let the aftermath preserve a specific detail, word, or gesture rather than converting all warmth into mourning.\n\nDo not require the narrator or characters to speak figuratively everywhere. A plain statement can interrupt a rhythmic passage and carry the decisive information. The style selects and repeats, but does not force a fate where every attachment is already ruin or every insight is melancholy. Warnings and alternatives must remain part of the actual story, and people may act capably even in defeat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqap::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse a clear present encounter as the anchor for reflection. Kishimoto's staging should keep the immediate conflict legible: one person arrives with an errand, a decision, or a memory, and the other responds. Let reflection move between that present difficulty and a precise earlier occasion, so that what is no longer available becomes part of the current stake. A remembered detail—a way of waiting, a shared route, a phrase whose use has changed—carries the sense of small irreversible change. Do not let the reflection erase the action; after the narrator or character returns to the present, the practical consequence should feel heavier, not postponed.\n\nShow the tragic arc through accumulated recognitions. A present decision can be traced to earlier refusals or loyalties, but each step must remain a choice for the person making it. Keep the alternative intelligible: the reflective passage may recall a moment when another course was open, and the return to present action should show why it is now lost. Use remembered ordinary pleasure as a source of meaning, not as a placeholder for sadness. The pleasure can remain pleasurable even while the character recognizes what has gone.\n\nLet recognition land in a concrete sentence or gesture. The reflection may prepare it by returning to the same remembered detail, but the tragic turn should happen in the scene, not in a summary. Afterward, preserve the loss rather than resolving it with comfort. The final movement can leave the real task unfinished or the relationship changed, without requiring every reflection to announce impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqaq::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a framed oral storytelling structure: a situated teller addresses an audience that has asked for or needs to hear a particular account. The teller's present circumstances may briefly frame the tale, but the frame should not overshadow the story. Within the account, keep Kishimoto's staging legible—each exchange is placed clearly, and an action's practical consequence is shown. Let the teller pause to reflect on a relationship or to mark what the listeners already know; these pauses deepen the audience's relation to the loss rather than providing editorial commentary.\n\nChoose the social/domestic route with reflective prose, not a fantastic or mystical apparatus. The tragic material arises from a household, a working partnership, a journey among neighbors, or a public undertaking, and from the ways people are bound by obligation and affection. Tell the story through incidents that the teller selects because they will be remembered. Use a recurring phrase or gesture as the teller's way of organizing repeated moments: first as a promise or sign of trust, then as evidence of a changed relation, finally as part of what the recognition means. Do not import a particular folkloric inventory; let the recurrence grow from the story's own concrete actions.\n\nThe teller may possess later knowledge, but must distinguish it from the characters' understanding at the time. When the teller comments that a choice would prove costly, do so without making the characters foolish. Keep the warnings and alternatives present in the told scenes themselves. End so that the aftermath preserves what cannot be restored: the teller may finish with a particular object, a name, or the audience's silence, rather than a moral lesson. The frame's closing may be brief, but the tragedy should remain a consequence of the characters' choices, not a preordained lesson told at them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqar::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Minimalist Prose\n\nStage each significant action clearly in one or two exact details. A relationship is shown by what the characters handle, where they stand, or what they do not say, rather than by a long analysis. Use direct clauses for the choice and its practical consequence. When a memory changes a present encounter, bring in only the single memory detail that alters the present response; let the reader infer the shared history from its effect.\n\nMake the tragic fall legible through repeated small actions. A recurring gesture or object can return once with meaning, but do not explain the comparison. If a character refuses an alternative, show the alternative as a concrete possibility in the scene—an offered route, a pause, a question—and then show the refusal without summarizing its morality. Keep the characters intelligent: their reasons may be implied by what they choose to notice and by the specific terms of their exchange. Let recognition arrive as a simple observation or a single direct sentence, not a reflective paragraph.\n\nUse omission to preserve what cannot be restored. The final beat may leave the character looking at an object, speaking a name, or completing an ordinary task, and the withheld explanation is the aftermath. Do not delete causality: the reader should be able to infer why the loss matters from the established details. A precise extended sentence may be better than several short fragments when the thought requires it. The style's economy is a selection method, not a ban on all explanation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqas::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite in sustained scene-first prose. Open with the present practical situation and let the narrator move among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience as the immediate scene requires. Use Kishimoto's clear staging: no matter how much the prose moves inward, keep the physical sequence and emotional stakes legible. When a relationship carries the conflict, show the bond through concrete interaction—a shared task, a pointed question, a remembered obligation that changes the current response—rather than telling the reader how close they are.\n\nLet the tragic arc emerge from the characters' reasoning as it is rendered in the prose. Present alternatives not as authorial warnings but as live possibilities: a pause that offers a different choice, a suggestion someone tries to make, a practical plan that would have worked. The narrative may follow the focal character's awareness that the alternative exists, and then show why it is refused or lost. Do not let the fall depend on sudden stupidity. Recognition can arrive through a change in what the character notices or says, and the prose should slow enough at that moment to make the loss felt, but not convert every turn into a summary of the theme.\n\nUse recollection selectively. A memory may return because something in the scene calls it: a tone of voice, an object, a task. Let it change what the character does next, but keep the present action moving. At the end, let the aftermath rest in a concrete detail or an unfinished conversation. The prose should orient the reader with paragraphing and transitions but avoid recapping what the scene already showed. Keep the narrator's access to private experience aligned with the chosen point of view; other characters' pasts may be inferred but not known. This combination does not require modern setting or understated emotion, but it does require the emotion to arise from the scene and its choices.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqat::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nLet scene selection establish the relationship's particular promise. Choose moments that show what these two people do for each other, how they speak when no one else is listening, and what the bond makes possible. Use the established premise, not a default set of familiar tropes. If there is affection, show its particular form; if there is rivalry or comfort, keep it rooted in this pair's history. Give these moments enough space so that the reader knows what will be lost before loss becomes irreversible.\n\nBuild the tragic arc out of private language and repeated moments. A phrase, gesture, or shared joke can return, but its second use must mean something different in the relationship. A callback should signal a change—one partner now hesitates, the old phrase is said with effort, or the familiar comfort has become an accusation. Let the present encounter be staged clearly: Kishimoto-style action is still significant even in an intimate setting. A task done together, a doorway blocked, a hand pulled away, or a decision made in the middle of ordinary domestic work can carry the turn. Do not let the emotional conflict depend on everyone becoming inarticulate or cruel; the loss is stronger if both remain intelligent and if the rejected alternative was real.\n\nLet recognition come through the relationship's own terms. The focal character may realize the change when the private language no longer works, or when a memory from an earlier happy moment returns with new meaning. Preserve the aftermath in a concrete relationship detail: a side of the bed, a photo, a voice message, an unfinished plan. The ending should preserve what cannot be restored without turning every scene into constant pain. Ordinary care can still be warm even near the turn, and the loss is a consequence of choices, not a genre-driven punishment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqau::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nTell the story as a retelling of deeds that will be remembered. Begin with a deliberate opening that names the people and the conflict; let compact declarations introduce a person's established quality or a public consequence. When an action is shown, make its staging clear—who moved first, what was at stake in the moment—so that the chronicle does not flatten conflict into summary. Use Kishimoto's relationship-driven conflict as the material of the account: rivals, companions, and family act for reasons that the audience sees in concrete scenes, not only in the teller's judgment.\n\nUse recurring phrases and names to organize the tragedy. A phrase may attach to a promise, a rivalry, or a task; return to it when the act has acquired a changed meaning, not as an ornamental epithet. The teller may know how things ended, but the scenes should preserve the characters' choices as they were made. Include the alternative so that listeners understand what was refused. When a warning appears, let it arise within the scene—a person speaks against the course, a practical difficulty is visible—rather than as an external admonition. Let recognition occur when the teller pauses to state how an earlier act will be remembered after the loss. The teller's later knowledge may color the account, but should not erase the characters' real deliberation.\n\nThe aftermath belongs to the telling itself. The teller may end by reciting a name, a phrase, or a small deed that no one else remembers, preserving what cannot be restored. Keep the source tradition coherent: do not mix unrelated epic conventions or import a specific pantheon or doom. The account can end with a task resumed or left unfinished; the chronicle's tonal weight comes from the relation of the act to what will be remembered, not from declaring every defeat heroic. Avoid making every character speak like a ceremonial performer; ordinary practical speech can interrupt the elevated register and still belong to the telling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqav::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nGround each consequential choice in the social and domestic obligations of the selected community: family care, work, housing, money, local reputation, or an institution whose rules are legible to the people living under them. Make the setting particular and practical. A room, workplace, or meeting hall matters because of who can enter it, what work happens there, and what the characters owe one another in that space. Let the narrator observe these conditions plainly rather than treating reserve, snow, or isolation as automatic atmosphere.\n\nStage relationship-driven scenes with Kishimoto's clear physical and emotional legibility. When two people meet, make their history visible in how they move, what they avoid, and what practical task brings them together. A remembered wound should enter the present through a specific trigger: a familiar gesture, an object still in use, a name spoken in a changed tone, or a duty that now falls to someone else. The memory changes what the encounter means rather than simply decorating it. An earned advance can occur when a character acts on knowledge or feeling that has been prepared by earlier scenes, but the advance need not repair the underlying loss.\n\nLet warnings and alternatives remain intelligible through the social world itself. Another character can name the risk, offer help, or make a reasonable counterproposal in terms the community would actually use: a neighbor offers to take a shift, a colleague explains what the rule will cost, a family member proposes a different arrangement. The tragic figure may refuse for a supportable reason: loyalty, pride, fear of shame, love, or a mistaken belief about what can still be saved. Show the refusal as an active choice with practical consequences, not as stubbornness the reader is merely told to expect.\n\nUse the social-realist route to keep private experience available in composed prose while distinguishing what a character knows from what the community knows. The narrator may report a focal character's thought, hesitation, or bodily awareness without entering other minds. A neighbor can see the result of a choice without understanding its motive, and the reader can understand the motive without the neighbor becoming omniscient. When the narration moves outward, let it register the visible aftermath of private decisions: a changed routine, an unanswered question, a task done differently.\n\nShow recognition as a change in understanding, not a sudden speech. A character may realize what a choice has cost while continuing to live with its consequences. Let the aftermath preserve what cannot be restored: an empty place at a table, a job that must be done differently, a relationship that remains civil but altered, or a debt that outlasts the person who incurred it. The narration may observe these conditions plainly without insisting that every scene end in punishment.\n\nLet the story move through ordinary time. Compress uneventful intervals, but preserve the changes that matter: a child grows old enough to ask a question, a house changes hands, an institution revises its rules too late. The tragedy accumulates through such changes rather than through a single melodramatic reversal. End a scene or installment on completed work, a pending obligation, or a plain acknowledgment of what has been lost, without requiring a confession or a redemptive turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaqaqaw::♢ * [STYLE] Tragedy / Masashi Kishimoto / Screenplay\n\nWrite the tragedy in concise scene blocks. Use a scene heading when location or time changes, then present visible and audible events in readable action lines. Keep the action specific enough to stage: who enters, what they carry, what they do with their hands, where they stand in relation to another person. Let the physical business carry the relationship. A pause, a turned back, or a task continued too long can show feeling without a line of dialogue explaining it.\n\nGive each speaker a clear cue and let the dialogue fit the immediate situation. Characters may argue, reassure, warn, or refuse. A warning should be intelligible to the audience as a real alternative, not a hollow signal that the fall is coming. The tragic figure can reject help for a supportable reason, and the scene can show that reason in action: loyalty to the wrong person, pride before a witness, fear of losing a role, or a mistaken belief about what can still be saved. Let the refusal appear as a visible choice—a door not opened, a call not made, a task continued past the point of return—rather than only as a spoken line.\n\nUse memories as present visual or auditory triggers. A character may stop at an object, repeat a phrase another person once used, or perform a task in a way learned from someone now absent. The memory changes the present encounter without requiring a flashback. If a flashback is used, make it a clear scene change with its own heading and keep it short enough to serve the present choice.\n\nPreserve required private experience as clearly attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation. It is not dialogue and not available to the other characters. A character may think one thing while saying another, but give the difference a character-specific reason: embarrassment, caution, love, or the need to keep working. Do not turn every private thought into voice-over. Use voice-over only when the chosen sequence needs it and the active contract permits it.\n\nStage recognition through visible behavior and scene contrast. A character may return to a place that once held a different meaning, perform a familiar action with a changed result, or look at someone without speaking. The audience sees the change because the earlier scene established the baseline. Let the scene heading or action line register the changed condition: the same room now empty, the same task now done alone, the same gesture now producing no answer. Do not force a final explanatory speech. The aftermath can be shown in ordinary activity: a seat left empty, a job done alone, a door closed without anger.\n\nLet scenes end on completed action, a pending request, or a visible acknowledgment. A cut may move to a later time or another place, but do not use a cut to manufacture a false cliffhanger. The tragedy advances through accumulated consequences, not through a reversal in every scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
